window.GIQ={"meta":{"lede":"The clicker, interactive, lab and TA-review questions made for the GI histology lectures (2020–2026), set inside the lectures themselves. Each lecture is taught as organized notes in numbered sections and parts. Under every part come its core questions: together they cover everything the part's questions test, one clear version of each point. The rest (repeats of a core question and harder versions) wait in a “More questions” dropdown at the end of the part. Tap an answer to see why it is right, why the others are wrong, and the slides that teach it.","tier":1},"lectures":[{"id":"HIST-11","t":"Digestive tract (H15A, H15B)"},{"id":"HIST-12","t":"Glands of the digestive system (H16)"}],"groups":[{"l":"HIST-11","u":0,"label":"Lamina propria carries blood and lymph vessels","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-14.jpg","cap":"Layer table: lamina propria is loose connective tissue with blood and lymph vessels","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 14"},{"src":"sl/G018-6.jpg","cap":"Mucosa labelled: epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosa above the submucosa","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-54-1","s":"Which of these layers of the digestive system contains blood and lymphatic vessels?","o":["A. Lamina propria","B. Muscularis mucosa","C. Muscularis externa","D. Adventitia","E. Epithelium"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Lamina propria","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Lamina propria.</b> The lecture's layer table describes the lamina propria as loose connective tissue holding <b>blood and lymphatic vessels</b> (in the small intestine, the lacteals of the villi). The submucosa also has large vessels, but it is not a choice.<br>B. Muscularis mucosae: thin smooth muscle that moves the mucosa.<br>C. Muscularis externa: thick muscle coat for peristalsis.<br>D. Adventitia: outer connective tissue binding the organ to its neighbors.<br>E. Epithelium: has no blood vessels; it absorbs and secretes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":54,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":115,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":0,"label":"Lamina propria is part of the mucosa","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-6.jpg","cap":"Mucosa = epithelium, lamina propria and muscularis mucosa, labelled over submucosa","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 6"},{"src":"sl/G016-4.jpg","cap":"Layer diagram: mucosa includes lining epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-9-1","s":"A 60-year-old woman presents with recurrent diarrhea, fatigue, and weight loss. A biopsy of her small intestine reveals immune cell infiltration and inflammation in the lamina propria. This inflammation primarily affects which of the following layers of the GI tract?","o":["A. Submucosa","B. Serosa","C. Mucosa","D. Muscularis externa"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Mucosa","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Mucosa.</b> The mucosa has three parts: the <b>epithelium, the lamina propria</b> and the muscularis mucosae. So inflammation in the lamina propria is inflammation of the mucosa.<br>A. Submucosa: connective tissue below the muscularis mucosae, outside the mucosa.<br>B. Serosa: outermost covering with mesothelium.<br>D. Muscularis externa: the thick inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle layers.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":9,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":0,"label":"Adventitia: esophageal outer coat, no mesothelium","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-11.jpg","cap":"Adventitia: thick connective tissue, no mesothelium; serosa: thin, mesothelium-covered","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/G018-22.jpg","cap":"Esophagus cross-section: adventitia outside; only distal one to two centimeters has serosa","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-5-1","s":"A 54-year-old man presents with dysphagia and unintentional weight loss. An upper endoscopy reveals a mass in the esophagus. Biopsy of the affected tissue shows no serosa and a thick layer of connective tissue directly connecting the esophagus to adjacent structures. Based on the GI tract's histological organization, which of the following best describes the outermost layer of this patient's esophagus?","o":["A. Adventitia","B. Serosa","C. Muscularis mucosae","D. Submucosa"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Adventitia","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Adventitia.</b> Most of the esophagus is bound directly to its neighbors, so its outer coat is a thick layer of loose connective tissue with <b>no mesothelium</b> (no \"finished edge\"). Only the short stretch below the diaphragm has a serosa.<br>B. Serosa: thin connective tissue covered by mesothelium (simple squamous); covers organs hanging free in the peritoneal cavity, like the stomach.<br>C. Muscularis mucosae: thin smooth muscle inside the mucosa.<br>D. Submucosa: connective tissue under the mucosa, with glands and vessels; not the outer layer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 10 (Terminology)","H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":5,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":0,"label":"Epithelium varies most along the tract","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-5.jpg","cap":"Stratified squamous mucosa lines mouth, oropharynx, esophagus and anal canal","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G018-6.jpg","cap":"Simple columnar mucosa lines stomach, small and large intestine","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-47-1","s":"The GI tract is compartmentalized into organs that are specialized for digestion of food and absorption of nutrients. Most variation and specialization along the length of the GI tract occur in which of the following tissue layers?","o":["A. Epithelium of mucosa","B. Lamina propria","C. Muscularis externa","D. Muscularis mucosae","E. Submucosa"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Epithelium of mucosa","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Epithelium of the mucosa.</b> The lining changes most along the tract: <b>stratified squamous</b> in the mouth, esophagus and anal canal; <b>simple columnar</b> with different cell types in the stomach, small intestine and colon. Each organ's special job comes from its epithelium.<br>B. Lamina propria: similar loose connective tissue throughout.<br>C. Muscularis externa: two layers almost everywhere (three in the stomach).<br>D. Muscularis mucosae: thin and similar.<br>E. Submucosa: similar; glands only in the esophagus and duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 40 (Q- The GI tract is compartmentalized into organs that are specialized for digest)","H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":47,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":198,"f":"F0754"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":211,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":1,"label":"Myenteric plexus: muscularis externa, controls motility","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-6.jpg","cap":"Myenteric plexus between inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle layers","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 6"},{"src":"sl/G016-5.jpg","cap":"Enteric nervous system: myenteric (Auerbach) plexus inside the muscle coat","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-144-1","s":"The Myenteric (Auerbach’s) plexus is located in which layer of the digestive tract?","o":["A. Mucosa","B. Submucosa","C. Muscularis externa","D. Adventitia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Muscularis externa","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Muscularis externa.</b> The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies <b>between the inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle layers</b> of the muscularis externa, where it drives peristalsis.<br>A. Mucosa: epithelium, lamina propria and muscularis mucosae; no main nerve plexus.<br>B. Submucosa: holds the other plexus, the submucosal (Meissner) plexus.<br>D. Adventitia: outer connective tissue coat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))","H15B slide 8 (GENERAL ORGANIZATION)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":144,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3952-77-1","s":"A 62-year-old woman presents with complaints of persistent abdominal pain and irregular bowel movements. During your examination, you explain the structure of the gastrointestinal tract and how various layers contribute to its function, particularly emphasizing the role of the myenteric plexus in regulating gut motility. You discuss how the layer of the GI wall containing the autonomic neurons of the myenteric plexus is predominantly composed of a specific tissue type that facilitates these neural functions. In the wall of the gastrointestinal tract, the layer containing the autonomic neurons of the myenteric plexus is characterized by the abundance of what tissue?","o":["A. Lamina propria","B. Epithelia of different types in different GI regions","C. Smooth muscle","D. Dense regular connective tissue","E. Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Smooth muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Smooth muscle.</b> The myenteric plexus sits inside the <b>muscularis externa</b>, between its inner circular and outer longitudinal layers, and that layer is smooth muscle (except in the upper esophagus).<br>A. Lamina propria: loose connective tissue of the mucosa.<br>B. Epithelia that differ by region: the lining layer of the mucosa.<br>D. Dense regular connective tissue: tendons and ligaments, not the gut wall.<br>E. Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT): immune cells in the lamina propria and submucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))","H15B slide 6 (100x)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":77,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0754-144-1"},{"id":"F3950-7-1","s":"A 45-year-old woman complains of intermittent abdominal pain and bloating. She is diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome (IBS). During a colonoscopy, the mucosa appears normal. The patient's symptoms are attributed to uncoordinated contractions of smooth muscle. Which structure is most likely involved in coordinating the abnormal contractions in this patient?","o":["A. Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus","B. Submucosal (Meissner) plexus","C. Muscularis mucosae","D. Serosa"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus.</b> This nerve network lies between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa and <b>coordinates gut motility</b> (peristalsis). Faulty signaling here gives uncoordinated contractions while the mucosa looks normal.<br>B. Submucosal (Meissner) plexus: mainly controls secretion and local blood flow.<br>C. Muscularis mucosae: thin muscle that moves the mucosa; not a nerve center.<br>D. Serosa: outer covering with mesothelium; no motor control.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))","H15B slide 8 (GENERAL ORGANIZATION)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":7,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":1,"label":"Meissner plexus lies in the submucosa","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-5.jpg","cap":"Enteric nervous system: submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G015-14.jpg","cap":"Layer table: submucosa holds the submucosal (Meissner) nerve plexus","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-40-1","s":"While working as a pathology resident, you and your colleague come across the following slide of the colon. You note some unexpected tissue (white arrow) that it appears to be neural tissue, and your colleague believes it to be pathological, but you know that it is actually...","o":["A. Myenteric plexus - found in between the inner and outer layers of the muscularis externa","B. Meissner's plexus - found in the submucosa","C. Auerbach's plexus - found in the subepithelium","D. Peyer's patches - found in the submucosa"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Meissner's plexus - found in the submucosa","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Meissner (submucosal) plexus.</b> The arrow points to pale nerve cells in the connective tissue just under the colon's mucosa, which is the <b>submucosa</b>. This nerve network is normal; it controls secretion and local blood flow.<br>A. Myenteric plexus: real, but it sits between the inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle layers, deeper than the arrow.<br>C. Auerbach's plexus: another name for the myenteric plexus; it is not under the epithelium.<br>D. Peyer's patches: lymphoid nodules of the ileum, not nerve tissue.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-40.jpg","in":"Colon mucosa with goblet-cell-rich crypts above; white arrow points to a small cluster of pale ganglion/nerve cells in the connective tissue just beneath the mucosa (submucosa), above smooth muscle.","tg":["H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))","H15B slide 8 (GENERAL ORGANIZATION)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":40,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":82,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":198,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":180,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":2,"label":"Oral mucosa has no muscularis mucosae","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-24.jpg","cap":"Internal lip: epithelium on lamina propria, with no muscularis mucosae beneath","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G015-15.jpg","cap":"Esophagus by contrast: muscularis mucosae labelled under the lamina propria","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-207-1","s":"13. All of the following structures may be found in oral mucous membrane except","o":["A. lamina propria","B. basal lamina","C. stratified squamous epithelium","D. muscularis mucosae"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"muscularis mucosae","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Muscularis mucosae.</b> Oral mucosa is just <b>stratified squamous epithelium on a basal lamina plus a lamina propria</b>. It has no muscularis mucosae, so the lamina propria blends into the submucosa or sits on bone. A muscularis mucosae first appears in the esophagus.<br>A. Lamina propria: under every oral epithelium.<br>B. Basal lamina: under every epithelium.<br>C. Stratified squamous epithelium: lines the whole oral cavity.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 27 (The oral cavity)","H15A slide 26 (Oral Mucosa)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":207,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":2,"label":"Nonkeratinized and parakeratinized surface cells keep nuclei","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-26.jpg","cap":"Oral epithelium: parakeratinized surface keeps nuclei; keratinized surface loses them","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-16-1","s":"Does the outermost layer of non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium in the oral cavity contain nuclei?","o":["A. Yes","B. No"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Yes","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Yes.</b> In nonkeratinized (and parakeratinized) oral epithelium the flat surface cells <b>keep their nuclei</b> as they are shed. Only fully keratinized epithelium, like skin, has dead surface cells with no nuclei.<br>Common mix-up: \"stratified squamous\" does not mean keratinized. The cheeks, soft palate and floor of the mouth are nonkeratinized, and their top cells are nucleated.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 27 (The oral cavity)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":16,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0754-150-1","s":"What are parakeratinized cells?","o":["A. Epithelial cells with keratin and no nucleus","B. Epithelial cells with no keratin","C. Epithelial cells keratinized, but can still see the nucleus","D. Epithelial cells with kerato-hyaline granules next to the nucleus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Epithelial cells keratinized, but can still see the nucleus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Keratinized cells that still show a nucleus.</b> Parakeratinized epithelium makes keratin at the surface, but its surface cells <b>keep small, shrunken nuclei</b>. Fully (ortho)keratinized cells, as in skin, lose their nuclei.<br>A. Keratin and no nucleus: full keratinization.<br>B. No keratin: nonkeratinized epithelium.<br>D. Keratohyalin granules beside the nucleus: cells of the granular layer, a step on the way to keratin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 27 (The oral cavity)","H15A slide 25 (The lips)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":150,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":2,"label":"Keratinized mucosa: gingiva and hard palate","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-28.jpg","cap":"Palate: masticatory mucosa over hard palate and gingiva; soft palate behind","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 28"},{"src":"sl/G015-27.jpg","cap":"Keratinized: gum, hard palate; nonkeratinized: soft palate, lips, cheeks, floor","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-235-1","s":"19. A structure in the oral cavity that is covered for the most part by stratified squamous keratinized epithelium is the","o":["A. hard palate","B. soft palate","C. cheek","D. tooth"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"hard palate","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Hard palate.</b> Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium (masticatory mucosa) covers the <b>gingiva and hard palate</b>, the surfaces rubbed hardest during chewing.<br>B. Soft palate: nonkeratinized lining mucosa.<br>C. Cheek: nonkeratinized lining mucosa.<br>D. Tooth: covered by enamel, not by epithelium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 27 (The oral cavity)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":235,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F1221-226-2","s":"17. Mucoperiosteum may be found in which one of the following regions?","o":["A. soft palate","B. hard palate","C. lip","D. cheek","E. tongue"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"hard palate","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hard palate.</b> Mucoperiosteum means the mucosa's lamina propria is <b>fused directly to the periosteum of bone</b>. That happens in masticatory mucosa lying on bone: the hard palate (and gingiva).<br>A. Soft palate: lining mucosa over muscle, no bone.<br>C. Lip: skeletal muscle core.<br>D. Cheek: lining mucosa over the buccinator muscle.<br>E. Tongue: mucosa bound to skeletal muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 27 (The oral cavity)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":226,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3950-12-1","s":"A 35-year-old man presents with a persistent ulcer on his hard palate that has not healed for several weeks. On examination, the lesion appears well-defined, and the surrounding tissue is firm and resilient to abrasion. Biopsy reveals stratified squamous epithelium with keratinization. Which of the following is the most likely location of this lesion?","o":["A. Soft palate","B. Gingiva","C. Cheeks","D. Floor of the mouth"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gingiva (gum)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gingiva.</b> A firm, abrasion-resistant lining with <b>keratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b> is masticatory mucosa. It covers only the <b>gingiva (gums) and the hard palate</b>, where the lamina propria sits right on bone; gingiva is the only such site offered.<br>A. Soft palate: nonkeratinized lining mucosa.<br>C. Cheeks: nonkeratinized lining mucosa.<br>D. Floor of the mouth: nonkeratinized lining mucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 27 (The oral cavity)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":12,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F1221-235-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":3,"label":"Lip core is skeletal muscle","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-9.jpg","cap":"Lip: skeletal muscle core (M) between oral mucosa and skin","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G015-25.jpg","cap":"Lip summary: skeletal muscle core; external, vermilion and internal surfaces","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-170-1","s":"Q1. What is true about the structure of the lips?","o":["A. Composed of smooth muscle with some areas of simple squamous non keratinized epithelium","B. Composed of some areas of parakeratinized stratified squamous epithelium with a core of smooth muscle","C. Composed of some areas of parakeratinized stratified squamous epithelium with a core of skeletal muscle","D. Composed of Burt’s Bees"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Composed of some areas of parakeratinized stratified squamous epithelium with a core of skeletal muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Partly parakeratinized stratified squamous epithelium over a skeletal muscle core.</b> The lip's core is <b>skeletal muscle</b> (orbicularis oris), which makes it so mobile. All three surfaces are stratified squamous: keratinized skin outside, the thin vermilion, and oral mucosa inside.<br>A. Smooth muscle and simple squamous: both wrong; the lip has skeletal muscle and stratified epithelium.<br>B. Smooth muscle core: wrong muscle type.<br>D. Joke option.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 25 (The lips)","H15A slide 24 (The lips)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":170,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":163,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":3,"label":"Lip sagittal section: three surfaces","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-9.jpg","cap":"Lip sagittal section: oral mucosa inside, skin with follicles outside, vermilion tip","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G015-21.jpg","cap":"Low-power lip labelled: oral mucosa, skin, hair follicle, vermilion, muscle core","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-33-1","s":"What is this? Be specific on the left and right.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The lips: left = internal portion (oral mucosa); right = external portion (thin skin with hair follicles); vermillion zone at the tip","a":null,"why":"<b>Lip, sagittal section.</b> <b>Left (internal portion):</b> thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous oral mucosa with minor salivary glands beneath and no hair. <b>Right (external portion):</b> thin skin with <b>hair follicles</b> and sebaceous glands. <b>Tip (vermilion zone):</b> very thin keratinized epithelium over capillary loops, no glands. The core is skeletal muscle.<br>Common mix-up: the side with hair follicles is always the skin (outer) side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-33.jpg","in":"Sagittal section of lip: thick stratified squamous epithelium on the left surface, skeletal muscle core, minor glands, hair follicles and sebaceous glands on the right surface; boxes cover the labels.","tg":["H15A slide 21 (The lips)","H15A slide 25 (The lips)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":33,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":121,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":3,"label":"External lip: thin skin with hair follicles","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-22.jpg","cap":"External lip: thin epidermis, hair follicle and sebaceous gland in the dermis","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/G018-10.jpg","cap":"Lip panels compared: external skin, vermilion zone, internal oral mucosa","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-7-1","s":"The following image was most likely obtained from which part of the lip?","o":["A. External portion","B. Vermillion","C. Internal portion"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"External portion","a":"A","why":"<b>A. External portion.</b> The picture shows <b>thin skin</b>: a thin keratinized epidermis with a granular layer and melanin, and a <b>hair follicle and sebaceous gland</b> in the dermis. Only the outer, skin side of the lip has hair and glands.<br>B. Vermilion: very thin epidermis over tall papillae full of capillaries; no hair or glands.<br>C. Internal portion: very thick nonkeratinized oral mucosa, no hair follicles.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-7.jpg","in":"Thin skin: thin epidermis (EP) with stratum granulosum (SG) and melanin (M), hair follicle (HF) and sebaceous gland (SGl) in the dermis.","tg":["H15A slide 22 (The lips)","H15A slide 25 (The lips)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":7,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":9,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":11,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT week 11","sl":9,"f":"F0745"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":64,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":129,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":3,"label":"Vermilion zone: thin epidermis, capillaries, no glands","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-23.jpg","cap":"Vermilion zone: thin epidermis, tall papillae with vessels near the surface","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 23"},{"src":"sl/G018-10.jpg","cap":"Lip panels: vermilion is thin keratinized epithelium over capillary loops","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-133-1","s":"Q3. The following image was most likely obtained from which part of the lip?","o":["A. Vermillion","B. External portion","C. Internal portion"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Vermillion","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Vermilion.</b> A thin epidermis, <b>tall dermal papillae carrying blood vessels close to the surface</b> (the red color), and <b>no hair follicles or glands</b>: that is the vermilion zone.<br>B. External portion: thin skin plus hair follicles and sebaceous glands.<br>C. Internal portion: very thick nonkeratinized epithelium whose flat surface cells keep their nuclei.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-133.jpg","in":"H&E: thin keratinized epithelium (EP) with a tall dermal papilla (circled, arrowheads) carrying blood vessels (BV) close to the surface; no hair follicles or glands; inset shows stratum granulosum (SG) and a Merkel cell (MC).","tg":["H15A slide 23 (The lips)","H15A slide 25 (The lips)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":133,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3950-14-1","s":"A 40-year-old woman complains of recurrent dryness and cracking of her lips, particularly in the red area between her skin and the oral mucosa. She notes that the dryness worsens in cold weather. Examination reveals a dry, erythematous region without visible sweat or salivary glands. Which area of the lips is affected?","o":["A. Internal mucous surface","B. Vermilion zone","C. Outer surface","D. Floor of the mouth"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vermilion zone","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vermilion zone.</b> The red part of the lip has a very thin keratinized epidermis over <b>capillary loops</b> (the pink-red color) and <b>no sweat or oil glands</b>. Only saliva from the tongue keeps it moist, so it dries and cracks in cold weather.<br>A. Internal mucous surface: thick wet oral mucosa with minor salivary glands.<br>C. Outer surface: thin skin with hair follicles, sebaceous and sweat glands.<br>D. Floor of the mouth: nonkeratinized lining mucosa, not part of the lip.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 23 (The lips)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":14,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":3,"label":"Internal lip: thick oral mucosa, nucleated surface","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-24.jpg","cap":"Internal lip: very thick epithelium; arrows on flat surface cells keeping nuclei","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G018-10.jpg","cap":"Lip panels: internal oral mucosa, nonkeratinized stratified squamous with minor glands","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-131-1","s":"Q2. The following image was most likely obtained from which part of the lip?","o":["A. Vermillion","B. External portion","C. Internal portion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal portion","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Internal portion.</b> A <b>very thick stratified squamous epithelium</b> with no hair follicles or glands, and flat surface cells that <b>keep their nuclei</b> (arrows): oral mucosa lining the inside of the lip.<br>A. Vermilion: thin epidermis with capillaries close to the surface.<br>B. External portion: thin skin with hair follicles and sebaceous glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-131.jpg","in":"H&E: very thick stratified squamous epithelium (EP, boxed in red) with tall connective-tissue papillae (arrowheads), no adnexa; inset of surface with stratum granulosum (SG) and arrows on flattened surface cells that retain nuclei.","tg":["H15A slide 24 (The lips)","H15A slide 25 (The lips)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":131,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0745-8-1","s":"Lip: What are the arrows pointing to? Is this internal or external lip?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Arrows: parakeratinized cells (nuclei still present in the keratin layer); internal portion of the lip (thick stratified squamous oral mucosa)","a":null,"why":"<b>Internal portion of the lip.</b> The epithelium is very thick stratified squamous with tall connective tissue papillae and <b>no hair follicles or glands</b>: oral mucosa. The arrows mark flat surface cells that <b>still have nuclei</b> (parakeratinized). Fully keratinized skin cells lose their nuclei.<br>Common mix-up: the vermilion zone also lacks hair, but its epithelium is thin with capillaries close to the surface.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0745-8.jpg","in":"Two H&E panels of lip: thick stratified squamous epithelium (EP) with tall connective-tissue papillae and no hair follicles/glands; high-power inset of the surface (SG) with arrows on flattened superficial cells that still retain nuclei.","tg":["H15A slide 24 (The lips)","H15A slide 25 (The lips)","H15A slide 27 (The oral cavity)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT week 11","sl":8,"f":"F0745"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0052-131-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":3,"label":"Cold sores: herpes simplex virus type 1","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-46.jpg","cap":"Herpes simplex type 1 cold sore on the lip","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-66-1","s":"A 19-year-old woman presents with painful cold sores on her lower lip. Physical examination reveals several vesicles and ulcers in the lesion area. Infection with which of the following pathogens is the common cause of cold sores?","o":["A. Borrelia vincentii","B. Epstein-Barr virus","C. Herpes simplex virus type 1","D. Human herpes virus 8","E. Streptococcus pyogenes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Herpes simplex virus type 1","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Herpes simplex virus type 1.</b> It infects lip epithelium and kills the cells, giving <b>painful clustered vesicles and ulcers</b> (cold sores). The virus hides in nearby nerves and returns with stress, fever or illness.<br>A. Borrelia vincentii: trench mouth (necrotizing gingivitis).<br>B. Epstein-Barr virus: hairy leukoplakia on the sides of the tongue, and mononucleosis.<br>D. Human herpesvirus 8: Kaposi sarcoma.<br>E. Streptococcus pyogenes: pharyngitis and tonsillitis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 44 (Q1- A 19-year-old woman presents with painful cold sores on her lower lip. Physi)","H15A slide 45 (Q1- A 19-year-old woman presents with painful cold sores on her lower lip. Physi)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":66,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":173,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":4,"label":"Tongue: skeletal muscle in three planes","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-20.jpg","cap":"Tongue core: striated bundles cut obliquely (O), across (C), lengthwise (L)","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/G015-38.jpg","cap":"Tongue: mass of skeletal muscle intersecting in all three planes","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 38"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-35-1","s":"Sam got drunk last night and decided that it was a good idea to get a tongue piercing. What is true about the tongue?","o":["A. Composed of a mass of skeletal muscle in 3 different planes","B. Composed of skeletal muscle circumferentially layering on top of each other","C. Composed of smooth muscle with a Myenteric plexus","D. Composed of dense regular CT"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Composed of a mass of skeletal muscle in 3 different planes","a":"A","why":"<b>A. A mass of skeletal muscle in three planes.</b> The tongue is a mass of <b>skeletal (striated) muscle</b> whose bundles run and cross in <b>all three planes</b>. That interlacing gives the tongue its great mobility; mucosa covers it.<br>B. Circumferential layers: describes gut-type muscle coats, not the tongue.<br>C. Smooth muscle with a myenteric plexus: the gut wall from the lower esophagus down.<br>D. Dense regular connective tissue: tendons and ligaments.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 38 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":35,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":175,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":166,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0754-153-1","s":"How are the muscle fibers of the tongue organized?","o":["A. Circular, oblique and longitudinal","B. Circular and longitudinal","C. Longitudinal","D. Circular, oblique, longitudinal and vertically"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Circular, oblique and longitudinal","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Circular, oblique and longitudinal.</b> This is the intended \"three planes\" answer: the lecture says tongue muscle fibers <b>intersect in all three planes</b>, so in a section the bundles look cut lengthwise, across and obliquely. Strictly, the intrinsic fibers run <b>longitudinally, transversely and vertically</b>.<br>B. Circular and longitudinal: the two-layer muscularis externa of the gut.<br>C. Longitudinal only: one plane.<br>D. Four directions: there are three planes, not four.","ex":"","fl":["Keyed A is the intended 'three planes' choice, but its wording is inaccurate: tongue skeletal muscle bundles run longitudinally, transversely and vertically, interlacing at right angles (G015:38 'intersect in all three planes'); there is no circular/oblique layer, so no option is strictly correct."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 38 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":153,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3980-35-1"},{"id":"F0755-216-1","s":"What direction does the muscle fibers of the tongue arranged in ?","o":["A. Longitudinal","B. Longitudinal and circular","C. Longitudinal, circumferential and oblique"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Longitudinal, circumferential and oblique","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Longitudinal, circumferential and oblique.</b> This is the intended \"three directions\" answer: tongue skeletal muscle bundles <b>cross in all three planes</b>. Strictly, the intrinsic fibers run <b>longitudinally, transversely and vertically</b>, so in sections they look cut lengthwise, across and obliquely.<br>A. Longitudinal only: one plane.<br>B. Longitudinal and circular: the two-layer muscularis externa of the gut.","ex":"","fl":["Keyed C is the intended 'three directions' choice, but tongue skeletal muscle fibres run longitudinally, transversely and vertically (G015:38 'intersect in all three planes'), not circumferentially/obliquely; no option is worded correctly."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 38 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":216,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3980-35-1"},{"id":"F3955-13-1","s":"A tongue is examined at autopsy (shown in the image). Identify the tissue indicated by the letters:","o":["A. Dense irregular connective tissue","B. Dense regular connective tissue","C. Peripheral nerve","D. Skeletal muscle","E. Smooth muscle"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Skeletal muscle","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Skeletal muscle.</b> The letters mark bundles of long, pink, multinucleated <b>striated fibers cut in three directions</b>: O (oblique), C (cross) and L (lengthwise). Skeletal fibers crossing in all three planes are the signature of the tongue.<br>A. Dense irregular connective tissue: wavy collagen with few nuclei.<br>B. Dense regular connective tissue: parallel collagen with flat nuclei, as in tendons.<br>C. Peripheral nerve: wavy axons wrapped in perineurium.<br>E. Smooth muscle: small spindle cells with central nuclei.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-13.jpg","in":"Bundles of pink striated fibers cut in three planes: O (oblique), C (cross), L (longitudinal).","tg":["H15A slide 38 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":13,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":70,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":177,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":68,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":135,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":4,"label":"Glands drain into lingual, not palatine, tonsil crypts","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-31.jpg","cap":"Tongue root: palatine tonsils at the sides, lingual tonsil behind the papillae","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-190-3","s":"12. All of the following characterize palatine tonsils except","o":["A. stratified squamous epithelium","B. ducts of mucous glands open into crypts","C. crypts","D. lymphatic nodules"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"ducts of mucous glands open into crypts","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ducts of mucous glands opening into crypts.</b> That describes the <b>lingual tonsil</b>, whose shallow crypts are flushed by mucous glands. Palatine tonsils have no such glands, so debris collects in their deep crypts.<br>A. Stratified squamous epithelium: covers the palatine tonsil and lines its crypts.<br>C. Crypts: deep, branching crypts are typical of palatine tonsils.<br>D. Lymphatic nodules: line the crypts of every tonsil.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":190,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":5,"label":"Filiform papillae: pointed, keratinized, no taste buds","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Four papilla types: filiform panel pointed and keratinized, without taste buds","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/G015-35.jpg","cap":"Tongue surface: pointed filiform (FI) beside mushroom-shaped fungiform (F) papillae","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-219-1","s":"Which type of papillae has no taste buds?","o":["A. Fungiform","B. Filiform","C. Vallate","D. Foliate"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Filiform","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Filiform.</b> Filiform papillae are pointed and <b>heavily keratinized and have no taste buds</b>; their job is friction to move food.<br>A. Fungiform: a few taste buds on the top.<br>C. Vallate: hundreds of taste buds on the side walls.<br>D. Foliate: taste buds in the clefts between the ridges.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":219,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3950-22-1","s":"Which of the following papillae are most likely responsible for providing the rough texture that facilitates food movement during mastication?","o":["A. Fungiform papillae","B. Vallate papillae","C. Filiform papillae","D. Foliate papillae"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Filiform papillae","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Filiform papillae.</b> They are the most numerous papillae: <b>long, pointed and heavily keratinized</b>, giving the tongue a rough surface that grips food during chewing. They have no taste buds.<br>A. Fungiform: mushroom-shaped and scattered, with a few taste buds.<br>B. Vallate: eight to twelve large papillae in a V near the sulcus terminalis, full of taste buds.<br>D. Foliate: ridges on the sides of the tongue; small in adults.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)","H15A slide 34 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":22,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3980-71-1","s":"Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Fungiform papillae","C. Circumvallate papillae","D. Foliate papillae"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Filiform papillae","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Filiform papillae.</b> Rows of <b>slender, pointed, cone-shaped papillae with heavily keratinized tips</b> and no taste buds are filiform. They are the most numerous papillae and make the tongue rough.<br>B. Fungiform: mushroom-shaped, lightly keratinized, a few taste buds on top.<br>C. Circumvallate: one huge papilla sunk in a trench, taste buds on its sides.<br>D. Foliate: parallel ridges with taste buds in the clefts between them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-71.jpg","in":"Dorsal tongue surface with a row of pointed, conical papillae capped by keratinized squamous epithelium, no taste buds; skeletal muscle below.","tg":["H15A slide 34 (The tongue)","H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":71,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":17,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":19,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":21,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":23,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":141,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-49-1"},{"id":"G018-49-1","s":"Q2. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Filiform papillae","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Filiform papillae.</b> Rows of slender, pointed cones capped with keratin (the pale layer at the tips). They have no taste buds. They give the tongue its rough surface and are the most numerous papillae.<br>B. Foliate: parallel ridges separated by deep clefts, with taste buds on the walls.<br>C. Fungiform: mushroom-shaped, with a connective-tissue core and taste buds on top.<br>D. Circumvallate: one large papilla in a circular trench, with taste buds on its walls.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-49.jpg","in":"Row of pointed, slanted papillae capped with keratin; no taste buds. Label blacked out.","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":49,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":5,"label":"Fungiform papilla: mushroom shape, connective tissue core","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Fungiform papilla: mushroom-shaped with connective tissue core, taste buds on top","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/G018-12.jpg","cap":"Fungiform (F) papilla among pointed filiform (FI) papillae","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-139-1","s":"Q6. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Fungiform papillae","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Fungiform papillae.</b> A single <b>mushroom-shaped</b> papilla with a broad, lightly keratinized top and a <b>connective tissue core</b>, standing among pointed filiform papillae. Its few taste buds sit on the top surface (arrows).<br>A. Filiform: the pointed papillae around it.<br>B. Foliate: rows of parallel ridges with taste buds in deep clefts.<br>D. Circumvallate: very large, sunk in a trench, with glands below.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-139.jpg","in":"H&E: a mushroom-shaped papilla with a connective-tissue core, between pointed papillae; arrows on its dorsal epithelium (name label blacked out).","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)","H15A slide 34 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":139,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":5,"label":"Circumvallate papilla: trench, taste buds, glands","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-35.jpg","cap":"Vallate papilla: trench, taste buds (TB) on walls, glands (GL) below","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 35"},{"src":"sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Circumvallate papilla with taste buds on its walls and serous glands beneath","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-137-1","s":"Q5. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Circumvallate papillae","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Circumvallate papillae.</b> One <b>large dome sunk below the surface and ringed by a deep trench</b>, with <b>taste buds on its side walls</b> and <b>serous glands</b> beneath that empty into the trench.<br>A. Filiform: pointed, keratinized, no taste buds.<br>B. Foliate: several parallel ridges with taste buds in the clefts.<br>C. Fungiform: mushroom-shaped, stands above the surface, few taste buds on top.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-137.jpg","in":"H&E: a large dome-shaped papilla sunk below the surface and surrounded by a deep trench, with taste buds in its lateral walls and serous glands beneath (name label blacked out).","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)","H15A slide 34 (The tongue)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":137,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-48-1"},{"id":"F0722-123-1","s":"What is this? Be specific.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Tongue – circumvallate papilla","a":null,"why":"<b>Circumvallate (vallate) papilla of the tongue.</b> It is the <b>largest papilla</b>, sunk below the surface and ringed by a deep <b>trench</b>. Taste buds (labelled TB) line its side walls, and small serous (von Ebner) glands (labelled GL) below empty into the trench to rinse it. Eight to twelve sit in a V just in front of the sulcus terminalis.<br>Common mix-up: fungiform papillae are mushroom-shaped and stand above the surface, with taste buds on top.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-123.jpg","in":"Large dome papilla sunk in a trench with taste buds (TB) on its walls, glands (GL) at the base of the trench, stratified squamous surface (SS).","tg":["H15A slide 34 (The tongue)","H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":123,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-48-1"},{"id":"G018-48-1","s":"Q1. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Circumvallate papillae","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Circumvallate papillae.</b> One large, flat-topped papilla sunk below the surface in a circular trench. Taste buds line its side walls, and serous (von Ebner) glands below it empty into the trench. These papillae form the V-shaped row just in front of the sulcus terminalis.<br>A. Filiform: many small, pointed, keratinized cones with no taste buds.<br>B. Foliate: several parallel ridges with clefts between them, on the sides of the tongue.<br>C. Fungiform: a single mushroom-shaped papilla raised above the surface, with taste buds on its top.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-48.jpg","in":"One large flat-topped papilla in a deep trench; taste buds labeled on its walls; serous glands labeled below. The name of the papilla is blacked out.","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":48,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":5,"label":"Von Ebner glands flush vallate papillae","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Circumvallate papilla with serous (von Ebner) glands opening into its trench","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-24-1","s":"Which of the following structures is most directly involved in flushing the vallate papillae to facilitate gustation?","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Von Ebner glands","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Sulcus terminalis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Von Ebner glands","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Von Ebner glands.</b> These <b>serous salivary glands</b> sit under the circumvallate (vallate) papillae and empty into the trench around each papilla. Their watery secretion <b>flushes the trench</b> so the taste buds can sample new tastes.<br>A. Filiform papillae: most numerous; keratinized, no taste buds.<br>C. Fungiform papillae: mushroom-shaped, a few taste buds, no glands of their own.<br>D. Sulcus terminalis: the V-shaped groove between the front two-thirds and back third of the tongue.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"'Supplemental Slide 126 Tongue': von Ebner's glands and their duct beneath a vallate papilla (not needed to answer).","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":24,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":5,"label":"Foliate papillae: parallel ridges with taste-bud clefts","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Foliate papillae: parallel ridges, taste buds in clefts, serous glands below","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/G015-33.jpg","cap":"Diagrams of filiform, fungiform, vallate and foliate papillae","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-143-1","s":"Q8. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Foliate papillae","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Foliate papillae.</b> A <b>row of parallel leaf-like ridges</b> separated by deep clefts, with <b>taste buds in the cleft walls</b> and serous glands and ducts below. Foliate papillae sit on the sides of the tongue and are best developed in children.<br>A. Filiform: pointed, keratinized, no taste buds.<br>C. Fungiform: a single mushroom shape.<br>D. Circumvallate: one large papilla in a ring-shaped trench, not a row of ridges.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-143.jpg","in":"H&E: a series of parallel leaf-like ridges separated by deep clefts (arrows), with taste buds in the cleft walls, ducts and serous glands below (name label blacked out).","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)","H15A slide 34 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":143,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-50-1"},{"id":"G018-50-1","s":"Q3. Name the type of papillae:","o":["A. Filiform papillae","B. Foliate papillae","C. Fungiform papillae","D. Circumvallate papillae"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Foliate papillae","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Foliate papillae.</b> Several parallel, leaf-like ridges separated by deep, narrow clefts. Taste buds line the walls of the clefts, and ducts of serous glands open into the clefts. They sit on the sides of the back of the tongue.<br>A. Filiform: pointed keratinized cones, no taste buds.<br>C. Fungiform: a single mushroom shape, not a row of ridges with clefts.<br>D. Circumvallate: one large papilla surrounded by a circular trench, not several parallel ridges.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-50.jpg","in":"Several parallel ridges separated by narrow clefts (arrows); taste buds, ducts and serous glands labeled. Label blacked out.","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":50,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":5,"label":"Four papilla types side by side","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-17.jpg","cap":"Four papilla types side by side: filiform, foliate, fungiform, circumvallate","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/G015-34.jpg","cap":"Pointed filiform papilla (A) next to mushroom-shaped fungiform papilla (B)","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"F0745-13-1","s":"Tongue Papillae (Filiform, Fungiform, Vallate, Foliate): identify the papillae labelled A–E.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A: filiform; B: fungiform; C: fungiform; D: filiform; E: vallate","a":null,"why":"<b>A filiform, B fungiform, C fungiform, D filiform, E vallate.</b> <b>Filiform</b> (A, D): slender, pointed, heavily keratinized, no taste buds. <b>Fungiform</b> (B, C): mushroom-shaped with a broad top and a connective tissue core. <b>Vallate</b> (E): one large papilla sunk in a trench, taste buds on its walls and glands at its base. Foliate papillae (parallel ridges) are not shown.<br>Common mix-up: pointed tip = filiform; rounded cap = fungiform.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0745-13.jpg","in":"Three tongue micrographs: (left) a pointed keratinized papilla A next to a mushroom-shaped papilla B; (bottom) dorsal surface with a broad rounded papilla (arrow C) and a slender pointed papilla (arrow D); (right) a large papilla sunk in a trench with taste buds (TB) and glands (GL) in its walls (arrow E).","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)","H15A slide 34 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT week 11","sl":13,"f":"F0745"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0052-139-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":6,"label":"Taste buds line vallate papilla walls","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-37.jpg","cap":"Taste buds: pale oval cell clusters within the papilla epithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 37"},{"src":"sl/G015-35.jpg","cap":"Vallate papilla walls lined by taste buds (TB) facing the trench","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-179-1","s":"First-year medical students are reviewing histologic features of the tongue. The sides of a circumvallate papilla are examined. Identify the oval pale-stained structure indicated by the arrow.","o":["A. Circumvallate papilla","B. Fungiform papilla","C. Mucous gland","D. Serous salivary gland","E. Taste bud"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Taste bud","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Taste bud.</b> A <b>pale oval cluster of spindle-shaped cells inside the stratified epithelium</b> on the side of a circumvallate papilla, opening toward the trench, is a taste bud.<br>A. Circumvallate papilla: the whole structure being examined, not the oval inside it.<br>B. Fungiform papilla: a different papilla type.<br>C. Mucous gland: lies in the connective tissue below, with pale bubbly cells.<br>D. Serous (von Ebner) gland: lies below the papilla in connective tissue and empties into the trench.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"H&E of the lateral wall of a circumvallate papilla: the arrow points to a pale oval intraepithelial structure of spindle-shaped cells opening onto the trench.","tg":["H15A slide 49 (Q3- First-year medical students are reviewing histologic features of the tongue.)","H15A slide 50 (Q3- First-year medical students are reviewing histologic features of the tongue.)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":179,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0755-218-1"},{"id":"F0755-218-1","s":"Identify the pale-stain oval structure. What is it and what papillae is the slide depicting?","o":["A. Supporting cell: filiform","B. Taste bud: Vallate","C. Goblet cell: Funfigorm","D. Supporting cell: Foliate"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Taste bud: Vallate","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Taste bud; vallate papilla.</b> The arrow marks a <b>pale oval taste bud</b> inside the epithelium on the wall of a papilla bordered by a <b>deep trench</b>, which makes it a vallate (circumvallate) papilla.<br>A. Supporting cell; filiform: filiform papillae have no taste buds.<br>C. Goblet cell; fungiform: tongue epithelium has no goblet cells.<br>D. Supporting cell; foliate: the arrow marks a whole bud, and foliate papillae have clefts between ridges, not a ring-shaped trench.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0755-218.jpg","in":"H&E of the lateral wall of a papilla bordered by a deep trench: the arrow points to a pale oval intraepithelial taste bud.","tg":["H15A slide 49 (Q3- First-year medical students are reviewing histologic features of the tongue.)","H15A slide 50 (Q3- First-year medical students are reviewing histologic features of the tongue.)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":218,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3952-82-1","s":"During a charity event, renowned chef and television personality Gordon Ramsay mingles with guests while sharing insights about his culinary experiences and the importance of taste in cooking. As he passionately describes the flavors that can elevate a dish, he emphasizes the role of the tongue in detecting different tastes, highlighting the various structures involved in this process. When asked where taste buds are found in the highest concentration in the oral cavity, he confidently explains the specific type of papillae that plays a crucial role in flavor perception. In which of the following structures of the oral cavity would taste buds be localized in the highest concentration?","o":["A. Fungiform papillae","B. Gingiva","C. Filiform papillae","D. Ventral surface of the tongue","E. Vallate papillae"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Vallate papillae","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Vallate papillae.</b> About two hundred fifty taste buds line the side walls of <b>each vallate papilla</b>, the highest concentration in the mouth. Serous von Ebner glands rinse their trenches.<br>A. Fungiform papillae: only a few taste buds on top.<br>B. Gingiva: keratinized gum, no taste buds.<br>C. Filiform papillae: keratinized, no taste buds.<br>D. Ventral surface of the tongue: smooth lining mucosa without papillae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 39 (The tongue)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":82,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":6,"label":"Taste bud cells and microvilli in the pore","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-36.jpg","cap":"Taste bud diagram: gustatory pore, microvilli, support, gustatory and basal cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 36"},{"src":"sl/G018-18.jpg","cap":"Taste bud: gustatory microvilli reach the taste pore; supporting and basal cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-156-1","s":"What do the gustatory cells have that helps with absorption of chemicals, helping for taste identification?","o":["A. Cilia","B. Stereocilia","C. Microvilli","D. Goblet cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Microvilli","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Microvilli.</b> Each gustatory cell ends in <b>microvilli that project into the taste pore</b>. Tastants dissolved in saliva bind receptors on these microvilli.<br>A. Cilia: motile hairs of airway epithelium.<br>B. Stereocilia: long microvilli of the epididymis and inner-ear hair cells.<br>D. Goblet cells: mucus-secreting cells; not in taste buds.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 36 (Gustatory pore)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":156,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3955-26-1"},{"id":"F0747-50-1","s":"Taste buds: Identify the structures above (the three blank labels on the taste-bud diagram, top to bottom, between 'Gustatory pore' and 'Basal cell'). Check your answers by sliding the boxes...","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"(1) Microvilli (at the gustatory pore); (2) Support (sustentacular) cell; (3) Gustatory (taste) cell","a":null,"why":"<b>Microvilli, supporting cell, gustatory (taste) cell.</b> <b>Gustatory cells</b> send apical microvilli up into the gustatory pore, where tastants dissolved in saliva touch them. <b>Supporting cells</b> sit between them. <b>Basal cells</b> at the bottom are stem cells that replace taste cells every seven to ten days, and afferent nerve fibers synapse on the gustatory cells.<br>Common mix-up: taste cells carry microvilli, not cilia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0747-50.jpg","in":"Line drawing of a taste bud in lingual epithelium (Junqueira) beside an H&E taste bud; labels shown: Lingual epithelium, Gustatory pore, Basal cell, Afferent nervous fiber; three purple boxes hide the labels for the apical microvilli, a dark spindle-shaped cell and a pale spindle-shaped cell.","tg":["H15A slide 36 (Gustatory pore)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Histology E2 2023 TA: GI part only","sl":50,"f":"F0747"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3955-26-1"},{"id":"F3955-26-1","s":"Name the structures numbered as #1, #2 and #3, respectively:","o":["A. Supporting cells; Cilia; Sensory cells","B. Supporting cells; Taste pore; Sensory cells","C. Goblet cells; Stereocilia; Supporting cells","D. Sensory cells; Microvilli; Basal cells","E. Basal cells; Taste pore; Sensory cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sensory cells; Microvilli; Basal cells","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Sensory cells; microvilli; basal cells.</b> #1 marks the pale elongated <b>gustatory (sensory) cells</b> inside the bud. #2 marks their <b>microvilli</b> reaching into the taste pore. #3 marks a small <b>basal (stem) cell</b> at the base, above the nerve.<br>A. Supporting cells; cilia: #1 are sensory cells, and taste cells bear microvilli, not cilia.<br>B. Taste pore for #2: #2 points to the microvilli inside the pore.<br>C. Goblet cells; stereocilia: neither is in a taste bud.<br>E. Basal cells for #1: #1 is inside the bud, not at its base.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-51.jpg","in":"High-power taste bud: #1 on elongated pale cells inside the bud, #2 at the apical opening where processes project into the pore, #3 on a small cell at the bud base above the nerve.","tg":["H15A slide 36 (Gustatory pore)","H15A slide 37 (The tongue)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":51,"f":"G018"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":26,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":72,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":145,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":7,"label":"Enamel covers the crown","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-41.jpg","cap":"Tooth diagram: enamel covers the crown; cementum covers the root","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-28-1","s":"A woman presents to the dentist with a cavity. It has penetrated the outermost layer of the tooth. What is the outermost layer of the tooth?","o":["A. Enamel","B. Dentin","C. Periodontal ligament","D. Cementum"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Enamel","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Enamel.</b> The crown is covered by <b>enamel</b>, the hardest tissue in the body (about ninety-six percent hydroxyapatite), made by ameloblasts. A cavity must cross enamel before it reaches dentin.<br>B. Dentin: the bulk of the tooth, under the enamel and cementum.<br>C. Periodontal ligament: collagen fibers holding the root in its socket.<br>D. Cementum: covers the root, not the crown.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 41 (Teeth)","H15A slide 42 (The teeth)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":28,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":7,"label":"Tooth mineral: hydroxyapatite (calcium phosphate)","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-42.jpg","cap":"Teeth text: dentine is seventy percent hydroxyapatite by wet weight","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-235-3","s":"23. The major inorganic component of dentin is","o":["A. collagen","B. lactate","C. hydroxyapatite","D. lipid","E. desmosine"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"hydroxyapatite","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hydroxyapatite.</b> Dentin is a calcified tissue harder than bone, about <b>seventy percent hydroxyapatite</b> (calcium phosphate crystals).<br>A. Collagen: the main organic part (type I).<br>B. Lactate: a metabolite.<br>D. Lipid: trace only.<br>E. Desmosine: a cross-link of elastin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 42 (The teeth)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":235,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F1221-190-2","s":"11. The major inorganic constituent of enamel by weight is","o":["A. collagen","B. sodium","C. phosphate","D. carbonate"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"phosphate","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Phosphate.</b> Enamel is about ninety-six percent <b>calcium hydroxyapatite</b>, a calcium phosphate crystal. By weight, phosphate is its largest inorganic part, and calcium is not offered.<br>A. Collagen: organic, and enamel has none.<br>B. Sodium: a trace ion.<br>D. Carbonate: a minor substitute in the crystal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 42 (The teeth)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":190,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"harder","like":"F1221-235-3"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":7,"label":"Collagen: in dentin, absent from enamel","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-42.jpg","cap":"Ameloblasts secrete enamel matrix; dentine is ninety percent type I collagen","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-235-2","s":"20. The major organic component of dentin is","o":["A. collagen","B. lactate","C. hydroxyapatite","D. lipid","E. desmosine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"collagen","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Collagen.</b> Odontoblasts secrete predentin made mostly of <b>type I collagen</b> (the lecture says about ninety percent of the organic part), which then mineralizes.<br>B. Lactate: a metabolite, not matrix.<br>C. Hydroxyapatite: the inorganic mineral, about seventy percent of dentin.<br>D. Lipid: trace only.<br>E. Desmosine: a cross-link of elastin, not dentin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 42 (The teeth)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":235,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F1221-226-3","s":"18. All of the following cell types may produce collagen fibers except?","o":["A. odontoblasts","B. ameloblasts","C. cementoblasts","D. osteoblasts","E. Chondroblasts"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"ameloblasts","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ameloblasts.</b> Ameloblasts secrete an enamel matrix of proteins such as amelogenin with <b>no collagen</b>. All the others build collagen-based matrices.<br>A. Odontoblasts: type I collagen of dentin.<br>C. Cementoblasts: collagen of cementum.<br>D. Osteoblasts: type I collagen of bone (osteoid).<br>E. Chondroblasts: type II collagen of cartilage.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 42 (The teeth)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":226,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":7,"label":"Cementoblasts first make cementoid","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-41.jpg","cap":"Cementum layer over root dentin, laid down by cementoblasts","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-226-1","s":"16. Cementoblasts first produce","o":["A. secondary cementum","B. Sharpey's fibers","C. cementoid","D. primary cementum","E. cementocytes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"cementoid","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cementoid.</b> Like osteoblasts making osteoid and odontoblasts making predentin, cementoblasts first secrete an <b>uncalcified matrix, cementoid</b>, which then mineralizes into cementum.<br>A. Secondary cementum: later, cellular cementum.<br>B. Sharpey's fibers: ligament collagen trapped in cementum and bone.<br>D. Primary cementum: the first mineralized (acellular) layer, formed from cementoid.<br>E. Cementocytes: cementoblasts that became trapped in their matrix.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 41 (Teeth)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":226,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":7,"label":"Periodontal ligament inserts into cementum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-41.jpg","cap":"Periodontal ligament between root cementum and alveolar bone","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-190-1","s":"10. Most principal fibers of the periodontal ligament insert into","o":["A. enamel","B. cementum","C. dentin","D. free gingiva"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"cementum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Cementum.</b> The periodontal ligament's collagen bundles (Sharpey fibers) run from the <b>cementum of the root to the alveolar bone</b>, slinging the tooth in its socket.<br>A. Enamel: covers the crown above the gum; nothing inserts there.<br>C. Dentin: lies under the cementum; the fibers do not reach it.<br>D. Free gingiva: gum tissue held by separate gingival fibers, not the principal ligament fibers.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 41 (Teeth)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":190,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":7,"label":"Scurvy loosens teeth: collagen needs vitamin C","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-41.jpg","cap":"Tooth diagram: periodontal ligament anchors root cementum to alveolar bone","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 41"},{"src":"sl/G031-10.jpg","cap":"Vitamin C (ascorbate) is needed for prolyl hydroxylation of collagen","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/G031-11.jpg","cap":"Scurvy: sore, spongy, bleeding gums and petechiae","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-179-3","s":"9. Avitaminosis (scurvy) results in a loosening of the teeth in the sockets chiefly because ascorbic acid (vitamin C) is needed by","o":["A. osteoblasts to form bone","B. cells to produce collagen","C. odontoblasts to form dentin","D. cells to fight viruses","E. cementoblasts to form cementum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"cells to produce collagen","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Cells need vitamin C to make collagen.</b> Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is the cofactor for the prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases that finish collagen. The <b>periodontal ligament is collagen with fast turnover</b>, so in scurvy it weakens and teeth loosen; gums get spongy and bleed.<br>A. Osteoblasts forming bone: bone suffers too, but loose teeth come from the ligament.<br>C. Odontoblasts forming dentin: dentin does not anchor the tooth.<br>D. Fighting viruses: not the reason.<br>E. Cementoblasts: cementum is only the root's covering.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 41 (Teeth)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":179,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":8,"label":"Esophagus: nonkeratinized stratified squamous lining","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-55.jpg","cap":"Esophagus: thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 55"},{"src":"sl/G018-23.jpg","cap":"Esophagus: squamous epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae, submucosal mucous glands","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 23"},{"src":"sl/G018-24.jpg","cap":"Esophagus transmural: squamous lining over inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-179-2","s":"8. In humans, the lining epithelium of the esophagus is","o":["A. transitional","B. simple columnar","C. stratified squamous, keratinized","D. stratified squamous, non-keratinized","E. stratified cuboidal"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"stratified squamous, non-keratinized","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Stratified squamous, nonkeratinized.</b> The esophagus has a thick, wet, <b>nonkeratinized stratified squamous</b> lining that resists friction from swallowed food.<br>A. Transitional: urinary tract.<br>B. Simple columnar: stomach and intestines (and Barrett esophagus, which is abnormal).<br>C. Keratinized: skin, gingiva and hard palate.<br>E. Stratified cuboidal: sweat gland ducts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":179,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F1221-166-1","s":"4. Which of the following lacks goblet cells?","o":["A. nasal cavity","B. ileum","C. trachea","D. esophagus","E. vermiform appendix"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"esophagus","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Esophagus.</b> The esophagus is lined by <b>nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b>, which has no goblet cells; its mucus comes from submucosal esophageal glands.<br>A. Nasal cavity: respiratory epithelium with goblet cells.<br>B. Ileum: many goblet cells (they increase from duodenum to ileum).<br>C. Trachea: respiratory epithelium with goblet cells.<br>E. Appendix: colon-type crypts with goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)","H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":166,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3980-64-1","s":"From what organ of the digestive tract is this section?","o":["A. Esophagus","B. Stomach","C. Duodenum","D. Jejunum","E. Ileum","F. Large intestine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Esophagus.</b> A <b>thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b> (surface cells keep their nuclei), with connective tissue papillae pushing up into it, lines the esophagus. Every other choice has simple columnar epithelium.<br>B. Stomach: surface mucous cells and pits.<br>C. Duodenum: villi, plus Brunner glands.<br>D. Jejunum: tall villi, prominent plicae.<br>E. Ileum: villi with Peyer's patches.<br>F. Large intestine: straight crypts full of goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-64.jpg","in":"Thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium with papillae of lamina propria pushing up into it.","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":64,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":135,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3955-28-1"},{"id":"F3955-28-1","s":"From which organ could this section could have been obtained?","o":["A. Outer lip","B. Stomach","C. Esophagus","D. Jejunum","E. Colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Esophagus.</b> A <b>thick stratified squamous epithelium whose surface cells keep their nuclei</b> (nonkeratinized) is esophageal mucosa.<br>A. Outer lip: thin keratinized skin with hair follicles; its surface cells have no nuclei.<br>B. Stomach: simple columnar epithelium with pits.<br>D. Jejunum: villi lined by enterocytes and goblet cells.<br>E. Colon: straight crypts full of goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-28.jpg","in":"High power: thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium with nucleated surface cells over lamina propria.","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":28,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":34,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":38,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":147,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0052-149-1","s":"Q11. From which organ could this section could have been obtained?","o":["A. Outer lip","B. Stomach","C. Esophagus","D. Jejunum","E. Colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Esophagus.</b> A <b>stratified squamous lining</b>, a submucosa with mucous glands, and a <b>very thick muscularis externa</b> of interlacing bundles fit the esophagus. The squamous lining alone rules out every gut organ below it.<br>A. Outer lip: thin skin with hair follicles over a skeletal muscle core; no muscularis externa.<br>B. Stomach: simple columnar epithelium with pits.<br>D. Jejunum: villi.<br>E. Colon: crypts full of goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-149.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: stratified squamous epithelium on the right, submucosa with glands, and very thick muscularis externa of interlacing bundles to the left.","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":149,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-21-1"},{"id":"G018-21-1","s":"From which organ could this section have been obtained?","o":["A. Outer surface of lip","B. Inner surface of lip","C. Tongue","D. Oral mucosa","E. Esophagus"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagus","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Esophagus.</b> The lining is non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium. Under it sit a thin muscularis mucosae, a submucosa with a mucous gland, and then thick skeletal muscle in two layers at right angles (the inner layer cut along its length, the outer layer cut across). A muscularis externa of skeletal muscle in two neat layers is the upper third of the esophagus.<br>A. Outer surface of lip: thin skin, keratinized, with hair follicles and sweat glands.<br>B. Inner surface of lip: also non-keratinized, but it has no muscularis mucosae, and its skeletal muscle (orbicularis oris) is one sheet, not two layers at right angles; labial salivary glands sit under it.<br>C. Tongue: skeletal muscle bundles run in all three planes, and the top surface carries papillae.<br>D. Oral mucosa (cheek, floor of the mouth): no muscularis mucosae and no two-layer muscularis externa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-21.jpg","in":"Two pictures: low power of a wall with stratified squamous epithelium on one side, glands and vessels beneath, then two layers of skeletal muscle at right angles; high power of the non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium.","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)","H15A slide 53 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":21,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":8,"label":"Candida esophagitis: white plaques, immunocompromised","slides":[{"src":"sl/F3955-107.jpg","cap":"Endoscopy: patchy white Candida plaques on red esophageal mucosa","where":"2024 Middletown lab clicker, slide 107"},{"src":"sl/F3955-109.jpg","cap":"Candida hyphae in esophageal squamous debris; silver stain turns fungus black","where":"2024 Middletown lab clicker, slide 109"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-104-1","s":"A 30-year-old man with AIDS complains of severe pain on swallowing. Upper GI endoscopy shows elevated, white plaques on a hyperemic and edematous esophageal mucosa. Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnosis?","o":["A. Barrett esophagus","B. Candida esophagitis","C. Herpetic esophagitis","D. Reflux esophagitis","E. Squamous cell carcinoma in situ"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Candida esophagitis","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Candida esophagitis.</b> A patient with AIDS (acquired immunodeficiency syndrome), <b>painful swallowing</b> and <b>raised white plaques</b> on red mucosa has Candida esophagitis. The plaques are fungal hyphae, fibrin and dead cells.<br>A. Barrett esophagus: salmon-pink columnar patches from reflux.<br>C. Herpetic esophagitis: vesicles and small punched-out ulcers.<br>D. Reflux esophagitis: redness and erosions near the stomach junction.<br>E. Squamous cell carcinoma in situ: a flat or irregular lesion, not white plaques.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":104,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":8,"label":"Esophageal varices: dilated submucosal veins","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-14.jpg","cap":"Layer table: submucosa holds the large blood and lymphatic vessels","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 14"},{"src":"sl/G015-58.jpg","cap":"Esophagus: mucosa, muscularis mucosae, then submucosa with gland and duct","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 58"}],"q":[{"id":"F3961-28-1","s":"A 45-year-old male alcoholic with a history of portal hypertension presents with vomiting of blood (hematemesis) and hypotension. He denies any history of vomiting non-blood material or retching prior to vomiting blood. During the work-up he dies suddenly. Which of the following histologic changes is most likely to be seen in a biopsy specimen taken from his esophagus?","o":["A. Metaplastic columnar epithelium","B. Decreased ganglion cells in myenteric plexus","C. Dilated blood vessels in the submucosa","D. Mucosal outpouchings","E. Numerous intraepithelial neutrophils"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Dilated blood vessels in the submucosa","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Dilated blood vessels in the submucosa.</b> An alcoholic with portal hypertension who vomits blood without retching has <b>esophageal varices</b>: swollen veins in the submucosa of the lower esophagus that link the portal and systemic circulations. They can burst and bleed fatally.<br>A. Metaplastic columnar epithelium: Barrett esophagus from acid reflux.<br>B. Fewer myenteric ganglion cells: achalasia.<br>D. Mucosal outpouchings: diverticula.<br>E. Many neutrophils in the epithelium: acute esophagitis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 14 (1- Mucosa (M))","H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":28,"f":"F3961"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":9,"label":"Esophagus thirds: skeletal, mixed, smooth","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-60.jpg","cap":"Diagram: upper third skeletal, middle mixed, lower third smooth muscle","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/G015-59.jpg","cap":"Middle third: big skeletal fibers mixed with smooth muscle bundles","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 59"},{"src":"sl/G018-22.jpg","cap":"Esophagus cross-section; text lists the muscularis externa by thirds","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-153-1","s":"1. The esophagus is subdivided into three portions along its course on the basis of a transition in the composition of the","o":["A. submucosa","B. mucosal layer","C. adventitia","D. muscularis externa","E. none of the above"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"muscularis externa","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Muscularis externa.</b> The esophagus is divided into thirds by the <b>muscle type of its muscularis externa</b>: skeletal (upper), mixed (middle), smooth (lower).<br>A. Submucosa: has mucous glands all along; does not define the thirds.<br>B. Mucosal layer: nonkeratinized stratified squamous throughout.<br>C. Adventitia: outer coat all the way to the diaphragm.<br>E. None of the above: D is correct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":153,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0754-158-1","s":"The bottom ⅓ of the esophagus’ muscularis externa is composed of what type of muscle?","o":["A. Skeletal and smooth muscle","B. Skeletal muscle","C. Smooth muscle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Smooth muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Smooth muscle.</b> The lower third of the esophageal muscularis externa is <b>smooth muscle only</b>, like the rest of the gut below it.<br>A. Skeletal and smooth: the middle third.<br>B. Skeletal: the upper third, continuous with the pharyngeal muscles.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":158,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3950-34-1"},{"id":"F3950-34-1","s":"A 58-year-old male presents to the clinic with a 3-month history of progressive dysphagia (difficulty swallowing) for solids and liquids. He reports occasional chest pain and regurgitation of food. Upon examination, the patient appears to have lost weight and shows signs of dehydration. A barium swallow study reveals a significant narrowing in the lower third of the esophagus. Which of the following best describes the muscular composition of the esophagus in this patient?","o":["A. The entire esophagus is composed of smooth muscle.","B. The upper third is composed of skeletal muscle, the middle third is a mix of skeletal and smooth muscle, and the lower third is composed of smooth muscle.","C. The entire esophagus is composed of skeletal muscle.","D. The upper third is composed of smooth muscle, the middle third is skeletal muscle, and the lower third is composed of striated muscle."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The upper third is composed of skeletal muscle, the middle third is a mix of skeletal and smooth muscle, and the lower third is composed of smooth muscle.","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Upper third skeletal, middle third mixed, lower third smooth.</b> Swallowing starts with voluntary (skeletal) muscle and finishes with involuntary peristalsis, so the <b>muscularis externa shifts from skeletal to smooth</b> along the esophagus.<br>A. All smooth: wrong for the upper two thirds.<br>C. All skeletal: wrong for the lower two thirds.<br>D. Smooth on top, striated at the bottom: the order is reversed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":34,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3955-36-1","s":"What portion of the esophagus does this section corresponds to?","o":["A. Upper third","B. Middle third","C. Lower third"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle third","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Middle third.</b> The muscularis externa here <b>mixes large pink skeletal fibers</b> (nuclei at the edge of each fiber) <b>with bundles of small smooth muscle cells</b> (one central nucleus each). A mix of both types occurs only in the middle third.<br>A. Upper third: skeletal fibers only.<br>C. Lower third: smooth muscle only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-52.jpg","in":"Muscularis externa with large pink cross-cut skeletal fibers (peripheral nuclei) mixed with smooth muscle bundles (central nuclei).","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":52,"f":"G018"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":36,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":76,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":151,"f":"F0052"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":221,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0745-20-1","s":"Name the layers, what section is this from (upper, mid, or lower)?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Middle third. Left: inner circular smooth muscle layer of the muscularis externa (cut lengthwise); middle: thin connective tissue with nerve bundles (myenteric plexus zone); right: outer longitudinal layer cut across, mixing large skeletal fibers with smooth muscle bundles","a":null,"why":"<b>Middle third of the esophagus.</b> The right layer mixes <b>large pink skeletal fibers with small smooth muscle cells</b>, which happens only in the middle third. In this cross-section the left band of smooth muscle cut lengthwise is the <b>inner circular layer</b>; the loose tissue with nerves between the layers is the <b>myenteric plexus</b> zone; the right is the <b>outer longitudinal layer</b>, cut across.<br>Common mix-up: the left band is not muscularis mucosae, a thin layer right under the epithelium.","ex":"","fl":["The source labeled the left band muscularis mucosae and the middle zone submucosa, but this micrograph is a cross-section of the muscularis externa alone: inner circular smooth muscle, the myenteric plexus zone, and an outer layer of mixed skeletal and smooth fibers."],"ch":true,"img":"img/F0745-20.jpg","in":"H&E of esophageal wall: band of smooth muscle at far left, loose connective tissue with vessels/nerves in the middle, and a muscle layer at right containing large cross-cut striated fibres mixed with smooth muscle bundles.","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT week 11","sl":20,"f":"F0745"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3955-36-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":9,"label":"Striated muscularis externa means esophagus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-60.jpg","cap":"Esophageal muscularis externa by thirds: skeletal, skeletal plus smooth, smooth","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/G015-59.jpg","cap":"Middle esophagus: large skeletal fibers mixed with smooth muscle bundles","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 59"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-166-2","s":"5. Which one of the following has skeletal muscle in the muscularis externa?","o":["A. colon","B. appendix","C. stomach","D. esophagus","E. small intestine"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"esophagus","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Esophagus.</b> It is the only part of the digestive tube with <b>skeletal muscle in its muscularis externa</b>: all skeletal in the upper third, mixed in the middle third.<br>A. Colon: smooth muscle; outer layer forms teniae coli.<br>B. Appendix: smooth muscle.<br>C. Stomach: three layers of smooth muscle.<br>E. Small intestine: smooth muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":166,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3980-52-1"},{"id":"F3980-52-1","s":"You are working in a pathology lab and are given an unlabeled slide from the GI system. The mucosa and submucosa are poorly preserved, with only the thick muscularis well-stained, showing striated fibers. Which region of the GI tract is this most likely?","o":["A. Pyloric sphincter","B. Esophagus","C. Colon","D. Corpus of the stomach","E. Ileum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Esophagus.</b> The esophagus is the only part of the tube with <b>striated (skeletal) muscle in its muscularis externa</b>: skeletal in the upper third, mixed in the middle third, smooth in the lower third.<br>A. Pyloric sphincter: thickened smooth circular muscle.<br>C. Colon: smooth muscle; outer layer in three teniae coli.<br>D. Body of the stomach: three smooth muscle layers.<br>E. Ileum: smooth muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":52,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":139,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":9,"label":"Achalasia: myenteric plexus lost at LES","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-5.jpg","cap":"Enteric nervous system: myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between muscle layers; submucosal (Meissner) plexus","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G016-6.jpg","cap":"Myenteric plexus between inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle, 100x","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F3961-37-1","s":"A 49-year-old woman presents to hospital with increasing problems swallowing food (progressive dysphasia). X-ray studies with contrast reveal that she has a markedly dilated esophagus above the level of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES). No lesions are seen within the lumen of the esophagus. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this disorder?","o":["A. Decreased LES resting pressure","B. Absence of myenteric plexus in the body of the esophagus","C. Absence of myenteric plexus at the LES","D. Absence of submucosal plexus in the body of the esophagus","E. Absence of submucosal plexus at the LES"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Absence of myenteric plexus at the LES","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Absence of myenteric plexus at the LES (lower esophageal sphincter).</b> This is <b>achalasia</b>. The <b>myenteric (Auerbach) plexus</b>, between the circular and longitudinal muscle, controls motility. Losing its inhibitory neurons at the LES keeps the sphincter shut; the esophagus dilates above it.<br>A. Low LES pressure: lets acid reflux; achalasia has high pressure.<br>B. Plexus loss in the body only: weak peristalsis, no shut sphincter.<br>D. Submucosal (Meissner) plexus: controls secretion and blood flow, not motility.<br>E. Submucosal plexus at the LES: same; it does not relax the sphincter.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 5 (ENTERIC NERVOUS SYSTEM)","H15B slide 6 (100x)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":37,"f":"F3961"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":10,"label":"Esophagogastric junction: squamous to columnar","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-63.jpg","cap":"Esophagogastric junction: squamous esophagus meets columnar cardia with gastric pits","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 63"},{"src":"sl/G017-7.jpg","cap":"Squamocolumnar junction on histology and endoscopy: Z-line between white and tan","where":"2026 digestive interactive, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-4-1","s":"At the esophagogastric junction, the epithelial lining of the digestive tract undergoes an abrupt change. Which epithelial types are present on each side of this junction?","o":["A. Simple columnar and stratified squamous nonkeratinized","B. Stratified squamous nonkeratinized and simple columnar","C. Stratified squamous keratinized and simple cuboidal","D. Simple cuboidal and pseudostratified columnar","E. Transitional and stratified squamous nonkeratinized"],"g":["A","B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Stratified squamous nonkeratinized and simple columnar","a":"B","why":"<b>A and B. Stratified squamous nonkeratinized and simple columnar.</b> At the esophagogastric junction the esophagus's <b>nonkeratinized stratified squamous</b> epithelium changes abruptly to the stomach's <b>simple columnar</b> epithelium. A and B name the same pair (B in esophagus-to-stomach order).<br>C. Keratinized and cuboidal: neither lines this junction.<br>D. Cuboidal and pseudostratified columnar: duct and airway types.<br>E. Transitional: urinary tract only.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only B, but A names the same two epithelia in reverse order and the stem sets no order, so A and B are both correct."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 64 (Abrupt change from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium )","H15A slide 63 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":4,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F4081-8-1"},{"id":"F3980-31-1","s":"Where is this?","o":["A. Transition from esophagus to cardia of stomach","B. Transition from cardia to body of stomach","C. Transition from pylorus to duodenum","D. Transition from rectum to anus"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Transition from esophagus to cardia of stomach","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Esophagus to cardia of the stomach.</b> <b>Thick stratified squamous epithelium ends abruptly at simple columnar epithelium with gastric pits and mucous glands</b>: the esophagogastric junction.<br>B. Cardia to body: both are simple columnar gastric mucosa; no squamous epithelium.<br>C. Pylorus to duodenum: columnar to columnar, with villi and Brunner glands appearing.<br>D. Rectum to anus: also columnar to squamous, but the columnar side has crypts packed with goblet cells, not gastric pits.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-31.jpg","in":"Abrupt junction: thick stratified squamous epithelium on the left meets simple columnar epithelium with gastric pits and mucous glands on the right.","tg":["H15A slide 63 (Esophagus)","H15A slide 64 (Abrupt change from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium )"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":31,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":74,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":182,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":223,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":169,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0755-189-1","s":"Digital slides illustrating various organs of the GI tract are examined in the histology laboratory. The specimen shown in the image was obtained from which of the following anatomic locations?","o":["A. Esophagogastric junction","B. Fundus of the stomach","C. Gastroduodenal junction","D. Ileocecal junction","E. Pylorus of the stomach"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagogastric junction","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Esophagogastric junction.</b> The section shows <b>stratified squamous epithelium (right) changing abruptly to glandular gastric (cardiac) mucosa with pits (left)</b>, here with a lymphoid aggregate at the junction.<br>B. Fundus: simple columnar throughout, with deep glands full of parietal cells.<br>C. Gastroduodenal junction: gastric pits change to villi and Brunner glands; no squamous epithelium.<br>D. Ileocecal junction: villi change to colonic crypts.<br>E. Pylorus: deep pits and short coiled mucous glands, no squamous epithelium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0755-189.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: stratified squamous epithelium at the right end of the section abruptly continuous with glandular (gastric cardiac) mucosa to the left; a lymphoid aggregate at the junction; thick muscularis below.","tg":["H15A slide 63 (Esophagus)","H15A slide 64 (Abrupt change from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium )","H15A slide 82 (Q4- Digital slides illustrating various organs of the GI tract are examined in t)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":189,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3980-31-1"},{"id":"F4081-8-1","s":"A 45-year-old man undergoes an upper endoscopy for dysphagia. Biopsy of the distal esophagus shows a transition from non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells. Which of the following normal anatomic transitions in epithelial type occurs at the same boundary?","o":["A. Simple columnar epithelium → simple cuboidal epithelium","B. Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium → pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","C. Simple columnar epithelium → non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","D. Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium → simple columnar epithelium","E. Simple cuboidal epithelium → stratified columnar epithelium"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium → simple columnar epithelium","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Nonkeratinized stratified squamous → simple columnar.</b> The biopsy shows Barrett esophagus at the distal end. The normal boundary there is the <b>esophagogastric junction</b>, where esophageal stratified squamous epithelium gives way to the stomach's simple columnar epithelium.<br>A. Columnar → cuboidal: not a gut junction.<br>B. Squamous → ciliated pseudostratified: an airway junction (pharynx to larynx or nasal cavity).<br>C. Columnar → squamous: the anal canal's order (rectum to anus), reversed here.<br>E. Cuboidal → stratified columnar: duct epithelia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 64 (Abrupt change from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium )","H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":8,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":11,"label":"Reflux esophagitis: symptoms, treatment, biopsy","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-57.jpg","cap":"Normal esophageal mucosa: squamous epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 57"},{"src":"sl/G015-65.jpg","cap":"Reflux disease progression: normal lining, Barrett, dysplasia, invasive carcinoma","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-2-1","s":"A 35 year-old man comes to your office complaining of several months of heartburn unresponsive to oral antacids. He noted an intermittent burning sensation in the center of his chest, particularly following dinner when he typically sits in his recliner to watch a favorite TV show. Occasionally he notes a sour taste in his mouth and a sensation of something stuck in his throat. He keeps a container of TUMS by his chair because they typically provide some relief. Other than TUMS, he is not currently taking any medication. He gave no history of cancer, radiation therapy or immunosuppressive conditions. He loves coffee and drinks four or five cups per day. He often has a beer while watching TV after dinner. On physical examination, he was noted to be overweight (BMI 29); no additional contributory findings were identified. What is your impression and what are your recommendations?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Reflux esophagitis / gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD); lifestyle modifications (smaller dinner, sit upright, cut back on beer and coffee), a proton pump inhibitor, return in 8 weeks","a":null,"why":"<b>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) with reflux esophagitis.</b> Burning chest pain after big meals while reclining, a sour taste, and relief from antacids mean stomach acid is flowing back through a weak lower esophageal sphincter; coffee, alcohol and extra weight worsen it. Treat with <b>lifestyle changes</b> (smaller dinner, sit upright, less beer and coffee) plus a <b>proton pump inhibitor</b>, and recheck in eight weeks.<br>Common mix-up: untreated reflux can lead to Barrett esophagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":2,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":2,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1},{"id":"G017-8-1","s":"[Case context, slides 2-11: 35-year-old overweight man, months of post-dinner heartburn while reclining, sour taste, relieved by TUMS; coffee and beer after dinner.] The biopsy obtained at esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD) is illustrated below (labeled: elongation of vascular papillae; basal cell hyperplasia; intraepithelial eosinophils). Diagnosis:","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Reflux esophagitis","a":null,"why":"<b>Reflux esophagitis.</b> Acid injury to the squamous lining shows three classic changes: <b>basal cell hyperplasia</b> (a thicker basal layer from faster turnover), <b>elongated vascular papillae</b> reaching toward the surface, and <b>eosinophils inside the epithelium</b>. The epithelium is still stratified squamous.<br>Common mix-up: Barrett esophagus is different; there the squamous lining is replaced by columnar epithelium with goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Left: normal esophagus (SSE, LP, MM). Right: biopsy of stratified squamous epithelium with elongated vascular papillae reaching toward the surface, thickened basal cell zone, and scattered intraepithelial eosinophils (labels are given in the stem).","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":8,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":6,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":11,"label":"Reflux esophagitis: differential diagnosis","slides":[],"q":[{"id":"G017-4-1","s":"[Case context, slides 2-11: 35-year-old overweight man, months of post-dinner heartburn while reclining, sour taste, relieved by TUMS; coffee and beer after dinner.] In 8 weeks he returns noting his symptoms are still present; he has developed an occasional cough. You explain that in most cases of GERD, his symptoms should have responded to the treatment you prescribed. Since they did not, additional investigation is prudent to exclude the possibility of another cause. What are other diagnostic possibilities (differential diagnosis)?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Eosinophilic esophagitis; infection; medications; pill esophagitis; radiation injury","a":null,"why":"<b>Eosinophilic esophagitis, infection, medications (pill esophagitis), and radiation injury.</b> Heartburn that does not respond to a proton pump inhibitor may not be acid reflux. Infections include fungi (<b>Candida</b>) and viruses (herpes simplex virus, cytomegalovirus). Pills that injure the lining include NSAIDs (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs), doxycycline and ferrous sulfate.<br>Common mix-up: Barrett esophagus is a complication of reflux, not another cause of it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":4,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":3,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":11,"label":"Metaplasia: one mature epithelium replaces another","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-66.jpg","cap":"Metaplasia example: squamous esophagus replaced by columnar (Barrett) epithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-12-1","s":"In cigarette smokers, ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium lining the airways may be replaced by stratified squamous epithelium. In chronic reflux esophagitis, stratified squamous epithelium lining the esophagus may be replaced by simple columnar epithelium. This replacement of the one epithelial type by another epithelial type in response to injury is referred to as:","o":["A. Neoplasia","B. Metaplasia","C. Dysplasia","D. Differentiation","E. Quiescence"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Metaplasia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Metaplasia.</b> Metaplasia is the <b>replacement of one mature cell type by another</b> that better withstands an injury: squamous for ciliated cells in a smoker's airway, columnar for squamous in an acid-washed esophagus. It can reverse if the injury stops.<br>A. Neoplasia: uncontrolled new growth (a tumor).<br>C. Dysplasia: disordered, atypical growth; a precancer step after metaplasia.<br>D. Differentiation: normal maturation of a cell.<br>E. Quiescence: a resting, nondividing state.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":12,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":8,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":11,"label":"Columnar esophageal lining means Barrett metaplasia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-66.jpg","cap":"Normal squamous esophagus beside abnormal columnar (Barrett) esophagus","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 66"},{"src":"sl/G015-65.jpg","cap":"Barrett diagram: goblet cells and glands replacing the squamous lining","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-162-1","s":"GERD induces esophageal metaplasia into what type of cells?","o":["A. Simple columnar","B. Simple cuboidal","C. Simple Squamous","D. Simple urothelium"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Simple columnar","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Simple columnar.</b> In Barrett esophagus, acid reflux turns the esophageal <b>stratified squamous epithelium into simple columnar epithelium</b> (intestinal type, with goblet cells), so the lower esophagus starts to look like intestine.<br>B. Simple cuboidal: lines small ducts and kidney tubules.<br>C. Simple squamous: mesothelium and endothelium.<br>D. Urothelium: the transitional epithelium of the urinary tract.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 64 (Abrupt change from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium )"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":162,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3950-32-1","s":"A man comes in complaining of acid reflux. During the biopsy of the esophagus you find columnar epithelial cells in the lining of the esophagus. Is this normal?","o":["A. Yes","B. No"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"No","a":"B","why":"<b>B. No.</b> The esophagus is normally lined by <b>nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b> all the way to the esophagogastric junction, where it changes abruptly to the stomach's simple columnar epithelium. Columnar cells in the esophageal lining after long acid reflux mean <b>Barrett esophagus</b>, a metaplasia that raises adenocarcinoma risk.<br>Common mix-up: columnar cells belong just below the junction (cardia), not above it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 60 (Mucosa:)","H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":32,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0754-162-1"},{"id":"F0753-75-1","s":"What happens at this junction (esophagogastric junction) if a person has untreated GERD?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Barrett's esophagus – metaplasia of the distal esophageal stratified squamous epithelium into simple columnar (intestinal-type, goblet-cell-containing) epithelium","a":null,"why":"<b>Barrett esophagus.</b> Normally the junction is an abrupt change from stratified squamous to simple columnar epithelium. With untreated gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), acid makes the <b>distal esophageal squamous lining turn into simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells</b> (metaplasia), so the columnar zone creeps up the esophagus and adenocarcinoma risk rises.<br>Common mix-up: the change is squamous to columnar, not the reverse.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":75,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":183,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":170,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0754-162-1"},{"id":"F0755-224-1","s":"In which location does Esophageal metaplasia tend to occur? What change of epithelium do you seen in esophageal metaplasia?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A: esophagogastric junction (transition between esophagus and cardia of stomach); B: changes from stratified squamous to simple columnar epithelium","a":null,"why":"<b>Distal esophagus at the esophagogastric junction; stratified squamous becomes simple columnar.</b> Acid refluxing from the stomach hits the lowest esophagus first, so Barrett metaplasia starts there and creeps upward. The squamous lining is replaced by columnar epithelium with goblet cells.<br>Common mix-up: the metaplasia is in the esophagus, not in the stomach's cardia, which is normally columnar.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Diagram of normal lining → Barrett's esophagus → dysplasia → invasive carcinoma, and an H&E of abnormal (columnar) esophagus.","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 64 (Abrupt change from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium )"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":224,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0754-162-1"},{"id":"F4081-19-1","s":"Identify which is normal and which is metaplastic, its location and cause of disease","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left = normal esophagus (nonkeratinized stratified squamous); right = abnormal (metaplastic) esophagus: columnar epithelium with goblet cells = Barrett's esophagus (distal esophagus), caused by GERD","a":null,"why":"<b>Left normal, right metaplastic (Barrett esophagus).</b> The left panel shows the normal <b>thick stratified squamous</b> lining. The right shows <b>glandular columnar epithelium with goblet cells</b>. It forms in the <b>distal esophagus near the esophagogastric junction</b> after long-standing acid reflux (gastroesophageal reflux disease).<br>Common mix-up: goblet cells are never normal in the esophagus or stomach; they mark intestinal-type metaplasia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-19.jpg","in":"Left: thick stratified squamous epithelium; right: glandular columnar epithelium with many goblet cells.","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":19,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":12,"label":"Heartburn plus red distal mucosa: Barrett esophagus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-66.jpg","cap":"Normal squamous esophagus versus abnormal columnar Barrett mucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 66"},{"src":"sl/G015-65.jpg","cap":"Barrett diagram: goblet cells and glands replacing the squamous lining","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-10-1","s":"[Case context, slides 2-11: 35-year-old overweight man, months of post-dinner heartburn while reclining, sour taste, relieved by TUMS; coffee and beer after dinner.] You prescribe an alternate (next generation) proton pump inhibitor and during the next 8 weeks the patient's symptoms abate. Surveillance EGD is recommended. Three years later, a surveillance EGD is performed. The findings are illustrated below: irregular tongues of simple columnar epithelium replace stratified squamous epithelium. Diagnosis:","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Barrett esophagus","a":null,"why":"<b>Barrett esophagus.</b> After years of reflux, <b>tongues of salmon-colored simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells</b> replace the pale stratified squamous lining of the distal esophagus. This metaplasia is a response to acid and raises the risk of adenocarcinoma, so it needs surveillance endoscopy.<br>Common mix-up: reflux esophagitis keeps a squamous lining (thick basal layer, eosinophils); Barrett has replaced it with columnar cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Endoscopy: salmon-coloured tongues of columnar mucosa (green stars) extending up into pale squamous mucosa (red triangle). Biopsy: columnar epithelium with goblet cells (stars) beside stratified squamous epithelium (triangle).","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":10,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":7,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3961-42-1"},{"id":"F3961-42-1","s":"A 45-year-old man presents with increasing \"heartburn,\" especially after eating or when lying down. Endoscopic examination finds a red velvety plaque located the distal esophagus. Biopsies from this area, taken approximately 4 cm proximal to the gastroesophageal junction reveal metaplastic columnar epithelium as seen in the next slide. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Acquired achalasia","B. Barrett's esophagus","C. Hamartomatous polyp","D. Metastatic adenocarcinoma","E. Reflux esophagitis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Barrett's esophagus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Barrett's esophagus.</b> Reflux symptoms, a <b>red velvety plaque</b> a few centimeters above the gastroesophageal junction, and <b>metaplastic columnar epithelium</b> on biopsy define Barrett esophagus.<br>A. Acquired achalasia: the lower esophageal sphincter fails to relax after loss of myenteric neurons.<br>C. Hamartomatous polyp: a disorganized growth of normal tissues.<br>D. Metastatic adenocarcinoma: cancer from elsewhere; nothing invasive is described.<br>E. Reflux esophagitis: inflamed but still squamous lining, no columnar change.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":42,"f":"F3961"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3955-95-1","s":"A 55-year-old smoker comes in complaining of heartburn especially at night and before meals. These pains have been present for at least 20 years but seem to have worsened in the last two months. On endoscopy, you see an area of velvety red mucosa in the distal esophagus. An esophageal biopsy is taken. See the accompanying figure. The pathologic condition shown in this figure most likely represents which of the following?","o":["A. Adenocarcinoma of the esophagus","B. Barrett esophagus","C. Candidiasis of the esophagus","D. Displaced gastric cardia mucosa","E. Squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Barrett esophagus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Barrett esophagus.</b> Decades of heartburn and <b>velvety red mucosa in the distal esophagus</b>, with squamous epithelium beside <b>glandular columnar mucosa with goblet cells</b>, is Barrett metaplasia.<br>A. Adenocarcinoma: invasive malignant glands; no invasion is described.<br>C. Candidiasis: white plaques with yeast and hyphae.<br>D. Displaced gastric cardia mucosa: gastric mucosa has no goblet cells.<br>E. Squamous cell carcinoma: malignant squamous cells, not columnar glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Squamous epithelium (left) abruptly next to glandular columnar mucosa with goblet-cell-type glands (right).","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":95,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3961-42-1"},{"id":"F3980-68-1","s":"A 55-year-old presents to his primary physician for chest pain. Endoscopy shows pink mucosa extending 5 cm above the gastroesophageal junction. Biopsy on the lower esophagus shows nonciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells. What is the cause of this patient's condition?","o":["A. Uncontrolled growth of esophageal epithelium","B. H. pylori infection","C. Esophageal exposure to gastric acid","D. Incomplete relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophageal exposure to gastric acid","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Esophageal exposure to gastric acid.</b> Pink columnar mucosa reaching up the lower esophagus, with <b>goblet cells</b> on biopsy, is Barrett esophagus: <b>metaplasia caused by long-term acid reflux</b> (gastroesophageal reflux disease).<br>A. Uncontrolled growth: describes cancer (neoplasia), not metaplasia.<br>B. Helicobacter pylori: gastritis and peptic ulcers of the stomach and duodenum.<br>D. Incomplete relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter: achalasia, which causes trouble swallowing, not acid damage.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":68,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":126,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":12,"label":"Barrett progresses to adenocarcinoma","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-65.jpg","cap":"Normal lining, Barrett, low- then high-grade dysplasia, invasive carcinoma","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65"}],"q":[{"id":"F0745-39-1","s":"Barrett's Esophagus: What can this cause?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Invasive carcinoma (adenocarcinoma) – via low- to high-grade dysplasia","a":null,"why":"<b>Adenocarcinoma of the esophagus.</b> Barrett esophagus can progress step by step: <b>normal squamous lining → Barrett metaplasia → low-grade dysplasia → high-grade dysplasia → invasive adenocarcinoma</b>. That is why these patients get surveillance endoscopy.<br>Common mix-up: squamous cell carcinoma comes from the squamous lining (smoking, alcohol), not from Barrett.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Side-by-side H&E: normal non-keratinized stratified squamous esophageal mucosa vs columnar glandular (Barrett) mucosa.","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT week 11","sl":39,"f":"F0745"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3955-88-1"},{"id":"F3955-88-1","s":"A 58-year-old man has a long history of heavy smoking, alcohol abuse, and severe heartburn. Two years ago, esophagoscopy revealed an area of ulceration 3 cm above the esophago-gastric junction. Biopsies of the ulcer edge revealed patches of intestinal type epithelium. Recently, the patient has experienced difficulty in swallowing solid foods and has lost 10 kg in body weight. The best working diagnosis is which of the following?","o":["A. Adenocarcinoma of the esophagus","B. Bronchogenic carcinoma with invasion of the esophagus","C. Esophageal varices","D. Squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus","E. Squamous cell carcinoma of the oropharynx with obstruction"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Adenocarcinoma of the esophagus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Adenocarcinoma of the esophagus.</b> Severe heartburn and patches of <b>intestinal-type epithelium</b> near the esophagogastric junction mean <b>Barrett esophagus</b>; new trouble swallowing solids and weight loss mean it has progressed to adenocarcinoma.<br>B. Bronchogenic carcinoma: a lung cancer, not linked to Barrett.<br>C. Varices: bleeding veins from portal hypertension.<br>D. Squamous cell carcinoma: arises from squamous lining (smoking, alcohol), usually mid-esophagus; Barrett leads to adenocarcinoma.<br>E. Oropharyngeal carcinoma: wrong site.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 65 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)","H15A slide 66 (Esophogeal Metaplasia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":88,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":13,"label":"Rugae are stomach folds","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-29.jpg","cap":"Stomach transmural section: rugae, pits and glands, submucosa, muscularis, serosa","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 29"},{"src":"sl/G015-69.jpg","cap":"Stomach section with rugae, gastric pits and glands labelled","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 69"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-191-1","s":"Rugae are found in which of the following segments of the GI tract?","o":["A. Esophagus","B. Large intestine","C. Rectum/anal canal","D. Small intestine","E. Stomach"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Stomach.</b> <b>Rugae</b> are the large longitudinal folds of the stomach's mucosa and submucosa. They flatten as the stomach fills.<br>A. Esophagus: longitudinal folds when collapsed, but not called rugae.<br>B. Large intestine: semilunar folds between haustra.<br>C. Rectum/anal canal: transverse rectal folds and anal columns.<br>D. Small intestine: plicae circulares, permanent circular folds.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 72 (SPECIAL FEATURES)","H15A slide 84 (Q5- Rugae are found in which of the following segments of the GI tract?)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":191,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F1211-197-1","s":"What area of the digestive tract is depicted histologically?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The Stomach! (rugae are the large folds in the stomach)","a":null,"why":"<b>Stomach.</b> The mucosa is thrown into <b>large folds with submucosal cores (rugae)</b>; it is thick and glandular with <b>no villi</b>, and a thick muscularis externa lies below. Rugae occur only in the stomach and flatten when it fills.<br>Common mix-up: plicae circulares of the small intestine are also folds with a submucosal core, but they are covered with villi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1211-197.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: thick glandular mucosa thrown into large folds with submucosal cores, over a thick muscularis externa; no villi.","tg":["H15A slide 72 (SPECIAL FEATURES)","H15A slide 84 (Q5- Rugae are found in which of the following segments of the GI tract?)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":197,"f":"F1211"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":199,"f":"F1211"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":201,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0755-191-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":13,"label":"Stomach muscularis: oblique, circular, longitudinal","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-68.jpg","cap":"Stomach diagram: longitudinal, circular and oblique muscle layers","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 68"},{"src":"sl/G018-27.jpg","cap":"Stomach muscularis externa: inner oblique, middle circular, outer longitudinal","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-222-1","s":"The muscular layers of the stomach are arranged in which orientation?","o":["A. Longitudinal and circular","B. Circular only","C. Longitudinal and oblique","D. Longitudinal, circular and oblique"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Longitudinal, circular and oblique","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Longitudinal, circular and oblique.</b> The stomach's muscularis externa has <b>three layers</b>: inner oblique, middle circular and outer longitudinal. The extra oblique layer helps churn food into chyme.<br>A. Longitudinal and circular: the two-layer pattern of the rest of the gut.<br>B. Circular only: wrong.<br>C. Longitudinal and oblique: leaves out the circular layer, which thickens into the pyloric sphincter.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 72 (SPECIAL FEATURES)","H15A slide 73 (Stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":222,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":13,"label":"Stomach: simple columnar surface mucous cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-70.jpg","cap":"Gastric mucosa: simple columnar surface mucous cells (arrow), no goblet cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 70"},{"src":"sl/G015-71.jpg","cap":"Gastric pits lined by surface mucous cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 71"}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-153-1","s":"Q13. From which organ could this section have been obtained?","o":["A. Outer lip","B. Stomach","C. Esophagus","D. Jejunum","E. Colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Stomach.</b> Tall <b>simple columnar cells, each with a pale cap of mucus</b> (surface mucous cells), line every surface and pit, and there are <b>no goblet cells or villi</b>: gastric mucosa.<br>A. Outer lip: thin keratinized skin.<br>C. Esophagus: stratified squamous epithelium.<br>D. Jejunum: villi with enterocytes and scattered goblet cells.<br>E. Colon: straight crypts packed with goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-153.jpg","in":"H&E: tall simple columnar epithelium with pale apical mucus cups (arrow) lining pits/folds, over lamina propria; no goblet cells or villi.","tg":["H15A slide 72 (SPECIAL FEATURES)","H15A slide 71 (Gastric Pits)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":153,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1},{"id":"G018-53-1","s":"Q6. Which cell type is indicated by the arrow?","o":["A. Enteroendocrine cell","B. Surface mucous cell","C. Enterocyte","D. Mucous neck cell","E. Chief cell"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Surface mucous cell","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Surface mucous cell.</b> The arrow is on the tall columnar cells that line the stomach surface and the gastric pits. Each cell has a pale, mucus-filled top, and the cells form one continuous sheet with no goblet cells between them. They make the thick, alkaline mucus layer that protects the lining from acid.<br>A. Enteroendocrine cell: small, scattered cells deep in the glands, with granules at the base; they need special stains to be seen.<br>C. Enterocyte: the absorptive cell of the intestine, with a brush border, sitting among goblet cells on villi.<br>D. Mucous neck cell: in the neck of the gastric gland, below the pit, not on the surface.<br>E. Chief cell: a purple (basophilic) cell at the base of the fundic glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-53.jpg","in":"Gastric surface and pits lined by tall columnar cells with pale apical cytoplasm; arrow on the surface lining.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 71 (Gastric Pits)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":53,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":14,"label":"Cardia: mucous zone at the esophageal entry","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-67.jpg","cap":"Stomach regions: cardia, fundus, body, pylorus","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 67"},{"src":"sl/G015-73.jpg","cap":"Cardia makes mucus; body and fundus make acid; pylorus makes mucus, gastrin","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 73"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-52-1","s":"A medical student is studying the anatomy of the stomach and learns about its four main regions. During a practical examination, she is asked to identify the region that primarily functions in mucus production and transitions between the esophagus and the stomach. Which region is she identifying?","o":["A. Cardia","B. Body","C. Fundus","D. Pylorus"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cardia","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Cardia.</b> The cardia is a narrow ring, a few centimeters wide, where the esophagus enters the stomach. Its <b>short coiled glands make mucus</b> that protects against acid.<br>B. Body: oxyntic glands making acid (parietal cells) and pepsinogen (chief cells).<br>C. Fundus: the same oxyntic glands as the body.<br>D. Pylorus: also mucous glands (plus gastrin-making G cells), but it is the exit into the duodenum, not the entry.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Stomach regions diagram with cardia circled (on the answer slide).","tg":["H15A slide 73 (Stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":52,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":14,"label":"Gastrin comes from pyloric G cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-34.jpg","cap":"Pyloric mucosa: mucous glands and gastrin-secreting G cells; no parietal cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 34"},{"src":"sl/G015-80.jpg","cap":"Pyloric cells: high concentration of gastrin-producing G cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 80"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-166-1","s":"In which region of the stomach is gastrin produced?","o":["A. Cardia","B. Body","C. Fundus","D. Pylorus"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pylorus","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Pylorus.</b> <b>G cells</b>, the enteroendocrine cells that make gastrin, live in the pyloric (antral) glands. Gastrin raises acid secretion and gastric motility.<br>A. Cardia: mucous glands like the pylorus, but no G cells.<br>B. Body: parietal and chief cells, not gastrin.<br>C. Fundus: the same as the body.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 73 (Stomach)","H15A slide 80 (Mucous cells, Chief Cells, Parietal cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":166,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":14,"label":"Stomach regions: pit depth versus gland length","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-75.jpg","cap":"2026 lecture labels: cardia, body/fundus, pylorus (same pictures as the question)","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 75"},{"src":"sl/G015-73.jpg","cap":"Cardia, body, pylorus glands: pits deepen and glands shorten toward pylorus","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 73"},{"src":"sl/G018-34.jpg","cap":"Pyloric mucosa: deep pits over short coiled mucous glands","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-78-1","s":"What organ is this from?","o":["A. Tongue","B. Esophagus","C. Stomach","D. Small Intestine","E. Large Intestine"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Stomach (pylorus).</b> <b>Long, deep gastric pits</b> lined by pale surface mucous cells over <b>short, coiled mucous glands</b>: pyloric mucosa, with no villi and no goblet cells.<br>A. Tongue: stratified squamous epithelium over skeletal muscle.<br>B. Esophagus: stratified squamous epithelium.<br>D. Small intestine: villi with enterocytes and goblet cells.<br>E. Large intestine: straight crypts packed with goblet cells, flat surface.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0753-78.jpg","in":"H&E of gastric mucosa with long, deep pits lined by pale mucous columnar cells and short coiled glands beneath.","tg":["H15A slide 73 (Stomach)","H15A slide 74 (Stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":78,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":187,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":174,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3980-66-1","s":"Name the Parts of the Stomach: [three micrographs, left, middle, right]","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left: pylorus. Middle: cardia. Right: body/fundus (the labels on 2026 lecture slide G015:75, which uses these same pictures).","a":null,"why":"<b>Left: pylorus. Middle: cardia. Right: body/fundus.</b> The 2026 lecture (slide G015:75) labels these same three pictures this way, so learn these labels for the exam.<br><b>Pylorus:</b> deep pits over short, coiled, pale mucous glands.<br><b>Body/fundus:</b> shallow pits over long, tightly packed glands, then the thick muscularis externa below.<br><b>Cardia:</b> the middle picture, as the lecture labels it. Pits are lined by surface mucous cells.<br>Common mix-up: the stomach has <b>no goblet cells</b>; goblet cells start in the small intestine.","ex":"","fl":["Middle image is body/fundus (oxyntic) mucosa, not cardia: short pits over long straight glands packed with pink (acidophilic) parietal cells with round central nuclei (checked at high resolution). Cardiac glands are short, coiled, pale mucous glands (G015:73). The stomach also has no goblet cells; cardia has mucous gland cells.","Middle image is body/fundus (oxyntic) mucosa, not cardia: short pits over long straight glands packed with pink (acidophilic) parietal cells with round central nuclei (same picture as F3980 slide 66, checked at high resolution). Cardiac glands are short, coiled, pale mucous glands (G015:73). The stomach also has no goblet cells; cardia has mucous gland cells.","The source said the cardia is \"mostly goblet cells\". The stomach has no goblet cells; the pale cells lining the pits are surface mucous cells."],"ch":true,"img":"img/F3980-66.jpg","in":"Left: deep branching pits over short coiled pale glands (pylorus). Middle: short pits lined by surface mucous cells over long straight glands of pink parietal cells; arrowhead at pit-gland junction. Right: shallow pits over thick layer of deep glands with pink parietal cells above and purple chief cells at the base, muscularis below.","tg":["H15A slide 73 (Stomach)","H15A slide 75 (Cardia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":66,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":133,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":15,"label":"Parietal cells: acid and intrinsic factor","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-76.jpg","cap":"Fundic gland diagram: parietal cells secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 76"},{"src":"sl/G015-78.jpg","cap":"Fundic glands: pink parietal cells (PC) above purple chief cells (CC)","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 78"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-129-1","s":"Which of the following cell type aids in absorption of vitamin B12?","o":["A. Chief cells","B. Enteroendocrine cells","C. Paneth cells","D. Parietal cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal cells","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Parietal cells.</b> Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete <b>intrinsic factor</b>, which binds vitamin B12 so the ileum can absorb it; they also secrete hydrochloric acid. Losing them causes pernicious anemia.<br>A. Chief cells: pepsinogen and gastric lipase.<br>B. Enteroendocrine cells: hormones such as gastrin and serotonin.<br>C. Paneth cells: antibacterial lysozyme and defensins in small-intestine crypts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":129,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-54-1"},{"id":"F3950-48-1","s":"A 45-year-old male presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain and nausea after a heavy meal. An endoscopy reveals a significant amount of gastric acid in the lumen of his stomach. A biopsy shows that the mucosa is composed of a simple columnar epithelium with deep invaginations forming gastric pits. Which type of gastric cell is primarily responsible for secreting the hydrochloric acid observed in this patient?","o":["A. Mucous neck cells","B. Chief (zymogenic) cells","C. Parietal (oxyntic) cells","D. Enteroendocrine cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Parietal (oxyntic) cells.</b> Large, pink (acidophilic) parietal cells in the body and fundus glands secrete <b>hydrochloric acid</b> into their intracellular canaliculi, plus intrinsic factor.<br>A. Mucous neck cells: watery, more acidic mucus; the stem-cell zone.<br>B. Chief (zymogenic) cells: pepsinogen and gastric lipase.<br>D. Enteroendocrine cells: hormones such as gastrin and serotonin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":48,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":15,"label":"Chief cells: pepsinogen and gastric lipase","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-76.jpg","cap":"Fundic gland diagram: chief cells secrete pepsinogen and gastric lipase","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 76"},{"src":"sl/G015-78.jpg","cap":"Purple chief cells (CC) at gland bases, pink parietal cells above","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 78"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-50-1","s":"A 30-year-old female presents with complaints of indigestion and difficulty digesting fatty foods. Upon examination, her medical history reveals she has had several episodes of gastric discomfort following high-fat meals. In the stomach, triglycerides are initially digested by which of the following secretions?","o":["A. Pepsin","B. Amylase","C. Gastric lipase","D. Hydrochloric acid"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric lipase","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Gastric lipase.</b> Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and a <b>lipase precursor</b>; gastric lipase begins triglyceride digestion in the stomach.<br>A. Pepsin: digests proteins.<br>B. Amylase: digests starch; comes from saliva and pancreas, not the stomach.<br>D. Hydrochloric acid: lowers pH and activates pepsinogen, but does not split triglycerides.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":50,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":15,"label":"Gastric gland cells and their secretions","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-76.jpg","cap":"Fundic gland diagram: surface mucous, neck mucous, parietal, chief and G cells with secretions","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 76"},{"src":"sl/G018-32.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph of gastric gland: parietal, chief and enteroendocrine cells labelled","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-170-1","s":"Match the mucosal cell with the secretion: Epithelial Mucous Cell; Mucous neck cell; Parietal Cell; Chief Cell; Entero-Endocrine (G) Cell","o":["A. Pepsinogen and Lipase precursor","B. Peptide hormones (e.g. serotonin and gastrin)","C. HCl and Intrinsic factor for Vit 12 absorption","D. Alkaline mucous","E. Acidic mucous"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Epithelial mucous cell – D (alkaline mucous); Mucous neck cell – E (acidic mucous); Parietal cell – C (HCl and intrinsic factor); Chief cell – A (pepsinogen and lipase precursor); Entero-endocrine (G) cell – B (peptide hormones)","a":null,"why":"<b>Surface mucous cell → D (alkaline mucus); mucous neck cell → E (acidic mucus); parietal cell → C (HCl, hydrochloric acid, and intrinsic factor); chief cell → A (pepsinogen, lipase precursor); enteroendocrine (G) cell → B (peptide hormones).</b> On H&amp;E (hematoxylin and eosin), parietal cells are pink, chief cells purple, mucous cells pale, and enteroendocrine cells need special stains.<br>Common mix-up: surface cells make the thick <b>alkaline</b> protective layer; neck cells make thinner, more acidic mucus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 80 (Mucous cells, Chief Cells, Parietal cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":170,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3952-86-1","s":"At a renowned culinary festival, the late Anthony Bourdain captivates the crowd with stories from his adventures around the globe, exploring the intricate relationship between food and culture. As he prepares a flavorful broth inspired by his travels, he explains how the body’s digestive system works to break down the delicious meals we enjoy, particularly highlighting the role of gastric glands in the stomach. When asked about the cells responsible for producing digestive enzymes and their unique cellular structures, he describes a specific type of cell characterized by a well-developed rough endoplasmic reticulum and few Golgi complexes, emphasizing its importance in the digestive process. Those cells are most likely examples of what cell?","o":["A. Neck mucous cell","B. Parietal cell","C. Chief cell","D. Neuroendocrine cell","E. Stem cell"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Chief cell","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Chief cell.</b> Chief cells make the stomach's digestive <b>enzymes (pepsinogen and lipase precursor)</b>, so they are packed with <b>rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER)</b> and stain purple (basophilic) at the base of fundic glands. (The stem's 'few Golgi' is off; chief cells also have a good Golgi.)<br>A. Neck mucous cell: makes acidic mucus, not enzymes.<br>B. Parietal cell: makes acid and intrinsic factor; full of mitochondria, stains pink.<br>D. Neuroendocrine cell: makes hormones, stored in small basal granules.<br>E. Stem cell: undifferentiated, few organelles; it divides to replace cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":86,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":15,"label":"Parietal pink, chief purple on H&E","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-78.jpg","cap":"Fundic glands, 400x: pale-pink parietal cells (PC) above purple chief cells (CC)","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 78"},{"src":"sl/G018-30.jpg","cap":"Fundus/body mucosa: pits, neck and glands; parietal (P) and chief (C) cells labelled","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-58-1","s":"A biopsy is obtained from the stomach. A and B correspond respectively to:","o":["A. Parietal cell; Chief cell","B. Chief cell; Parietal cell","C. Chief cell; Enteroendocrine cell","D. Parietal cell; Enteroendocrine cell"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal cell; Chief cell","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Parietal cell; chief cell.</b> <b>A</b> is a large, round, <b>pale-pink (acidophilic)</b> cell with a central nucleus in the upper gland: a parietal cell. <b>B</b> is a <b>dark-purple (basophilic)</b> cell lining the deep gland: a chief cell.<br>B. Chief cell; parietal cell: reversed. Pink cells are parietal, purple cells are chief.<br>C. Chief cell; enteroendocrine cell: A is pink, so not a chief cell.<br>D. Parietal cell; enteroendocrine cell: B is dark purple; enteroendocrine cells are small and pale.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-58.jpg","in":"Gastric glands: A = pale pink cell with central nucleus bulging from the gland wall; B = dark purple basophilic cell lining the deeper gland.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 78 (50x)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":58,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":192,"f":"F1211"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":155,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0745-30-1","s":"What are these cells? What do they secrete?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A: Parietal (oxyntic) cells – HCl and intrinsic factor; B: Chief cells – pepsinogen and lipase precursor","a":null,"why":"<b>A = parietal (oxyntic) cell; B = chief cell.</b> A is a large, round, <b>pink (acidophilic)</b> cell with a central nucleus in the upper gland. It secretes <b>hydrochloric acid (HCl)</b> and <b>intrinsic factor</b>, needed to absorb vitamin B12. B is a <b>purple (basophilic)</b> cell at the gland base. It secretes <b>pepsinogen</b> and <b>lipase precursor</b>.<br>Common mix-up: students swap them. Pink = acid-making parietal cell; purple = enzyme-making chief cell, rich in rough endoplasmic reticulum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0745-30.jpg","in":"H&E of fundic gastric glands: arrow A on a pale-pink (acidophilic) rounded cell with central nucleus; arrow B on a dark-purple (basophilic) cell at the gland base.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15A slide 78 (50x)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT week 11","sl":30,"f":"F0745"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3955-58-1"},{"id":"G018-54-1","s":"Q7. The cell indicated by A produces:","o":["A. Pepsinogen","B. Intrinsic factor","C. Histamine","D. Gastrin","E. Lysozyme"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Intrinsic factor","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Intrinsic factor.</b> Cell A is large and round, stains pale pink (acidophilic, because it is full of mitochondria), and has a round central nucleus: a parietal (oxyntic) cell of a fundic gland. Parietal cells secrete hydrochloric acid (HCl) and intrinsic factor, which is needed to absorb vitamin B12. (Cell B is a purple chief cell.)<br>A. Pepsinogen: made by chief cells (cell B), not by cell A.<br>C. Histamine: made by enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells, a type of enteroendocrine cell.<br>D. Gastrin: made by G cells in the pyloric (antral) glands.<br>E. Lysozyme: made by Paneth cells of the small intestine (and by pyloric gland cells).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-54.jpg","in":"Fundic glands: arrow A on a round pink cell with a central nucleus; arrow B on a purple (basophilic) cell lower in the gland.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":54,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":16,"label":"Enteroendocrine cells: special stains, serotonin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-31.jpg","cap":"Enteroendocrine cells: seen with chromium, silver or immunostains; serotonin increases motility","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/G018-32.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: enteroendocrine cell (E) with basal granules beside parietal, chief cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F0745-32-1","s":"How can we find the EE (enteroendocrine) cells?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Immunostaining","a":null,"why":"<b>With special stains, mainly immunostaining (immunohistochemistry).</b> Enteroendocrine (EE) cells are small and stain poorly on routine H&amp;E (hematoxylin and eosin). They show up with <b>antibody stains for their hormones</b>, or with <b>chromium or silver salts</b>.<br>Common mix-up: a pale cell at the gland base on H&amp;E may be an EE cell, but only the special stain proves it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Same fundic-gland micrograph (parietal A, chief B); not needed to answer.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"MT week 11","sl":32,"f":"F0745"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G017-13-1"},{"id":"F1211-193-1","s":"BONUS: What cell does the red arrow point to?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A poorly stained G cell (enteroendocrine cell)","a":null,"why":"<b>An enteroendocrine (EE) cell.</b> The red arrow marks a <b>small cell with pale, almost unstained cytoplasm</b> in the deep gland, among dark-purple chief cells. EE cells stain poorly on H&amp;E (hematoxylin and eosin) and need <b>special stains</b> to be seen clearly. They secrete hormones such as serotonin and gastrin.<br>Common mix-up: parietal cells are also light, but they are large, round and pink, with a central nucleus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1211-193.jpg","in":"Same gastric-gland micrograph: the red arrow points to a small cell with pale, unstained cytoplasm at the base of a gland among dark chief cells.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":193,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":2,"kind":"harder","like":"G017-13-1"},{"id":"G017-13-1","s":"A researcher uses an immunostaining preparation to examine a slide from the gastric cardia mucosa. On his immunostained preparation, he can observe scattered cells in the glandular portion not seen in the H&E preparation. Which of the following best applies to these cells?","o":["A. Their secretions may increase motility of the GI tract","B. They ordinarily replace old or damaged gastric epithelial cells","C. They are capable of secreting mucus in the stomach","D. They are required for vitamin B12 absorption","E. Intracellular canaliculi are an ultrastructural feature of these cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Their secretions may increase motility of the GI tract","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Their secretions may increase gut motility.</b> Cells seen only with special stains (immunostaining, silver or chromium salts) are <b>enteroendocrine cells</b>. One type, the enterochromaffin cell, secretes <b>serotonin</b>, which increases motility.<br>B. Replacing old cells: stem cells in the isthmus and neck.<br>C. Secreting mucus: surface mucous and mucous neck cells, visible on routine hematoxylin and eosin stain.<br>D. Needed for vitamin B12 absorption: parietal cells (intrinsic factor).<br>E. Intracellular canaliculi: parietal cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Immunostain of gastric glands with scattered dark brown-stained single cells in the glandular epithelium (enteroendocrine cells); not needed to answer.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":13,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":9,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3961-7-1","s":"A researcher uses an immunostaining preparation to examine a slide from the gastric mucosa. He can observe a set of cells from the glandular portion which had not been observed in the H&E preparation (see the figure below). Which of the following best applies to these cells?","o":["A. Their secretions may increase motility of the GI tract","B. They ordinarily replace old or damaged gastric epithelial cells","C. They are capable of secreting mucus in the stomach","D. They are required for vitamin B12 absorption","E. Intracellular canaliculi are an ultrastructural feature of these cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Their secretions may increase motility of the GI tract","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Their secretions may increase gastrointestinal (GI) motility.</b> Gland cells seen only with <b>immunostaining</b>, not on routine H&amp;E (hematoxylin and eosin), are <b>enteroendocrine cells</b>. Some of them (enterochromaffin cells) secrete <b>serotonin</b>, which increases gut motility.<br>B. Replacing old cells: stem cells in the gland neck (isthmus).<br>C. Secreting mucus: surface and neck mucous cells.<br>D. Needed for vitamin B12 absorption: parietal cells (intrinsic factor).<br>E. Intracellular canaliculi: a feature of acid-secreting parietal cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Immunostained gastric mucosa (per slide 8: gastrin stain highlighting G cells in antral mucosa); not needed to answer. Source file deleted; text dump only, picture not available.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":7,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":5,"f":"F0731"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G017-13-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":17,"label":"Pernicious anemia: no B12, large red cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-85.jpg","cap":"Pernicious anemia: no parietal-cell intrinsic factor, so B12 deficiency and large red cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 85"},{"src":"sl/G017-17.jpg","cap":"Smear: hypersegmented neutrophil, oval macrocytes and Howell-Jolly body in B12 deficiency","where":"2026 digestive interactive, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"G017-21-1","s":"Pernicious anemia: Lack of intrinsic factor (IF), normally secreted by parietal cells, causes: what vitamin deficiency? what type of anemia?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Vitamin B12 deficiency; megaloblastic anemia","a":null,"why":"<b>Vitamin B12 deficiency, causing megaloblastic anemia.</b> <b>Parietal cells</b> secrete intrinsic factor (IF). IF binds vitamin B12 so the ileum can absorb it. Without IF, B12 falls, DNA synthesis in the marrow slows, and red cells come out <b>large</b> (megaloblastic).<br>Common mix-up: low acid alone does not cause the anemia; the missing IF does.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":21,"f":"G017"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3980-37-1"},{"id":"F3980-37-1","s":"A woman comes into your office complaining of weakness and fatigue. She has a past history of stomach bypass surgery. You do some tests and find that she is deficient in vitamin B12. You decide to do a blood smear. What will you see?","o":["A. Crescent shaped red blood cells","B. Small red blood cells","C. Big red blood cells","D. Fragmented red blood cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Big red blood cells (Megaloblastic anemia)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Big red blood cells.</b> After stomach bypass, the <b>parietal cells</b> that make intrinsic factor no longer serve the food stream, so vitamin B12 is not absorbed. Low B12 slows DNA synthesis in the marrow, and red cells come out <b>large</b>: megaloblastic anemia.<br>A. Crescent (sickle) cells: sickle cell disease.<br>B. Small red cells: iron deficiency.<br>D. Fragmented red cells (schistocytes): cells torn in damaged small vessels.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":37,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":195,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":177,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0755-227-1","s":"A patient presents to your office with symptoms of fatigue, diarrhea, loss of appetite. He stated that a few months ago he had a procedure done which you ultimately found out was stomach bypass and he has lost about 45 lbs. The symptoms began after the surgery. Which condition represents the clinical presentation? What in addition would a CBC exam show?","o":["A. Barrett’s esophagus; parietal cells","B. Pernicious Anemia; Megaloblastic anemia","C. No condition, the symptoms are due to the surgery","D. Vitamin B 12 deficiency, sideroblastic anemia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pernicious Anemia; Megaloblastic anemia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pernicious anemia; megaloblastic anemia.</b> Stomach bypass cuts off the <b>parietal cells</b>, so there is no <b>intrinsic factor</b> and vitamin B12 is not absorbed. The CBC (complete blood count) would show <b>large red cells</b>: megaloblastic anemia.<br>A. Barrett's esophagus: columnar metaplasia from acid reflux, not from bypass.<br>C. 'No condition': the symptoms have a clear cause.<br>D. Sideroblastic anemia: iron stuck in red-cell mitochondria; B12 deficiency gives megaloblastic, not sideroblastic, anemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":227,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3980-37-1"},{"id":"G017-16-1","s":"[Case context, slides 16-18: 60-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis; fatigue, worsening numbness/tingling, unsteadiness, foul-smelling diarrhea, pale conjunctiva, HR 100; Hb 10, MCV 110 fL; low vitamin B12; EGD: diffuse atrophic gastritis of fundus and body, H. pylori negative, autoimmune pattern.] Given the principal complaint of weakness, you decide to investigate her hematologic status first. You order a complete blood count with manual differential. Her red cell indices were notable for a Hb of 10 g/dL, Hct of 33 and MCV of 110 fL. An image from the peripheral blood smear is presented here. Are there significant findings?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Yes: hypersegmented neutrophil, oval macrocytes and a Howell-Jolly body (megaloblastic anemia)","a":null,"why":"<b>Yes: megaloblastic anemia.</b> The smear shows <b>oval macrocytes</b> (big, oval red cells), a <b>hypersegmented neutrophil</b> (too many nuclear lobes), and a <b>Howell-Jolly body</b> (a small nuclear leftover inside a red cell). With an MCV (mean corpuscular volume, the average red cell size) of one hundred ten, this fits vitamin B12 deficiency.<br>Common mix-up: iron deficiency gives small, pale red cells, not big oval ones.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G017-16.jpg","in":"Peripheral blood smear with large oval red cells, a neutrophil with many nuclear lobes, and a red cell containing a small dark nuclear remnant (Howell-Jolly body).","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":16,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":12,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":17,"label":"Pernicious anemia: parietal cells lost","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-76.jpg","cap":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete HCl and intrinsic factor for vitamin B12 absorption","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 76"},{"src":"sl/G015-85.jpg","cap":"Pernicious anemia: intrinsic factor lost; stomach bypass surgery can also cause it","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 85"}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-21-1","s":"A 65-year-old woman has fatigue and a smooth, red tongue. Labs show macrocytic anemia. Her vitamin B12 level is very low. Further testing shows antibodies against intrinsic factor. Which of the following is the most likely cause of her anemia?","o":["A. Poor dietary intake of B12","B. Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells","C. Chronic blood loss","D. Folate deficiency","E. Iron deficiency"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells.</b> <b>Antibodies against intrinsic factor (IF)</b> mean pernicious anemia. The immune attack wipes out parietal cells, which make IF, so B12 is not absorbed. Result: macrocytic anemia and a smooth red tongue.<br>A. Poor diet: rare, and it would not make anti-IF antibodies.<br>C. Chronic blood loss: iron deficiency, small red cells.<br>D. Folate deficiency: also macrocytic, but B12 would be normal.<br>E. Iron deficiency: small, pale red cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":21,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1},{"id":"G017-19-1","s":"[Case context, slides 16-18: 60-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis; fatigue, worsening numbness/tingling, unsteadiness, foul-smelling diarrhea, pale conjunctiva, HR 100; Hb 10, MCV 110 fL; low vitamin B12; EGD: diffuse atrophic gastritis of fundus and body, H. pylori negative, autoimmune pattern.] Pernicious anemia: Autoimmune etiology is associated with: which pattern of gastritis? and antibodies to:","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Atrophic autoimmune gastritis; antibodies to the parietal cell H+/K+ pump and to intrinsic factor (IF)","a":null,"why":"<b>Atrophic autoimmune gastritis, with antibodies to the parietal cell H+/K+ pump (the proton pump) and to intrinsic factor (IF).</b> The attack hits the <b>fundus and body</b>, where parietal cells live. Losing them means no acid and no IF, so vitamin B12 is not absorbed.<br>Common mix-up: Helicobacter pylori gastritis mostly involves the antrum and has no such autoantibodies; she tested negative.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":19,"f":"G017"}],"tier":1},{"id":"G017-23-1","s":"[Case context, slides 16-18: 60-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis; fatigue, worsening numbness/tingling, unsteadiness, foul-smelling diarrhea, pale conjunctiva, HR 100; Hb 10, MCV 110 fL; low vitamin B12; EGD: diffuse atrophic gastritis of fundus and body, H. pylori negative, autoimmune pattern.] An image from the gastric body biopsy is presented below with an image from a more normal gastric body for comparison. Absence of which cell type in the patient's gastric body is most likely responsible for her clinical findings?","o":["A. Chief cells","B. Parietal cells","C. Surface mucous cells","D. Enteroendocrine cells","E. Mucous neck cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Parietal cells.</b> Autoimmune atrophic gastritis destroys the <b>parietal cells</b> of the fundus and body. They make <b>intrinsic factor</b>, so vitamin B12 is not absorbed: megaloblastic anemia plus nerve damage (numbness, unsteadiness).<br>A. Chief cells: make pepsinogen; losing them does not cause B12 deficiency.<br>C. Surface mucous cells: protective alkaline mucus.<br>D. Enteroendocrine cells: hormones such as gastrin and serotonin.<br>E. Mucous neck cells: acidic mucus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Biopsy (chronic atrophic gastritis): thin mucosa with few, sparse glands and inflamed lamina propria vs normal body mucosa packed with tall oxyntic glands.","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":23,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":15,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F4081-21-1"},{"id":"F3961-12-1","s":"A 42-year-old man presents to your office six months after his stomach bypass surgery. He has lost 40 pounds since the surgery and is happy with the results. However, he reports feeling constantly fatigued and irritable and has also noticed that his tongue appears to be swollen. A CBC examination yields the photomicrograph below (megaloblastic anemia). An underlying problem to which of the following cells could best explain this patient's clinical presentation?","o":["A. Chief Cells","B. Parietal cells","C. Goblet Cells","D. Enteroendocrine cells","E. Mucous neck cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Parietal cells.</b> After stomach bypass, food skips most of the stomach, so the <b>parietal cells</b> no longer supply <b>intrinsic factor</b>. Without it, vitamin B12 is not absorbed: megaloblastic anemia, fatigue and a swollen tongue.<br>A. Chief cells: make pepsinogen and lipase; not needed for B12.<br>C. Goblet cells: intestinal mucus.<br>D. Enteroendocrine cells: hormones.<br>E. Mucous neck cells: acidic mucus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Peripheral smear (per notes: hypersegmented neutrophil, oval macrocytes, Howell-Jolly body); stem states megaloblastic anemia, so not needed. Source file deleted; text dump only, picture not available.","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)","H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":12,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":8,"f":"F0731"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F4081-21-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":17,"label":"H. pylori gastritis raises gastric lymphoma risk","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-85.jpg","cap":"Peptic ulcer disease is usually caused by Helicobacter pylori","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 85"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-91-1","s":"Harold, a fifty-eight year-old grocery store manager, had recently been waking up in the middle of the night with abdominal pain. This was happening several nights a week. He was also experiencing occasional discomfort in the middle of the afternoon. Harold decided to schedule an appointment with his physician. An endoscopic procedure was performed, and biopsies taken. See the accompanying figure. If left untreated, the patient would be at an increased risk for which of the following?","o":["A. Gastric melanoma","B. Gastric nerve sheath tumor","C. Gastric lymphoma","D. Gastric neuroblastoma","E. Gastric Squamous cell carcinoma"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric lymphoma","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Gastric lymphoma.</b> The curved rods in the surface mucus are <b>Helicobacter pylori</b>, the usual cause of peptic ulcer disease. Long-term infection makes gastric lymphoid tissue grow, raising the risk of <b>MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue) lymphoma</b>, and of gastric adenocarcinoma.<br>A. Melanoma: a melanocyte cancer, not linked to H. pylori.<br>B. Nerve sheath tumor: from Schwann cells; no infection link.<br>D. Neuroblastoma: a childhood neural crest tumor, usually adrenal.<br>E. Squamous cell carcinoma: the stomach has simple columnar epithelium, not squamous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-91.jpg","in":"High-power gastric surface mucous cells with many small curved/spiral rods in the mucus over the epithelium (Helicobacter pylori).","tg":["H15A slide 85 (Pernicious anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":91,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":17,"label":"Gastric leiomyoma: benign smooth muscle tumor","slides":[{"src":"sl/F3955-126.jpg","cap":"Leiomyoma: bundles of spindle cells, positive for smooth muscle myosin","where":"2024 Middletown lab clicker, slide 126"},{"src":"sl/G018-27.jpg","cap":"Stomach wall: muscularis externa of oblique, circular and longitudinal smooth muscle","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-124-1","s":"A 54-years-old male patient presented to his primary care physician with continuing epigastric discomfort and heart burn. He stated that he had been experiencing these same symptoms for the past 8 months. He stated that taking OTC medicines did help with the discomfort but then he decided to stop taking them. At times, he did experience moderate nausea. His general physical examination was unremarkable. No palpable mass in epigastrium was detected on abdominal examination. Upper GI endoscopy showed submucosal lesion in the cardia of the stomach. A CT scan revealed a 2.5 x 3.0 cm mass situated in that area. A laparoscopic wedge resection of the mass was successfully performed. The mass was sent to pathology for histological examination. [Slides 125-126: gastric submucosal lesion; composed of bundles of elongated cells with oblong nuclei; cells positive for smooth muscle myosin heavy chain.] Based on the clinical findings and endoscopic image obtained plus the histological photomicrographs, the most accurate diagnosis for this lesion is which of the following?","o":["A. Gastric lipoma","B. Gastric schwannoma","C. Gastric leiomyosarcoma","D. Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","E. Gastric leiomyoma"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric leiomyoma","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Gastric leiomyoma.</b> A small, well-defined submucosal mass of <b>bland spindle cells in bundles</b> that stain for <b>smooth muscle myosin</b> is a benign smooth muscle tumor. It arises from the muscularis and is common in the cardia.<br>A. Lipoma: mature fat cells.<br>B. Schwannoma: Schwann (nerve sheath) cells; stains for S-100, not smooth muscle myosin.<br>C. Leiomyosarcoma: smooth muscle too, but atypical nuclei and many mitoses.<br>D. GIST (gastrointestinal stromal tumor): from interstitial cells of Cajal; stains for KIT (CD117).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Bland spindle cells in interlacing bundles (H&E) staining brown for smooth muscle myosin heavy chain.","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":124,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":18,"label":"Plicae circulares: small intestine, not stomach","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-37.jpg","cap":"Small intestine surface: plicae circulares, then villi, then microvilli","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 37"},{"src":"sl/G016-12.jpg","cap":"Small intestine folds (plicae) carry villi; villi carry microvilli","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-153-2","s":"2. Plicae circularis (valves of Kerkring) are present in","o":["A. stomach","B. ileum","C. duodenum","D. jejunum","E. all but the stomach"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"all but the stomach","a":"E","why":"<b>E. All but the stomach.</b> <b>Plicae circulares</b> are permanent circular folds of mucosa with a <b>submucosal core</b>. They line the <b>duodenum, jejunum and ileum</b> and are best developed in the jejunum.<br>A. Stomach: has <b>rugae</b> instead, folds that flatten when the stomach fills.<br>B. Ileum: has plicae, but so do the other two segments.<br>C. Duodenum: has plicae, but so do the others.<br>D. Jejunum: best plicae, but not the only place.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 10 (Digests food and absorbs end products of digestion.)","H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 22 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":153,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":18,"label":"Micrograph: fold with submucosal core = plica","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-21.jpg","cap":"Jejunum: prominent plicae circulares (P) with thin submucosal cores (SM)","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/G021-16.jpg","cap":"Definitions: plica has submucosal core; villus has lamina propria core; microvilli apical","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-16-1","s":"Identify (the structure circled in red)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Plicae circulares!","a":null,"why":"<b>Plica circularis (circular fold).</b> The red circle encloses one whole tall fold. Its core is <b>submucosa (SM)</b> rising from above the muscularis, and its surface carries many <b>villi (V)</b>. Plicae are best developed in the <b>jejunum</b>.<br>Common mix-up: a single villus is far smaller and has only a lamina propria core; a plica is a fold of mucosa plus submucosa carrying many villi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0749-16.jpg","in":"Low-power small intestine (Junqueira): tall folds each covered by many villi (V), with cores of submucosa (SM) arising above the muscularis (M) and serosa (S); one whole fold, from its submucosal core up through its villi, is circled in red; P labels another fold.","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 21 (Prominent plicae circulares)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":16,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-58-1"},{"id":"F3955-70-1","s":"Name the highlighted structure:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. Microvilli","C. Villi","D. Rugae"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Plicae circularis","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Plicae circulares.</b> The oval circles a whole <b>fold with a core of submucosa</b>, and the fold is covered by many villi. A fold that carries villi is a plica.<br>B. Microvilli: tiny projections on single cells, seen only as the brush border at high power.<br>C. Villi: the small finger-like projections on the fold, with only a lamina propria core.<br>D. Rugae: stomach folds, which carry no villi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-70.jpg","in":"Red oval around one whole large fold with a submucosal core that carries many villi (a plica).","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":70,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":72,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":167,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-58-1"},{"id":"G018-58-1","s":"Q11. Name the circled structure:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. Brush border (microvilli)","C. Villi","D. Rugae"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Plicae circularis","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Plicae circularis.</b> The circle holds a whole fold that has a core of submucosa (connective tissue) and is covered with many villi. Plicae circulares (valves of Kerckring) are permanent circular folds of mucosa plus submucosa: the largest of the three ways the small intestine enlarges its surface (plicae, then villi, then microvilli).<br>B. Brush border (microvilli): far too small to see at this power; it is the striped edge on top of each absorptive cell.<br>C. Villi: the many finger-like projections on the surface of the fold; each has only a lamina propria core.<br>D. Rugae: folds of the stomach wall, which flatten out when the stomach fills.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-58.jpg","in":"Low power small intestine; an oval circles one entire tall fold (with a connective-tissue core) covered by villi.","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 10 (Digests food and absorbs end products of digestion.)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":58,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":18,"label":"Micrograph: finger-like projection = villus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-37.jpg","cap":"Light-microscope level: intestinal villi with lamina propria cores","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 37"},{"src":"sl/G021-16.jpg","cap":"Villi: finger-like fronds of columnar epithelium around a lamina propria core","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-169-1","s":"Q21. Name the highlighted structure:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. Microvilli","C. Villi","D. Rugae"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Villi","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Villi.</b> The small oval outlines one <b>finger-like projection</b> of simple columnar epithelium with a <b>lamina propria core</b>, one of many sitting on a larger fold.<br>A. Plicae circulares: the big fold underneath, with a submucosal core.<br>B. Microvilli: too small to see one by one; they form the brush border of each cell.<br>D. Rugae: stomach folds without villi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-169.jpg","in":"Higher-power H&E of a fold covered by many finger-like projections; a small red oval outlines one projection of epithelium with a lamina-propria core.","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":169,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-59-1"},{"id":"G018-59-1","s":"Q12. Name the circled structure:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. MicrovillIi","C. Villus","D. Rugae"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Villus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Villus.</b> The circle holds one finger-like projection of mucosa: epithelium wrapped around a core of lamina propria, sticking out from the side of a larger fold. Villi are the middle size of the three surface enlargements of the small intestine.<br>A. Plicae circularis: the whole large fold that carries the villi; its core is submucosa.<br>B. Microvilli: tiny projections of the cell membrane, seen only as the brush border at high power.<br>D. Rugae: stomach folds, not found in the small intestine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-59.jpg","in":"Medium power: one fold with many finger-like projections; an oval (and arrow) on a single projection.","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 10 (Digests food and absorbs end products of digestion.)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":59,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":18,"label":"Brush border = microvilli","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-39.jpg","cap":"Brush border on enterocytes, and its microvilli by electron microscopy","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-74-1","s":"Name the structure shown by the arrow:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. Microvilli","C. Villi","D. Rugae"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Microvilli","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Microvilli.</b> The arrow points to the thin, fuzzy, <b>pink striated (brush) border</b> on the apical surface of columnar absorptive cells. Under the light microscope, the brush border is the packed <b>microvilli</b>.<br>A. Plicae circulares: large folds with a submucosal core, seen at low power.<br>C. Villi: whole finger-like projections with a lamina propria core.<br>D. Rugae: stomach folds.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-74.jpg","in":"High power of columnar absorptive cells; arrow on the pink fuzzy striated (brush) border at the apical surface.","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":74,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":171,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-60-1"},{"id":"G018-60-1","s":"Q13. Name the structure shown by the arrow:","o":["A. Plicae circularis","B. Brush border (microvilli)","C. Villi","D. Rugae"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Brush border (microvilli)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Brush border (microvilli).</b> The arrow points to the thin, pink, finely striped band on the top (apical) surface of the columnar absorptive cells (enterocytes). It is made of thousands of microvilli on each cell and carries the brush-border enzymes for the last steps of digestion.<br>A. Plicae circularis: large folds with a submucosal core, seen at low power.<br>C. Villi: finger-like projections made of many cells, not a band on top of single cells.<br>D. Rugae: folds of the stomach wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-60.jpg","in":"High power of a villus tip: arrow on the pink apical border of the columnar cells.","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 12 (Mucosa features)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":60,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":18,"label":"Crypts: all small intestine and colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-13.jpg","cap":"Small intestine diagram: crypts of Lieberkühn dip down between the villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/G016-26.jpg","cap":"Large intestine: simple columnar intestinal glands (crypts) with goblet cells, no villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-60-1","s":"Which gastrointestinal organ(s) contain Crypts of Lieberkühn?","o":["A. Esophagus","B. Fundus of Stomach","C. Duodenum","D. Jejunum","E. Ileum","F. Large intestine"],"g":["C","D","E","F"],"k":["C","D","E","F"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum, Jejunum, Ileum, Large intestine","a":"C,D,E,F","why":"<b>C, D, E, F. Duodenum, jejunum, ileum and large intestine.</b> <b>Crypts of Lieberkühn</b> are tubular glands of the mucosa that dip down between the villi all along the small intestine. The <b>large intestine lacks villi but keeps its crypts</b>.<br>A. Esophagus: stratified squamous lining; its mucous glands sit in the submucosa, not crypts.<br>B. Fundus of stomach: gastric pits leading to gastric glands (parietal and chief cells), not intestinal crypts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":60,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":137,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":18,"label":"Micrograph: glands at mucosa base = crypts","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-19.jpg","cap":"Duodenum: crypts sit above the muscularis mucosae; Brunner glands lie below it","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/G018-38.jpg","cap":"Crypt of Lieberkühn: tubular gland between villi, lined by intestinal cell types","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-49-1","s":"Identify the glandular structures located between the lines (shown in the image).","o":["A. Brunner glands","B. Cardiac glands","C. Crypts of Lieberkühn","D. Fundic glands","E. Pyloric glands"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Crypts of Lieberkühn","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Crypts of Lieberkühn.</b> The lines bracket <b>tubular glands at the base of the mucosa</b>, between the villus bases and the muscularis mucosae. Villi above mean small intestine; the submucosa below holds only vessels (arrows), no glands.<br>A. Brunner glands: pale mucous glands below the muscularis mucosae, in the submucosa.<br>B. Cardiac glands: stomach, next to the esophagus.<br>D. Fundic glands: stomach glands of parietal and chief cells.<br>E. Pyloric glands: coiled mucous glands of the stomach outlet.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-49.jpg","in":"Small intestine with tall villi; two black lines bracket the tubular glands in the lower mucosa between villus bases and muscularis mucosae; arrows mark vessels in the gland-free submucosa below.","tg":["H15B slide 43 (Q3-  Identify the glandular structures located between the lines (shown in the i)","H15B slide 18 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":49,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":213,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0754-177-1","s":"What are these?","o":["A. Crypts of Lieberkuhn","B. Brunners Glands","C. Goblet Cells","D. Plicae circulares","E. Payer’s Patches"],"g":["A"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Brunners Glands","a":"B","why":"<b>A. Crypts of Lieberkühn.</b> The arrows end on <b>tubular glands at the base of the mucosa</b>, between the villi and just above the muscularis mucosae. They are lined by columnar cells with goblet cells: intestinal crypts.<br>B. Brunner glands: pale, foamy mucous lobules <b>below</b> the muscularis mucosae, in the duodenal submucosa; none are in this field.<br>C. Goblet cells: single pale cells inside these glands, not whole glands.<br>D. Plicae circulares: large folds with a submucosal core.<br>E. Peyer's patches: dark-blue lymphoid nodules of the ileum.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed B (Brunner's glands), but the arrows point to tubular glands inside the mucosa between the villus bases, above the muscularis mucosae: crypts of Lieberkühn."],"ch":true,"img":"img/F0754-177.jpg","in":"H&E of duodenal wall: tall villi above; arrows point to pale-staining glands lying deep to the crypts, below the muscularis mucosae in the submucosa (Brunner's glands).","tg":["H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":177,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3980-49-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":19,"label":"Enterocytes: terminal digestion and absorption","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-38.jpg","cap":"Enterocyte microvilli enlarge surface for terminal digestion and absorption","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/G018-39.jpg","cap":"Enterocytes: brush border of microvilli by light and electron microscopy","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-64-1","s":"A 35-year-old woman presents with chronic diarrhea and weight loss (Celiac Disease). A biopsy of the small intestine reveals a significant reduction in villi and enterocytes. Which of the following best describes the primary function of the cells that are most affected in this condition?","o":["A. Secretion of mucus for lubrication","B. Absorption of nutrients","C. Production of antimicrobial peptides","D. Hormonal regulation of digestion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Absorption of nutrients","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Absorption of nutrients.</b> Celiac disease destroys villi and the <b>enterocytes</b> on them. Enterocytes are tall columnar cells whose <b>microvilli</b> finish digestion and absorb nutrients, so losing them causes diarrhea and weight loss.<br>A. Mucus for lubrication: goblet cells.<br>C. Antimicrobial peptides: Paneth cells.<br>D. Hormones: enteroendocrine cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":64,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1},{"id":"G017-30-1","s":"A sample of small intestinal mucosa from a patient with celiac disease and a sample of normal small intestinal mucosa for comparison are illustrated below. Given the histologically apparent structural alterations of the small intestinal mucosa seen here (celiac disease: villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia), what normal functions would you expect to be most abnormal in the patient with celiac disease?","o":["A. Concentration of bile","B. Terminal digestion and nutrient absorption","C. Water resorption","D. Initiation of carbohydrate digestion","E. Initiation of protein and triglyceride digestion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Terminal digestion and nutrient absorption","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Terminal digestion and nutrient absorption.</b> Celiac disease flattens the villi (<b>villous atrophy</b>) and deepens the crypts. Fewer villi and microvilli mean less surface and fewer <b>brush-border enzymes</b> on enterocytes, so the last steps of digestion and absorption fail.<br>A. Concentrating bile: the gallbladder.<br>C. Water resorption: mostly the colon.<br>D. Starting carbohydrate digestion: salivary amylase in the mouth.<br>E. Starting protein and triglyceride digestion: pepsin and gastric lipase in the stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Celiac mucosa: flat surface with no villi and long hyperplastic crypts; normal mucosa: tall villi over short crypts (labels in stem).","tg":["H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)","H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":30,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":24,"f":"F0702"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":48,"f":"F3961"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3950-64-1"},{"id":"F4081-11-1","s":"A 21-year-old woman undergoes endoscopy for chronic diarrhea. Biopsies of different segments of her GI tract are compared to normal histology. One sample shows simple columnar epithelium with tall cells possessing dense microvilli forming a prominent brush border, along with enzymes anchored to this border that complete the final steps of carbohydrate and protein digestion. Which of the following regions of the digestive tract normally has this apical surface modification and associated function?","o":["A. Esophagus – Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium for protection from abrasion","B. Stomach – Simple columnar epithelium with surface mucous cells for acid protection","C. Duodenum – Villi with microvilli that increase absorptive surface area and contain brush-border enzymes","D. Colon – Simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells for mucus secretion and water reabsorption","E. Anal canal – Stratified squamous epithelium for increased friction resistance"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum – Villi with microvilli that increase absorptive surface area and contain brush-border enzymes","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Duodenum: villi with microvilli and brush-border enzymes.</b> Tall columnar <b>enterocytes</b> with a dense <b>microvillus brush border</b> cover small-intestine villi. Enzymes anchored in that border finish carbohydrate and protein digestion.<br>A. Esophagus: stratified squamous, no microvilli.<br>B. Stomach: surface mucous cells make alkaline mucus; no brush border.<br>D. Colon: no villi; short, sparse microvilli; it absorbs water, it does not finish digestion.<br>E. Anal canal: stratified squamous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":11,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":19,"label":"Enterocytes re-make triglycerides","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-40.jpg","cap":"Enterocyte remakes triglycerides in smooth endoplasmic reticulum; chylomicrons exit to lacteal","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 40"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-66-1","s":"A 50-year-old male patient is diagnosed with a condition affecting the epithelial cells in the ileum, characterized by an inability to transport lipids properly. Upon further evaluation, it is found that the patient has a deficiency in chylomicron formation. Which cell type in the small intestine is primarily responsible for the reesterification of lipids into triglycerides?","o":["A. Goblet cells","B. M Cells","C. Paneth cells","D. Enterocytes"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Enterocytes","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Enterocytes.</b> Fatty acids and monoglycerides diffuse into enterocytes. There they are <b>re-made into triglycerides in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum</b>, coated with protein in the Golgi, and sent out as <b>chylomicrons</b> to the lacteals.<br>A. Goblet cells: secrete mucus.<br>B. M (microfold) cells: carry antigens to immune cells.<br>C. Paneth cells: secrete lysozyme and defensins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":66,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3952-75-1"},{"id":"F3952-75-1","s":"A 28-year-old man presents to the clinic with symptoms of malabsorption, including weight loss and persistent diarrhea. During your evaluation, you discuss the role of absorptive cells in the small intestine, emphasizing their importance in nutrient absorption, particularly lipids, and the synthesis of triglycerides from these absorbed lipids. You explain that these specialized cells play a crucial role in the digestive process, helping maintain overall health. Which of the following is true of the absorptive cells of the small intestine?","o":["A. Also called enteroendocrine cells","B. Have many microvilli covering their basal surfaces","C. Absorb lipids by active transport","D. Synthesize triglycerides from absorbed lipids","E. Undergo mitosis at tips of villi and are sloughed off into crypts"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Synthesize triglycerides from absorbed lipids","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Synthesize triglycerides from absorbed lipids.</b> <b>Enterocytes</b> re-make triglycerides in their smooth endoplasmic reticulum and package them as chylomicrons for the lacteals.<br>A. Enteroendocrine cells: a different, hormone-making cell.<br>B. Microvilli cover the <b>apical</b> (lumen) surface, not the basal one.<br>C. Lipids enter by <b>passive diffusion</b> (and carriers), not active transport.<br>E. Reversed: cells divide in the <b>crypts</b>, move up, and are shed at villus tips.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","L56 slide 19 (Lipid Absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":75,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":19,"label":"Fat leaves villi via lacteals","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-40.jpg","cap":"Enterocyte packs triglycerides into chylomicrons that enter the lymphatic lacteal","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 40"},{"src":"sl/G016-12.jpg","cap":"Villus diagram: central lacteal beside arteriole and venule","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-205-1","s":"Q7. What is true about fat absorption in the GI tract?","o":["A. Fat is taken up by sinusoidal capillaries in the villi","B. Fat is absorbed via the GLUT2 transporter","C. Fat is taken up via fenestrated capillaries in the villi","D. Fat is absorbed via lacteals in the villi"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Fat is absorbed via lacteals in the villi","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Fat is absorbed via lacteals in the villi.</b> Enterocytes re-make fats into triglycerides, pack them into <b>chylomicrons</b>, and release them at their base. Chylomicrons are too big for blood capillaries, so they enter the <b>lacteal</b>, the lymphatic capillary in the center of each villus.<br>A. Sinusoidal capillaries: in liver and bone marrow, not villi.<br>B. GLUT2 (glucose transporter 2): moves sugars, not fat.<br>C. Fenestrated villus capillaries: take up sugars, amino acids and short-chain fatty acids, not chylomicrons.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L56 slide 23 (Chylomicrons)","H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":205,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":187,"f":"F1211"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":19,"label":"Crypt stem cells mostly make enterocytes","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-13.jpg","cap":"Regenerative stem cells at crypt base renew enterocytes, goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine cells","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F3952-67-1","s":"A 35-year-old woman presents with abdominal pain and diarrhea that has persisted for several weeks. During the examination, you discuss the structure and function of her small intestine, explaining how nutrients are absorbed and how the intestinal mucosa is lined with various cell types that contribute to this process. You highlight the role of intestinal glands, including the presence of stem cells in the crypts, and you focus on the predominant cell type that arises from these stem cells, which is crucial for nutrient absorption. Intestinal glands (or crypts) in the mucosa of the small intestine include stem cells. What is the most abundant cell type derived from these stem cells?","o":["A. Enterocytes","B. Goblet cells","C. Paneth cells","D. Chief cells","E. Parietal cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Enterocytes","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Enterocytes.</b> Stem cells in the <b>crypts</b> make every small-intestine lining cell, and the most numerous are the absorptive <b>enterocytes</b>. They climb the villus and are shed at its tip in about a week.<br>B. Goblet cells: made by the same stem cells, but far fewer.<br>C. Paneth cells: few, at the crypt base.<br>D. Chief cells: stomach.<br>E. Parietal cells: stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 11 (Plicae: increases absorption surface.)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":67,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":20,"label":"Paneth granules: lysozyme and defensins","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-38.jpg","cap":"Paneth cells: innate immunity; granules release lysozyme, defensins, phospholipase A2","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/G016-18.jpg","cap":"Crypt base: Paneth cells packed with bright red apical granules (arrow)","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-232-1","s":"What cells do you find in the Crypts of Lieberkuhn whose function is to secrete definsins and lysozomes?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Paneth cells","a":null,"why":"<b>Paneth cells.</b> They sit at the <b>base of the crypts of Lieberkühn</b> and have <b>large red (eosinophilic) apical granules</b>. The granules release <b>defensins, lysozyme and phospholipase A2</b>, which kill bacteria (innate immunity) and keep the crypt clean.<br>Common mix-up: goblet cells also line the crypts, but they secrete mucus and look pale, not red.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"High-power H&E of a crypt base with cells containing bright red apical granules (not needed to answer).","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":232,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G017-27-1"},{"id":"G017-27-1","s":"The granules within the cells at the pointer are rich in which of the following substances?","o":["A. Defensins","B. Cholecystokinin","C. Pepsin","D. IgA","E. Mucin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Defensins","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Defensins.</b> Cells at the <b>crypt base</b> packed with <b>bright red (eosinophilic) apical granules</b> are <b>Paneth cells</b>. Their granules release <b>defensins, lysozyme and phospholipase A2</b>, which kill bacteria (innate immunity).<br>B. Cholecystokinin: from enteroendocrine cells.<br>C. Pepsin: made as pepsinogen by stomach chief cells.<br>D. IgA (immunoglobulin A): from plasma cells in the lamina propria.<br>E. Mucin: from goblet cells, which look pale, not red.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G017-27.jpg","in":"High-power crypt base of small intestine; arrows point to cells packed with bright red/magenta apical granules (Paneth cells).","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":27,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":21,"f":"F0702"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":24,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":17,"f":"F0731"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":19,"f":"F0744"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3952-71-1","s":"A 42-year-old woman presents with recurrent gastrointestinal infections and unexplained weight loss. During your examination, you discuss the role of various intestinal cells in maintaining gut health, particularly the function of Paneth cells located in the crypts of the small intestine. You explain that these cells play a vital role in secreting antimicrobial peptides that help regulate the intestinal microbiome, and you consider how a reduction in their numbers could impact her gastrointestinal health. Which of the following would most likely result from a reduction in the number of Paneth cells?","o":["A. Thinning of the glycocalyx","B. Reduced breakdown of fats","C. Elevated levels of undigested proteins","D. Decreased mucus in the intestine","E. Increased number of intestinal bacteria"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased number of intestinal bacteria","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Increased number of intestinal bacteria.</b> <b>Paneth cells</b> at the crypt base release <b>lysozyme, defensins and phospholipase A2</b>, which kill bacteria and keep the crypt clean. Fewer Paneth cells, more bacteria.<br>A. Glycocalyx: made by enterocytes.<br>B. Fat breakdown: pancreatic lipase and bile.<br>C. Protein digestion: pancreatic proteases and brush-border peptidases.<br>D. Mucus: goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":71,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":20,"label":"Paneth cells: red granules at crypt base","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-17.jpg","cap":"Crypt base: Paneth cells with red granules (A); circled cell is dividing","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/G016-18.jpg","cap":"Paneth cells: crypt-base cells packed with bright red apical granules","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-14-1","s":"Identify [the cell indicated by the arrow / blank label].","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Paneth cells (at the base of the crypts of Lieberkühn, small intestine)","a":null,"why":"<b>Paneth cells.</b> The arrow marks cells at the <b>base of a crypt of Lieberkühn</b> packed with <b>large, bright red (eosinophilic) apical granules</b>. The granules hold lysozyme, defensins and phospholipase A2, which kill bacteria.<br>Common mix-up: stomach chief cells are also granular, but their cytoplasm is purple (basophilic) and they line gastric glands, not intestinal crypts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-14.jpg","in":"Base of a small-intestinal crypt: arrow (blank label) points to cells packed with large bright red apical granules flanking the crypt lumen (A).","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":14,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3955-60-1"},{"id":"F3955-60-1","s":"Name the cell shown by the letter A:","o":["A. Chief cell","B. Parietal cell","C. Enteroendocrine cell","D. Paneth cell"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Paneth cell","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Paneth cell.</b> A labels cells at the <b>base of an intestinal crypt</b> packed with <b>large, bright red apical granules</b>: Paneth cells, whose granules hold lysozyme and defensins. (The circled cell is a dividing stem cell.)<br>A. Chief cell: stomach gland cell with purple (basophilic) cytoplasm.<br>B. Parietal cell: stomach; large, pale pink, central nucleus, no coarse red granules.<br>C. Enteroendocrine cell: small and pale on H&amp;E (hematoxylin and eosin), with granules at its base, not its apex.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-60.jpg","in":"Crypt base with cells full of large bright red apical granules (A); a circled cell elsewhere.","tg":["H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)","H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":60,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":157,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0754-179-1","s":"(Micrograph with four labelled prompts) B. What types of cells are these? C. What types of cells are these? A. What is labeled A? D. What is this called? (hint- found in the small intestine)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"B. Paneth cells; C. stem cell proliferating; A. lysozymes and defensins; D. Crypts of Lieberkuhn","a":null,"why":"<b>B = Paneth cells; C = a dividing stem cell; A = Paneth granules (lysozyme, defensins); D = a crypt of Lieberkühn.</b> Paneth cells sit at the <b>very base of the crypt</b>, packed with <b>bright red apical granules</b>. The circled cell higher up shows a <b>mitotic figure</b>: crypt stem cells divide to renew the lining.<br>Common mix-up: Paneth granules are red (eosinophilic); stomach chief cells have purple (basophilic) cytoplasm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-179.jpg","in":"High-power H&E of the base of a small-intestinal crypt: arrow B to cells at the crypt base packed with bright red apical granules; A marks those red granules; circle C on a mitotic/proliferating nucleus higher up the crypt wall; D = the whole gland.","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":179,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3955-60-1"},{"id":"G018-55-1","s":"Q8. The granules in the cell indicated by the letter “A” contain:","o":["A. Pepsinogen","B. Intrinsic factor","C. Histamine","D. Gastrin","E. Lysozyme"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Lysozyme","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Lysozyme.</b> Cell A sits at the base of a crypt of Lieberkühn and is packed with large, bright red (eosinophilic) granules: a Paneth cell. Its granules hold antibacterial proteins: lysozyme, defensins and phospholipase A2. (The circled cell is a dividing stem cell.)<br>A. Pepsinogen: chief cells of the stomach.<br>B. Intrinsic factor: parietal cells of the stomach.<br>C. Histamine: enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells of the stomach.<br>D. Gastrin: G cells of the pyloric antrum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-55.jpg","in":"Base of an intestinal crypt: cell A filled with large bright red granules; a mitotic figure is circled nearby.","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 17 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":55,"f":"G018"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G017-27-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":20,"label":"Intestinal enteroendocrine cells: CCK and secretin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-38.jpg","cap":"Enteroendocrine cells secrete cholecystokinin and secretin","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/G018-41.jpg","cap":"Open enteroendocrine cell; its hormones (cholecystokinin, secretin) control digestive secretion","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-12-1","s":"Which of the following small intestine cell types secretes CCK and secretin?","o":["A. Regenerative cells","B. Paneth cells","C. Enteroendocrine cells","D. Goblet cells","E. Surface absorptive cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Enteroendocrine cells","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Enteroendocrine (EE) cells.</b> Small-intestine EE cells secrete <b>cholecystokinin (CCK)</b>, which releases pancreatic enzymes and bile, and <b>secretin</b>, which releases pancreatic bicarbonate.<br>A. Regenerative cells: stem cells at the crypt base.<br>B. Paneth cells: lysozyme and defensins.<br>D. Goblet cells: mucus.<br>E. Surface absorptive cells (enterocytes): absorb nutrients.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":12,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":21,"label":"Brunner glands → duodenum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-19.jpg","cap":"Duodenum: Brunner's glands fill the submucosa below the muscularis mucosae","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/G017-25.jpg","cap":"Brunner gland mucus, pH eight to nine-and-a-half, neutralizes acid chyme","where":"2026 digestive interactive, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-14-1","s":"Brunner’s glands are a defining feature of which portion of intestine?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Jejunum","C. Ileum","D. Cecum","E. Sigmoid colon"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Duodenum.</b> <b>Brunner glands</b> are branched mucous glands in the <b>submucosa</b> that make alkaline mucus to neutralize stomach acid. Among the intestines they are <b>exclusive to the duodenum</b>.<br>B. Jejunum: prominent plicae, thin submucosa without glands.<br>C. Ileum: Peyer's patches.<br>D. Cecum: large-intestine mucosa, no submucosal glands.<br>E. Sigmoid colon: crypts and goblet cells, no submucosal glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Labelled micrograph of duodenal wall showing Brunner's glands in the submucosa below the muscularis mucosae (labels give the answer away; not needed).","tg":["H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":14,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G017-24-1"},{"id":"F3952-69-1","s":"A 60-year-old man presents to the clinic with complaints of persistent abdominal discomfort and changes in bowel habits. During the physical examination, you discuss the anatomy of his gastrointestinal tract and highlight the various secretory functions that occur along its length. You explain that specific regions of the digestive tract contain large mucous glands in their submucosa, which play a crucial role in lubrication and protection of the intestinal lining. Large mucous glands are present in the submucosa of what digestive tract region?","o":["A. Corpus of the stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Pyloric stomach","D. Ileum","E. Large intestine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> Large mucous glands in the <b>submucosa</b> of the intestine are <b>Brunner glands</b>. They make alkaline mucus that neutralizes acid chyme and, among the intestines, are found only in the duodenum.<br>A. Corpus of the stomach: glands in the mucosa that secrete acid and enzymes.<br>C. Pyloric stomach: mucous glands, but in the mucosa, not the submucosa.<br>D. Ileum: Peyer's patches, no submucosal glands.<br>E. Large intestine: crypts in the mucosa only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":69,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G017-24-1"},{"id":"G017-24-1","s":"Histological evaluation of a portion of the gastrointestinal tract from a 55-year-old man with long-term gastrointestinal complaints shows ramified, tubular glands located in the submucosal layer. The glands contain secretions with pH close to 9.0. Which of the following segments of the gastrointestinal tract matches these findings?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Ascending colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> Branched tubular glands in the <b>submucosa</b> that make <b>alkaline mucus (pH near nine)</b> are <b>Brunner glands</b>. They neutralize acid chyme from the stomach and, among intestinal segments, exist only in the duodenum.<br>A. Stomach: its glands lie in the mucosa and secrete acid.<br>C. Jejunum: thin submucosa with no glands; tall plicae.<br>D. Ileum: Peyer's patches, no submucosal glands.<br>E. Ascending colon: crypts in the mucosa only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Small intestine with villi (mucosa) above and lobules of pale branched glands filling the submucosa (Brunner glands); stem gives the finding.","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":24,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":18,"f":"F0702"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":3,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":2,"f":"F0731"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":21,"label":"Micrograph: submucosal glands → duodenum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-19.jpg","cap":"Duodenum: Brunner's glands fill the submucosa below the muscularis mucosae","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/G018-36.jpg","cap":"Three segments: duodenum Brunner glands, jejunum plicae, ileum Peyer's patches","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-62-1","s":"Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Gallbladder","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> Leaf-like <b>villi</b> show it is small intestine, and the submucosa below is <b>packed with lobules of pale mucous glands</b>. Submucosal mucous glands in the intestine are <b>Brunner glands</b>, found only in the duodenum.<br>A. Gallbladder: folded mucosa, no villi, no submucosa.<br>C. Ileum: lymphoid nodules (Peyer's patches), no glands.<br>D. Colon: no villi; straight crypts full of goblet cells.<br>E. Jejunum: tall plicae with a thin, gland-free submucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-62.jpg","in":"Villi over a submucosa filled with lobules of pale mucous glands (Brunner glands).","tg":["H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":62,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":64,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":66,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":68,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":80,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":159,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-56-1"},{"id":"F0052-161-1","s":"Q17. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Gallbladder","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> Villi line the edge, so it is small intestine. Under the mucosa, the <b>submucosa is filled with lobules of pale-staining mucous glands</b>: <b>Brunner glands</b>, the mark of the duodenum.<br>A. Gallbladder: no villi and no submucosal glands.<br>C. Ileum: dark lymphoid nodules instead of glands.<br>D. Colon: no villi.<br>E. Jejunum: tall plicae, thin gland-free submucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-161.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: villous mucosa curving along the right/bottom with lobules of pale submucosal glands beneath it and muscularis externa at upper left.","tg":["H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 19 (Duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":161,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-56-1"},{"id":"G018-56-1","s":"Q9. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> The surface has villi, so this is small intestine, and the submucosa is filled with a thick layer of pale mucous glands: the Brunner (submucosal) glands. Brunner glands are found only in the duodenum; their alkaline mucus helps neutralize the acid coming from the stomach.<br>A. Stomach: gastric pits and glands, no villi.<br>C. Jejunum: tall plicae and villi, but no glands in the submucosa.<br>D. Ileum: Peyer patches (large lymphoid nodules), no submucosal glands.<br>E. Colon: no villi; straight crypts full of goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-56.jpg","in":"Low power: villous mucosa with a broad band of pale glands below the muscularis mucosae, then muscularis externa.","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":56,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":21,"label":"Jejunum: thin submucosa, no glands","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-21.jpg","cap":"Jejunum: prominent plicae circulares, thin non-remarkable submucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/G018-36.jpg","cap":"Only the duodenum has submucosal glands; jejunum has prominent plicae","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-174-1","s":"True or false? The submucosa of the jejunum is thin/non-remarkable, has no glands, and prominent plicae circularis","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"True","a":"A","why":"<b>A. True.</b> The jejunum has a <b>thin, unremarkable submucosa with no glands</b> and the <b>most prominent plicae circulares</b> of the gut. Its mucosa has villi, crypts and a rising number of goblet cells.<br>Common mix-up: the duodenum also has plicae, but its submucosa is filled with Brunner glands; the ileum has Peyer's patches.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 22 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 21 (Prominent plicae circulares)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":174,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-57-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":21,"label":"Micrograph: tall plicae, no glands → jejunum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-21.jpg","cap":"Jejunum: prominent plicae circulares, thin non-remarkable submucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/G018-36.jpg","cap":"Three segments: duodenum Brunner glands, jejunum prominent plicae, ileum Peyer's patches","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-165-1","s":"Q19. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Gallbladder","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Jejunum.</b> Several <b>tall, finger-like plicae circulares</b> rise from the wall, each with a <b>thin submucosal core</b> and covered by villi. There are <b>no submucosal glands</b> and no Peyer's patches. Prominent plicae plus a bare submucosa mean jejunum.<br>A. Gallbladder: no villi.<br>B. Duodenum: Brunner glands in the submucosa.<br>C. Ileum: lymphoid nodules (Peyer's patches).<br>D. Colon: no villi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-165.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: several tall, finger-like plicae circulares with thin submucosal cores, each covered by villi; no submucosal glands or Peyer's patches.","tg":["H15B slide 21 (Prominent plicae circulares)","H15B slide 22 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":165,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-57-1"},{"id":"F0052-163-1","s":"Q18. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Gallbladder","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Jejunum.</b> The wall throws up <b>tall folds (plicae circulares)</b> with <b>thin submucosal cores</b>, each covered by villi. There are <b>no submucosal glands</b> and no large lymphoid nodules.<br>A. Gallbladder: no villi or plicae.<br>B. Duodenum: submucosa full of Brunner glands.<br>C. Ileum: Peyer's patches (dark lymphoid nodules).<br>D. Colon: no villi; flat surface with straight crypts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-163.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: tall branching folds (plicae) with thin submucosal cores covered by villi; no submucosal glands or large lymphoid aggregates; muscularis and mesentery at top.","tg":["H15B slide 21 (Prominent plicae circulares)","H15B slide 22 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":163,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-57-1"},{"id":"G018-57-1","s":"Q10. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Jejunum.</b> Tall, well-developed plicae circulares (folds with a submucosal core), each covered with villi. The submucosa is thin and has no glands, and the lymphoid nodules are small. Plicae are most developed in the jejunum.<br>A. Stomach: rugae and gastric pits, no villi.<br>B. Duodenum: would show Brunner glands in the submucosa.<br>D. Ileum: would show large Peyer patches; its plicae are smaller.<br>E. Colon: no villi or plicae; straight crypts with goblet cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-57.jpg","in":"Low power: several tall folds of mucosa with a submucosal core, each covered by villi.","tg":["H15B slide 21 (Prominent plicae circulares)","H15B slide 22 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":57,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":22,"label":"Ileum: Peyer's patches with M cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-23.jpg","cap":"Ileum: dark Peyer's patch lymphoid nodules beneath the villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 23"},{"src":"sl/G016-24.jpg","cap":"Scanning electron micrograph: M-cell domes over Peyer patches, ringed by villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-181-1","s":"Which of these contains M cells, peyer’s patches, and plicae circularis?","o":["A. Appendix","B. Ileum","C. Colon","D. Jejunum","E. Duodenum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ileum.</b> <b>Peyer's patches</b> (large lymphoid nodules in the lamina propria and submucosa) and the <b>M (microfold) cells</b> covering them mark the ileum, which still has plicae circulares too.<br>A. Appendix: lymphoid follicles, but no plicae.<br>C. Colon: no plicae or villi; its lymphoid tissue is scattered, not Peyer's patches.<br>D. Jejunum: tall plicae, no Peyer's patches.<br>E. Duodenum: Brunner glands, no Peyer's patches.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 23 (Payer’s Patches)","H15B slide 25 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":181,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F4081-16-1","s":"Identify [this SEM image].","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Ileum: Peyer patch surface showing M (microfold) cells (dome epithelium surrounded by villi)","a":null,"why":"<b>Ileum: the dome over a Peyer patch, covered by M (microfold) cells.</b> In this scanning electron micrograph, round <b>domes with a fine net-like surface</b> sit among leaf-like villi. The domes are the epithelium over Peyer patch lymphoid nodules; their surface cells are M cells, which pass antigens to immune cells below.<br>Common mix-up: the leaf-like shapes around the domes are ordinary villi, not M cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-16.jpg","in":"Scanning electron micrograph: several round domes with a fine net-like surface, each ringed by leaf-like villi.","tg":["H15B slide 23 (Payer’s Patches)","H15B slide 24 (Peyer patch showing M (Microfold) cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":16,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"harder","like":"F0754-181-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":22,"label":"M cells pass antigens to immune cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-24.jpg","cap":"M cells endocytose antigens and pass them to pocketed lymphocytes and macrophages","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G018-42.jpg","cap":"Microfold (M) cells with intraepithelial pocket of lymphocytes and antigen-presenting cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-20-1","s":"Which of the following describes M-cells?","o":["A. Secrete a protective mucus layer made up of high molecular weight glycoproteins","B. Endocytose antigens and transport them to lymphocytes and macrophages","C. Secrete cholecystokinin and secretin","D. Produce lysozyme and regulate the bacterial line up","E. Contain microvilli and facilitate absorption"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Endocytose antigens and transport them to lymphocytes and macrophages","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Endocytose antigens and transport them to lymphocytes and macrophages.</b> <b>M (microfold) cells</b> cover Peyer's patches in the ileum. Their base has deep pockets holding lymphocytes and macrophages, so antigens taken from the lumen go straight to immune cells.<br>A. Protective mucus: goblet cells.<br>C. Cholecystokinin and secretin: enteroendocrine cells.<br>D. Lysozyme and bacterial control: Paneth cells.<br>E. Microvilli and absorption: enterocytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 24 (Peyer patch showing M (Microfold) cells)","H15B slide 25 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":20,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3980-45-1"},{"id":"F3980-45-1","s":"What cell works by sampling antigen from the intestinal lumen and trafficking it across the epithelium to immune cells?","o":["A. Macrophages","B. Enterocytes","C. Microfold cells","D. Mesothelial cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Microfold cells - aka M Cells","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Microfold (M) cells.</b> M cells sit in the epithelium over <b>Peyer's patches</b> in the ileum. They take up antigens from the lumen and carry them across to <b>lymphocytes and macrophages</b> held in pockets under their base.<br>A. Macrophages: receive the antigen under the epithelium; they do not form the lining.<br>B. Enterocytes: absorb nutrients.<br>D. Mesothelial cells: line the serosa on the outside of the gut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 25 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 23 (Payer’s Patches)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":45,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":190,"f":"F1211"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":208,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3950-68-1","s":"A patient presents with recurrent infections and a diagnosis of an immunodeficiency disorder. Further investigation reveals the presence of Peyer patches in the ileum that are not functioning properly. Which cell type in the intestinal mucosa is primarily responsible for sampling antigens and microorganisms to maintain mucosal immunity?","o":["A. Enterocytes","B. Goblet cells","C. Paneth cells","D. M (microfold) cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"M (microfold) cells","a":"D","why":"<b>D. M (microfold) cells.</b> M cells cover the <b>Peyer patches</b> of the ileum. They sample antigens and microbes from the lumen and pass them to the lymphocytes and macrophages in the patch, starting mucosal immunity.<br>A. Enterocytes: absorb nutrients.<br>B. Goblet cells: make mucus.<br>C. Paneth cells: release lysozyme and defensins to kill bacteria; they do not hand antigens to immune cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 25 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 23 (Payer’s Patches)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":68,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3980-45-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":23,"label":"Colon absorbs water and electrolytes","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-44.jpg","cap":"Colonocytes: short microvilli, dilated intercellular spaces, a sign of water transport","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44"},{"src":"sl/G016-30.jpg","cap":"Large intestine: transports sodium actively, water passively; forms stool","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-79-1","s":"A 55-year-old male presents to the clinic with complaints of intermittent abdominal pain and changes in bowel habits. Upon examination, he is noted to have increased bowel sounds and tenderness in the lower abdomen. A colonoscopy reveals significant diverticulosis in the sigmoid colon. Which of the following is a primary function of the large intestine that may be compromised in this patient?","o":["A. Absorption of nutrients","B. Production of bile","C. Absorption of water and electrolytes","D. Secretion of digestive enzymes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Absorption of water and electrolytes","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Absorption of water and electrolytes.</b> Colon absorptive cells <b>pump sodium actively, and water follows passively</b>, turning liquid chyme into stool.<br>A. Nutrient absorption: small intestine.<br>B. Bile production: liver.<br>D. Digestive enzymes: pancreas, stomach and small-intestine brush border; the colon makes mucus, not enzymes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":79,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3952-84-1","s":"At a health and wellness summit, actress and wellness advocate Gwyneth Paltrow speaks passionately about the importance of gut health and its impact on overall well-being. As she discusses her dietary choices and the role of various nutrients in digestion, she highlights the specific functions of the cells lining the large intestine. When asked about the primary role of colonocytes, she confidently shares their key function. What is the main function of colonocytes in the large intestine?","o":["A. Reuptake of mucus secreted by goblet cells","B. Absorption of amino acids produced by proteolytic enzymes","C. Absorption of water and electrolytes","D. Neutralization of acid produced in the stomach","E. Protection against friction produced by feces"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Absorption of water and electrolytes","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Absorption of water and electrolytes.</b> Colonocytes (colon absorptive cells) <b>pump sodium actively, and water follows passively</b>. Their short microvilli and wide spaces between cells are signs of water transport.<br>A. Reuptake of mucus: mucus is not reabsorbed; it lubricates stool.<br>B. Amino acid absorption: small-intestine enterocytes.<br>D. Acid neutralization: Brunner glands and pancreatic bicarbonate in the duodenum.<br>E. Protection from friction: goblet-cell mucus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":84,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3950-79-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":23,"label":"Colonic goblet cells lubricate stool","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-29.jpg","cap":"Colon gland at high power: many pale, bubbly goblet cells (arrowhead)","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 29"},{"src":"sl/G018-44.jpg","cap":"Colon glands: goblet cells beside columnar absorptive cells (colonocytes)","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F3950-81-1","s":"A 32-year-old woman presents with complaints of diarrhea and abdominal cramping. She reports having increased mucus in her stools and a sensation of incomplete evacuation. Histological examination of a biopsy from the colon reveals an increase in goblet cells. What is the primary role of the goblet cells in the colon?","o":["A. Absorption of water","B. Lubrication of fecal matter","C. Production of digestive enzymes","D. Secretion of hormones"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lubrication of fecal matter","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Lubrication of fecal matter.</b> Colonic <b>goblet cells</b> secrete mucus that lubricates the drying stool and protects the lining. More goblet cells, more mucus in the stool.<br>A. Water absorption: the columnar absorptive cells (colonocytes).<br>C. Digestive enzymes: not made in the colon.<br>D. Hormones: enteroendocrine cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":81,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":23,"label":"Colonic mucosa: simple columnar, goblet cells, no villi","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-26.jpg","cap":"Colon wall: simple columnar epithelium, goblet cells, intestinal glands, no villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/G018-44.jpg","cap":"Colon mucosa lacks villi; tubular glands of colonocytes and goblet cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F3961-20-1","s":"A 40-year-old woman undergoes an investigative colonoscopy. During the exam, you notice inflammatory changes involving the mucosa of the entire colon with sparing of the rectum. Which of the following epithelial cell types would be seen in a biopsy taken from the adjacent uninvolved intestinal mucosa?","o":["A. Simple cuboidal epithelium","B. Simple squamous epithelium","C. Simple columnar epithelium","D. Stratified cuboidal epithelium","E. Stratified squamous epithelium"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Simple columnar epithelium","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Simple columnar epithelium.</b> Normal colon is lined by one layer of <b>tall columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells</b>, forming crypts. The spared mucosa next to the inflamed areas looks normal.<br>A. Simple cuboidal: small ducts and kidney tubules.<br>B. Simple squamous: lines vessels and the serosa.<br>D. Stratified cuboidal: ducts of sweat and salivary glands.<br>E. Stratified squamous: esophagus and anal canal below the pectinate line.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 28 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":20,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":15,"f":"F0731"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3950-83-1","s":"A 60-year-old male undergoes a colonoscopy as part of a routine screening. The procedure reveals multiple polyps in the colon. The physician explains that the mucosa of the large intestine is distinct from that of the small intestine. Which of the following statements is true regarding the mucosal structure of the large intestine?","o":["A. The mucosa contains villi for increased surface area.","B. The mucosa has major folds throughout its length.","C. The mucosa is lined by columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells.","D. The mucosa is primarily responsible for nutrient absorption."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The mucosa is lined by columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells.","a":"C","why":"<b>C. The mucosa is lined by columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells.</b> Colonic mucosa is <b>simple columnar epithelium</b> of absorptive cells (colonocytes) and many goblet cells, forming straight crypts.<br>A. Villi: absent in the large intestine.<br>B. Major folds throughout: no; the colon has no plicae, and only the rectum has real folds.<br>D. Nutrient absorption: small intestine; the colon absorbs water and salts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":83,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3961-20-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":23,"label":"Micrograph: pale mucus-filled cells = goblet cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-29.jpg","cap":"Colon gland at high power: pale, bubbly goblet cells (arrowhead)","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 29"},{"src":"sl/G018-44.jpg","cap":"Colon glands lined by colonocytes and goblet cells, stained for mucus","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-37-1","s":"What are the cells in the squares?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Goblet cells","a":null,"why":"<b>Goblet cells.</b> The boxed glands are lined mostly by <b>pale, rounded, mucus-filled cells</b> with the nucleus pushed to the base. In the colon, goblet cells are <b>very numerous</b>; their mucus lubricates stool.<br>Common mix-up: the thin pink columnar cells between them are the absorptive cells (colonocytes).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0749-37.jpg","in":"Same colon micrograph: the two boxes enclose glands whose epithelium is dominated by pale, mucus-filled cells.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":37,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":23,"label":"Colonic gland stem cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-44.jpg","cap":"Colon tubular glands are lined by colonocytes, goblet cells and stem cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44"},{"src":"sl/G016-13.jpg","cap":"Regenerative stem cell layer at the crypt base renews the epithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-39-1","s":"What are these cells?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Stem Cells","a":null,"why":"<b>Stem (regenerative) cells.</b> In these colon glands, the arrowhead marks a pale, bubbly goblet cell. The arrows mark cells with <b>narrow, dark, crowded nuclei and no mucus droplet</b> in the lower gland wall, the stem-cell zone. They divide to replace the gland's absorptive and goblet cells.<br>Common mix-up: the clear, bubbly cells are goblet cells; the stem cells sit between and below them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0749-39.jpg","in":"High-power H&E of colonic glands: pale goblet cells (arrowhead) and four long arrows pointing to small, dark, narrow nuclei among the columnar cells along the gland walls/base.","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 18 (Crypts of Lieberkühn)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":39,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":23,"label":"Micrograph: crypts, goblet cells, no villi → colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-28.jpg","cap":"Colon mucosa: flat surface, straight glands (Gl) full of goblet cells, no villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 28"},{"src":"sl/G016-27.jpg","cap":"Colon mucosa: straight crypts packed with goblet cells, lymphoid tissue below","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-76-1","s":"Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Colon.</b> The mucosa has a <b>flat surface with no villi</b> and is filled with <b>straight tubular crypts packed with goblet cells</b>.<br>A. Stomach: gastric pits over glands of pink parietal and purple chief cells; no goblet cells.<br>B. Duodenum: villi plus Brunner glands in the submucosa.<br>C. Ileum: villi and Peyer's patches.<br>E. Jejunum: villi on tall plicae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-76.jpg","in":"Flat mucosa with straight tubular crypts full of goblet cells, no villi.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":76,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":78,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":80,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":173,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-61-1"},{"id":"F0749-35-1","s":"What part of the intestines is this?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Large intestine; Colon","a":null,"why":"<b>Large intestine (colon).</b> The surface is <b>flat, with no villi</b>; the small arrows mark gland openings. The mucosa is filled with <b>straight tubular glands (crypts) packed with goblet cells</b>, above a thin muscularis mucosae (MM) and the submucosa.<br>Common mix-up: small-intestine mucosa also has crypts, but villi project above them; here there are none.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0749-35.jpg","in":"H&E of mucosa with a flat surface (arrows at gland openings), no villi, straight tubular glands (Gl) full of goblet cells, lamina propria (LP), muscularis mucosae (MM) and submucosa (SubM); two glands boxed.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":35,"f":"F0749"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":189,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-61-1"},{"id":"F0052-175-1","s":"Q24. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Colon.</b> The mucosa is crowded with <b>round crypt profiles</b> (straight tubular glands cut across), with a <b>flat surface and no villi</b>. That is colonic mucosa.<br>A. Stomach: gastric pits over glands of pink parietal and purple chief cells.<br>B. Duodenum: villi and Brunner glands.<br>C. Ileum: villi and Peyer's patches.<br>E. Jejunum: villi on tall plicae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-175.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: mucosa crowded with crypts cut transversely and longitudinally (round gland profiles), flat surface, no villi.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 27 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":175,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0052-177-1","s":"Q25. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Colon.</b> Broad mucosal folds are lined by <b>straight, parallel tubular glands (crypts)</b> that open on a <b>flat surface with no villi</b>.<br>A. Stomach: rugae also fold its mucosa, but its glands are pink parietal and purple chief cells under gastric pits.<br>B. Duodenum: villi plus Brunner glands.<br>C. Ileum: villi plus Peyer's patches.<br>E. Jejunum: villi on tall plicae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-177.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: broad mucosal folds lined by straight glands with flat luminal surface (no villi), lamina propria/submucosa cores.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 28 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":177,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G018-61-1"},{"id":"G018-61-1","s":"Q14. Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Ileum","D. Colon","E. Jejunum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Colon.</b> The surface is flat with no villi, and the mucosa is packed with straight tubular crypts full of pale goblet cells. That pattern is the large intestine, whose main jobs are absorbing water and making mucus.<br>A. Stomach: gastric pits leading into glands with parietal and chief cells, few goblet cells.<br>B. Duodenum: villi plus Brunner glands in the submucosa.<br>C. Ileum: villi plus Peyer patches.<br>E. Jejunum: villi on tall plicae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G018-61.jpg","in":"Mucosa of straight tubular glands crowded with pale goblet cells; flat surface; muscularis mucosae and submucosa below.","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 27 (Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lab","n":"Digestive system lab deck Fall 2026 (practice questions)","sl":61,"f":"G018"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":24,"label":"Teniae coli: colon's outer longitudinal muscle","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-26.jpg","cap":"Large intestine tunics: outer longitudinal muscularis forms the tenia coli","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/G018-43.jpg","cap":"Large intestine: outer muscularis in three longitudinal ribbons, the taenia coli","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 43"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-118-1","s":"In which layer of the GI tract wall are teniae coli found?","o":["A. Epithelial layer","B. Lamina propria","C. Adventitia","D. Muscularis mucosa","E. Muscularis externa"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Muscularis externa","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Muscularis externa.</b> In the colon, the <b>outer longitudinal layer of the muscularis externa</b> is gathered into <b>three bands, the teniae coli</b>.<br>A. Epithelium: simple columnar lining with goblet cells.<br>B. Lamina propria: loose connective tissue rich in lymphoid tissue.<br>C. Adventitia: outer connective tissue where there is no serosa.<br>D. Muscularis mucosa: a thin muscle sheet at the base of the mucosa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":118,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3961-17-1"},{"id":"F3952-80-1","s":"A 70-year-old man presents with abdominal distension and changes in bowel habits. During your physical examination, you discuss the anatomy of the large intestine and its unique features, including the teniae coli, which are visible along its outer surface. You explain that these structures are formed by a specific layer of the intestinal tract wall, which plays a critical role in the motility and overall function of the large intestine. The teniae coli of the large intestine represent an organ-specific specialization of which layer of the intestinal tract wall?","o":["A. Epithelium","B. Lamina propria","C. Muscularis mucosa","D. Muscularis externa","E. Serosa"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Muscularis externa","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Muscularis externa.</b> <b>Teniae coli</b> are the colon's <b>outer longitudinal smooth muscle layer</b>, gathered into three ribbons.<br>A. Epithelium: simple columnar cells and goblet cells.<br>B. Lamina propria: loose connective tissue with lymphoid tissue.<br>C. Muscularis mucosa: thin muscle under the crypts.<br>E. Serosa: the outer covering of connective tissue and mesothelium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":80,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3961-17-1"},{"id":"F3961-17-1","s":"The teniae coli is an organ-specific specialization that forms three longitudinal bundles of smooth muscle. Where can we expect to find this organization?","o":["A. Esophagus, muscularis externa","B. Stomach, muscularis mucosa","C. Ileum, muscularis mucosa","D. Duodenum, muscoraris mucosa","E. Colon, muscularis externa"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon, muscularis externa","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Colon, muscularis externa.</b> The <b>teniae coli</b> are the colon's outer longitudinal muscle gathered into three bands, so they belong to the <b>muscularis externa</b>.<br>A. Esophagus: full outer longitudinal layer, no teniae.<br>B. Stomach: adds an inner oblique layer, not teniae; and the muscularis mucosa is the wrong layer.<br>C. Ileum: full longitudinal layer; muscularis mucosa is the wrong layer.<br>D. Duodenum: same; no teniae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 10 digestive","sl":17,"f":"F3961"},{"t":"2023 clicker","n":"CL10 digestive system clickers","sl":13,"f":"F0731"}],"tier":1},{"id":"G017-33-1","s":"In the gastrointestinal tract, the muscularis propria consists of an inner circular layer and an outer longitudinal layer of smooth muscle with an intervening neural plexus (Auerbach). Exceptions to this general plan are seen in the stomach where an inner oblique layer of smooth muscle is added and in a second location where the outer layer is condensed into three longitudinal ribbons (taenia) of smooth muscle. Where can we expect to find this modified organization and what plane of section would be required to demonstrate it?","o":["A. Esophagus, longitudinal section","B. Ileum, transverse section","C. Colon, longitudinal section","D. Colon, transverse section","E. Anal canal, external sphincter, transverse section"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Colon, transverse section","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Colon, transverse section.</b> In the colon, the outer longitudinal muscle gathers into <b>three bands, the teniae coli</b>. A <b>cross (transverse) section</b> shows all three as separate thick bundles around the wall, with thin muscle between them.<br>A. Esophagus: complete outer longitudinal layer, no teniae.<br>B. Ileum: complete outer longitudinal layer.<br>C. Colon, longitudinal section: right organ, but a lengthwise cut shows at most one strip, not three bands.<br>E. External anal sphincter: a ring of skeletal muscle, not teniae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Eroschenko drawing of a transverse section of colon wall with intestinal glands, muscularis externa and a boxed label 'Taeniae coli' pointing at the thickened outer longitudinal band.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 34 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 interactive","n":"Digestive system interactive Fall 2026","sl":33,"f":"G017"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W10 Digestive clicker session","sl":25,"f":"F0702"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":24,"label":"Large intestine: crypts and teniae, no villi","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-30.jpg","cap":"Large intestine: sodium active, water passive; crypts, no villi; goblet cells, MALT, teniae","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G016-26.jpg","cap":"Large intestine wall: intestinal glands, lymphatic nodule, tenia coli in muscularis","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-191-1","s":"What is true of the large intestine?","o":["A. Reabsorption of water actively","B. Passively transport Na+","C. No microvilli, villi, or crypts","D. Contains teniae coli and crypts of Lieberkuhn","E. No MALT, many goblet cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Contains teniae coli and crypts of Lieberkuhn","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Contains teniae coli and crypts of Lieberkühn.</b> The large intestine keeps its <b>crypts</b> (but lacks villi), and its outer longitudinal muscle forms three <b>teniae coli</b>.<br>A. Water is absorbed <b>passively</b>, not actively.<br>B. Sodium is transported <b>actively</b>, not passively.<br>C. It has crypts; only the villi are missing.<br>E. MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue) is <b>abundant</b> in the lamina propria, not absent.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":191,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":24,"label":"Diverticula bulge between teniae coli","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-43.jpg","cap":"Teniae coli: three longitudinal muscle ribbons; diverticula bulge out between them","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 43"},{"src":"sl/F3955-100.jpg","cap":"Opened colon: multiple diverticulum openings (arrows) in the mucosa","where":"2024 Middletown lab clicker, slide 100"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-99-1","s":"A 62-year-old man presents after fainting at home approximately one hour ago. He says that for the past day he has had increasing left-sided abdominal pain, and he also has noted bright red blood in his stool. He denies any history of vomiting or diarrhea. Physical examination finds a low-grade fever and gross blood on rectal examination. Further work-up finds abnormalities in his colon that are similar in appearance to the abnormalities seen in the gross picture of his colon seen in the next slide. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his GI bleeding?","o":["A. Angiodysplasia","B. Appendicitis","C. Diverticulosis","D. Hemorrhoids","E. Intussusception"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Diverticulosis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Diverticulosis.</b> An older man with <b>left-sided pain</b> and <b>bright red rectal bleeding</b> fits sigmoid diverticula. <b>Diverticula</b> are outpouchings of mucosa and submucosa that push out <b>between the teniae coli</b>, where the outer muscle layer is missing.<br>A. Angiodysplasia: dilated vessels, usually right colon, painless.<br>B. Appendicitis: right lower pain, no rectal bleeding.<br>D. Hemorrhoids: dilated anal veins; small, painless bleeds.<br>E. Intussusception: telescoping bowel, mostly in young children.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Opened colon with multiple small dark pouch openings in the mucosa between folds (arrows) = diverticula.","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":99,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":25,"label":"Appendix: small lumen ringed by lymphoid follicles","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-32.jpg","cap":"Appendix: small lumen ringed by lymphatic nodules in mucosa and submucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/G018-42.jpg","cap":"Appendix: blind evagination from cecum and a significant part of MALT","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-41-1","s":"What is this?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Appendix","a":null,"why":"<b>Appendix.</b> A small tube with a <b>narrow, star-shaped lumen</b>, ringed by <b>large lymphoid nodules</b> in the mucosa and submucosa, with few glands and a complete muscularis externa (no teniae coli). It is a blind pouch off the cecum and a big part of MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue).<br>Common mix-up: the ileum also has lymphoid nodules (Peyer's patches), but it has villi and a wide lumen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0749-41.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E cross-section of a small tube with a narrow star-shaped lumen, a ring of large dark lymphatic nodules in mucosa/submucosa, and muscularis externa (labels: lymphatic nodules, mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa).","tg":["H15B slide 34 (Appendix)","H15B slide 32 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":41,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0755-233-1"},{"id":"F0755-233-1","s":"What organ is depicted and what is it its distinguishing feature?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Appendix – MALT","a":null,"why":"<b>Appendix; distinguishing feature: abundant MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue).</b> Large <b>lymphoid follicles with germinal centers</b> (arrow) crowd the lamina propria and submucosa, while the mucosa has <b>few glands</b>. It also has a tiny lumen and no teniae coli.<br>Common mix-up: Peyer's patches of the ileum look similar, but the ileum has villi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0755-233.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: large lymphoid follicles with germinal centres (arrow) filling the lamina propria/submucosa beneath a mucosa with few intestinal glands.","tg":["H15B slide 34 (Appendix)","H15B slide 32 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":233,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0754-193-1","s":"What is this? Where is it found?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Appendix/ MALT – blind evagination off the cecum","a":null,"why":"<b>Appendix, a blind pouch off the cecum.</b> Its lamina propria and submucosa are filled with <b>large lymphoid follicles with pale germinal centers</b> (arrow), and the mucosa has <b>few, short glands</b>. That makes the appendix a significant part of MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue).<br>Common mix-up: the colon also has MALT, but only small amounts between many crypts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-193.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E: large lymphoid follicles with germinal centres (arrow) filling the lamina propria/submucosa beneath a mucosa with few, short intestinal glands.","tg":["H15B slide 34 (Appendix)","H15B slide 32 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":193,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0755-233-1"},{"id":"F0749-42-1","s":"Appendix. Whats the arrow pointing to?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"MALT","a":null,"why":"<b>MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue): a lymphoid follicle.</b> The arrow marks a large, round cluster of lymphocytes with a <b>pale germinal center</b>, filling the lamina propria and submucosa below the glands. These follicles make the appendix a major part of MALT.<br>Common mix-up: the pale center is not a gland; it is the germinal center of the follicle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0749-42.jpg","in":"Appendix cross-section plus a higher-power H&E: the arrow points to a large pale-centred lymphoid follicle (germinal centre) beneath intestinal glands.","tg":["H15B slide 34 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":42,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0755-233-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":25,"label":"Acute appendicitis: right lower quadrant pain","slides":[{"src":"sl/F3955-113.jpg","cap":"Acute appendicitis: swollen red appendix and inflamed wall at medium power","where":"2024 Middletown lab clicker, slide 113"},{"src":"sl/G016-32.jpg","cap":"Normal appendix: small lumen ringed by lymphatic nodules in mucosa and submucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-112-1","s":"You are called to the Surgical Pathology cutting/grossing room to examine a piece of tissue. The surgical procedure was done for right lower quadrant pain, which persisted and worsened over a period of 4 days, in a 17-year-old male. The gross and microscopic features/figures are both shown for this excised tissue on the next slide. The most likely diagnosis is which of the following:","o":["A. Crohn's disease","B. Ulcerative colitis","C. Adenomatous polyp","D. Acute appendicitis","E. Colonic adenocarcinoma"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Acute appendicitis","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Acute appendicitis.</b> A teenager with <b>right lower quadrant pain worsening over days</b> has acute appendicitis. The removed appendix is swollen and red, with inflammation through its wall. The appendix is a blind pouch off the cecum, rich in lymphoid tissue.<br>A. Crohn's disease: chronic and patchy, often the ileum; granulomas.<br>B. Ulcerative colitis: starts in the rectum; bloody diarrhea.<br>C. Adenomatous polyp: benign colon growth, usually silent.<br>E. Colonic adenocarcinoma: older adults.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Gross: swollen, red, congested appendix; micro: wall diffusely infiltrated by inflammatory cells.","tg":["H15B slide 34 (Appendix)","H15B slide 32 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":112,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":25,"label":"Appendix carcinoid: enteroendocrine cell tumor","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-31.jpg","cap":"Gastric cell types: enteroendocrine cells need special stains; EC cells secrete serotonin","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/F3955-119.jpg","cap":"Appendix carcinoid: nests of uniform salt-and-pepper cells; synaptophysin-positive stain","where":"2024 Middletown lab clicker, slide 119"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-118-1","s":"A 42-year-old female presents to the outpatient clinic with complaints of recurrent right lower abdominal pain that has been experienced over a two-year duration. Physical examination revealed a minimal degree of rebound tenderness. It was decided to take the patient to surgery for removal of her appendix. The patient underwent successful appendectomy and recovered four days later. Subsequently, the tip of the appendix was carefully examined with special attention paid to the tip of the appendix. See the next slide for a photomicrograph of the appendiceal tip. The tan-yellow appendiceal mass within the appendix and localized to the tip, measuring 1 cm in length and 0.5 cm in diameter, was diagnosed by histopathological examination. Follow-up examination one year after surgery revealed that the patient was well with no discomfort. [Slide 119: This lesion was submucosal, nodular with a characteristic nested pattern. The cells were small and uniform, with round nuclei, central prominent nucleoli and speckled chromatin pattern (salt and pepper nuclei). The lesional cells were also positive for synaptophysin.] After careful examination of all the histological sections, the pathologist determined that the most correct diagnosis for this patient was which of the following?","o":["A. Acute appendicitis","B. Appendiceal lipoma","C. Appendiceal leiomyoma","D. Neuroendocrine tumor (carcinoid tumor)","E. Appendiceal adenocarcinoma"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Neuroendocrine tumor (carcinoid tumor)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor.</b> A small yellow nodule at the appendix tip made of <b>nests of uniform cells</b> with <b>salt-and-pepper chromatin</b> that stain for <b>synaptophysin</b> (a neuroendocrine marker) is a carcinoid. It arises from <b>enteroendocrine cells</b> (enterochromaffin cells make serotonin).<br>A. Acute appendicitis: neutrophils through the wall, not a nested tumor.<br>B. Lipoma: mature fat cells.<br>C. Leiomyoma: bundles of spindle-shaped smooth muscle cells.<br>E. Adenocarcinoma: malignant glands from the lining epithelium, not synaptophysin-positive nests.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Nests of small uniform cells in the submucosa (H&E) that stain brown with synaptophysin.","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15B slide 34 (Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":118,"f":"F3955"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":26,"label":"Rectoanal junction: columnar to stratified squamous","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-36.jpg","cap":"Rectoanal junction: columnar glandular lining changes abruptly to stratified squamous","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 36"},{"src":"sl/G018-46.jpg","cap":"Anal canal: columnar lining above dentate line, stratified squamous below","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"F3952-73-1","s":"A 55-year-old man presents with rectal bleeding and discomfort during bowel movements. During your examination, you discuss the anatomy of the anal canal and its role in defecation, emphasizing the importance of the epithelial lining in maintaining integrity and function. You explain that as one moves from the rectum into the anal canal, there is a significant change in the type of epithelium lining the gut, which plays a critical role in protecting against mechanical stress and pathogens. Moving from the rectum, what shift in epithelial types occurs in the lining of the anal canal?","o":["A. From simple columnar to simple squamous","B. From simple squamous to simple columnar","C. From simple squamous to stratified squamous","D. From simple columnar to stratified squamous","E. From simple cuboidal to stratified cuboidal"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"From simple columnar to stratified squamous","a":"D","why":"<b>D. From simple columnar to stratified squamous.</b> The rectum is lined by <b>simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells</b>. At the pectinate line, the anal canal changes abruptly to <b>stratified squamous epithelium</b>, which resists friction.<br>A. Simple squamous: lines vessels and serosa, not the anal canal.<br>B. Reversed: the rectum is not squamous.<br>C. The rectum is not simple squamous.<br>E. Cuboidal: only a narrow transition zone has stratified cuboidal cells; the main shift is columnar to squamous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 37 (Rectum/Anus)","H15B slide 38 (Transition zone from simple columnar epithelium  stratified squamous epithelium)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":73,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3950-85-1"},{"id":"F3950-85-1","s":"A 48-year-old woman presents to the clinic with a complaint of rectal bleeding and discomfort during bowel movements. She reports that the bleeding occurs occasionally and is associated with straining during defecation. On physical examination, the physician notes the presence of hemorrhoids and recommends a colonoscopy to evaluate for any underlying pathology. During the colonoscopy, the physician observes that the mucosal lining of the rectum shows a distinct transition as it approaches the anal canal. The physician explains to the patient that this change in epithelial lining is an important anatomical feature of the anal-rectal junction. Which of the following best describes the histological change that occurs at the anal-rectal junction?","o":["A. Transition from stratified squamous epithelium to keratinized epithelium","B. Transition from simple columnar epithelium to stratified squamous epithelium","C. Transition from stratified cuboidal epithelium to transitional epithelium","D. Transition from simple squamous epithelium to simple cuboidal epithelium","E. Transition from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Transition from simple columnar epithelium to stratified squamous epithelium","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Simple columnar to stratified squamous epithelium.</b> Rectal mucosa is <b>simple columnar with many goblet cells</b>. At the anorectal junction (pectinate line) it changes abruptly to <b>stratified squamous</b>.<br>A. Non-keratinized to keratinized squamous: that is Hilton's line, lower in the anal canal.<br>C. Transitional epithelium: urinary tract.<br>D. Simple squamous to simple cuboidal: not in the gut lining.<br>E. Reversed direction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 38 (Transition zone from simple columnar epithelium  stratified squamous epithelium)","H15B slide 36 (Transition from columnar to squamous epithelium)","H15B slide 37 (Rectum/Anus)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 10","sl":85,"f":"F3950"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":26,"label":"Pectinate line: columnar meets squamous","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-36.jpg","cap":"Rectoanal junction: glandular columnar epithelium meets stratified squamous along drawn line","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 36"},{"src":"sl/G018-45.jpg","cap":"Anal canal: squamocolumnar junction at the pectinate (dentate) line","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-33-1","s":"What is this line called? What is it dividing?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Pectinate line – divides the colon/rectum (simple columnar with glands, left top) from the anus (stratified squamous, right bottom) at the rectoanal junction","a":null,"why":"<b>Pectinate (dentate) line.</b> It marks the <b>rectoanal junction</b>. Upper left: <b>simple columnar epithelium with glands and goblet cells</b> (rectum). Lower right: <b>non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b> (anal canal). The change is abrupt.<br>Common mix-up: Hilton's (white) line is lower; there non-keratinized squamous becomes keratinized skin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0749-33.jpg","in":"H&E of rectoanal junction: colonic glands with goblet cells at upper left, stratified squamous epithelium at right; a diagonal line drawn across the junction.","tg":["H15B slide 36 (Transition from columnar to squamous epithelium)","H15B slide 38 (Transition zone from simple columnar epithelium  stratified squamous epithelium)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":33,"f":"F0749"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":195,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":26,"label":"Hilton's line: squamous becomes keratinized","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-46.jpg","cap":"Anal canal: columnar, dentate line, non-keratinized squamous, Hilton's line, then skin","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 46"},{"src":"sl/G016-38.jpg","cap":"Pectinate line: columnar to non-keratinized squamous; Hilton's line: to keratinized","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 38"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-196-1","s":"What is the name of the transition from stratified squamous non-keratinized to stratified squamous keratinized?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Hilton's line","a":null,"why":"<b>Hilton's (white) line.</b> In the anal canal, <b>non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b> becomes <b>keratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b> (skin) at Hilton's line, below the pectinate line.<br>Common mix-up: the pectinate (dentate) line is the higher change, from simple columnar to non-keratinized stratified squamous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 38 (Transition zone from simple columnar epithelium  stratified squamous epithelium)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":196,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0755-234-1","s":"At the rectoanal junction, what specific transition in epithelium do you normally see? What is the name of the line that divides the two? What does Hilton's line represent?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A: columnar to non-keratinized squamous epithelium – pectinate line; B: Hilton's line = transition from non-keratinized to keratinized (stratified squamous)","a":null,"why":"<b>Simple columnar → non-keratinized stratified squamous, at the pectinate (dentate) line. Hilton's (white) line marks non-keratinized → keratinized stratified squamous.</b> So the anal canal has two lines: first the glandular rectal lining turns squamous, then the squamous lining becomes skin.<br>Common mix-up: keratin does not start at the pectinate line; it starts only below Hilton's line.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 36 (Transition from columnar to squamous epithelium)","H15B slide 38 (Transition zone from simple columnar epithelium  stratified squamous epithelium)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":234,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0754-196-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":26,"label":"Internal smooth vs external skeletal anal sphincter","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-47.jpg","cap":"Internal sphincter (A) smooth muscle; external sphincter (B) striated skeletal muscle","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 47"},{"src":"sl/F3955-86.jpg","cap":"Anal canal section: internal sphincter inside, external skeletal sphincter outside","where":"2024 Middletown lab clicker, slide 86"}],"q":[{"id":"F3955-84-1","s":"A biopsy from the anus is obtained. Name the structures labeled as #1 and #2","o":["A. Internal anal sphincter; External anal sphincter","B. External anal sphincter; Internal anal sphincter"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal anal sphincter; External anal sphincter","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Internal anal sphincter; external anal sphincter.</b> #1 is <b>smooth muscle</b> (spindle cells, central nuclei) next to the lining: the <b>internal</b> sphincter, a thickening of the inner circular layer. #2 is <b>skeletal muscle</b> (fibers cut across, nuclei at the edge) further out: the voluntary <b>external</b> sphincter.<br>B. Reversed: the external sphincter is skeletal muscle and lies outside the internal one.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3955-84.jpg","in":"#1 = band of smooth muscle (spindle cells, central nuclei) next to the epithelium; #2 = skeletal muscle fibers cut in cross-section further out.","tg":["H15B slide 37 (Rectum/Anus)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 10 digestive","sl":84,"f":"F3955"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":179,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":27,"label":"Goblet cells increase toward the colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G016-13.jpg","cap":"Goblet cell frequency rises: duodenum, then jejunum, then ileum","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/G018-44.jpg","cap":"Colon glands: colonocytes and many goblet cells, few enteroendocrine cells, stem cells","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F0749-18-1","s":"Which portion of the intestine contains the greatest number of goblet cells?","o":["A. Pylorus","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Appendix"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileum","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Ileum.</b> Goblet cells <b>increase along the gut</b>: fewest in the duodenum, more in the jejunum, most in the ileum, and even more in the colon. Among these choices, the ileum has the most.<br>A. Pylorus: stomach; surface mucous cells, no goblet cells.<br>B. Duodenum: fewest goblet cells of the small intestine.<br>C. Jejunum: more than the duodenum, fewer than the ileum.<br>E. Appendix: few glands; its mucosa is crowded by lymphoid tissue.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Histo week 11B","sl":18,"f":"F0749"}],"tier":2,"kind":"harder","like":"F1221-179-1"},{"id":"F1221-179-1","s":"7. Goblet cells are most numerous in the walls of the","o":["A. villi","B. gastric glands","C. crypts of Lieberkuhn (intestinal glands) in the ileum","D. crypts of Lieberkuhn (intestinal glands) in the duodenum","E. crypts of Lieberkuhn (intestinal glands) in the colon"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"crypts of Lieberkuhn (intestinal glands) in the colon","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Crypts of Lieberkühn in the colon.</b> Goblet cells <b>increase along the gut</b>: few in the duodenum, more in the jejunum and ileum, and <b>most in the colon</b>, where their mucus lubricates stool.<br>A. Villi: mostly absorptive enterocytes with scattered goblet cells.<br>B. Gastric glands: no goblet cells; stomach mucus comes from surface and neck mucous cells.<br>C. Ileal crypts: many, but fewer than the colon.<br>D. Duodenal crypts: the fewest.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)","H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":179,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":27,"label":"Intestinal segments: distinguishing features","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-36.jpg","cap":"Three segments: duodenum Brunner glands, jejunum prominent plicae, ileum Peyer's patches","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36"},{"src":"sl/G016-9.jpg","cap":"Distinguishing features: Brunner glands, jejunal plicae, ileal Peyer's patches, colon lacks villi","where":"2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-201-1","s":"On your first day of your internal medicine clinical rotation, the crabby attending insists on quizzing all of the medical students on unique histological features of the intestinal tract in order to pass time while waiting for his assistant to bring him coffee. Identify and match the characteristic features with their correct location before Dr. Grumpy’s coffee arrives: Brunner's glands; Lots of goblet cells, no villi; Peyer's patches and M cells; Plicae circularis, absence of glands","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Jejunum","C. Ileum","D. Colon"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Brunner's glands → Duodenum; Lots of goblet cells, no villi → Colon; Peyer's patches and M cells → Ileum; Plicae circularis, absence of glands → Jejunum","a":null,"why":"<b>Brunner glands → duodenum; many goblet cells, no villi → colon; Peyer's patches and M (microfold) cells → ileum; plicae circulares with no glands → jejunum.</b> Each segment has one tag: duodenum = <b>submucosal glands</b>; jejunum = <b>tallest plicae, thin bare submucosa</b>; ileum = <b>lymphoid nodules</b>; colon = <b>crypts full of goblet cells, no villi</b>.<br>Common mix-up: plicae exist in all three small-intestine segments, but only the jejunum has them as its main tag.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 9 (Distinguishing features)","H15B slide 30 (Reabsorption:)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":201,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Exam 2 Harlem TA 2020: GI part only","sl":183,"f":"F1211"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":43,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0755-231-1","s":"Distinguishing features Quiz: (1) In comparison to the duodenum doe the large intestine have more or less goblet cells present? (2) What type of gland is specific to the Duodenum and what is the function? (3) Do the frequency/amount of villi/microvilli increase or decrease as you pass through the small intestine (4) What is the distinguishing feature of the Jejunum? (5) What is the distinguishing feature of the Ileum?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"(1) More; (2) Brunner's glands – alkaline mucus that neutralizes acid chyme; (3) Decrease; (4) Prominent plicae circulares; (5) Peyer's patches","a":null,"why":"<b>(1) More; (2) Brunner glands, alkaline mucus that neutralizes acid chyme; (3) decrease; (4) prominent plicae circulares; (5) Peyer's patches.</b> Goblet cells <b>increase</b> along the gut and are most numerous in the colon. Villi and microvilli do the opposite: most in the duodenum, fewest in the ileum. Brunner glands sit in the duodenal <b>submucosa</b>.<br>Common mix-up: the two gradients run opposite ways; absorptive surface falls along the gut while goblet cells rise.","ex":"","fl":["Part (1): correct answer More. The large intestine has MORE goblet cells than the duodenum: goblet cells increase distally (duodenum < jejunum < ileum, G016:13) and the colon has 'numerous goblet cells' (G016:30; F0749 slide 32). The key 'Less' is wrong.","The source keyed part 1 as 'Less', but the large intestine has more goblet cells than the duodenum, because goblet cells increase toward the colon."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 20 (MUCOSA)","H15B slide 12 (Mucosa features)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":231,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":27,"label":"GI epithelial cells and their products","slides":[{"src":"sl/G018-31.jpg","cap":"Stomach cell types: surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, enteroendocrine","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/G018-38.jpg","cap":"Small intestine cell types: enterocyte, goblet, enteroendocrine, stem, Paneth","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/G018-42.jpg","cap":"Peyer patch microfold (M) cells pass antigens to lymphocytes","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-62-1","s":"Match the cell to its function","o":["A. Enteroendocrine cell","B. Paneth cell","C. Goblet cell","D. Chief cell","E. Parietal cell","F. Microfold Cell","G. Stem cell","H. Enterocyte","I. a. Production of lysozyme & defensins","J. b. Regenerates other epithelial cells","K. c. Production of Mucus","L. d. Absorbs nutrients","M. e. Production of peptide hormones","N. f. Production of HCl and intrinsic factor","O. g. Production of pepsinogen and lipase precursor","P. h. Transports antigens from lumen"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"1-e, 2-a, 3-c, 4-g, 5-f, 6-h, 7-b, 8-d","a":null,"why":"<b>Enteroendocrine - peptide hormones; Paneth - lysozyme and defensins; goblet - mucus; chief - pepsinogen and lipase precursor; parietal - hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor; microfold (M) cell - carries antigens from the lumen to immune cells; stem cell - regenerates the epithelium; enterocyte - absorbs nutrients.</b><br>Common mix-up: chief cells (basophilic, purple) make enzymes; parietal cells (acidophilic, pink) make acid and intrinsic factor.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 76 (Fundic/Body cells)","H15B slide 13 (Columnar absorptive cells (enterocyte))","H15B slide 25 (MUCOSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":62,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":131,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-11","u":27,"label":"Squamocolumnar junctions: cardia and pectinate line","slides":[{"src":"sl/G015-63.jpg","cap":"Esophagogastric junction: stratified squamous to simple columnar with gastric pits","where":"2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 63"},{"src":"sl/G018-45.jpg","cap":"Anal canal: squamocolumnar junction at the pectinate (dentate) line","where":"2026 digestive lab deck, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-57-1","s":"An unknown gastrointestinal slide shows a zone of transitional tissue from simple columnar epithelium to non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium with abundant mucous-producing cells. Which of the following answer choices describes this zone(s)?","o":["A. Vermillion Zone","B. Esophageal sphincter","C. Pyloric sphincter","D. Pectinate Line","E. Hilton's Line"],"g":["B","D"],"k":["B","D"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophageal sphincter and Pectinate Line (two answers)","a":"B,D","why":"<b>B and D. Esophageal sphincter region and pectinate line.</b> Two places join <b>simple columnar to nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium</b>: the esophagogastric junction at the lower esophageal sphincter, and the pectinate (dentate) line of the anal canal, next to the goblet-cell-rich rectum.<br>A. Vermilion zone: skin to oral mucosa, all stratified squamous.<br>C. Pyloric sphincter: simple columnar on both sides.<br>E. Hilton's line: nonkeratinized to keratinized stratified squamous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H15A slide 64 (Abrupt change from stratified squamous epithelium to simple columnar epithelium )","H15B slide 38 (Transition zone from simple columnar epithelium  stratified squamous epithelium)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":57,"f":"F3980"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":141,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":28,"label":"Serous cells: basophilic base, watery protein fluid","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-8.jpg","cap":"Serous acini: pyramidal cells, round nuclei, dark basal cytoplasm, apical granules","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/F0722-150.jpg","cap":"Serous versus mucous cells side by side: shape, nuclei, staining, secretion","where":"2024 TA review, slide 150"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-148-1","s":"What is true about the cells below?","o":["A. They are columnar cells","B. They secrete viscous mucus","C. Nuclei are present basally","D. The basal domain is basophilic with abundant RER","E. They stain poorly"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"The basal domain is basophilic with abundant RER","a":"D","why":"D. These are <b>serous cells</b>: pyramidal, round nuclei, and a <b>basal domain that stains dark blue (basophilic)</b> because it is packed with <b>rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER)</b> for making protein.<br>A. Columnar shape is a mucous cell trait; serous cells are pyramidal.<br>B. Viscous mucus comes from mucous cells; serous cells make watery protein fluid.<br>C. Nuclei pressed to the base is the mucous look; the lecture gives serous nuclei as round and central.<br>E. Poor (pale) staining is the mucous look; serous cells stain darkly.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-148.jpg","in":"Two panels of serous acini (A) of pyramidal granular cells with round nuclei.","tg":["H16 slide 8 (Serous cells)","H16 slide 9 (Mucous cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":148,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F4081-30-1","s":"A histologic section shows pyramidal cells with round, central nuclei. Their basal domains are basophilic due to abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum, and their apical cytoplasmic domains contain many granules. They are arranged in spherical acini. Based on these structural features, which functional outcome is most likely?","o":["A. Secretion of watery proteinaceous fluid","B. Formation of mucous droplets","C. Loss of microvilli","D. Flattening of round, central nuclei","E. Breakdown of pyramidal cell shape"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretion of watery proteinaceous fluid","a":"A","why":"A. Pyramidal cells, round central nuclei, a <b>dark-blue (basophilic) base full of rough endoplasmic reticulum</b>, apical granules and round acini mean <b>serous cells</b>. That machinery makes protein, so they secrete a <b>watery, protein-rich fluid</b>.<br>B. Mucous droplets come from pale columnar mucous cells.<br>C. Serous cells keep short apical microvilli; nothing here means losing them.<br>D. Flattened basal nuclei are a mucous cell trait.<br>E. The pyramidal shape is the normal, lasting form; it points secretion at the lumen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 8 (Serous cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":30,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0743-2-1","s":"Under the microscope, you analyze a biopsy slide stained by H&E. You observe a group of single-layered epithelial cells organized around a lumen. The cells are pyramidal in shape and present a certain polarity, with basophilic features and a well-rounded nucleus at the base. Concomitantly, a concentration of eosinophilic vacuoles in seen in their apical portion. This description best corresponds to:","o":["A. Endocrine holocrine glands","B. Exocrine mucous glands","C. Tensed urothelium","D. Exocrine serous glands","E. Exocrine mixed glands"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Exocrine serous glands","a":"D","why":"D. Pyramidal cells around a lumen, a <b>basophilic (dark-blue) base</b>, a round nucleus and <b>eosinophilic (pink) apical granules</b> are the signature of an <b>exocrine serous</b> acinus. The pink granules are protein (zymogen) granules.<br>A. Endocrine glands have no lumen; holocrine glands (sebaceous) shed whole cells.<br>B. Mucous glands look pale and foamy, with flattened basal nuclei.<br>C. Urothelium lines the bladder; it does not form acini around a lumen.<br>E. A mixed gland would also show pale mucous cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 8 (Serous cells)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":2,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0722-148-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":28,"label":"Mucous cells: pale mucin, flat basal nuclei","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-9.jpg","cap":"Mucous acini (M): pale foamy cells, flat basal nuclei, beside skeletal muscle (SM)","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/F0722-150.jpg","cap":"Serous versus mucous cells side by side: shape, nuclei, staining, secretion","where":"2024 TA review, slide 150"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-9-1","s":"Which of the following correctly describes a key histological difference between mucous and serous glands?","o":["A. Mucous acini have round, basophilic nuclei and stain darkly due to abundant zymogen granules","B. Serous acini have pale, foamy cytoplasm because they secrete glycoproteins","C. Mucous acini have flattened, basally located nuclei and stain lightly due to mucin content","D. Serous acini lack intercalated ducts, while mucous acini have highly developed striated ducts"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Mucous acini have flattened, basally located nuclei and stain lightly due to mucin content","a":"C","why":"C. <b>Mucous acini</b> stain <b>pale</b> because their mucin is lost in routine processing, and their <b>nuclei are flattened against the base</b>.<br>A. Round nuclei, dark staining and zymogen granules describe <b>serous</b> cells.<br>B. Pale, foamy cytoplasm is the <b>mucous</b> look; serous cells are dark and granular.<br>D. Backwards: serous glands like the parotid have long intercalated and striated ducts; the mostly mucous sublingual has short, few ducts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 8 (Serous cells)","H16 slide 9 (Mucous cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":9,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F4081-32-1","s":"The histologic image shows mucous cells from a salivary gland. Why are the apical granules in these cells hydrophilic?","o":["A. Because they contain large mucin granules","B. Because they contain abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum","C. Because their nuclei are round and central","D. Because they form spherical acini","E. Because their basal domains are basophilic"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Because they contain large mucin granules","a":"A","why":"A. Mucous cells store <b>large granules of mucin</b>, a heavily sugar-coated protein that binds water. That makes the granules <b>hydrophilic</b> and leaves them pale in routine stains.<br>B. Abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum is the serous cell's basal feature.<br>C. Round, central nuclei describe serous cells; mucous nuclei are flat and basal.<br>D. Spherical acini are the serous shape; mucous cells form cylindrical tubules.<br>E. A dark-blue (basophilic) base is a serous trait.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Mucous acini with pale, foamy cytoplasm and flattened basal nuclei (not needed to answer).","tg":["H16 slide 9 (Mucous cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":32,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3980-9-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":28,"label":"EM: pale mucous vs dark serous granules","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-10.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph of a mixed acinus: pale mucous (M), dark serous (S), myoepithelial (My)","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-212-1","s":"Don't forget EM! Which one is which? (cells labelled A and B in the electron micrograph of a mixed acinus: serous or mucous)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A = Mucous, B = Serous","a":null,"why":"A = <b>mucous</b> cell, B = <b>serous</b> cell. In electron microscopy (EM), mucous cells are packed with <b>large, pale (electron-lucent) mucin granules</b>. Serous cells hold <b>small, dark (electron-dense) zymogen granules</b> and sit near the base of the acinus.<br>Common mix-up: in EM, dark means dense protein. So the dark-granule cell is serous, just as serous cells look darker in light microscopy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-212.jpg","in":"TEM of a mixed salivary acinus: cells labelled A are filled with large pale, poorly electron-dense secretory granules; cells labelled B (at the base) contain smaller dark electron-dense granules.","tg":["H16 slide 8 (Serous cells)","H16 slide 9 (Mucous cells)","H16 slide 10 (Ultrastructure)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":212,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0722-151-1"},{"id":"F0722-151-1","s":"Label the three acini (epithelia cells) -","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Mucous cells (M, large pale granules); serous cells (S, small dense granules); myoepithelial cell (My, at the base)","a":null,"why":"Purple box = <b>mucous cell</b> (large pale mucin granules). Red box = <b>serous cell</b> (small dark, electron-dense granules near the lumen). Orange box = <b>myoepithelial cell</b>: a flat cell at the base, between the basal lamina and the secretory cells. On a nerve signal it contracts and squeezes secretion out.<br>Common mix-up: the myoepithelial cell is not a secretory cell; it has no secretory granules.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-151.jpg","in":"TEM of a mixed acinus: many pale mucous granules (M), a cluster of dark serous granules (S), a flattened myoepithelial cell (My) at the base.","tg":["H16 slide 10 (Ultrastructure)","H16 slide 11 (Myoepithelial cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":151,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":28,"label":"Myoepithelial cells squeeze out secretion","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-11.jpg","cap":"Myoepithelial basket cells hug acini and intralobular ducts; they contract to squeeze secretion","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-85-1","s":"What is the function of Myoepithelial cells?","o":["A. Their function is unknown","B. They secrete amylase","C. They squeeze out secretion from acini and ducts in response to nervous stimuli","D. They produce mucous"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"They squeeze out secretion from acini and ducts in response to nervous stimuli","a":"C","why":"C. <b>Myoepithelial cells</b> are flat, contractile 'basket' cells wrapped around acini and intralobular ducts. On <b>nerve stimulation</b> they contract and <b>squeeze secretion out</b>.<br>A. Their function is known: contraction.<br>B. Amylase is made by serous acinar cells, mostly in the parotid.<br>D. Mucus is made by mucous cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 11 (Myoepithelial cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":85,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":236,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F4081-34-1"},{"id":"F4081-34-1","s":"A histologic image shows basket cells located between the basal lamina and the secretory cells. These flattened cells with contractile processes are found in acini and intralobular ducts. What is their functional role?","o":["A. Producing viscid mucus","B. Forming cylindrical tubules","C. Squeezing out secretion in response to nervous stimuli","D. Compressing nuclei to the basal region","E. Forming intercellular canaliculi"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Squeezing out secretion in response to nervous stimuli","a":"C","why":"C. <b>Myoepithelial (basket) cells</b> sit between the basal lamina and the secretory cells. Their contractile processes <b>squeeze secretion out of acini and intralobular ducts</b> when nerves fire.<br>A. Viscid mucus is made by mucous cells.<br>B. Cylindrical tubules are the shape of mucous secretory units.<br>D. Flattened basal nuclei are a mucous cell feature.<br>E. Intercellular canaliculi are channels between serous cells that lead to the lumen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 11 (Myoepithelial cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":34,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":29,"label":"Intercalated vs striated duct functions","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-20.jpg","cap":"Functions list: acini, intercalated ducts, striated ducts and plasma cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/F0754-205.jpg","cap":"Duct table: intercalated absorb chloride, secrete bicarbonate; striated reabsorb sodium","where":"2022 TA review, slide 205"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-154-1","s":"What do the intercalated ducts do?","o":["A. Secrete HCO3- (Bicarbonate); absorb Cl-","B. Absorb Na+; secrete K+","C. Secrete Pâté","D. Secrete H+; Absorb K+"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Secrete HCO3- (Bicarbonate); absorb Cl-","a":"A","why":"A. Per the lecture, <b>intercalated ducts absorb chloride (Cl−) and secrete bicarbonate (HCO3−)</b>.<br>B. Absorbing sodium (Na+) and secreting potassium (K+) is the <b>striated duct</b>'s job.<br>C. A joke option; ducts do not secrete pâté.<br>D. Secreting hydrogen ions (H+) and absorbing K+ is what alpha-intercalated cells of the kidney's collecting duct do, not salivary ducts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 20 (Functions)","H16 slide 12 (Ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":154,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F4081-45-1","s":"Match the structures with functions","o":["A. Acini","B. Intercalated ducts","C. Striated ducts","D. Plasma cells","E. Reabsorbs Na & secretes K and HCO3","F. IgA","G. Absorbs Cl & secrete HCO3","H. Secrete sero/mucous fluid"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Acini - secrete sero/mucous fluid; Intercalated ducts - absorb Cl & secrete HCO3; Striated ducts - reabsorb Na & secrete K and HCO3; Plasma cells - IgA","a":null,"why":"Acini → <b>secrete sero/mucous fluid</b> (H). Intercalated ducts → <b>absorb chloride (Cl−) and secrete bicarbonate (HCO3−)</b> (G). Striated ducts → <b>reabsorb sodium (Na+) and secrete potassium (K+) and HCO3−</b> (E), making saliva hypotonic. Plasma cells → <b>immunoglobulin A (IgA)</b> (F).<br>Common mix-up: both duct types add bicarbonate; the sodium pump that makes saliva hypotonic is in the striated duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 20 (Functions)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":45,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":29,"label":"Identify a striated duct by its basal striations","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-13.jpg","cap":"Striated duct (SD) among serous acini, with drawing of basal striations","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/G024-23.jpg","cap":"Striated duct (SD): pale columnar cells with basal striations among serous acini","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-186-1","s":"Question 3 Name the highlighted structure:","o":["A. Serous gland","B. Mucous gland","C. Striated duct","D. Mixed gland","E. Excretory duct"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Striated duct","a":"C","why":"C. A ring of <b>tall pink columnar cells</b> with central round nuclei and <b>vertical stripes in the basal cytoplasm</b>, around a small lumen inside the lobule, is a <b>striated duct</b>.<br>A. A serous acinus is dark granular pyramidal cells around a tiny lumen.<br>B. Mucous cells are pale and foamy, with flat basal nuclei.<br>D. A mixed gland is a whole organ, not one circled tube.<br>E. Excretory (interlobular) ducts are larger and lie in connective-tissue septa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-186.jpg","in":"High-power H&E: a green outline circles a duct of tall columnar cells with central round nuclei and radially striated pale basal cytoplasm around a small lumen, among dark serous acini.","tg":["H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":186,"f":"F0052"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":4,"f":"F3959"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":29,"label":"Striated duct: basal folds, mitochondria, sodium reabsorption","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-13.jpg","cap":"Striated duct cell: basal membrane folds packed with mitochondria; micrograph of striations","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/G020-20.jpg","cap":"Functions list: striated ducts reabsorb sodium, secrete potassium and bicarbonate","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/F0754-204.jpg","cap":"Striated duct drawing and micrograph: basal infoldings with mitochondria power sodium reabsorption","where":"2022 TA review, slide 204"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-158-1","s":"What is a key feature of striated ducts?","o":["A. They are interlobular","B. They have a simple columnar epithelium","C. They are markedly present in the pancreas","D. Basal striations are present"],"g":["B","D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Basal striations are present","a":"D","why":"B and D. <b>Striated ducts</b> are lined by <b>simple columnar</b> cells with <b>basal striations</b>. The striations are folds of the basal membrane packed with mitochondria, which power sodium (Na+) reabsorption.<br>A. Striated ducts are intralobular (inside the lobule), not interlobular.<br>C. The pancreas has <b>no</b> striated ducts; that helps tell it from the parotid.<br>Note: the source's answer slide rewrote B as 'cuboidal'. As printed, B (columnar) is true.","ex":"","fl":["As printed on question slide 158, option B ('They have a simple columnar epithelium') is also TRUE: striated ducts are simple columnar with basal striations (G020:12). The answer slide silently rewrites B as 'simple cuboidal' to make it false. D is the intended single answer, but B as asked is not wrong."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 12 (Ducts)","H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":158,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor"},{"id":"F0753-89-1","s":"What is the function of striated ducts and which organelle is found in abundance there?","o":["A. Secrete mucous, ER","B. Secrete proteinacoeus fluid, ribosomes","C. Has apical infolding that increases surface area for ion transport; mitochondria","D. Has basal striations that increase the surface area for ion transport; mitochondria"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Has basal striations that increase the surface area for ion transport; mitochondria","a":"D","why":"D. <b>Striated ducts</b> have <b>basal</b> striations: deep folds of the basal membrane lined with many <b>mitochondria</b>. The folds add surface area; the mitochondria supply energy to pump sodium (Na+) out of the saliva.<br>A. Mucus with endoplasmic reticulum describes mucous cells.<br>B. Protein-rich fluid with ribosomes describes serous cells.<br>C. Right organelle, wrong side: the infoldings are basal, not apical.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":89,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":237,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0754-202-1"},{"id":"F0754-202-1","s":"Which of the following is true in regards to the structure labeled B below?","o":["A. The apical portion has abundant mitochondria which provide energy to concentrate saliva","B. You can commonly find this structure in the pancreas","C. The basal portion of this structure has abundant mitochondria which provide energy to concentrate saliva","D. It is commonly associated with centroacinar cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The basal portion of this structure has abundant mitochondria which provide energy to concentrate saliva","a":"C","why":"C. B is a <b>striated duct</b>: tall pink columnar cells around a lumen. Its <b>basal infoldings hold many mitochondria</b>, which power sodium (Na+) reabsorption. This modifies the saliva; it ends up hypotonic.<br>A. The mitochondria-rich folds are basal, not apical.<br>B. The pancreas has no striated ducts.<br>D. Centroacinar cells belong to the pancreas, which lacks striated ducts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-202.jpg","in":"H&E of a serous (parotid) gland: serous acini (A), connective tissue septum (CT); 'B' labels a duct with a central lumen lined by tall columnar cells with eosinophilic cytoplasm (striated duct).","tg":["H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)","H16 slide 20 (Functions)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":202,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3952-93-1","s":"What is the structure labeled w red and what is its function?","o":["A. Intercalated ducts, empty secretions into the duodenum","B. Striated duct, carry zymogens to the duodenum","C. Striated duct, reabsorb Na+ and Cl- and add HCO3-","D. Interlobular duct, empty secretions into the oral cavity"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Striated duct, reabsorb Na+ and Cl- and add HCO3-","a":"C","why":"C. The red-boxed duct is a <b>striated duct</b>: pink columnar cells with <b>basal striations</b>, among serous acini. It <b>reabsorbs sodium (Na+) and chloride (Cl−)</b> and adds bicarbonate (HCO3−), making saliva hypotonic.<br>A. Intercalated ducts are small cuboidal tubes, and salivary ducts drain to the mouth, not the duodenum.<br>B. Right duct, wrong job: it carries saliva to the mouth, not zymogens to the duodenum.<br>D. Interlobular ducts lie in septa, not among the acini.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3952-93.jpg","in":"Serous salivary gland at high power; a red box marks a duct lined by columnar cells with pale eosinophilic cytoplasm and basal striations, surrounded by dark granular serous acini (striated duct).","tg":["H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)","H16 slide 20 (Functions)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":93,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3949-23-1"},{"id":"F3949-23-1","s":"A Patient presents with xerostomia (dry mouth), dysphagia (difficulty swallowing), Ageusia (loss of taste), and swollen parotid glands. A biopsy of the parotid glands show inflammatory infiltrates in the interlobular connective tissue with damage to the acinar cells and striated ducts, reabsorption of which of the following will be most altered?","o":["A. HCO3-","B. Na+","C. Ca2+","D. H2O"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Na+","a":"B","why":"B. The <b>striated ducts</b> actively <b>reabsorb sodium (Na+)</b> and chloride, and secrete potassium and bicarbonate. Damaged striated ducts mainly lose Na+ reabsorption, so the saliva stays saltier.<br>A. Bicarbonate (HCO3−) is secreted into saliva by the ducts, not reabsorbed.<br>C. Calcium (Ca2+) is not what striated ducts pump; they move sodium, potassium and bicarbonate.<br>D. Salivary ducts are nearly water-tight; they do not reabsorb water, which is why saliva ends up hypotonic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 20 (Functions)","H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":23,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":30,"label":"Duct order: intercalated → striated → interlobular","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-12.jpg","cap":"Duct tree diagram with linings of intercalated, striated, interlobular and main ducts","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/F0754-205.jpg","cap":"Duct table: location, lining and secretion of each salivary duct","where":"2022 TA review, slide 205"}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-27-1","s":"Which structure found within salivary glands is responsible for connecting secretory acini to the striated ducts?","o":["A. Interlobular duct","B. Intercalated duct","C. Adipose tissue","D. Capsule","E. Plasma-cell–rich connective tissue"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Intercalated duct","a":"B","why":"B. The <b>intercalated duct</b> is the first, short duct, lined by simple cuboidal cells. It links each acinus to the striated duct. Order: acinus → intercalated → striated → interlobular → main duct.<br>A. Interlobular ducts are larger, lie in the septa between lobules, and come after the striated ducts.<br>C. Adipose tissue is stroma, not a duct.<br>D. The capsule wraps the whole gland.<br>E. Plasma-cell-rich connective tissue makes immunoglobulin A; it carries no saliva.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 12 (Ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":27,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F4081-37-1"},{"id":"F4081-37-1","s":"Which option correctly represents the flow of saliva through the duct system?","o":["A. Intercalated duct → Intralobular duct → Interlobular duct → Main duct","B. Interlobular duct → Intralobular duct → Intercalated duct → Main duct","C. Main duct → Interlobular duct → Intralobular duct → Intercalated duct","D. Intralobular duct → Intercalated duct → Main duct → Interlobular duct","E. Intercalated duct → Interlobular duct → Intralobular duct → Main duct"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Intercalated duct → Intralobular duct → Interlobular duct → Main duct","a":"A","why":"A. Saliva flows from small ducts to large: <b>intercalated → intralobular (striated) → interlobular → main duct</b>, which opens into the mouth.<br>B. Starts in the middle and runs backward to the intercalated duct.<br>C. Fully reversed: from the mouth back toward the acinus.<br>D. Puts the intralobular duct before the intercalated duct, and the main duct before the interlobular duct.<br>E. Puts the interlobular duct before the intralobular one; saliva must leave the lobule last.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 12 (Ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":37,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":30,"label":"Interlobular duct = duct in the septum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-16.jpg","cap":"Sublingual atlas plate: large interlobular excretory duct running in a connective-tissue septum","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/G020-28.jpg","cap":"Labelled pancreas: interlobular duct in a septum, intercalated duct, centroacinar cells, islets","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 28"},{"src":"sl/F0754-205.jpg","cap":"Duct table: interlobular ducts lie between lobules, within the septa","where":"2022 TA review, slide 205"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-206-1","s":"Identify (the structures marked by the orange dot and the blue dots)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Orange = Interlobular duct; Blue = Intralobular duct (striated duct specifically)","a":null,"why":"Orange = <b>interlobular duct</b>: it lies in the thick pink <b>connective-tissue septum between lobules</b>. Blue = <b>intralobular ducts</b>, here <b>striated ducts</b>: they sit <b>inside the lobule</b> among the dark serous acini.<br>Common mix-up: judge a duct by its location first. Size and lining vary, but a duct in the septum is always interlobular.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-206.jpg","in":"H&E of a mixed salivary gland lobule with dark serous and pale mucous acini; blue dots sit on small ducts inside the lobule, an orange dot sits on a larger duct in the connective-tissue septum between lobules.","tg":["H16 slide 12 (Ducts)","H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":206,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-9-1"},{"id":"F0722-153-1","s":"The arrows are pointing to the same structure. What is this structure?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Interlobular ducts","a":null,"why":"<b>Interlobular ducts</b>. Both arrows point to a duct lying <b>in the connective-tissue septum between lobules</b> (left: pancreas; right: parotid). Location is the easiest clue: inside a lobule = intralobular; in the septum = interlobular.<br>Common mix-up: a striated duct is also large and pink, but it sits inside the lobule among the acini.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-153.jpg","in":"Left: pancreas with an arrow on a duct in a connective-tissue septum; right: salivary gland with an arrow on a large duct running in the interlobular connective tissue.","tg":["H16 slide 12 (Ducts)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":153,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-9-1"},{"id":"F3959-9-1","s":"Which structure is shown by the arrow?","o":["A. Centroacinar duct","B. Intralobular duct","C. Striated duct","D. Interlobular duct","E. Intercalated duct"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Interlobular duct","a":"D","why":"D. The arrow points to a small duct lying <b>within the pale connective-tissue septum between lobules</b>: an <b>interlobular duct</b>, lined by cuboidal to columnar cells.<br>A. There is no 'centroacinar duct'; centroacinar cells sit inside the acini.<br>B. Intralobular ducts lie inside the lobule, among the acini.<br>C. The pancreas has no striated ducts.<br>E. Intercalated ducts are tiny tubes of flat cells inside the lobule, leaving each acinus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0753-95.jpg","in":"Same pancreas field as Q07; arrow on a small duct lined by low cuboidal cells inside the pale connective-tissue septum. [Picture viewed in F0719, same question; this source is text-only.]","tg":["H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 28 (Pancreatic ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":9,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":95,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":196,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":30,"label":"Name the parts of a salivary secretory unit","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-5.jpg","cap":"Labelled duct tree: serous acinus, demilune, mucous tubule, myoepithelial cells, intercalated and striated ducts","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-25-1","s":"Name the structures [blank labels on a diagram of a mixed salivary gland secretory unit].","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Myoepithelial cells; intercellular secretory canaliculi; serous acinus; intercalated duct(s); serous demilune; striated ducts; basal laminae; mucous tubule","a":null,"why":"Top row, left to right: <b>myoepithelial cells</b>, <b>intercellular secretory canaliculi</b>, myoepithelial cells. Left: <b>serous acinus</b> (round purple unit) and <b>intercalated duct</b> (small cuboidal tube). Right: <b>serous demilune</b> (purple cap on a tan mucous end). Middle: intercalated ducts. Bottom: <b>striated duct</b> (green, tall cells with basal stripes), <b>basal laminae</b>, <b>mucous tubule</b> (tan, pale cells, flat basal nuclei).<br>Common mix-up: the intercalated duct is short and cuboidal; the striated duct is wider and columnar.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-25.jpg","in":"Diagram of a duct tree: purple serous acini with myoepithelial cells and canaliculi, small intercalated ducts, tan mucous tubules capped by a serous demilune, green striated duct cross-section; blue boxes hide the labels.","tg":["H16 slide 7 (Salivary glands)","H16 slide 11 (Myoepithelial cells)","H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":25,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":31,"label":"Parotid purely serous; submandibular mostly serous","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-15.jpg","cap":"Parotid: all serous acini with intercalated and striated ducts, no mucous cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 15"},{"src":"sl/F0754-211.jpg","cap":"Parotid serous, sublingual mostly mucous, submandibular mostly serous, side by side","where":"2022 TA review, slide 211"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-207-3","s":"15. Which one of the following is a \"pure\" serous gland?","o":["A. parotid gland (adult)","B. palatine gland","C. submandibular gland","D. labial gland","E. sublingual gland"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"parotid gland (adult)","a":"A","why":"A. The adult <b>parotid</b> is the <b>purely serous</b> major salivary gland. All its acini are serous, with zymogen granules rich in amylase.<br>B. Palatine glands are mucous.<br>C. Submandibular is mixed, mostly serous.<br>D. Labial (lip) glands are mostly mucous.<br>E. Sublingual is mixed, mostly mucous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":207,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3980-6-1"},{"id":"F3980-6-1","s":"A histologic slide shows acini composed almost entirely of serous cells with numerous zymogen granules and well-developed intercalated ducts. Which salivary gland does this slide most likely represent?","o":["A. Parotid","B. Submandibular","C. Sublingual"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid","a":"A","why":"A. A gland made <b>almost entirely of serous acini</b> full of zymogen granules, with long intercalated ducts, is the <b>parotid</b>, the only purely serous major salivary gland.<br>B. Submandibular is mixed, mostly serous: you would also see pale mucous units and serous demilunes.<br>C. Sublingual is mixed but mostly mucous: mainly pale mucous cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 12 (Ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":6,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F1221-207-2","s":"14. In humans, which one of the following is correct?","o":["A. the parotid gland is chiefly a mixed gland","B. the submandibular gland is chiefly a mucous gland","C. the sublingual is chiefly a serous gland","D. the parotid is chiefly a mucous gland","E. the submandibular gland is chiefly a serous gland"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"the submandibular gland is chiefly a serous gland","a":"E","why":"E. The <b>submandibular</b> gland is <b>mixed but chiefly serous</b>: mostly dark serous acini, with some mucous tubules capped by serous demilunes.<br>A. The parotid is purely serous, not mixed.<br>B. Submandibular is chiefly serous, not mucous.<br>C. The sublingual is chiefly mucous.<br>D. The parotid has essentially no mucous cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":207,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":31,"label":"Serous demilune: serous cap on a mucous acinus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-19.jpg","cap":"Serous demilunes (S) capping mucous acini (M); mostly sublingual, also submandibular","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/G020-72.jpg","cap":"Arrows on dark serous crescents capping pale mucous tubules","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 72"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-160-1","s":"Identify this structure. What are the arrows point to? What are they surrounding? Where are they found?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Serous demilunes; they surround (cap) mucous acini; found in sublingual and submandibular glands","a":null,"why":"The arrows point to <b>serous demilunes</b>: half-moon caps of dark granular serous cells. They <b>surround (cap) mucous acini</b> (M, pale). They are found in the mixed glands: mostly the <b>sublingual</b>, also the <b>submandibular</b>.<br>Common mix-up: the parotid is purely serous, so it never has demilunes. A = a purely serous acinus; ID = intercalated duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-160.jpg","in":"Mucous acini (M) capped by crescents of dark serous cells (arrows); serous acinus (A) and intercalated duct (ID) labeled.","tg":["H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":160,"f":"F0722"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":43,"f":"F3949"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":239,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G024-9-1"},{"id":"G024-9-1","s":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the structure indicated by the arrow?","o":["A. The organ shown in the image is the parotid gland","B. The structure squeezes the secretion from the acini","C. The structure secretes trypsinogen","D. Contributes a serous component to the gland's seromucous secretion","E. It has basal infoldings"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Contributes a serous component to the gland's seromucous secretion","a":"D","why":"D. The arrow marks a <b>serous demilune</b>: a dark crescent of serous cells capping a pale mucous tubule. It adds the <b>serous (watery, protein) part</b> of the gland's mixed seromucous saliva.<br>A. Pale mucous tubules mean a mixed gland; the parotid is purely serous.<br>B. Squeezing secretion out is the myoepithelial cell's job.<br>C. Trypsinogen is made by pancreatic acinar cells.<br>E. Basal infoldings belong to striated ducts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G024-9.jpg","in":"Mixed salivary gland (mostly purple serous acini) with a cluster of pale, foamy mucous tubules; arrow points to a dark purple crescent of serous cells capping a mucous acinus (serous demilune).","tg":["H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)","H16 slide 11 (Myoepithelial cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":9,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":9,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":12,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":9,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":7,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0754-208-1","s":"Identify the orange structure. Where was this slide taken from?","o":["A. Serous demilune, submandibular gland","B. Centroacinar cell, pancreas","C. Serous demilune, parotid gland","D. Centroacinar cell, sublingual gland"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Serous demilune, submandibular gland","a":"A","why":"A. The orange markers sit on <b>serous demilunes</b>: dark serous crescents capping pale mucous acini (M). Demilunes mean a mixed gland, so the <b>submandibular</b> fits (sublingual would too, but is not offered).<br>B. Centroacinar cells are pale cells inside pancreatic acini, not caps on mucous acini.<br>C. The parotid is purely serous, so it has no demilunes.<br>D. Centroacinar cells belong to the pancreas, not the sublingual gland.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-208.jpg","in":"H&E of a mixed gland: pale mucous tubules (M) each capped by a crescent of darker, granular serous cells (orange markers + arrows), plus purely serous acini (A).","tg":["H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)","H16 slide 71 (? Mixed salivary (Submandibular) gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":208,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F4081-43-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":31,"label":"Serous demilunes mostly in the sublingual","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-19.jpg","cap":"Serous demilunes (S) capping mucous acini (M); mostly sublingual, also submandibular","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/F0754-210.jpg","cap":"Serous demilunes: half-moon serous caps on mucous acini in mixed glands","where":"2022 TA review, slide 210"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-166-3","s":"6. Which one of the following glands would possess numerous demilunes?","o":["A. parotid","B. sublingual","C. posterior lingual","D. von Ebner"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"sublingual","a":"B","why":"B. <b>Serous demilunes</b> cap mucous acini, so they occur only in mixed glands. Per the lecture they are <b>mostly seen in the sublingual</b>, and also in the submandibular.<br>A. The parotid is purely serous: no mucous acini to cap.<br>C. Posterior lingual glands, at the back of the tongue, are mucous.<br>D. Von Ebner glands, at the circumvallate papillae, are purely serous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":166,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F4081-43-1"},{"id":"F4081-43-1","s":"The histologic image shown demonstrates the structure indicated. Where is this structure mostly seen?","o":["A. Parotid gland","B. Sublingual gland","C. Lacrimal gland","D. Minor salivary glands of the palate","E. Parathyroid gland"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Sublingual gland","a":"B","why":"B. The arrows mark <b>serous demilunes</b>: crescents of serous cells (S) capping mucous acini (M). The lecture says they are <b>mostly seen in the sublingual</b> gland, and also in the submandibular.<br>A. The parotid is purely serous, so it has no mucous acini to cap.<br>C. The lacrimal gland is serous, without demilunes.<br>D. Minor palatine glands are mucous.<br>E. The parathyroid is an endocrine gland of chief and oxyphil cells, with no acini.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-43.jpg","in":"Pale mucous acini (M) each capped by a crescent of granular serous cells (arrows, S = serous demilunes); A = serous acinus.","tg":["H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":43,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":32,"label":"Parotid: all-serous acini, fat cells, striated ducts","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-75.jpg","cap":"Parotid: dark serous acini separated by many empty fat cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 75"},{"src":"sl/G020-15.jpg","cap":"Parotid: all serous acini with intercalated and striated ducts, no mucous cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F3959-28-1","s":"D6- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"B","why":"B. A lobulated gland of <b>uniformly dark purple serous acini</b>, with ducts in the septa and <b>no pale mucous areas</b>, is the <b>parotid</b>.<br>A. Submandibular is mixed: pale mucous units would be scattered among the dark acini.<br>C. Sublingual is mostly mucous, so it would look pale.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G022-25.jpg","in":"Low-power lobulated gland of uniformly dark purple serous acini with septal ducts. [Picture viewed in F0719, same question; this source is text-only.]","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":28,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":25,"f":"G022"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-32-1"},{"id":"F0052-192-1","s":"Question 6 Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Pancreas","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"C","why":"C. Clusters of <b>dark granular serous acini</b> separated by <b>large empty fat cells</b>, with no mucous cells and no islets, mean the <b>parotid</b>.<br>A. Spleen is lymphoid tissue with red and white pulp.<br>B. Pancreas is tightly packed acini with pale islets and little fat.<br>D. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules.<br>E. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-192.jpg","in":"H&E: clusters of dark granular serous acini separated by large clear adipocytes and pale connective tissue; no mucous cells, no islets.","tg":["H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 75 (Parotid)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":192,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-32-1"},{"id":"F3959-31-1","s":"D9- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"B","why":"B. Only <b>dark serous acini</b>, with <b>many fat cells</b> and a duct in connective tissue, mean the <b>parotid</b>. It is purely serous, and fat cells among its acini are typical.<br>A. Submandibular would show pale mucous units among the serous acini.<br>C. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G022-28.jpg","in":"Dark serous acini with many fat cells and a duct in connective tissue. [Picture viewed in F0719, same question; this source is text-only.]","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":31,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":28,"f":"G022"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-32-1"},{"id":"F3959-32-1","s":"D10- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"B","why":"B. High power shows <b>dark granular serous acini</b>, <b>fat cells</b> and a <b>pink striated duct</b>, with no mucous cells. That is the <b>parotid</b>.<br>A. Submandibular would also have pale mucous units and serous demilunes.<br>C. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules, with few striated ducts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G022-29.jpg","in":"High power: dark granular serous acini, fat cells and a pink striated duct. [Picture viewed in F0719, same question; this source is text-only.]","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":32,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":29,"f":"G022"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3952-91-1","s":"What gland is this section and what cell predominates?","o":["A. Pancreas, Beta cells","B. Parotid gland, Serous cells","C. Submandibular gland, Mucous cells","D. Submandibular gland, Serous cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland, Serous cells","a":"B","why":"B. Only <b>dark, granular serous acini</b> (A) with round nuclei, an intercalated duct (ID) and a striated duct (SD), and <b>no mucous cells</b>: the <b>parotid</b>, a purely serous gland.<br>A. Beta cells sit in pale pancreatic islets; there is no islet here, and the pancreas has no striated ducts.<br>C. Mucous cells would look pale and foamy; none are present.<br>D. Submandibular would show some pale mucous units among the serous acini.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3952-91.jpg","in":"High-power micrograph: densely granular, basophilic serous acini (A) with round nuclei, an intercalated duct (ID) and a striated duct (SD); no mucous cells.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 75 (Parotid)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":91,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-32-1"},{"id":"F0052-188-1","s":"Question 4 Identify the organ:","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Pancreas","C. Parotid gland","D. Stomach","E. Liver"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"C","why":"C. The picture is one <b>striated duct</b> (tall pink columnar cells with basal striations) among dark <b>serous acini</b>. Striated ducts plus serous acini mean a serous salivary gland: the <b>parotid</b>.<br>A. Duodenum shows villi, crypts and Brunner glands.<br>B. The pancreas has serous acini but <b>no striated ducts</b>.<br>D. Stomach shows pits and gastric glands.<br>E. Liver shows hepatocyte plates and sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-188.jpg","in":"Same high-power image: a striated duct (columnar cells with basal striations) surrounded by granular serous acini.","tg":["H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)","H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":188,"f":"F0052"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":5,"f":"F3959"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-32-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":33,"label":"Submandibular: mixed, mostly serous","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-17.jpg","cap":"Submandibular atlas plate: mostly dark serous acini with some pale mucous acini","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/G020-71.jpg","cap":"Submandibular at low power: mostly dark serous lobules with pale mucous patches","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 71"}],"q":[{"id":"F3959-29-1","s":"D7- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Submandibular gland","a":"A","why":"A. <b>Mostly dark serous acini</b> with a <b>minority of pale mucous tubules</b> and pink striated ducts: a mixed, mostly serous gland is the <b>submandibular</b>.<br>B. Parotid is purely serous; it has no pale mucous tubules.<br>C. Sublingual is mostly mucous: pale tubules would dominate, and striated ducts are few.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G022-26.jpg","in":"Mostly dark serous acini with a minority of pale mucous tubules and pink striated ducts. [Picture viewed in F0719, same question; this source is text-only.]","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":29,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":26,"f":"G022"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-30-1"},{"id":"F0052-184-1","s":"Question 2 Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Submandibular gland","B. Spleen","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Submandibular gland","a":"A","why":"A. <b>Dark serous acini mixed with pale mucous tubules</b>, some capped by serous demilunes, plus several striated ducts: a mixed, mostly serous gland is the <b>submandibular</b>.<br>B. Spleen is lymphoid tissue, not acini.<br>C. Parotid is purely serous: no pale mucous tubules.<br>D. Sublingual is mostly mucous: pale tubules would dominate.<br>E. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-184.jpg","in":"H&E: dark serous acini mixed with pale mucous tubules (some capped by serous crescents) and several striated ducts.","tg":["H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)","H16 slide 71 (? Mixed salivary (Submandibular) gland)","H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":184,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-30-1"},{"id":"F3959-30-1","s":"D8- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Submandibular gland","a":"A","why":"A. Pale mucous acini <b>capped by dark serous demilunes</b>, among many dark serous acini and pink striated ducts: a mixed, mostly serous gland is the <b>submandibular</b>.<br>B. Parotid is purely serous: no mucous acini, so no demilunes.<br>C. Sublingual also has demilunes, but pale mucous tubules dominate, and serous acini and striated ducts are scarce.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G022-27.jpg","in":"Pale mucous acini with dark demilune caps among dark serous acini and pink striated ducts. [Picture viewed in F0719, same question; this source is text-only.]","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)","H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":30,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":27,"f":"G022"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F4081-73-1","s":"What is this?","o":["A. Centrilobular necrosis of the liver","B. Pancreas of a type 1 diabetic","C. Cross-section of healthy colonic crypts","D. Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease","E. Healthy submandibular gland","F. Healthy pancreas"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Healthy submandibular gland","a":"E","why":"E. Lobules of acini with <b>pale mucous cells capped by darker serous cells (demilunes)</b>, separated by septa with ducts, mean a <b>mixed salivary gland</b>. The only salivary option is a healthy <b>submandibular</b> gland.<br>A. Centrilobular necrosis: dead liver cells around central veins; no acini.<br>B. Diabetic pancreas: serous acini with small islets; no mucous cells.<br>C. Colonic crypts: straight tubes of goblet cells, not lobules of acini.<br>D. Fatty liver: hepatocytes filled with fat droplets.<br>F. Healthy pancreas: serous acini and islets; no mucous cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-73.jpg","in":"Lobulated gland with many pale mucous acini capped by darker serous cells (demilunes), interlobular septa and ducts.","tg":["H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)","H16 slide 19 (Serous demilunes)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":73,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-30-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":33,"label":"Sublingual: mostly pale mucous acini","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-73.jpg","cap":"Sublingual gland: field of pale bluish mucous acini, almost no serous cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 73"},{"src":"sl/G020-9.jpg","cap":"Mucous acini (M) with flat basal nuclei beside skeletal muscle (SM)","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F3959-2-1","s":"Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Submandibular gland","B. Spleen","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sublingual gland","a":"D","why":"D. Under a capsule, the gland is almost all <b>pale bluish-gray mucous tubules</b>, with very few dark serous cells. A mostly mucous gland is the <b>sublingual</b>.<br>A. Submandibular is mixed but mostly serous: dark acini would dominate.<br>B. Spleen is lymphoid tissue (white and red pulp), not glandular acini.<br>C. Parotid is purely serous: dark granular acini, no mucous cells.<br>E. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids, not acini.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0753-87.jpg","in":"Capsule over a gland made almost entirely of pale bluish-grey mucous tubules with very few dark serous cells. [Picture viewed in F0719, same question; this source is text-only.]","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 16 (Sublingual gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":2,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":3,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":87,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":182,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-26-1"},{"id":"F3959-26-1","s":"D4- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Sublingual gland","a":"C","why":"C. Pale, bluish <b>mucous tubules fill the whole field</b>, with almost no dark serous cells. A gland that is mostly mucous is the <b>sublingual</b>.<br>A. Submandibular is mostly serous: dark purple acini would dominate, with scattered pale units.<br>B. Parotid is purely serous: uniformly dark granular acini, often with fat cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G022-23.jpg","in":"Gland of pale bluish mucous tubules almost throughout. [Picture viewed in F0719, same question; this source is text-only.]","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 16 (Sublingual gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":26,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":23,"f":"G022"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3959-27-1","s":"D5- [Identify the gland]","o":["A. Submandibular glands","B. Parotid gland","C. Sublingual gland"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Sublingual gland","a":"C","why":"C. Even at low power the whole strip stains <b>pale bluish</b>, the color of mucin. A gland that is mostly mucous is the <b>sublingual</b>.<br>A. Submandibular is mostly serous, so at low power it looks dark purple with pale patches.<br>B. Parotid is purely serous and looks uniformly dark purple.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G022-24.jpg","in":"Low-power strip of gland that stains pale bluish (mucous) throughout. [Picture viewed in F0719, same question; this source is text-only.]","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 16 (Sublingual gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":27,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":24,"f":"G022"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-26-1"},{"id":"F3952-89-1","s":"What gland is this section and what cell predominates?","o":["A. Parotid Gland, Mucous cells","B. Submandibular gland, Serous cells","C. Submandibular gland, mucous cells","D. Sublingual gland, mucous cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sublingual gland, mucous cells","a":"D","why":"D. The field is almost all <b>pale, foamy mucous acini</b> (M) with flattened basal nuclei, beside skeletal muscle (SM) of the mouth floor. A mostly mucous gland is the <b>sublingual</b>.<br>A. The parotid is purely serous, with no mucous cells.<br>B. The submandibular is mostly serous, so dark acini would dominate.<br>C. A mainly mucous field points to the sublingual, not the mostly serous submandibular.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3952-89.jpg","in":"H&E micrograph: field of pale, foamy mucous tubules/acini (labelled M) with an intralobular duct (ID) and bundles of striated (skeletal) muscle (SM) at lower right; no serous acini evident.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 16 (Sublingual gland)","H16 slide 73 (Mucous salivary (Sublingual) gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":89,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-26-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":33,"label":"Name all three major salivary glands","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-16.jpg","cap":"Sublingual atlas plate: mostly pale mucous acini with serous demilunes","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/G020-17.jpg","cap":"Submandibular atlas plate: mostly dark serous acini with some pale mucous acini","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/F0754-211.jpg","cap":"Parotid serous, sublingual mostly mucous, submandibular mostly serous, side by side","where":"2022 TA review, slide 211"}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-41-1","s":"Name the glands [A, B, C].","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A = Parotid (exclusively serous); B = Sublingual (mixed but predominantly mucous); C = Submandibular (mixed serous and mucous)","a":null,"why":"A = <b>parotid</b>: all dark purple <b>serous</b> acini, with ducts and some fat. B = <b>sublingual</b>: mostly <b>pale mucous</b> tubules, with a few dark serous caps. C = <b>submandibular</b>: <b>mixed</b>, mostly dark serous acini with some pale mucous units.<br>Common mix-up: B and C are both mixed. Decide by the main color: mostly pale = sublingual; mostly dark = submandibular.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-41.jpg","in":"A: all dark purple serous acini with ducts and fat; B: mostly pale mucous tubules with a few dark cells and a large duct; C: mostly dark serous acini mixed with some pale mucous units and several ducts.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 16 (Sublingual gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":41,"f":"F4081"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":145,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F1221-118-1","s":"Quiz yourself (blank slide): which gland/organ is shown, and what are its identifying features?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left to right: parotid (pure serous acini, striated ducts), submandibular (mixed, mostly serous, serous demilunes), sublingual (mostly mucous, serous demilunes)","a":null,"why":"Left to right: <b>parotid</b> (all dark <b>serous</b> acini, with striated ducts); <b>submandibular</b> (<b>mixed</b>: serous acini plus pale mucous tubules capped by <b>serous demilunes</b>); <b>sublingual</b> (mostly <b>pale mucous</b> acini, beside skeletal muscle of the mouth floor).<br>Memory aid: Parotid = Protein (serous); Submandibular = Serous + Mucous; Sublingual = Slimy (mucous).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1221-118.jpg","in":"Drawing of the three major salivary glands (boxed) beside three unlabelled H&E panels: all-serous dark acini; mixed serous acini and pale mucous tubules; mostly pale mucous acini with skeletal muscle.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 16 (Sublingual gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":118,"f":"F1221"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":120,"f":"F1221"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":122,"f":"F1221"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":124,"f":"F1221"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":126,"f":"F1221"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":129,"f":"F1221"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":133,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F4081-41-1"},{"id":"F3980-4-1","s":"Correctly match each histological slide with the type of salivary gland it represents (left → right).","o":["A. Submandibular","B. Sublingual","C. Parotid"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left = Parotid gland; middle = Sublingual gland; right = Submandibular gland","a":null,"why":"Left = <b>parotid</b>: every acinar cell has a round central nucleus and granular cytoplasm, around a big pink <b>striated duct</b>; no mucous cells. Middle = <b>sublingual</b>: small ducts among <b>pale, frothy mucous cells</b> with flattened nuclei. Right = <b>submandibular</b>: <b>dark purple serous cells next to pale mucous cells</b> (mixed), around a striated duct.<br>Common mix-up: sublingual and submandibular are both mixed; sublingual is mostly pale, submandibular mostly dark.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-4.jpg","in":"Three high-power panels each centered on a duct: left, pink duct among pale cells; middle, small ducts among pale foamy mucous cells; right, striated duct among dark purple serous acini with a few pale mucous cells.","tg":["H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)","H16 slide 17 (Submandibular gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":4,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F4081-41-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":34,"label":"Islet of Langerhans: pale endocrine cluster","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-24.jpg","cap":"Labelled pancreas: pale islet cells among dark serous acini; exocrine plus endocrine","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G020-77.jpg","cap":"Low-power pancreas: pale round islets scattered among packed acini","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 77"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-14-1","s":"Which of the following structures is outlined in the slide below and what is its function","o":["A. Pancreatic acinus — secretes digestive enzymes into the duodenum","B. Islet of Langerhans — secretes hormones that regulate blood glucose","C. Centroacinar duct cell — initiates bicarbonate-rich fluid secretion","D. Intercalated duct — transports zymogen-rich secretions to intralobular ducts"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Islet of Langerhans — secretes hormones that regulate blood glucose","a":"B","why":"B. The circle holds a <b>pale cluster of cords of cells with capillaries</b> among darker acini: an <b>islet of Langerhans</b>. It is endocrine; it releases insulin and glucagon into the blood to <b>regulate blood glucose</b>.<br>A. Pancreatic acini are the darker zymogen cells around the circle.<br>C. A centroacinar cell is a single pale nucleus inside an acinus, not a large cluster.<br>D. Intercalated ducts are small tubes of flat cells; this is a solid cluster of cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-14.jpg","in":"Pancreas H&E: blue dashed circle around a pale-staining cluster of cords of cells with capillaries, surrounded by darker purple/pink serous acini.","tg":["H16 slide 24 (Mixed gland)","H16 slide 25 (Capsule, Septa, lobules)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":14,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F4081-71-1","s":"Label the cells with endocrine vs exocrine functions: [A, B]","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A = endocrine (islet of Langerhans; islet cells secrete hormones directly into the blood); B = exocrine (acinar cells secrete digestive enzymes into ducts)","a":null,"why":"A = <b>endocrine</b>: it sits in a <b>pale round cluster</b> of cells, an <b>islet of Langerhans</b>, which releases hormones (insulin, glucagon) straight into capillaries. B = <b>exocrine</b>: a <b>dark acinar cell with pink apical zymogen granules</b>; it secretes digestive enzymes into ducts.<br>Common mix-up: the pale cells make hormones; the dark, granular cells make enzymes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-71.jpg","in":"Pancreas: A sits in a pale round cluster of cells (islet); B sits among dark purple acini with pink apical zymogen granules.","tg":["H16 slide 24 (Mixed gland)","H16 slide 25 (Capsule, Septa, lobules)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":71,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F1221-154-2"},{"id":"F1221-154-2","s":"Which of the following describes cells of the endocrine pancreas?","o":["A. Acinar cells that feed into a ductal system","B. Serous cells that surround paler central cells like a bullseye on H&E","C. Clusters of paler cells on H&E that have blood supply but no direct ductal system access","D. Cells that appear to have stellate form and exist in the sinusoidal space"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Clusters of paler cells on H&E that have blood supply but no direct ductal system access","a":"C","why":"C. <b>Islets of Langerhans</b> are <b>clusters of paler cells</b> on hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E) stain, threaded with capillaries. They <b>release hormones into the blood</b>, so they need no duct.<br>A. Acinar cells that feed a duct system are the exocrine pancreas.<br>B. Serous cells around a pale central (centroacinar) cell form an exocrine acinus.<br>D. Star-shaped cells in the sinusoids are Kupffer cells of the liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 24 (Mixed gland)","H16 slide 25 (Capsule, Septa, lobules)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":154,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":34,"label":"Identify pancreas: packed acini with islets","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-77.jpg","cap":"Low-power pancreas: pale round islets scattered among packed acini","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 77"},{"src":"sl/G020-28.jpg","cap":"Labelled pancreas: acini, islets, septa with interlobular duct, centroacinar cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"F3959-6-1","s":"Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Pancreas","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas","a":"B","why":"B. Densely packed serous acini with <b>pale, round clusters of cells (islets of Langerhans)</b> mean <b>pancreas</b>. No other organ has islets.<br>A. Spleen is lymphoid tissue, with no acini.<br>C. Parotid has serous acini but no islets; look for fat cells and striated ducts.<br>D. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules.<br>E. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0753-91.jpg","in":"Serous acini with two pale round clusters of cells (islets). [Picture viewed in F0719, same question; this source is text-only.]","tg":["H16 slide 24 (Mixed gland)","H16 slide 25 (Capsule, Septa, lobules)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":6,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":7,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":8,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":91,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":190,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0052-194-1","s":"Question 7 Which organ is depicted below?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Pancreas","C. Parotid gland","D. Sublingual gland","E. Liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas","a":"B","why":"B. A uniform field of <b>tightly packed serous acini</b> with <b>pink apical zymogen granules</b>, thin septa and a duct in connective tissue, but <b>no fat cells, mucous cells or striated ducts</b>: the <b>pancreas</b>.<br>A. Spleen is lymphoid tissue without acini.<br>C. Parotid would show fat cells and pink striated ducts among its acini.<br>D. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules.<br>E. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-194.jpg","in":"H&E: uniform field of basophilic acini of pyramidal cells with a connective-tissue septum containing a duct at the lower right; no adipocytes or mucous cells.","tg":["H16 slide 25 (Capsule, Septa, lobules)","H16 slide 77 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":194,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-6-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":35,"label":"Acinar cell: apical zymogen granules face the lumen","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-27.jpg","cap":"Acinar cell electron micrograph and diagram: apical zymogen granules around a small lumen","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/G024-22.jpg","cap":"Labelled acinar cell electron micrograph: apical zymogen granules, basal rough endoplasmic reticulum","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-16-1","s":"A histologic section shows pyramidal cells arranged around a small central lumen with apical zymogen granules. Which statement best explains how this structure supports their function?","o":["A. The pyramidal orientation directs enzyme secretion into the central lumen for delivery to ducts","B. The broad basal surfaces maximize nutrient absorption for enzyme modification","C. The narrow lumen allows these cells to regulate pressure and alter secretions","D. The radial pattern enables both endocrine and exocrine release from the same unit"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The pyramidal orientation directs enzyme secretion into the central lumen for delivery to ducts","a":"A","why":"A. Pancreatic acinar cells are <b>pyramidal</b>: a broad base full of rough endoplasmic reticulum and a narrow apex full of <b>zymogen granules</b>. All apexes face the <b>small central lumen</b>, so enzymes are released into it and flow on to the ducts.<br>B. The base holds the protein-making machinery; it is not an absorptive surface.<br>C. The lumen does not regulate pressure; fluid is added later by duct cells.<br>D. Acini are only exocrine; hormones come from separate islets.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 8 (Serous cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":16,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":35,"label":"Centroacinar cell: pale nucleus in acinus center","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-26.jpg","cap":"Pancreas micrograph with arrow on centroacinar nucleus; acinus diagram with centroacinar cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/G024-24.jpg","cap":"Acinus diagram and micrograph: centroacinar cells begin the intercalated duct inside the acinus","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-48-1","s":"Which cells are located in the center of pancreatic acini and are characteristic for having no zymogen granules, unlike the surrounding pyramidal cells?","o":["A. Pyramidal cells","B. Zymogen cells","C. Basal rough ER cells","D. Golgi cells","E. Centroacinar cells"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Centroacinar cells","a":"E","why":"E. <b>Centroacinar cells</b> sit in the <b>center of pancreatic acini</b>. They are pale and have no zymogen granules because they are the first cells of the <b>intercalated duct</b>, tucked into the acinus.<br>A. Pyramidal cells are the acinar cells, which do have granules.<br>B. Zymogen cells are the granule-filled acinar cells.<br>C. Basal rough endoplasmic reticulum is part of the acinar cell, not a separate cell.<br>D. The Golgi is an organelle, not a cell type.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":48,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-10-1"},{"id":"F3959-10-1","s":"Which structure is shown by the arrow?","o":["A. Centroacinar cell","B. Serous cell","C. Striated duct","D. Intercalated duct"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Centroacinar cell","a":"A","why":"A. The arrow is on a <b>small pale nucleus in the center of an acinus</b>: a <b>centroacinar cell</b>, the first cell of the intercalated duct. The nearby islet confirms pancreas.<br>B. Serous (acinar) cells are the dark pyramidal cells with pink apical granules around the lumen.<br>C. The pancreas has no striated ducts.<br>D. The intercalated duct is the tube leaving the acinus; inside the acinus its cells are called centroacinar cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G022-10.jpg","in":"Pancreas with an islet at top; arrow on a small pale nucleus sitting in the lumen-center of an acinus. [Picture viewed in F0719, same question; this source is text-only.]","tg":["H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":10,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2024 lab","n":"HL11 accessory digestive lab deck Fall 2024 (practice questions)","sl":10,"f":"G022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":198,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3952-95-1","s":"What gland is this section and what is the arrow pointing at?","o":["A. Endocrine pancreas, Striated ducts","B. Exocrine Pancreas, centroacinar cells","C. Endocrine pancreas, centroacinar cells","D. Submandibular gland, serous acini"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Exocrine Pancreas, centroacinar cells","a":"B","why":"B. Small acini (A) of pyramidal zymogen cells, and the arrow on a <b>pale, flattened nucleus inside an acinus</b>: a <b>centroacinar cell</b>, found only in the <b>exocrine pancreas</b>.<br>A. A field of acini is exocrine, and the pancreas has no striated ducts.<br>C. Centroacinar cells belong to the exocrine duct system, not the endocrine islets.<br>D. Submandibular serous acini have no centroacinar cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3952-95.jpg","in":"H&E micrograph of exocrine pancreas: small acini (A) of pyramidal zymogenic cells with a fibroblast (F); the arrow points to a pale, flattened nucleus in the centre of an acinus (centroacinar cell).","tg":["H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":95,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-10-1"},{"id":"F0722-170-1","s":"Name the structure the arrow is pointing to. Also, where is this found? [Secondary Q: what stimulates the intercalated duct?]","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Centroacinar cell (pancreas); centroacinar cells line the initial segment of the intercalated duct and secrete bicarbonate; secretin stimulates the intercalated duct","a":null,"why":"<b>Centroacinar cell</b>, found in the <b>pancreas</b>: the pale nucleus in the center of the acinus. Centroacinar cells line the <b>first part of the intercalated duct</b> and secrete <b>bicarbonate</b>. The intercalated duct is stimulated by <b>secretin</b>.<br>Common mix-up: cholecystokinin (CCK) drives the acinar cells to release enzymes; secretin drives the ducts to release bicarbonate fluid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-170.jpg","in":"Pancreatic acini (A); arrow on a small pale nucleus (circled) in the center of an acinus; F = fibroblast.","tg":["H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 31 (Functions of Exocrine pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":170,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3959-10-1"},{"id":"G024-21-1","s":"A biopsy is performed and the specimen below is obtained. Which of the following is true of the organ from which the specimen was taken?","o":["A. The apical cytoplasm of acinar cells contains prominent RER and Golgi","B. The basal cytoplasm of acinar cells contains prominent zymogen granules","C. Centroacinar cells are continuous with intercalated ducts","D. Serous demilunes are usually present","E. Striated ducts are present"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Centroacinar cells are continuous with intercalated ducts","a":"C","why":"C. The specimen is <b>exocrine pancreas</b>, with pale centroacinar nuclei in the acinar centers. <b>Centroacinar cells</b> are the first cells of the <b>intercalated duct</b>, so they are continuous with it.<br>A. Rough endoplasmic reticulum is basal; that is why the base stains purple.<br>B. Zymogen granules are apical (pink), not basal.<br>D. Serous demilunes belong to mixed salivary glands.<br>E. The pancreas has no striated ducts; they belong to salivary glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G024-21.jpg","in":"Exocrine pancreas: serous acinar cells with basal purple basophilia and apical pink zymogen granules, pale centroacinar nuclei in acinar centers; no ducts with striations or demilunes.","tg":["H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":21,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":21,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":27,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":21,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":23,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":36,"label":"Centroacinar cells mean pancreas","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-26.jpg","cap":"Pancreas micrograph with arrow on centroacinar nucleus; acinus diagram with centroacinar cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/G020-30.jpg","cap":"Compare table: islets, centroacinar cells in pancreas; myoepithelial cells, striated ducts in salivary","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/F0754-215.jpg","cap":"Parotid striated duct with no centroacinar cells versus pancreas with centroacinar cells","where":"2022 TA review, slide 215"}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-93-1","s":"What cell is the distinguishing feature of the pancreas in relation to serous acini?","o":["A. Striated ducts","B. Myoepithelial cells","C. Intercalated duts","D. Centroacinar cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Centroacinar cells","a":"D","why":"D. <b>Centroacinar cells</b> are pale cells inside the center of the acinus. Only the <b>pancreas</b> has them, so they tell it apart from a serous salivary gland like the parotid.<br>A. Striated ducts are in salivary glands, not the pancreas.<br>B. Myoepithelial cells wrap salivary acini; the pancreas has none.<br>C. Intercalated ducts are in both, so they do not tell them apart.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 30 (Compare)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":93,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":242,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0754-213-1"},{"id":"F0722-167-1","s":"Where can this histological section be found??","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Pancreas","a":null,"why":"<b>Pancreas</b>. The acini are <b>pyramidal serous cells</b> with pink apical zymogen granules, and the arrows point to <b>pale centroacinar cells in the center of the acini</b>. Only the pancreas has centroacinar cells.<br>Common mix-up: the parotid also has all-serous acini, but it has striated ducts and fat cells and no centroacinar cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-167.jpg","in":"Serous acini of pyramidal cells with basal basophilia and apical eosinophilic zymogen; arrows from a blacked-out label point to acini.","tg":["H16 slide 25 (Capsule, Septa, lobules)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":167,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0754-213-1"},{"id":"F0754-213-1","s":"Where was this slide taken from?","o":["A. Parotid Gland","B. Liver","C. Sublingual Gland","D. Pancreas"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas","a":"D","why":"D. Pyramidal serous acinar cells with pink apical zymogen granules, and the arrow on a <b>pale nucleus in the center of an acinus</b>: a <b>centroacinar cell</b>. These are found only in the <b>pancreas</b>.<br>A. The parotid has serous acini too, but no centroacinar cells; it has striated ducts and fat.<br>B. Liver is plates of hepatocytes with sinusoids.<br>C. Sublingual is mostly pale mucous tubules.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-213.jpg","in":"High-power H&E of serous acini (A) of pyramidal zymogenic cells with a fibroblast (F); the arrow points to a pale flattened nucleus in the centre of an acinus (centroacinar cell); no striated ducts.","tg":["H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)","H16 slide 30 (Compare)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":213,"f":"F0754"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":39,"f":"F3949"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":41,"f":"F3949"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":45,"f":"F3949"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":47,"f":"F3949"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":49,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":36,"label":"Pancreas vs parotid: islets, centroacinar cells, striated ducts","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-30.jpg","cap":"Compare table: islets, centroacinar cells in pancreas; myoepithelial cells, striated ducts in salivary","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/F0754-215.jpg","cap":"Parotid striated duct with no centroacinar cells versus pancreas with centroacinar cells","where":"2022 TA review, slide 215"},{"src":"sl/G020-75.jpg","cap":"Parotid: dark serous acini separated by many empty fat cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 75"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-155-1","s":"Which option(s) lack striated ducts?","o":["A. Parotid","B. Sublingual","C. Submandibular","D. Pancreas"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas","a":"D","why":"D. The <b>pancreas lacks striated ducts</b>. Its acini drain through intercalated ducts, which start as centroacinar cells, into intralobular and interlobular ducts.<br>A. The parotid has prominent striated ducts.<br>B. The sublingual has striated ducts, but they are short and few.<br>C. The submandibular has long, well-developed striated ducts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 30 (Compare)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":155,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3980-12-1","s":"A histologic section shows clusters of serous acinar cells with basophilic basal cytoplasm, apical zymogen granules, and well-developed intercalated ducts. No centroacinar cells are visible within the acini. [Which organ?]","o":["A. Pancreas","B. Parotid gland","C. Submandibular gland","D. Sublingual gland"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Parotid gland","a":"B","why":"B. All-serous acini with zymogen granules and well-developed intercalated ducts, but <b>no centroacinar cells</b>: a purely serous salivary gland, the <b>parotid</b>.<br>A. The pancreas would show centroacinar cells and islets.<br>C. Submandibular is mixed: pale mucous units and demilunes.<br>D. Sublingual is mostly mucous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 30 (Compare)","H16 slide 14 (Classification of salivary glands)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":12,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0754-213-1"},{"id":"F4081-67-1","s":"Identify the pancreas vs parotid gland:","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left = pancreas (islet of Langerhans = pale round clump among acini); right = parotid gland (adipocytes and many ducts)","a":null,"why":"Left = <b>pancreas</b>: uniform acini with a <b>pale round islet of Langerhans</b>, which only the pancreas has. Right = <b>parotid</b>: dark serous acini with many <b>fat cells (adipocytes)</b> and many ducts.<br>Common mix-up: both are all-serous glands. Look for islets and centroacinar cells (pancreas) versus fat cells and striated ducts (parotid).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-67.jpg","in":"Left: uniform purple-pink acini with one pale round cluster of cells (islet). Right: dark serous acini with scattered white fat cells and many ducts.","tg":["H16 slide 30 (Compare)","H16 slide 24 (Mixed gland)","H16 slide 15 (Parotid gland)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":67,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":36,"label":"Centroacinar cells → pancreas; striated duct → salivary","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-30.jpg","cap":"Table: pancreas has islets, centroacinar cells; serous salivary has striated ducts, myoepithelium","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G020-27.jpg","cap":"Diagram: pale centroacinar cells inside the acinus, continuing into the terminal duct","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/G020-13.jpg","cap":"Striated duct cell: basal infoldings packed with mitochondria to reabsorb sodium","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-138-1","s":"Who am I? (two high-power micrographs)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left: exocrine pancreas – pale centroacinar cells in the center of darkly stained serous acini; right: parotid – striated duct (poorly staining basal infoldings full of mitochondria to absorb Na+)","a":null,"why":"<b>Left = exocrine pancreas</b>: small pale <b>centroacinar cells</b> sit in the center of dark serous acini. They are the start of the intercalated duct pushed into the acinus, and only the pancreas has them.<br><b>Right = serous salivary gland (parotid)</b>: a <b>striated duct</b> of tall columnar cells with pale basal striations (folded membrane packed with mitochondria to reabsorb sodium).<br>Mix-up: pancreas has islets and centroacinar cells; salivary glands have striated ducts and myoepithelial cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1221-138.jpg","in":"Two H&E panels: (left) granular serous acini, one with small pale nuclei at its centre; (right) a duct of tall columnar cells with radially striated pale basal cytoplasm around a small lumen, surrounded by dark serous acini.","tg":["H16 slide 30 (Compare)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 13 (Striated ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":138,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0754-213-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":37,"label":"Cholecystokinin → acinar zymogens; secretin → duct bicarbonate","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-27.jpg","cap":"Acinar cell with apical zymogen granules; centroacinar cells start the duct","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/G021-32.jpg","cap":"Cholecystokinin drives acinar enzymes; secretin drives duct water and bicarbonate","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F3949-19-1","s":"This cell is triggered to release its secretory granules by CCK from duodenal cells when food enters that region of the stomach. What are in the secretory granule?","o":["A. Vit A","B. Mucin","C. Zymogens","D. Bile"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Zymogens","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Zymogens.</b> CCK (cholecystokinin) from duodenal enteroendocrine cells makes <b>pancreatic acinar cells</b> release their apical <b>zymogen granules</b>: inactive proteases plus amylase, lipase and nucleases. Enteropeptidase activates them in the duodenum.<br>A. Vitamin A: stored by Ito (hepatic stellate) cells of the liver.<br>B. Mucin: made by mucous cells, not serous pancreatic acini.<br>D. Bile: made by hepatocytes; CCK only makes the gallbladder contract to release it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Picture of the cell (pancreatic acinar cell) not needed; stem names the CCK trigger. [Source file deleted; text dump only, picture not available.]","tg":["H16 slide 31 (Functions of Exocrine pancreas)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":19,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F4081-51-1","s":"Which function is carried out by the intercalated ducts of the exocrine pancreas when stimulated by secretin?","o":["A. Secretion of protease, amylase, lipase, and nucleases","B. Activation of zymogens by enteropeptidase in the duodenum","C. Production of trypsin inhibitor for protection","D. Secretion of alkaline fluid to neutralize the gastric content","E. Secretion of enzymes in inactive form"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretion of alkaline fluid to neutralize the gastric content","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Secretin acts on the <b>intercalated ducts</b> (and centroacinar cells), which secrete an <b>alkaline, bicarbonate-rich fluid</b> that neutralizes stomach acid in the duodenum.<br>A. Protease, amylase, lipase, nucleases: made by <b>acinar cells</b> under CCK (cholecystokinin), not by ducts.<br>B. Enteropeptidase activates zymogens in the duodenal lumen; that is not a duct job.<br>C. Trypsin inhibitor is made by acinar cells.<br>E. Inactive enzymes (zymogens) also come from acinar cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 31 (Functions of Exocrine pancreas)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":51,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":37,"label":"Pancreas self-protection: trypsin inhibitor, alkaline ducts","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-32.jpg","cap":"Lecture list: activation only in duodenum, trypsin inhibitor, alkaline duct pH","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/G021-32.jpg","cap":"Protection: inactive zymogens, trypsin inhibitor in granules, high pH in ducts","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F3949-21-1","s":"How can the Pancreas protect itself from autodigestion?","o":["A. Secretion of Trypsin Inhibitor","B. Acidifying pH in the pancreatic duct system","C. Activating the zymogens immediately once released from acinar cells and still in the pancreas","D. Releasing CCK when stimulated"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Secretion of Trypsin Inhibitor","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Trypsin inhibitor.</b> Acinar cells package it with the zymogens, so any trypsin activated too early inside the pancreas is blocked. The lecture's protective factors: activation only in the duodenum, trypsin inhibitor, <b>alkaline</b> duct fluid.<br>B. The ducts are kept <b>alkaline</b> (bicarbonate), not acidic.<br>C. Early activation inside the pancreas causes pancreatitis; zymogens stay inactive until enteropeptidase meets them in the duodenum.<br>D. CCK (cholecystokinin) comes from the duodenum and triggers enzyme release; it does not protect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 32 (Protective factors)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":21,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3952-97-1","s":"What is one way the pancreas protects itself against autodigestion?","o":["A. Trypsin inhibitor secreted by endocrine pancreas to keep trypsinogen inactive.","B. Maintain a low pH in the intercalated ducts to keep the zymogens inactive.","C. HCO3- secretion by the centroacinar and intercalated duct cells to keep the zymogens inactivated.","D. Allowing the activation of zymogens in the pancreas intercalated ducts"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"HCO3- secretion by the centroacinar and intercalated duct cells to keep the zymogens inactivated.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Centroacinar and intercalated duct cells secrete <b>bicarbonate (HCO3-)</b>, keeping the duct system <b>alkaline</b>, which keeps zymogens inactive until the duodenum.<br>A. Trypsin inhibitor is real protection, but it comes from the <b>exocrine acinar cells</b>, not the endocrine islets.<br>B. The ducts are alkaline, not low pH.<br>D. Zymogens must <b>not</b> activate in the ducts; enteropeptidase activates them only in the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 32 (Protective factors)","H16 slide 31 (Functions of Exocrine pancreas)","H16 slide 29 (Pancreatic ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":97,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":37,"label":"Alcohol → acute and chronic pancreatitis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-33.jpg","cap":"Lecture's pancreas clinical correlates: acute and chronic pancreatitis","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-172-1","s":"Does Alcoholism lead to acute or chronic pancreatitis?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Both: alcohol is a major cause of acute pancreatitis (second only to gallstones) and the most common cause of chronic pancreatitis in adults.","a":null,"why":"<b>Both.</b> Alcohol causes <b>acute</b> pancreatitis (second most common cause after gallstones) and, after years of drinking, <b>chronic</b> pancreatitis, where it is the most common cause in adults.<br>How: trypsin is activated too early inside the gland and digests acinar tissue; repeated attacks leave fibrosis, calcifications and too little enzyme output (fatty stools).<br>Mix-up: 'acute' alone is incomplete. For chronic pancreatitis with calcifications, think alcohol first.","ex":"","fl":["Incomplete/incorrect as an either-or answer: alcohol causes BOTH. It is the second most common cause of acute pancreatitis (after gallstones) and the most common cause of chronic pancreatitis in adults. Correct answer: both (chronic pancreatitis is the classic long-term result of alcoholism).","The source answered only 'acute'; heavy alcohol use causes both, and it is the most common cause of chronic pancreatitis."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 33 (Clinical implications)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":172,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":38,"label":"Dual blood supply: portal vein + hepatic artery","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-40.jpg","cap":"Portal vein nutrient-rich, oxygen-poor, 75% of flow; hepatic artery oxygen-rich, 25%","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 40"},{"src":"sl/G021-41.jpg","cap":"Portal venule and hepatic arteriole blood mixing in sinusoids, flowing to central vein","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 41"},{"src":"sl/G020-39.jpg","cap":"Liver architecture: triad branches feed sinusoids that drain to the central vein","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-55-1","s":"Which of the following describes the dual blood supply of the liver?","o":["A. Hepatic vein and hepatic artery","B. Bile ducts and portal vein","C. Portal artery and hepatic vein","D. Hepatic artery and portal vein","E. Bile ducts and hepatic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic artery and portal vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Hepatic artery and portal vein.</b> The <b>portal vein</b> brings about three-quarters of the liver's blood: nutrient-rich but oxygen-poor, from the gut and spleen. The <b>hepatic artery</b> brings the other quarter: oxygen-rich.<br>A. The hepatic vein drains blood out of the liver to the inferior vena cava.<br>B. Bile ducts carry bile out; they are not a blood supply.<br>C. There is no 'portal artery', and the hepatic vein is outflow.<br>E. Bile ducts again are not blood vessels.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 40 (Dual blood supply of liver)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":55,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3980-19-1"},{"id":"F3980-19-1","s":"A liver histology slide shows sinusoids receiving blood from two distinct sources that mix before flowing toward the central vein. The slide also demonstrates a portal triad containing a branch of the portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct. Which statement best describes the dual blood supply shown in this tissue?","o":["A. The hepatic artery delivers nutrient-rich blood that mixes with oxygen-rich blood from the portal vein in the sinusoids","B. The portal vein supplies oxygenated blood that drains into sinusoids independently of the hepatic artery","C. Blood from the hepatic artery and portal vein mixes in the sinusoids to provide hepatocytes with oxygen and nutrients simultaneously","D. The hepatic artery drains into the central vein first, followed by portal venous flow entering the sinusoids afterward"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Blood from the hepatic artery and portal vein mixes in the sinusoids to provide hepatocytes with oxygen and nutrients simultaneously","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Portal venule and hepatic arteriole both empty into the <b>sinusoids</b>, where the blood <b>mixes</b>, so each hepatocyte gets oxygen and absorbed nutrients at once; the mix then drains to the central vein.<br>A. Reversed: the <b>portal vein</b> brings nutrient-rich, oxygen-poor blood; the <b>hepatic artery</b> brings oxygen-rich blood.<br>B. Portal blood is oxygen-poor, and it mixes with arterial blood rather than flowing separately.<br>D. Neither vessel drains straight into the central vein; both pass through the sinusoids first.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 40 (Dual blood supply of liver)","H16 slide 47 (FLOW OF BLOOD AND BILE)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":19,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F1221-135-1","s":"Portal triads are composed of three major tubes. Branches of the hepatic artery carry oxygenated blood to the hepatocytes, while branches of the portal vein carry blood with nutrients from the small intestine. The bile duct carries bile products away from the hepatocytes, to the larger ducts and gall bladder. Given that the portal vein carries mostly deoxygenated blood, what do the relative sizes of the portal vein and hepatic artery suggest about oxygen levels in the liver?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The portal vein is larger, suggesting that the blood in the liver is mostly poorly oxygenated. The blood in this vein has already been through the GI tract. The blood in the smaller hepatic artery is better oxygenated.","a":null,"why":"<b>The portal vein is the larger vessel, so most liver blood is oxygen-poor.</b> Lecture: portal vein = <b>nutrient-rich, oxygen-poor, about three-quarters</b> of hepatic blood flow; hepatic artery = <b>oxygen-rich, about one-quarter</b>.<br>The two mix in the sinusoids, and oxygen falls steadily from the portal triad to the central vein. That gradient is why hepatocytes by the central vein (zone 3) suffer first in hypoxia.<br>Mix-up: the portal vein brings nutrients, not oxygen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 40 (Dual blood supply of liver)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":135,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":38,"label":"Portal triad: portal vein, hepatic artery, bile duct","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-43.jpg","cap":"Labelled: central vein versus portal triad (portal venule, bile ductule, hepatic artery)","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 43"},{"src":"sl/G020-39.jpg","cap":"Diagram: triad branches at lobule corners; central vein in the center","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-97-1","s":"Which three components make up the portal triad?","o":["A. Hepatic vein. Portal vein and hepatic artery","B. Central vein, hepatic artery, bile duct","C. Hepatic artery, bile duct and portal vein","D. Central vein. Portal vein, hepatic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic artery, bile duct and portal vein","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hepatic artery, bile duct and portal vein.</b> These three branches run together in the connective tissue at each corner of the classic lobule.<br>A. The hepatic vein carries blood out of the liver; it is not in the triad.<br>B. The central vein drains the lobule's center; it is not in the triad, and the portal vein is missing.<br>D. Central vein again, and it leaves out the bile duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"H&E of a portal area with arrows on its vessels and duct (not needed to answer).","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":97,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":244,"f":"F0755"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":43,"f":"F0744"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F4081-57-1","s":"Which of the following is NOT included in the portal triad?","o":["A. Portal vein","B. Hepatic artery","C. Bile ductule","D. Central vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Central vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Central vein.</b> It sits in the <b>center</b> of the classic lobule and drains the sinusoids toward the hepatic veins; it is not in the triad.<br>A. Portal vein (venule): triad member; large, thin-walled, nutrient-rich blood.<br>B. Hepatic artery (arteriole): triad member; small, thick-walled, oxygen-rich blood.<br>C. Bile ductule: triad member; cuboidal epithelium carrying bile toward the gallbladder.<br>The triad sits at the lobule's corners.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":57,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"harder","like":"F0753-97-1"},{"id":"F0722-175-1","s":"What are the components of the portal triad? Secondary Q: which structure in the portal triad is oxygen rich?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Bile duct, portal vein, hepatic artery; the hepatic artery is the oxygen-rich one","a":null,"why":"<b>Portal triad = branch of the portal vein + branch of the hepatic artery + bile ductule</b>, held in connective tissue at the lobule corners (with lymphatics and nerves).<br><b>Oxygen-rich member: the hepatic arteriole.</b> The portal venule carries nutrient-rich but oxygen-poor blood from the gut; the bile ductule carries bile, not blood.<br>Mix-up: the central vein is not a triad member; it drains the lobule's center.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Labeled liver lobules (central vein, portal triad with portal venule, bile ductule, hepatic artery).","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 40 (Dual blood supply of liver)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":175,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0753-97-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":39,"label":"Limiting plate rings the portal triad","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-45.jpg","cap":"Limiting plate: hepatocytes immediately surrounding the portal triad, smooth and uniform","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/G020-53.jpg","cap":"Blue outline: the wall of hepatocytes ringing the portal canal","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 53"}],"q":[{"id":"F3949-25-1","s":"What is the term for the hepatocytes immediately surrounding the portal triad?","o":["A. Kupffer cells","B. Limiting plate","C. Langerhan cells","D. Sinusoids"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Limiting plate","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Limiting plate</b>: the ring of hepatocytes immediately around the portal triad, separating the portal connective tissue from the lobule (lecture: 'smooth and uniform').<br>A. Kupffer cells: macrophages inside the sinusoids.<br>C. Langerhans: islets of Langerhans are pancreatic endocrine units; Langerhans cells are skin dendritic cells.<br>D. Sinusoids: blood channels between hepatocyte plates, not a layer of hepatocytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":25,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":39,"label":"Classic lobule: central vein center, triads at corners","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-44.jpg","cap":"Pig lobule: central vein centered; venule, arteriole, ductule at a corner","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 44"},{"src":"sl/G021-36.jpg","cap":"Trichrome pig liver: blue septa outline lobules, triads where septa meet","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 36"},{"src":"sl/G020-48.jpg","cap":"Hepatocytes: large polygonal, often binucleated cells arranged in plates","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 48"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-22-1","s":"Which structures are being shown below?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left: hepatocytes (arrows); right: a (classic hepatic) lobule (hexagon outlined around a central vein)","a":null,"why":"<b>Left: hepatocytes</b>: large polygonal cells with round central nuclei (often two) and pink cytoplasm, lined up in plates between pale sinusoids.<br><b>Right: classic hepatic lobule</b>: a roughly hexagonal unit with a <b>central vein</b> in the middle, hepatocyte plates radiating from it, and portal triads at the corners.<br>Mix-up: human lobule borders are faint (little connective tissue), so you judge the hexagon from the central vein and the surrounding triads.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-22.jpg","in":"Left: high-power liver with three arrows on large polygonal cells with round central nuclei in plates. Right: low-power liver with a black hexagon drawn around a central vein.","tg":["H16 slide 42 (Hepatic lobule)","H16 slide 48 (HEPATOCYTES)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":22,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0744-46-1"},{"id":"F0744-46-1","s":"Identify the portal triads and central veins in this pig liver. How is a classic hepatic lobule identified?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Central vein = the single vessel in the middle of each polygon; portal triads = the vessel/duct groups at the corners where the blue septa meet. A classic lobule is a hexagon with a central vein at its center and portal triads at its corners (in pig it is outlined by connective tissue; in human the limits are judged from plates radiating from the central vein toward the triads)","a":null,"why":"<b>Central vein</b>: the lone open vessel in the <b>center</b> of each polygon, with hepatocyte plates radiating toward it.<br><b>Portal triads</b>: a venule, an arteriole and a bile ductule grouped in the blue connective tissue at the <b>corners</b> where septa meet.<br><b>Classic lobule</b>: the hexagon drained by one central vein, triads at its corners.<br>Pig vs human: in pig, connective tissue outlines every lobule; in human it is minimal, so you judge the lobule from the central vein and the triads.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0744-46.jpg","in":"Pig liver (trichrome): polygonal lobules outlined by blue connective tissue, a white central vein in each, and portal triads at the angles.","tg":["H16 slide 42 (Hepatic lobule)","H16 slide 41 (LIVER)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":46,"f":"F0744"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":39,"label":"Portal triad micrograph: venule, arteriole, ductule","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-45.jpg","cap":"Portal triad: large portal vein branch, small artery, cuboidal bile duct","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/G020-44.jpg","cap":"Venule (V), arteriole (A), ductule (D) at lobule corner; central vein (C) alone","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F0052-200-1","s":"Question 10 Identify the structures labeled as 1, 2 and 3 respectively:","o":["A. Bile ductule, venule, arteriole","B. Bile ductule, arteriole, venule","C. Arteriole, capillary, venule","D. Arteriole, venule, capillary"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile ductule, arteriole, venule","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Bile ductule, arteriole, venule.</b><br>1 = <b>bile ductule</b>: a ring of <b>cuboidal epithelial cells</b> around a small lumen.<br>2 = <b>hepatic arteriole</b>: the small vessel with a thick wall and narrow lumen.<br>3 = <b>portal venule</b>: the largest, thin-walled lumen holding red cells.<br>A. Swaps the arteriole and the venule.<br>C and D. No capillary is labelled; capillaries are not part of the triad (the liver's capillary-like channels are the sinusoids inside the lobule).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0052-200.jpg","in":"H&E portal area: #1 a small duct lined by cuboidal cells, #2 a small thick-walled vessel, #3 a large thin-walled vessel containing red cells; curved arrow on the limiting plate.","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":200,"f":"F0052"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":11,"f":"F3959"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3980-24-1","s":"[Label the structures] 1 ______ 2 ______ 3 ______","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"1 = Portal vein (branch); 2 = Hepatic artery (branch); 3 = Bile duct (ductule) ('Simone Biles Duct')","a":null,"why":"<b>1 = portal venule</b>: the <b>largest</b> lumen with a <b>thin wall</b> (branch of the portal vein).<br><b>2 = hepatic arteriole</b>: small, round, <b>thick muscular wall</b>, narrow lumen (branch of the hepatic artery).<br><b>3 = bile ductule</b>: a ring of <b>cuboidal epithelial cells</b> (cholangiocytes) around a small lumen.<br>Mix-up: the big vessel in a triad is the portal venule, not the central vein; the central vein sits alone in the lobule's center, with no artery or duct beside it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3980-24.jpg","in":"Portal tract: 1 = large thin-walled vessel with wide lumen; 2 = small round vessel with thick muscular wall; 3 = small duct lined by cuboidal epithelium.","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":24,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0052-200-1"},{"id":"F4081-69-1","s":"Identify the following: What organ is this? Label A, Label B, Label C, Label D","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Liver; A = portal venule; B = sinusoid; C = bile ductule; D = hepatic arteriole","a":null,"why":"<b>Liver</b>: plates of large polygonal hepatocytes with sinusoids between them.<br><b>A = portal venule</b>: the large thin-walled vessel with a ductule and an arteriole beside it, so it belongs to a portal triad (not a central vein).<br><b>B = sinusoid</b>: a thin channel between hepatocyte plates, with red cells inside.<br><b>C = bile ductule</b>: a ring of cuboidal cells.<br><b>D = hepatic arteriole</b>: a small round vessel with a thicker wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F4081-69.jpg","in":"Liver H&E: A = large thin-walled vessel in a portal area; B = bracketed channel between hepatocyte plates; C = small duct of cuboidal cells; D = small round vessel next to it.","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":69,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0052-200-1"},{"id":"F0754-221-1","s":"Identify: A: B: C: D: Orange box:","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A: Bile Duct; B: Hepatic Artery; C: Bile Duct; D: Portal Vein; Orange box: Central Vein","a":null,"why":"Portal area at the bottom, central vein at the top (Junqueira figure 16-12b).<br><b>A and C = bile ductules</b>: tubes lined by cuboidal cells.<br><b>B = hepatic arteriole</b>: small round vessel with its own wall.<br><b>D = portal venule</b> (labelled V): the largest, thin-walled space in the portal connective tissue.<br><b>Orange box = central vein</b>: a lone vessel in the middle of the hepatocyte plates, far from the triad, with no artery or duct beside it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-221.jpg","in":"Low-power H&E of liver: portal area at bottom with structures A, B, C (small vessels/ducts) and D (large thin-walled vein, V); an orange box sits on a vessel at the top of the field, away from the portal area.","tg":["H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":221,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0052-200-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":39,"label":"Liver stains: sinusoids, central vein, portal triad","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-46.jpg","cap":"Same four panels: sinusoids, reticulin stain, central vein, portal triad","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 46"},{"src":"sl/G020-43.jpg","cap":"Low power: central vein apart from the triad of venule, ductule, artery","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 43"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-178-1","s":"MATCH (answer can be mentioned more than once or not at all): central vein; portal triad; sinusoids; kupffer cells [to images A-D]","o":["A. central vein","B. portal triad","C. sinusoids","D. kupffer cells"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A and B = sinusoids; C = central vein; D = portal triad (Kupffer cells not used)","a":null,"why":"<b>a = sinusoids</b>: pale channels between hepatocyte plates (H&amp;E, hematoxylin and eosin).<br><b>b = sinusoids</b>: silver (reticulin) stain; black reticular fibers outline the sinusoids along the plates.<br><b>c = central vein</b>: one round vessel with plates radiating from it and only a thin rim of connective tissue.<br><b>d = portal triad</b>: connective tissue holding a big thin-walled portal venule, a small thick-walled hepatic arteriole and a cuboidal bile ductule.<br>Kupffer cells need ink or special stains; not shown.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-178.jpg","in":"A: H&E liver plates with sinusoids; B: reticulin (silver) stain outlining sinusoids; C: trichrome-like stain around a central vein; D: portal area with PV, HA, BD.","tg":["H16 slide 42 (Hepatic lobule)","H16 slide 43 (LIVER - HISTOLOGY)","H16 slide 41 (LIVER)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":178,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":40,"label":"Hepatocyte polarity: apical canalicular, basal sinusoidal","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-48.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte surfaces: sinusoidal (basal) and canalicular (apical) domains, tight junctions","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 48"},{"src":"sl/G021-35.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte diagram: apical bile canaliculus; basolateral side facing perisinusoidal space","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F0744-40-1","s":"Is the hepatocyte a polarized cell? Identify the perisinusoidal surfaces and the canalicular surface","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Yes. The perisinusoidal (basolateral) surfaces face the sinusoids/space of Disse and carry microvilli; the canalicular (apical) surfaces are the small grooves between neighbouring hepatocytes that form the bile canaliculi (green marks)","a":null,"why":"<b>Yes, the hepatocyte is polarized.</b><br><b>Sinusoidal surface = basal (basolateral) domain</b>: faces the space of Disse and is covered with microvilli; it takes up nutrients and releases plasma proteins into the blood.<br><b>Canalicular surface = apical domain</b>: small grooves on two neighboring hepatocytes, sealed by tight junctions, form the <b>bile canaliculus</b>, where bile is secreted.<br>Mix-up: bile leaves through the apical (canalicular) side, not the sinusoidal side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Diagram of one hepatocyte between two sinusoids: microvillous surfaces toward the sinusoids/space of Disse; green marks at bile canaliculi between adjacent cells.","tg":["H16 slide 48 (HEPATOCYTES)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":40,"f":"F0744"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":45,"f":"F3959"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":40,"label":"Hepatocyte organelles: proteins, detoxification, glycogen","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-49.jpg","cap":"Organelle jobs: glycogen granules, plasma-protein synthesis, detoxification and bile acids","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 49"},{"src":"sl/G021-35.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte functions and stores: bile, plasma proteins, glycogen, lipid droplets","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F0744-41-1","s":"Correlate hepatocyte ultrastructure and function: What organelles are engaged in synthesis of plasma proteins? What organelle processes toxic compounds? Bile and bile acids are secreted into what structure? Glucose is stored as what material? Triglyceride is stored in what form?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Rough ER (with Golgi) makes plasma proteins; smooth ER processes toxic compounds; bile and bile acids go into the bile canaliculus; glucose is stored as glycogen granules; triglyceride is stored as lipid droplets","a":null,"why":"<b>Plasma proteins</b> → <b>rough endoplasmic reticulum</b> (with Golgi), released into the space of Disse.<br><b>Toxic compounds</b> → <b>smooth endoplasmic reticulum</b> (detoxification, bile acid synthesis, bilirubin conjugation).<br><b>Bile and bile acids</b> → secreted into the <b>bile canaliculus</b> at the apical surface.<br><b>Glucose</b> → stored as <b>glycogen granules</b> (PAS, periodic acid-Schiff, positive).<br><b>Triglyceride</b> → stored as <b>lipid droplets</b>.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Labeled hepatocyte EM-style diagram (RER, SER, Golgi, mitochondria, glycogen, lipid, lysosomes, bile canaliculus, tight junctions, perisinusoidal space, fenestrated endothelium).","tg":["H16 slide 49 (Hepatocytes)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":41,"f":"F0744"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F4081-62-1","s":"What function of the liver is demonstrated best with PAS staining?","o":["A. Synthesis of plasma proteins","B. Synthesis of glycogen","C. Detoxification of drugs","D. Synthesis of bile"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Synthesis of glycogen","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Glycogen synthesis.</b> PAS (periodic acid-Schiff) stains <b>carbohydrates</b> magenta, so it lights up the <b>glycogen granules</b> hepatocytes build from glucose taken up from the perisinusoidal space.<br>A. Plasma proteins are made on rough endoplasmic reticulum; PAS does not show them.<br>C. Drug detoxification happens in smooth endoplasmic reticulum.<br>D. Bile synthesis (smooth endoplasmic reticulum, secretion into canaliculi) is not what PAS shows.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 49 (Hepatocytes)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":62,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":40,"label":"Liver makes urea, does not break it down","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-60.jpg","cap":"Lecture list of liver functions, including synthesis of urea","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/G021-35.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte functions: bile, plasma proteins, urea formation, detoxification, storage","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-176-1","s":"Which of the following is not a function of the liver?","o":["A. forms bile","B. stores glycogen and is a buffer for blood glucose","C. breaking down Urea","D. detoxification","E. processing of steroid hormones and vitamin D"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"breaking down Urea","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Breaking down urea</b> is not a liver job. The liver <b>makes</b> urea from ammonia (urea cycle); the kidneys excrete it.<br>A. Forms bile: yes, hepatocytes secrete it into canaliculi.<br>B. Stores glycogen and buffers blood glucose: yes.<br>D. Detoxification: yes, by smooth endoplasmic reticulum enzymes.<br>E. Processes steroid hormones and vitamin D: yes (the liver does the first activation step of vitamin D).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 60 (FUNCTIONS)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":176,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":41,"label":"Space of Disse: blood-hepatocyte exchange","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-53.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: fenestrated endothelium; space of Disse full of hepatocyte microvilli","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 53"},{"src":"sl/G020-48.jpg","cap":"Diagram: fenestrated endothelium, space of Disse between sinusoid and hepatocytes","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 48"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-246-1","s":"What is the function of the Space of Disse","o":["A. Allows for the passage of bile","B. Allows for exchange of materials between hepatocytes and blood","C. It is where basal stem cells reside","D. Allows for the exchange of materials between the blood and the canal of hering"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Allows for exchange of materials between hepatocytes and blood","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Exchange between hepatocytes and blood.</b> The space of Disse lies between the fenestrated sinusoid endothelium and the hepatocytes; hepatocyte microvilli project into it and plasma bathes them.<br>A. Bile runs in bile canaliculi between hepatocytes, never in the space of Disse.<br>C. No basal stem cells there; it holds Ito cells. Liver progenitor cells sit near the canals of Hering.<br>D. The canal of Hering carries bile; it does not exchange with blood.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":246,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F1221-137-1","s":"The sinusoids have a discontinuous endothelium. This means that the sinusoidal endothelial cells do not have a continuous basement membrane; they are separated from the hepatocytes by an area known as the space of Disse. What functional role does the discontinuous endothelium serve in the liver sinusoids?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"It allows maximal contact between the blood and the hepatocytes, which absorb materials from it and secrete materials into it.","a":null,"why":"<b>Maximal exchange between blood and hepatocytes.</b> Sinusoid endothelium is <b>fenestrated and discontinuous</b> with a sparse basal lamina, so plasma passes freely into the <b>space of Disse</b> and bathes hepatocyte microvilli.<br>There hepatocytes take up nutrients, bilirubin and toxins, and release plasma proteins (albumin, clotting factors) and glucose into the blood.<br>Mix-up: blood cells stay in the sinusoid; only plasma enters the space of Disse.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)","H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":137,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0755-246-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":41,"label":"Sinusoids: endothelium + Kupffer cells, drain centrally","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-47.jpg","cap":"Sinusoid from portal field to central vein: endothelium, Kupffer cell, space of Disse","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 47"},{"src":"sl/G024-14.jpg","cap":"Electron micrographs: endothelial cells line the sinusoid; Kupffer cell inside it","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 14"},{"src":"sl/G024-16.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte plates one to two cells thick between sinusoids","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"G024-12-1","s":"Which of the following is true of the structures identified by the 2 thin arrows and the arrowhead?","o":["A. The structures marked by the arrows and the structure marked by the arrowhead are not continuous","B. The lumen marked by the arrows is associated with both endothelial cells and Kupffer cells","C. The structure marked by the arrowhead is within zone 1 of the hepatic acinus","D. The structure marked by the arrowhead contains 1-2 layers of smooth muscle in its wall","E. The structures marked by the arrows are apposed to plates of hepatocytes that are usually 3-4 layers thick"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The lumen marked by the arrows is associated with both endothelial cells and Kupffer cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The arrows mark <b>sinusoids</b>, lined by <b>fenestrated endothelial cells</b> with <b>Kupffer cells</b> (macrophages) in the lining. The arrowhead is the <b>central vein</b>.<br>A. Sinusoids <b>are</b> continuous with the central vein; they drain into it.<br>C. The central vein is in <b>zone 3</b>; zone 1 is next to the portal triad.<br>D. The central vein has only a thin connective tissue wall, no smooth muscle layers.<br>E. Hepatocyte plates are <b>one to two cells</b> thick.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/G024-12.jpg","in":"Liver H&E: arrowhead in a thin-walled central vein; two thin arrows on sinusoids radiating between hepatocyte plates toward it.","tg":["H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)","H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)","H16 slide 42 (Hepatic lobule)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":12,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":12,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":16,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":12,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":13,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":41,"label":"Kupffer in sinusoid; Ito in space of Disse","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-50.jpg","cap":"Diagram: Kupffer cell in the sinusoid; stellate cell in the space of Disse","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 50"},{"src":"sl/G020-52.jpg","cap":"Ito cell (HS) with lipid droplets in the perisinusoidal space (PS)","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-182-1","s":"Where do you find Kupffer cells?","o":["A. Inside the perisinusoidal space","B. In the hepatic artery","C. In the bile duct","D. In the sinusoidal space"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"In the sinusoidal space","a":"D","why":"<b>D. In the sinusoidal space.</b> Kupffer cells are macrophages lying <b>within the sinusoid</b>, part of its lining, where they meet gut-derived blood (and injected India ink).<br>A. The perisinusoidal space (space of Disse) holds Ito cells and hepatocyte microvilli.<br>B. The hepatic artery carries oxygen-rich blood into the triad; macrophages don't live in it.<br>C. Bile ducts are lined by cholangiocytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)","H16 slide 50 (OTHER CELLS)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":182,"f":"F1221"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":54,"f":"F0744"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":16,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F1221-182-2","s":"Where do you find Hepatic Stellate cells?","o":["A. Inside the perisinusoidal space","B. In the hepatic artery","C. In the bile duct","D. In the sinusoidal space"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Inside the perisinusoidal space","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Inside the perisinusoidal space</b> (space of Disse), between the sinusoid endothelium and the hepatocytes. Hepatic stellate (Ito) cells store vitamin A and fat and cause fibrosis in cirrhosis.<br>B. The hepatic artery is a portal triad vessel.<br>C. Bile ducts are lined by cholangiocytes.<br>D. The sinusoid itself holds Kupffer cells (macrophages), not stellate cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":182,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":41,"label":"Interstitium: matrix, fluid, fibroblasts, not endothelium","slides":[{"src":"sl/F3980-29.jpg","cap":"Interstitium: extracellular matrix and fluid between capillaries, lymphatics and cells","where":"2025 TA review, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"F3980-27-1","s":"Which of the following is NOT considered part of the interstitium?","o":["A. Ground substance composed of glycosaminoglycans and proteoglycans","B. Capillary endothelial cells forming the microvascular barrier","C. Fibroblasts producing extracellular matrix components","D. Collagen fibers forming the structural scaffold between cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Capillary endothelial cells forming the microvascular barrier","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Capillary endothelial cells</b> line the vessel and form the barrier around the blood; they are not part of the space outside it. The <b>interstitium</b> is the tissue space between cells and vessels: extracellular matrix (ground substance plus fibers), interstitial fluid and resident cells.<br>A. Ground substance of glycosaminoglycans and proteoglycans: part of it; its gel slows fluid flow.<br>C. Fibroblasts: part of it; they make the matrix.<br>D. Collagen fibers: part of it; the scaffold.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"TA review Exam 7 GI & lymphatics: GI part only","sl":27,"f":"F3980"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":42,"label":"Kupffer cells: sinusoidal macrophages, antigen-presenting","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-51.jpg","cap":"India ink: black Kupffer cells (K) along sinusoids; macrophage functions listed","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 51"},{"src":"sl/G024-14.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: Kupffer cell (macrophage) inside the sinusoid lumen","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-101-1","s":"Which of these cells function as macrophages in the liver?","o":["A. Kupffer cells","B. Ito cells","C. Stellate cells","D. Langerhans cells"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Kupffer cells","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Kupffer cells</b>: the liver's resident macrophages, lying within the sinusoid lining; they eat aged red blood cells, bacteria and debris and present antigens.<br>B and C. Ito cells = hepatic stellate cells (one cell, two names): fat- and vitamin A-storing cells in the space of Disse that cause fibrosis. Kupffer cells are 'stellate macrophages', but plain 'stellate cells' means Ito cells.<br>D. Langerhans cells are dendritic antigen-presenting cells of the skin epidermis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":101,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":245,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F4081-64-1"},{"id":"F4081-64-1","s":"Which statement accurately describes the residential macrophages of the liver?","o":["A. Found within the sinusoids, present antigens, dispose of old RBCs","B. Found within the space of Disse and secrete albumin and insulin","C. Specialized star-shaped hepatocytes, degrade vitamin A and bile","D. Compose the walls of the capillaries, have flattened nuclei, filter blood"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Found within the sinusoids, present antigens, dispose of old RBCs","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Kupffer cells are <b>stellate macrophages inside the sinusoid lining</b>; they present antigens and phagocytose aged red blood cells, bacteria and debris.<br>B. The space of Disse holds Ito cells, not Kupffer cells; albumin comes from hepatocytes and insulin from pancreatic beta cells.<br>C. Star-shaped cells that store vitamin A are Ito (hepatic stellate) cells, not hepatocytes, and they store it rather than degrade it.<br>D. Flattened cells forming the sinusoid wall are endothelial cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":64,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0754-225-1","s":"What is the name and function of the cell highlighted below?","o":["A. Ito Cell, secrete vitamin A","B. Kupffer Cell, macrophage","C. Hepatocyte, contains glycogen granules","D. Cholangiocytes, epithelium"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Kupffer Cell, macrophage","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Kupffer cell, macrophage.</b> On this electron micrograph the cell lies <b>inside the sinusoid lumen</b>, against the hepatocytes, which is where Kupffer cells live.<br>A. An Ito cell would sit <b>outside</b> the endothelium in the perisinusoidal space, filled with lipid droplets.<br>C. The hepatocyte is the large cell with the round nucleus, labelled separately.<br>D. Cholangiocytes line bile ducts, not sinusoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-225.jpg","in":"TEM of rat liver: a cell lying inside the liver sinusoid lumen next to a hepatocyte nucleus; its label is covered by an orange box (perisinusoidal space and lipid droplet also labelled).","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":225,"f":"F0754"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":227,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0722-173-1","s":"Name this cell and its function. Also, found where?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Kupffer cells: stellate macrophages of the liver that act as antigen-presenting cells and phagocytose old RBCs, bacteria and debris; located within the sinusoids","a":null,"why":"<b>Kupffer cells</b>, shown <b>black</b> because they swallowed injected India ink particles.<br><b>Function</b>: resident liver macrophages; they phagocytose aged red blood cells, bacteria and debris, and act as antigen-presenting cells.<br><b>Location</b>: <b>within the sinusoids</b>, part of the sinusoid lining between hepatocyte plates.<br>Mix-up: Ito cells (hepatic stellate cells) sit outside the endothelium in the space of Disse and store vitamin A and fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-173.jpg","in":"Liver from an ink-injected animal: black-stained cells (K) along the sinusoids between hepatocyte plates.","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":173,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3952-99-1","s":"What are the cells stained with india ink, and what is their function?","o":["A. Kupffer cells, antigen presenting cell","B. Hepatocyte, secrete elastin","C. Kupffer cell, regeneration capacity","D. Ito cells, antigen presenting cell"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Kupffer cells, antigen presenting cell","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Kupffer cells, antigen-presenting cells.</b> Liver macrophages phagocytose injected India ink, so the Kupffer cells in the sinusoids turn black.<br>B. Hepatocytes don't take up ink, and they don't secrete elastin.<br>C. Right cell, wrong job: regeneration comes from hepatocytes dividing and from progenitor cells near the canals of Hering.<br>D. Ito cells (hepatic stellate cells) store vitamin A and fat and make matrix; they are not the ink-eating antigen-presenting cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Liver section after India-ink injection: black ink-laden cells (K) lining the sinusoids between hepatocyte plates; a grey box marks one. The stem's 'India ink' already identifies phagocytic Kupffer cells.","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":99,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0722-173-1"},{"id":"F3949-27-1","s":"Identify the cells stained black, what is their function?","o":["A. Hepatocytes, detox the blood that flows through","B. Kupffer cells, secrete Vit A","C. Hepatic stellate cells, secrete elastin","D. Kupffer cells serve as APC in the sinusoids"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Kupffer cells serve as APC in the sinusoids","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Kupffer cells, antigen-presenting cells in the sinusoids.</b> Black cells in a liver section are macrophages that ate injected India ink.<br>A. Hepatocytes detoxify, but they don't take up ink; they are the large polygonal cells in plates.<br>B. Right cell, wrong job: vitamin A belongs to Ito (hepatic stellate) cells.<br>C. Hepatic stellate cells sit in the space of Disse and make collagen-rich matrix after injury, not elastin, and they don't stain black.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Liver with black (ink-laden) cells along the sinusoids, per key and notes. [Source file deleted; text dump only, picture not available.]","tg":["H16 slide 51 (Kupffer cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":27,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0722-173-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":43,"label":"Ito cells: vitamin A, fat, fibrosis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-52.jpg","cap":"Ito cell (HS) with lipid droplets in the space of Disse; functions listed","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 52"},{"src":"sl/G024-7.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: hepatic stellate cell in the perisinusoidal space of Disse","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/G024-4.jpg","cap":"Trichrome: spider-web pericellular fibrosis laid down by activated stellate cells","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"F3952-101-1","s":"What perisinusoidal liver cells are important for synthesis of extracellular matrix components and storage of fat-soluble vitamins?","o":["A. Hepatocytes","B. Hepatic stellate, Ito cells","C. Kupffer cells","D. Canal of Herring"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic stellate, Ito cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hepatic stellate (Ito) cells</b>: perisinusoidal cells in the space of Disse that store <b>fat and vitamin A</b> in lipid droplets and make <b>extracellular matrix</b> (fibrosis after injury).<br>A. Hepatocytes form the plates; they are not the perisinusoidal cells.<br>C. Kupffer cells are macrophages inside the sinusoid.<br>D. The canal of Hering is a small bile channel lined by cholangiocytes, linking canaliculi to ductules; it is not a cell.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":101,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F0722-181-1","s":"Identify. FUNCTION??","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Ito cells (hepatic stellate cells, perisinusoidal lipocytes) in the space of Disse: store fat and vitamin A; secrete matrix after injury, leading to fibrosis","a":null,"why":"<b>Ito cell (hepatic stellate cell, perisinusoidal lipocyte)</b>, labelled HS: a cell full of clear <b>lipid droplets</b> in the <b>perisinusoidal space (space of Disse)</b>, between the endothelium (E) and the hepatocytes (H).<br><b>Function</b>: stores fat and vitamin A; after injury it secretes extracellular matrix (collagen), causing <b>fibrosis</b>.<br>Mix-up: Kupffer cells (K) sit inside the sinusoid (S) itself and are macrophages.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-181.jpg","in":"Plastic section of liver: a cell with large clear lipid droplets (HS) in the perisinusoidal space (PS) between hepatocytes (H) and sinusoid (S); Kupffer cells (K) and endothelium (E) labeled.","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":181,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3949-37-1","s":"A 60 year old patient comes to the office for evaluation of jaundice. Medical history is significant for uncontrolled type 2 DM and morbid obesity. There is no family history of liver disease. Mild scleral icterus is present. The abdomen is nontender and nondistended. Lab revealed elevated transaminases. A liver biopsy is obtained and trichrome staining shows the following. Which of the following cells is directly responsible for the findings indicated by the arrow?","o":["A. Cholangiocytes","B. Hepatocytes","C. Ito Cells","D. Sinusoidal endothelial cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ito Cells","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ito cells.</b> Trichrome stains collagen; the arrowed scar in fatty liver disease (obesity, diabetes) is laid down by activated <b>Ito (hepatic stellate) cells</b> in the space of Disse, which secrete matrix after injury.<br>A. Cholangiocytes line bile ducts.<br>B. Hepatocytes are the injured cells (fat, inflammation); they don't make the scar.<br>D. Sinusoidal endothelial cells are fenestrated cells that let plasma reach hepatocytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Trichrome-stained liver with an arrow on fibrous (collagen) bands, per the answer slide (cirrhosis). [Source file deleted; text dump only, picture not available.]","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 61 (Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":37,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3952-101-1"},{"id":"G024-5-1","s":"[Case context, slides 2-4: 45-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome and central obesity; fatigue, dull RUQ pain; transaminases twice the upper limit of normal; ultrasound shows fatty liver (NAFLD); biopsy shows steatosis, inflammation and 'spider web like' pericellular fibrosis on trichrome = NASH.] An activated hepatic stellate cell with a myofibroblast-like phenotype has been implicated as a primary effector cell in hepatic fibrogenesis. Given this information and the \"spider web like\" histologic appearance of fibrosis in this patient's biopsy, The most likely location of this early fibrosis is:","o":["A. Portal triads","B. Canals of Hering","C. Perisinusoidal space of Disse","D. Hepatocyte cytoplasm"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Perisinusoidal space of Disse","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Perisinusoidal space of Disse.</b> Activated <b>hepatic stellate (Ito) cells</b> live there; after injury they become myofibroblast-like and lay collagen around single hepatocytes, giving the 'spider web' (pericellular) pattern.<br>A. Portal triad fibrosis is a portal pattern (chronic viral hepatitis, biliary disease), not pericellular.<br>B. Canals of Hering are small bile channels near the triad, lined by cholangiocytes.<br>D. Matrix is laid down outside cells, not in hepatocyte cytoplasm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 53 (SPACES)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":5,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":5,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":5,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":5,"f":"G025"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":43,"label":"Fatty liver: ultrasound finds fat, biopsy confirms steatohepatitis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G024-4.jpg","cap":"Steatohepatitis biopsy: fat droplets, inflammatory clusters, spider-web trichrome fibrosis","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"G024-2-1","s":"A 45 year-old man visits his physician as routine follow-up for management of his type 2 diabetes mellitus and metabolic syndrome. He noted he had been feeling more tired than usual with mild dull pain in the right upper quadrant. The physical examination of the abdomen was limited by the patient's abdominal (central) obesity (waist circumference 42 inches). Although the patient's serum transaminase levels had always been within the normal range, at this visit the values were twice the upper limit of normal. Concerned about these new findings, the physician orders an abdominal ultrasound examination. Why?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Abdominal ultrasonography can detect fat in the liver (increased echogenicity), i.e., hepatic steatosis / non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD)","a":null,"why":"<b>To detect fat in the liver.</b> Fatty liver looks <b>brighter (increased echogenicity)</b> on ultrasound. With diabetes, metabolic syndrome, central obesity and newly raised liver enzymes (transaminases), the likely cause is <b>NAFLD</b> (nonalcoholic fatty liver disease): fat in hepatocytes without heavy drinking or another cause.<br>Fat builds up first in <b>zone 3</b> hepatocytes, around the central vein.<br>Next step: ultrasound can't separate simple fat from NASH (nonalcoholic steatohepatitis); that needs a biopsy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)","H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":2,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":2,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":2,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":2,"f":"G025"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G024-3-1"},{"id":"G024-3-1","s":"[Case context, slides 2-4: 45-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome and central obesity; fatigue, dull RUQ pain; transaminases twice the upper limit of normal; ultrasound shows fatty liver (NAFLD); biopsy shows steatosis, inflammation and 'spider web like' pericellular fibrosis on trichrome = NASH.] The term nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) is applied to NAFLD patients who demonstrate steatohepatitic injury which confers an increased risk of advanced fibrotic liver disease and its serious complications (e.g., decompensated cirrhosis, carcinoma). How can the physician determine if his patient with NAFLD has NASH and the associated risk of advanced liver disease?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Liver biopsy (gold standard; ultrasound cannot distinguish NAFL from NASH): steatosis + inflammation + pericellular ('spider web') fibrosis on trichrome = NASH","a":null,"why":"<b>Liver biopsy</b> is the gold standard. Ultrasound sees fat but cannot tell simple fatty liver (NAFL, nonalcoholic fatty liver) from <b>NASH</b> (nonalcoholic steatohepatitis).<br>NASH on biopsy = <b>steatosis</b> (large fat droplets in hepatocytes) + <b>inflammation</b> (clusters of inflammatory cells) + <b>pericellular 'spider web' fibrosis</b> on trichrome stain (collagen stains blue).<br>That fibrosis is laid down by activated Ito (hepatic stellate) cells in the space of Disse.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 52 (Ito cells)","H16 slide 61 (Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":3,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":3,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":3,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":3,"f":"G025"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":43,"label":"Cirrhosis: fibrosis, low albumin, bruising","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-52.jpg","cap":"Ito cells secrete matrix after injury, causing fibrosis","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 52"},{"src":"sl/G020-49.jpg","cap":"Hepatocyte rough endoplasmic reticulum makes plasma proteins such as albumin","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 49"}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-78-1","s":"Which of the following statements is most consistent with a patient with chronic and severe alcohol use disorder?","o":["A. Scarred and fibrotic liver, hypoalbuminemia, easy bruising","B. High blood sugar, constant thirst, weight loss","C. Diarrhea, stunted growth, itchy rash on elbows and knees","D. Auto-asplenia, painful enlarged joints, anemia"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Scarred and fibrotic liver, hypoalbuminemia, easy bruising","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Long, heavy alcohol use leads to <b>cirrhosis</b>: a <b>scarred, fibrotic liver</b> (scar laid down by Ito cells). Fewer working hepatocytes make less <b>albumin</b> (hypoalbuminemia) and fewer <b>clotting factors</b> (easy bruising).<br>B. High blood sugar, thirst, weight loss: diabetes mellitus.<br>C. Diarrhea, poor growth, itchy rash on elbows and knees: celiac disease (dermatitis herpetiformis).<br>D. Autosplenectomy, painful joints, anemia: sickle cell disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 61 (Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":78,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":44,"label":"Bile canaliculi → canals of Hering → ductules","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-54.jpg","cap":"Canaliculi between hepatocytes; canal of Hering cholangiocytes; interlobular ductule","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 54"},{"src":"sl/G021-42.jpg","cap":"Electron micrograph: canaliculus sealed by tight junctions; drawing of canals of Hering","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"F4081-60-1","s":"The structure labeled \"BC\" is a bile canaliculus. What is its function?","o":["A. Collect bile from the adjacent hepatocytes, and bring it to the hepatic vein","B. Direct bile into the space of Disse for filtration","C. Collect bile from the adjacent hepatocytes and form the bile ductules","D. Direct bile from the gallbladder to supply the hepatocytes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Collect bile from the adjacent hepatocytes and form the bile ductules","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Bile canaliculi are tiny channels formed by grooves between neighboring hepatocytes, sealed by tight junctions. They collect bile and carry it toward the portal triad, into the <b>canals of Hering</b> and then the <b>bile ductules</b>.<br>A. The hepatic vein carries blood, not bile.<br>B. Bile never enters the space of Disse; tight junctions keep bile away from blood.<br>D. Backward: bile flows from hepatocytes to the gallbladder, not the other way.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"TEM of hepatocyte with a small lumen between adjacent hepatocytes (BC) circled; not needed (stem names it).","tg":["H16 slide 54 (BILIARY PASSAGE)","H16 slide 47 (FLOW OF BLOOD AND BILE)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":60,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3949-32-1","s":"What are morphological differences between Bile canaliculi and Canal of Herring differ?","o":["A. Bile canaliculi are lined by a simple squamous, and canals of herring are lined by cholangiocytes","B. Bile canaliculi are lined by hepatocytes bound by tight junctions and Canal of Hering are lined by cholangiocytes (cuboidal epithelium specialized for water reabsorption)","C. Bile canaliculi are lined by Hepatocytes and Canal of Hering are lined by a simple squamous epithelium"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile canaliculi are lined by hepatocytes bound by tight junctions and Canal of Hering are lined by cholangiocytes (cuboidal epithelium specialized for water reabsorption)","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Bile canaliculi</b> have no lining cells of their own: they are walled by the membranes of <b>two hepatocytes</b> sealed by tight junctions. The <b>canal of Hering</b> is the first channel lined by <b>cholangiocytes</b> (cuboidal epithelium).<br>A. Canaliculi are not lined by simple squamous cells.<br>C. Canaliculi are hepatocyte-lined, but the canal of Hering is cuboidal, not simple squamous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 54 (BILIARY PASSAGE)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":32,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":44,"label":"Canal of Hering → liver regeneration","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-54.jpg","cap":"Canal of Hering: cuboidal cholangiocytes; contributes to regeneration after injury","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 54"}],"q":[{"id":"F0755-247-1","s":"Which part of the liver contributes to its regeneration following an injury?","o":["A. Interlobular Bile ductule","B. Bile Canaliculi","C. Space of Mall","D. Intralobular bile ductule"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Intralobular bile ductule","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Intralobular bile ductule (canal of Hering)</b>: a cholangiocyte-lined channel holding progenitor cells that can make new hepatocytes and cholangiocytes after injury (lecture: contributes to regeneration).<br>A. Interlobular bile ductule: sits in the portal triad and drains bile.<br>B. Bile canaliculi: grooves between hepatocytes with no lining cells of their own.<br>C. Space of Mall: the periportal space between the triad's connective tissue and the limiting plate; drains lymph.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 54 (BILIARY PASSAGE)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":247,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":44,"label":"Blood to central vein; bile out to triad","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-47.jpg","cap":"Blood flows portal field to central vein; bile flows back toward ductule","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 47"},{"src":"sl/G020-56.jpg","cap":"Classic lobule, portal lobule and acinus compared: shapes, centers, roles","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 56"},{"src":"sl/G021-41.jpg","cap":"Blood and bile flow in opposite directions along the hepatocyte plate","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"G024-26-1","s":"Which of the following statements is correct regarding the structure of liver?","o":["A. Terminal hepatic venule (central vein) is located at the center of the portal lobule","B. The zone 1 is more susceptible to hypoxic injury","C. Hepatic arteriole carries blood rich in nutrients absorbed from GI tract","D. The flow of bile is always directed towards the center of classic hepatic lobule","E. Hepatic sinusoids drain into the central vein"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic sinusoids drain into the central vein","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Sinusoids drain into the central vein</b>: blood runs from the portal triad at the lobule edge, through the sinusoids, into the central vein.<br>A. The central vein is the center of the <b>classic</b> lobule; the portal lobule is centered on a portal triad.<br>B. <b>Zone 3</b> (by the central vein) is the hypoxia-prone zone; zone 1 gets the most oxygen.<br>C. The hepatic arteriole brings oxygen; the <b>portal venule</b> brings absorbed nutrients.<br>D. Bile flows <b>outward</b> to the portal triad, opposite to blood.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 56 (Classical lobule - Anatomical)","H16 slide 47 (FLOW OF BLOOD AND BILE)","H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":26,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":26,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":34,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":26,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":28,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":45,"label":"Portal lobule: bile-drainage triangle","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-56.jpg","cap":"Portal lobule: green triangle of three central veins around one triad","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 56"},{"src":"sl/G020-58.jpg","cap":"Classic lobule, portal lobule and hepatic acinus outlined on one map","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 58"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-218-1","s":"What type of hepatic lobule is depicted below?","o":["A. Hepatic Acinus","B. Portal lobule","C. Classic lobule"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Portal lobule","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Portal lobule</b>: a <b>triangle</b> with a <b>portal triad in its center</b> and the central veins of three classic lobules at its corners. It is the area whose bile drains into one bile duct (exocrine view).<br>A. Hepatic acinus: a <b>diamond</b> between two central veins, centered on the vessels running between two triads; it shows blood supply and oxygen zones.<br>C. Classic lobule: a <b>hexagon</b> centered on a central vein, triads at the corners.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0754-218.jpg","in":"Diagram of three adjacent hexagonal classic lobules; a shaded triangle joins their three central veins around a single portal triad at its centre.","tg":["H16 slide 56 (Classical lobule - Anatomical)","H16 slide 55 (Lobules of the liver)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":218,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":45,"label":"Zone 3 least oxygen → hypoxic injury","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-59.jpg","cap":"Acinus zones: zone 1 by triad, zone 3 by central vein, hypoxia-prone","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 59"},{"src":"sl/G024-15.jpg","cap":"Zone 3 circled at the central vein; zone 1 beside the portal tract","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F0753-99-1","s":"Which zone has the poorest oxygen supply?","o":["A. 1","B. 2","C. 3"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"3","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Zone 3</b> (centrilobular), nearest the central vein. Oxygen-rich arterial blood enters at the portal triad and is used up along the sinusoid, so zone 3 gets the <b>poorest oxygen</b>; it does more anaerobic work and detoxification and is first hurt by hypoxia.<br>A. Zone 1 (periportal): next to the triad, richest oxygen; does protein synthesis.<br>B. Zone 2: intermediate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0753-99-1.jpg","in":"H&E of liver with a portal area at lower left and zones 1, 2, 3 numbered toward the central vein (not needed to answer).","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"2022 Histo TA exam review 2: GI part only","sl":99,"f":"F0753"},{"t":"2025 review","n":"Digestive system supplemental review Fall 2025","sl":40,"f":"G021"},{"t":"2024 Middletown lab clicker","n":"lab clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":12,"f":"F3959"},{"t":"2023 faculty review","n":"Digestive system review 2023","sl":47,"f":"F0744"},{"t":"2021 Harlem lab clicker","n":"lab clickers Exam 2: GI part only","sl":202,"f":"F0052"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F3949-35-1","s":"Which zone is highly oxygenated and which zone is least oxygenated?","o":["A. Zone 3 AKA Centrilobular zone","B. Zone 2","C. Zone 1 AKA Periportal zone"],"g":["A","C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Highly oxygenated = Zone 1 (periportal, nearest the portal triad); least oxygenated = Zone 3 (centrilobular)","a":"C","why":"<b>Most oxygen: C, zone 1 (periportal)</b>, next to the portal triad, where the hepatic arteriole delivers fresh oxygen. <b>Least oxygen: A, zone 3 (centrilobular)</b>, by the central vein, last in line along the sinusoid.<br>So zone 1 does oxidative work such as protein synthesis and is hit by viral hepatitis; zone 3 does drug and ammonia detoxification and is hit first by hypoxia.<br>B. Zone 2: intermediate on both counts.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only C (zone 1, most oxygenated), but the stem also asks for the least-oxygenated zone, which is A (zone 3); both are correct."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA weekly","n":"TA review week 11","sl":35,"f":"F3949"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor"},{"id":"F0743-20-1","s":"A 25-year-old man drinks an unknown substance and a few hours later is taken to the ER with acute abdominal pain. The substance induced acute liver damage due to hypoxia (low oxygen supply). Which of the zone's shown below would be the most prone to initially undergo this type of damage?","o":["A. 1","B. 2","C. 3"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"3","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Zone 3</b> (centrilobular), around the central vein. Blood loses oxygen as it moves along the sinusoid from the portal triad, so zone 3 gets the <b>least oxygen</b> and is the <b>first to die in hypoxia</b>.<br>A. Zone 1 (periportal): best oxygen supply; viral hepatitis targets it.<br>B. Zone 2 (intermediate): middle oxygen supply.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Liver H&E with 1, 2, 3 marked from portal tract to central vein (stem asks by number, so the picture shows which number is pericentral).","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":20,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0753-99-1"},{"id":"F1221-167-2","s":"Which portion of the hepatic acinus would be most likely affected by ischemic damage?","o":["A. Zone 1","B. Zone 2","C. Zone 3","D. Danger Zone"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Zone 3","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Zone 3</b> (centrilobular): farthest from the oxygen-rich blood entering at the portal triad, so it is the <b>first to undergo ischemic necrosis</b> (shock, heart failure).<br>A. Zone 1 (periportal): best oxygen; hit by viral hepatitis.<br>B. Zone 2: intermediate oxygen.<br>D. 'Danger zone' is not a hepatic zone.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":167,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0753-99-1"},{"id":"F0755-249-1","s":"Which zone of the portal acinus is most affected by hypoxia? What is zone three prone to? What is zone one affected by? Where is is nearest to?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Zone 3 is most affected by hypoxia; zone 3 is prone to ischemic necrosis, fat accumulation and alcohol/acetaminophen damage; zone 1 is affected by viral hepatitis and is nearest to the portal triad","a":null,"why":"<b>Zone 3 (centrilobular, by the central vein)</b>: least oxygen, so <b>most affected by hypoxia</b>; prone to <b>ischemic necrosis, fat accumulation, and acetaminophen and alcohol damage</b> (it does most drug and ammonia detoxification).<br><b>Zone 1 (periportal)</b>: <b>nearest the portal triad</b>, most oxygen, oxidative metabolism; <b>most affected in viral hepatitis</b>.<br>Memory hook: zone 1 is first to meet incoming blood; zone 3 is last in line for oxygen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"2021 Histology Exam 2 TA review: GI part only","sl":249,"f":"F0755"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0753-99-1"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":45,"label":"Zone 3: acetaminophen, alcohol, fat, toxins","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-59.jpg","cap":"Zone 3 by central vein: detoxification, hypoxia, fat, acetaminophen and alcohol damage","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 59"},{"src":"sl/G024-19.jpg","cap":"Zone III: least oxygen, drug metabolism; vulnerable to ischemic and toxic injury","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 19"}],"q":[{"id":"G024-18-1","s":"A 65 year-old man undergoes an uncomplicated open heart operation for replacement of a stenotic aortic valve. Post-operatively, he is prescribed extra strength acetaminophen (Tylenol) for pain control. Routine measurement of serum transaminase measurements on post-operative day 5 reveal elevations to five times the normal values indicating hepatocellular injury. Which of the acinar zones shown below would be the most likely site of injury?","o":["A. Zone 1","B. Zone 2","C. Zone 3"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Zone 3","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Zone 3</b> (centrilobular, around the central vein). It gets the <b>least oxygen</b> and holds the most drug-metabolizing enzymes, so it is hit by both <b>ischemia</b> (heart surgery, low blood pressure) and <b>acetaminophen</b> (Tylenol) toxicity.<br>A. Zone 1 (periportal): most oxygen; injured first in viral hepatitis.<br>B. Zone 2 (intermediate): not the first site for either problem.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Liver H&E with a portal tract (lower left) and central vein (upper right); numbers 1, 2, 3 placed along the line from portal tract to central vein.","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":18,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":18,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":23,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":18,"f":"G025"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F1221-167-1","s":"Which portion of the hepatic acinus would be most likely affected by ingestion of toxic chemicals?","o":["A. Zone 1","B. Zone 2","C. Zone 3","D. Danger Zone"],"g":["C"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Zone 1","a":"A","why":"<b>C. Zone 3</b> (centrilobular). The lecture ties <b>drug detoxification</b> and <b>acetaminophen and alcohol damage</b> to zone 3: its hepatocytes hold the most drug-metabolizing enzymes (smooth endoplasmic reticulum), which turn many chemicals into toxic products, and it has the least oxygen.<br>A. Zone 1: first to meet incoming blood, but the lecture links it to viral hepatitis (board books list a few direct toxins, such as cocaine, there).<br>B. Zone 2: intermediate.<br>D. 'Danger zone' is not a zone.","ex":"","fl":["Both answers have a basis: First Aid says ingested toxins (e.g. cocaine) reach periportal zone 1 first, but the 2026 lecture (G020:59) ties drug, acetaminophen and alcohol injury to centrilobular zone 3 (most CYP450). On this year's exam, zone 3 is the safer answer for drug/alcohol toxicity.","The source keyed A (zone 1, first to meet ingested substances); the 2026 lecture places drug and toxin injury (acetaminophen, alcohol) in zone 3."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":167,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"G024-18-1"},{"id":"F3952-103-1","s":"Which zone of the hepatic lobule is most affected by cirrhosis?","o":["A. Zone 1","B. Zone 2","C. Zone 3","D. Portal Triad"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Zone 3","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Zone 3</b> (centrilobular). The usual causes of cirrhosis in the lecture, <b>alcohol</b> and <b>fatty liver</b>, injure zone 3 first: it gets the least oxygen and gathers fat first, and the scarring starts around the central vein.<br>A. Zone 1 (periportal): mainly hit by viral hepatitis.<br>B. Zone 2: intermediate; not the first target.<br>D. The portal triad is connective tissue with vessels, not an acinar zone of hepatocytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":103,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":45,"label":"Zone 1 periportal: viral hepatitis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-59.jpg","cap":"Zone 1 periportal, nearest the triad: most affected in viral hepatitis","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 59"},{"src":"sl/G020-56.jpg","cap":"Hepatic acinus: zone I most oxygenated, zone III least","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 56"}],"q":[{"id":"F0754-223-1","s":"A patient presents to the ER with fever, nausea, vomiting and RUQ pain. Serologic testing reveals the most likely diagnosis to be viral hepatitis. Which zone of the hepatic acinus is most likely to be damaged?","o":["A. Intermediate zone","B. Centrilobular zone","C. Periportal zone"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Periportal zone","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Periportal zone (zone 1).</b> It sits next to the portal triad, so it is first to meet incoming blood, with its viruses and the immune cells that attack infected hepatocytes; the lecture lists zone 1 as <b>most affected in viral hepatitis</b>.<br>A. Intermediate zone (zone 2): not a typical first target.<br>B. Centrilobular zone (zone 3): hypoxia, acetaminophen, alcohol and fat injury.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 59 (ZONES OF PORTAL ACINUS)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":223,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":46,"label":"Gallbladder epithelium: simple columnar, absorbs water","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-65.jpg","cap":"Simple columnar cells with microvilli and mitochondria; electron micrograph beside","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 65"},{"src":"sl/G021-44.jpg","cap":"Columnar cells with microvilli and folded basolateral membrane for water uptake","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-153-3","s":"3. The epithelium of the gallbladder is composed of","o":["A. simple columnar epithelium","B. psuedostratified epithelium","C. ciliated columnar epithelium","D. stratified squamous epithelium","E. simple squamous epithelium"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"simple columnar epithelium","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Simple columnar epithelium</b>, with microvilli, on a highly folded mucosa.<br>B. Pseudostratified epithelium lines the airways and epididymis, not the gallbladder.<br>C. Ciliated columnar lines the uterine tube and airways; gallbladder cells have microvilli, not cilia.<br>D. Stratified squamous lines the esophagus and skin.<br>E. Simple squamous lines vessels and body cavities (endothelium, mesothelium).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":153,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3952-107-1"},{"id":"F0722-165-1","s":"What type of epithelium is this? Found where?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli (abundant mitochondria); gallbladder","a":null,"why":"<b>Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli</b>, from the <b>gallbladder</b>. Clues: one layer of tall cells with apical <b>microvilli</b> (MV), many <b>mitochondria</b> and lateral infoldings; the drawing shows highly folded mucosa over smooth muscle with no glands.<br>Why: these cells pump sodium and absorb water to <b>concentrate bile</b>.<br>Mix-up: intestinal enterocytes also have microvilli, but intestinal mucosa has goblet cells, intestinal glands (crypts) and a muscularis mucosae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-165.jpg","in":"TEM of columnar cells with apical microvilli (MV) and many mitochondria, plus a drawing of the folded gallbladder mucosa with the epithelium highlighted.","tg":["H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":165,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F3952-107-1"},{"id":"F3952-107-1","s":"Cholangiocytes of the gallbladder mucosa are made of what tissue type and have what function?","o":["A. Stratified squamous epithelium specialized for bile secretion","B. Simple columnar epithelium specialized for water absorption","C. Simple columnar epithelium specialized for bile secretion","D. Simple cuboidal epithelium specialized for serous secretion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Simple columnar epithelium specialized for water absorption","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Simple columnar epithelium specialized for water absorption.</b> Gallbladder lining cells have microvilli, many mitochondria and lateral infoldings; they pump sodium and absorb water to <b>concentrate bile</b>.<br>A. The gallbladder lining is not stratified squamous.<br>C. Right epithelium, wrong job: hepatocytes secrete bile; the gallbladder only stores and concentrates it.<br>D. Simple cuboidal serous secretion fits gland acini, not the gallbladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)","H16 slide 66 (Function & Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":107,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":46,"label":"Gallbladder wall: no muscularis mucosae or submucosa","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-64.jpg","cap":"Labelled wall: epithelium, lamina propria, smooth muscle; no muscularis mucosae or submucosa","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 64"},{"src":"sl/G021-44.jpg","cap":"Folded mucosa, lamina propria (no muscularis mucosae), muscularis, serosa or adventitia","where":"2025 supplemental review, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-164-1","s":"Does the Gallbladder have a muscularis mucosa or submucosa??","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"No. The gallbladder mucosa is highly folded and it lacks a muscularis mucosa and a submucosa, but has a muscular layer and an adventitia","a":null,"why":"<b>No, neither.</b> The gallbladder wall is only: <b>mucosa</b> (simple columnar epithelium + lamina propria, highly folded) → <b>muscular layer</b> (randomly arranged smooth muscle with elastic fibers) → <b>adventitia</b> (serosa on the free surface).<br>Mix-up: the gut tube has a muscularis mucosae, then a submucosa, then the muscularis externa. Missing those two layers is a key way to call 'gallbladder' on a slide.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":164,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0754-231-1"},{"id":"F3952-105-1","s":"What are the walls of the gallbladder?","o":["A. Lamina propria, muscularis mucosa, muscularis externa,","B. Lamina propria, muscularis propria, external adventitia","C. Lamina propria, muscularis mucosa, muscularis externa","D. Lamina propria, muscularis mucosa, adventitia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lamina propria, muscularis propria, external adventitia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Lamina propria, muscularis (propria), adventitia.</b> The gallbladder wall: folded mucosa (simple columnar epithelium on lamina propria), a layer of randomly arranged smooth muscle, then a thick adventitia (serosa on the free surface). There is <b>no muscularis mucosae and no submucosa</b>.<br>A and C. Both list a muscularis mucosae plus a gut-style muscularis externa, which the gallbladder lacks.<br>D. Also lists a muscularis mucosae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Micrograph of gallbladder wall: folded mucosa with lamina propria (LP), smooth-muscle layer (M) and adventitia (A).","tg":["H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)","H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Histo TA Exam 2 review: GI part only","sl":105,"f":"F3952"}],"tier":2,"kind":"repeat","like":"F0754-231-1"},{"id":"F0754-231-1","s":"A 16 y/o female presents to the urgent care clinic with fatty, foul-smelling stool. She was diagnosed many years ago with Cystic Fibrosis and you are concerned she has a mucus plug blocking her bile duct. Describe the organization of the gallbladder.","o":["A. Simple cuboidal epithelium with cilia","B. Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli, lamina propria, and muscularis mucosa","C. Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium with microvilli","D. Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli, lamina propria, and smooth muscle"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli, lamina propria, and smooth muscle","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli, lamina propria, smooth muscle</b>, then adventitia: that is the gallbladder wall. Microvilli and many mitochondria help it absorb water to concentrate bile.<br>A. Simple cuboidal with cilia: not gallbladder; its lining cells are tall columnar with microvilli.<br>B. Lists a muscularis mucosae, which the gallbladder lacks.<br>C. Nonkeratinized stratified squamous lines the mouth and esophagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)","H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Exam 2 review 2022: GI part only","sl":231,"f":"F0754"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":46,"label":"Gallbladder: folded mucosa, no true villi","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-74.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder: branching mucosal folds that mimic villi, no glands beneath","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 74"},{"src":"sl/G020-65.jpg","cap":"Mucosa folds mimic villi; simple columnar epithelium with microvilli","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 65"},{"src":"sl/G024-31.jpg","cap":"Folded mucosa, lamina propria, random muscle bundles, adventitia against the liver","where":"2025 accessory glands interactive, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"F0722-162-1","s":"Identify this structure. What's its function?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Gallbladder; it stores and concentrates bile and contracts in response to CCK from the duodenum","a":null,"why":"<b>Gallbladder</b>: a <b>highly folded mucosa</b> of tall simple columnar cells; the branching folds mimic villi, but there are <b>no glands, goblet cells or muscularis mucosae</b> beneath them (unlike the intestine).<br><b>Function</b>: <b>stores and concentrates bile</b> by absorbing water; its smooth muscle <b>contracts in response to CCK</b> (cholecystokinin) from the duodenum after a fatty meal, pushing bile out through the cystic duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0722-162.jpg","in":"Low-power pale section of a highly folded mucosa (branching folds that mimic villi) with no glands below.","tg":["H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)","H16 slide 66 (Function & Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Histo TA review Exam 2: GI part only","sl":162,"f":"F0722"}],"tier":1},{"id":"G024-30-1","s":"A 45 year-old man with a history of cholelithiasis presents to the ED with severe right upper quadrant abdominal pain radiating to the right shoulder, nausea and fever. He is taken to the operating room where a cholecystectomy is performed. The resected gallbladder and a representative histologic section are displayed at right. All of the following are true EXCEPT:","o":["A. The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile","B. Inflammation of the gallbladder is usually associated with gallstones","C. The gallbladder mucosa contains villi which increase mucosal surface area","D. The gallbladder is covered partly by serosa and partly adventitia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The gallbladder mucosa contains villi which increase mucosal surface area","a":"C","why":"<b>C is false.</b> The gallbladder has <b>no true villi</b>; its mucosa is thrown into <b>folds that mimic villi</b> when the organ is empty. It concentrates bile by absorbing water, not by absorbing food.<br>A. True: it stores and concentrates bile.<br>B. True: cholecystitis is usually caused by gallstones blocking the cystic duct.<br>D. True: serosa on the free (peritoneal) surface, adventitia where it attaches to the liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Opened gallbladder full of faceted brown gallstones; inflamed, ulcerated gallbladder wall section (cholecystitis). Not needed to answer.","tg":["H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)","H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)","H16 slide 66 (Function & Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Accessory glands interactive Fall 2025","sl":30,"f":"G024"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"W11 Accessory digestive clicker session","sl":30,"f":"F0714"},{"t":"2024 Middletown clicker","n":"lecture clickers week 11 accessory digestive","sl":45,"f":"F3965"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"HC11 accessory digestive clicker Fall 2024","sl":30,"f":"G025"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"CL11 digestive system clickers","sl":32,"f":"F0743"}],"tier":1},{"id":"F4081-76-1","s":"A 43 year old female with a history of sickle cell anemia presents with right upper quadrant pain. She says it's especially worse after eating fatty foods. She undergoes surgery, and the patient sample is pictured below. Which describes the histological features of the affected tissue?","o":["A. Sac-like groups of cells with a granular, basophilic basolateral surface, extensive RER, and centroacinar cells","B. A layer of simple cuboidal epithelium, highly folded lamina propria, a thick layer of smooth muscle, thick adventitia and serosa","C. Stratified squamous epithelium with layers of mucosa, mucus glands, submucosa, skeletal muscle and smooth muscle","D. Mucous cells, chief cells, parietal cells, enteroendocrine cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"A layer of simple cuboidal epithelium, highly folded lamina propria, a thick layer of smooth muscle, thick adventitia and serosa","a":"B","why":"<b>B, the gallbladder.</b> Right upper quadrant pain after fatty meals plus sickle cell disease (pigment stones from hemolysis) means gallstones and cholecystectomy. Gallbladder: <b>highly folded mucosa, no muscularis mucosae or submucosa, smooth muscle layer, adventitia and serosa</b>. Wording slip: its epithelium is simple <b>columnar</b> with microvilli, and the muscle layer is thin.<br>A. Acini with centroacinar cells: pancreas.<br>C. Stratified squamous, skeletal muscle: esophagus.<br>D. Mucous, chief, parietal, enteroendocrine cells: stomach.","ex":"","fl":["B is still the best choice (the only gallbladder option), but its wording is wrong in one point: gallbladder mucosa is lined by simple COLUMNAR epithelium with microvilli (G020:65), not simple cuboidal; its muscle layer is thin and irregularly arranged."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Opened gallbladder filled with dark pigment gallstones (pigment stones fit sickle cell hemolysis).","tg":["H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)","H16 slide 65 (Mucosa)","H16 slide 66 (Function & Clinical correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 sister campus TA review","n":"Histo 2025 GI tract, GI glands, lymphatic: GI part only","sl":76,"f":"F4081"}],"tier":2,"kind":"minor"}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":47,"label":"Pancreas acini vs liver plates and sinusoids","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-26.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic acini with centroacinar cells; diagram of centroacinar cells in the acinus","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/G020-46.jpg","cap":"Liver: hepatocyte plates with sinusoids; central vein with radiating plates","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-199-1","s":"What's all this then!? (identify the three micrographs)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left: serous acini with centroacinar cells in the center of the acinus – exocrine pancreas; middle: central vein surrounded by columns of hepatocytes and sinusoids (liver); right: hepatocytes and sinusoids (liver)","a":null,"why":"Left: <b>exocrine pancreas</b>: serous acini with pale <b>centroacinar cells in their centers</b>. Middle: <b>liver</b>: plates of hepatocytes radiating from a <b>central vein</b>, with sinusoids between them. Right: <b>liver</b> at high power: polygonal <b>hepatocytes</b> separated by narrow <b>sinusoids</b>.<br>Common mix-up: liver shows cell plates and sinusoids, not round glandular acini around a lumen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1221-199.jpg","in":"Three H&E panels: (left) serous acini of granular pyramidal cells with pale small nuclei in the acinar centres; (middle) liver parenchyma radiating from a round empty vein; (right) high-power plates of polygonal cells separated by narrow vascular channels.","tg":["H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 42 (Hepatic lobule)","H16 slide 76 (Central vein)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":199,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1}]},{"l":"HIST-12","u":47,"label":"Four images: triad, hepatocyte, pancreas, gallbladder","slides":[{"src":"sl/G020-45.jpg","cap":"Portal triad: portal vein branch, hepatic artery branch, bile duct branch","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/G020-27.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic acinar cell electron micrograph with zymogen granules; centroacinar cells","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/G020-65.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder: folded mucosa, simple columnar cells with microvilli","where":"2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 65"}],"q":[{"id":"F1221-140-1","s":"WHAT IS GOING ON HERE?! (identify the four images)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Top left: portal triad (hepatic artery, portal venule, bile duct); top right: hepatocytes EM (mitochondria, bile canaliculus, junctional complex); bottom left: pancreatic acinar cells (intercalated duct, rough ER, zymogen granules, centroacinar cell); bottom right: gallbladder (smooth muscle, epithelial cells, villus-like mucosal folds)","a":null,"why":"<b>Top left: portal triad</b>: big thin-walled portal venule, small thick-walled hepatic arteriole, bile duct of cuboidal cells.<br><b>Top right: hepatocytes on electron micrograph</b>: many mitochondria; a tiny bile canaliculus between two cells, sealed by junctional complexes.<br><b>Bottom left: pancreatic acinar cells</b>: basophilic base (rough endoplasmic reticulum), apical zymogen granules, pale centroacinar cells.<br><b>Bottom right: gallbladder</b>: tall simple columnar cells on branching folds that mimic villi, smooth muscle below, no glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1221-140.jpg","in":"Four panels: an H&E portal area with three tubes; a TEM of two adjoining hepatocytes with mitochondria and a small canaliculus between them; an H&E of basophilic acinar cells with apical granules and a small duct; an H&E of a folded mucosa of tall columnar cells over a smooth-muscle wall.","tg":["H16 slide 45 (Portal venule)","H16 slide 27 (Pancreatic acini)","H16 slide 64 (Gall bladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"Exam 2 TA review 2021 by week: GI weeks only","sl":140,"f":"F1221"}],"tier":1}]}],"sum":{},"units":[{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The four layers of the wall","p":"<p>From the esophagus to the anal canal, the digestive tube is built on <b>one plan of four layers</b>. They are listed here from the lumen outward.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G016-4.jpg\" alt=\"The four layers of the digestive tube, with the two nerve plexuses\"><figcaption>The four layers of the digestive tube, with the two nerve plexuses <small>· 2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 4</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>1. Mucosa (the lining)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Epithelium</b>: faces the lumen. Its type changes from organ to organ, because each organ has a different job (see the end of this part).</li>\n<li><b>Lamina propria</b>: loose connective tissue under the epithelium. It holds small blood vessels, lymph vessels and many immune cells (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, MALT). In the small intestine, the lymph vessel in the core of each villus is called a lacteal.</li>\n<li><b>Muscularis mucosae</b>: a thin sheet of smooth muscle at the base of the mucosa. It moves the mucosa on its own. Its thickness depends on the organ.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2. Submucosa</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Connective tissue with the larger blood vessels and lymph vessels.</li>\n<li>Holds the submucosal (Meissner) nerve plexus (1.2).</li>\n<li>Holds glands in only two places: the esophagus and the duodenum.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3. Muscularis externa</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Usually two layers of smooth muscle: inner circular and outer longitudinal.</li>\n<li>The myenteric (Auerbach) nerve plexus lies between the two layers (1.2).</li>\n<li>Exceptions: the stomach adds a third, oblique layer; the upper esophagus has skeletal muscle.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4. Serosa or adventitia (the outer coat)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Serosa</th><th>Adventitia</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Made of</td><td>Thin loose connective tissue covered by <b>mesothelium</b>, a simple squamous epithelium</td><td>Thicker loose connective tissue with vessels, nerves and fat, and <b>no mesothelium</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Edge on a slide</td><td>Smooth, finished edge</td><td>No finished edge; it blends into the neighboring tissue</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Organs that hang free in the peritoneal cavity: stomach, jejunum, ileum, transverse and sigmoid colon</td><td>Organs bound directly to their neighbors: most of the esophagus, and retroperitoneal parts such as duodenum parts 2–4</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>A <b>mesentery</b> is a sheet of connective tissue covered by mesothelium on both sides. It holds the loops of gut, carries their vessels and is continuous with the serosa.</p>\n<h4>Where the layers come from</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Endoderm: the epithelium of the gut tube and of the glands that grow from it (liver, pancreas).</li>\n<li>Mesoderm: connective tissue, smooth muscle, blood vessels and mesentery.</li>\n<li>Ectoderm: the lining of the mouth and the salivary glands. The enteric nerve cells and the tooth-forming cells come from the neural crest.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What changes along the tube</h4>\n<p>The deeper layers look much alike everywhere. The <b>epithelium</b> is the layer that changes most:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stratified squamous (protects against rough food): oral cavity, pharynx, esophagus, anal canal.</li>\n<li>Simple columnar (secretes and absorbs): stomach, small intestine, large intestine.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The plan of the digestive tube","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The gut's own nervous system","p":"<p>The gut has its own nervous system, the <b>enteric nervous system</b>. It is two networks of nerve cells and fibers inside the wall. It can run the gut <b>on its own, independent of the brain and spinal cord</b>. Sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves only adjust it.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G016-5.jpg\" alt=\"The two plexuses of the enteric nervous system\"><figcaption>The two plexuses of the enteric nervous system <small>· 2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Plexus</th><th>Where it lies</th><th>What it controls</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Submucosal (Meissner) plexus</b></td><td>In the submucosa, just under the muscularis mucosae</td><td>Gland secretion, local blood flow, and movements of the mucosa (through the muscularis mucosae)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus</b></td><td>In the muscularis externa, in the thin connective tissue between the inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle layers</td><td>Contraction of the muscularis externa: peristalsis and the rest of gut motility</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Both plexuses look like small, pale clusters of large nerve cell bodies (ganglion cells) with nerve fibers, set in connective tissue.</li>\n<li>Name the layer first. A cluster in the submucosa is Meissner. A cluster between the two muscle layers is Auerbach.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>When a plexus fails</h4>\n<p>Loss of myenteric nerve cells leaves the muscle unable to relax and coordinate. At the lower esophageal sphincter this causes achalasia (3.2).</p>","s":"The plan of the digestive tube","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The lining of the mouth","p":"<p>The mouth is lined by <b>oral mucosa</b>: stratified squamous epithelium on a lamina propria. The mouth has <b>no muscularis mucosae</b>. The lamina propria runs straight into a submucosa (where there is one), or onto bone or muscle.</p>\n<h4>Three kinds of surface</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Surface cells</th><th>Where</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Keratinized</b></td><td>Dead, flat cells full of keratin, with <b>no nuclei</b>; a granular layer (keratohyalin granules) lies below them</td><td>Gingiva (gums) and hard palate, the surfaces that take the force of chewing (masticatory mucosa); also the tips of the filiform papillae of the tongue</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Parakeratinized</b></td><td>Keratin is made, but the flat surface cells <b>keep small, dark nuclei</b></td><td>Parts of the gingiva and hard palate; patches of the lip</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Nonkeratinized</b></td><td>Living flat cells <b>with nuclei</b> all the way to the surface; the surface stays wet</td><td>Soft palate, inner lips, cheeks, floor of the mouth, underside of the tongue, pharynx</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G015-26.jpg\" alt=\"Parakeratinized (left) and keratinized (right) oral epithelium\"><figcaption>Parakeratinized (left) and keratinized (right) oral epithelium <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Mucoperiosteum</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Over the hard palate (and the attached gingiva), the lamina propria is fused directly to the periosteum of the bone underneath.</li>\n<li>This firm layer is called mucoperiosteum. It keeps the lining from sliding during chewing.</li>\n<li>The soft palate, cheeks and floor of the mouth instead have a loose submucosa, so their lining moves.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The lips","p":"<p>A lip cut in sagittal section has a <b>core of skeletal muscle</b> (the orbicularis oris), covered by three different surfaces. Going from the face into the mouth:</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G018-10.jpg\" alt=\"The three surfaces of the lip\"><figcaption>The three surfaces of the lip <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Region</th><th>Epithelium</th><th>What else is there</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>External portion</b> (skin side)</td><td>Thin skin: a thin keratinized epidermis on a dermis</td><td>Hair follicles, sebaceous glands and sweat glands</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Vermilion zone</b> (the red margin)</td><td>Very thin keratinized epidermis; the transition from skin to oral mucosa</td><td>Tall dermal papillae bring capillary loops close to the surface, which makes the pink-red color. Many sensory nerve endings (Meissner corpuscles). <b>No hair follicles and no glands</b>, so it is kept moist only by saliva and dries and cracks easily.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Internal portion</b> (mouth side)</td><td>Oral mucosa: thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium whose surface cells keep their nuclei; some areas are parakeratinized</td><td>Minor salivary glands (labial glands) below; no hair</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Telling the three apart on a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Hair follicles or glands in the dermis: external portion.</li>\n<li>Thin epidermis with vessels pushed up close to the surface, and nothing in the connective tissue below: vermilion.</li>\n<li>Very thick epithelium with nucleated surface cells and mucous glands below: internal portion.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Cold sores</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Cold sores (herpes labialis) are clusters of painful vesicles on the lip that break into ulcers.</li>\n<li>They are caused by <b>herpes simplex virus type 1</b>. The virus kills the infected epithelial cells, then hides in the sensory ganglion and can return.</li>\n<li>Other mouth infections to keep apart: Epstein-Barr virus (infectious mononucleosis, hairy leukoplakia of the tongue), human herpesvirus 8 (Kaposi sarcoma), Streptococcus pyogenes (strep throat), Borrelia vincentii (trench mouth, a necrotizing gum infection).</li>\n</ul>","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The tongue: muscle, surfaces and tonsils","p":"<h4>Body of the tongue</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A mass of <b>skeletal (striated) muscle</b>. Its fibers run in <b>all three planes</b> (lengthwise, across and up-down) and cross one another. So any one section shows bundles cut lengthwise, across and obliquely. This gives the tongue its great mobility.</li>\n<li>The bundles are separated by connective tissue and fat. (One lecture slide says smooth muscle separates them; Junqueira says connective tissue.)</li>\n<li>Origin: the muscle comes from the occipital myotomes; the lining comes from the pharyngeal (branchial) arches.</li>\n<li>Jobs: taste, chewing, swallowing, speech.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G015-32.jpg\" alt=\"Tongue muscle cut in three planes: O oblique, C cross, L longitudinal\"><figcaption>Tongue muscle cut in three planes: O oblique, C cross, L longitudinal <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 32</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Surfaces</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Ventral (under) surface: smooth, thin nonkeratinized lining.</li>\n<li>Dorsal (top) surface: rough. A V-shaped groove, the <b>sulcus terminalis</b>, divides it into two parts. The anterior two-thirds carries the papillae (2.4). The posterior one-third (the root) carries the lingual tonsil.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Tonsils at the back of the mouth</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Lingual tonsil</th><th>Palatine tonsil</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Root of the tongue</td><td>Side walls of the oropharynx</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Covering</td><td>Stratified squamous epithelium</td><td>Stratified squamous epithelium, which also lines its crypts</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Crypts</td><td>Shallow</td><td>Deep and branching</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Glands</td><td>Ducts of mucous glands open into the crypts and flush them</td><td>No glands drain into the crypts, so debris collects in them</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lymphoid nodules</td><td>Yes, around the crypts</td><td>Yes, around the crypts</td></tr>\n</table></div>","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The lingual papillae","p":"<p>Papillae are raised folds of the epithelium, each with a core of connective tissue, on the dorsal surface of the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. There are <b>four types</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G018-17.jpg\" alt=\"The four papillae: filiform, foliate, fungiform, circumvallate\"><figcaption>The four papillae: filiform, foliate, fungiform, circumvallate <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 17</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Shape and location</th><th>Keratin</th><th>Taste buds</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Filiform</b></td><td>The most numerous. Slender, pointed cones in rows over the whole dorsal surface. They give the tongue its rough texture and grip food (friction) during chewing.</td><td>Heavily keratinized tips</td><td><b>None</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Fungiform</b></td><td>Mushroom-shaped: narrow stalk, broad round top with a connective tissue core. Scattered singly among the filiform papillae, more near the tip.</td><td>Thin</td><td>A few, on the upper surface</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Circumvallate</b> (vallate)</td><td>The largest: 8–12 domes in a V just in front of the sulcus terminalis. Each sits level with the surface, ringed by a deep trench (a moat).</td><td>None</td><td>Many, in the epithelium of the side walls facing the trench</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Foliate</b></td><td>Rows of parallel, leaf-like ridges on the sides of the back of the tongue, separated by deep clefts. Well developed in children, small in adults.</td><td>None</td><td>Many, in the walls of the clefts</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Von Ebner glands</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Small <b>serous</b> (watery) salivary glands in the tongue under the circumvallate papillae.</li>\n<li>Their ducts empty into the base of the trench. The steady flow rinses the trench, clearing old food so the taste buds can sense new tastes.</li>\n<li>They also secrete lingual lipase.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Rows of pointed, keratinized spikes: filiform.</li>\n<li>One broad mushroom among spikes: fungiform.</li>\n<li>One large dome in a trench, with glands below: circumvallate.</li>\n<li>A row of parallel ridges with clefts: foliate.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Taste buds","p":"<p>A <b>taste bud</b> is a pale, oval cluster of spindle-shaped cells set inside the stratified epithelium. It reaches from the basal lamina to the surface, where it opens through a small <b>taste pore</b> (gustatory pore).</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G015-36.jpg\" alt=\"Taste bud: gustatory pore, taste cells with microvilli, supporting and basal cells\"><figcaption>Taste bud: gustatory pore, taste cells with microvilli, supporting and basal cells <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 36</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Where taste buds are</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Side walls of the circumvallate papillae: the most, about 250 per papilla.</li>\n<li>Walls of the foliate clefts, and the tops of fungiform papillae (a few).</li>\n<li>None on the filiform papillae.</li>\n<li>Also scattered in the soft palate, pharynx and epiglottis.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Cells of a taste bud</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Gustatory (taste, sensory) cells</b>: long, pale cells. Their apical <b>microvilli</b> (not cilia, not stereocilia) reach into the taste pore. There, dissolved substances bind receptors on the microvilli. At their base the cells synapse with afferent nerve fibers.</li>\n<li><b>Supporting (sustentacular) cells</b>: long cells between the taste cells.</li>\n<li><b>Basal cells</b>: small stem cells at the base of the bud. They replace the taste cells, which live only about 10 days.</li>\n<li>Afferent (sensory) nerve fibers enter the bud at its base.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Tastes</h4>\n<p>Salty, sweet, bitter, sour and umami (the taste of glutamate). Water, calcium and fat may be extra tastes (lecture slide 30).</p>","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.5"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Teeth","p":"<p>The H15A lecture covers teeth briefly (slides 40–42). The lab deck leaves them out.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G015-41.jpg\" alt=\"Parts of a tooth\"><figcaption>Parts of a tooth <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 41</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Parts of a tooth</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Crown (above the gum) and root (in the bony socket of the jaw).</li>\n<li>Pulp: soft connective tissue with vessels and nerves in the center. They enter through the apical foramen at the root tip and run up the root canal.</li>\n<li>Gingiva (gum) surrounds the neck of the tooth.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Three hard tissues</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Tissue</th><th>Where</th><th>Made by</th><th>Made of</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Enamel</b></td><td>Covers the crown; the outermost layer there; the hardest tissue in the body</td><td>Ameloblasts: tall columnar cells from ectoderm. They are lost when the tooth erupts, so enamel cannot be repaired.</td><td>About 96% hydroxyapatite (a calcium phosphate crystal). Its small organic part is enamel proteins such as amelogenin, with <b>no collagen</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Dentin</b></td><td>Most of the tooth, under the enamel and cementum, around the pulp</td><td>Odontoblasts (from neural crest), lining the pulp. Their long processes sit in dentinal tubules. They lay down uncalcified predentin first, which then calcifies.</td><td>About 70% hydroxyapatite. Its organic part is about 90% <b>type I collagen</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Cementum</b></td><td>Covers the root</td><td>Cementoblasts. They first lay down uncalcified cementoid, which then calcifies (like osteoid becoming bone).</td><td>Bone-like: hydroxyapatite and type I collagen</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>What holds a tooth in its socket</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The <b>periodontal ligament</b>: dense collagen fibers running from the cementum of the root to the alveolar bone of the socket. Their ends, buried in cementum and bone, are called Sharpey fibers.</li>\n<li>This collagen is replaced quickly. Making collagen needs vitamin C (it is the cofactor for the enzymes that add hydroxyl groups to proline and lysine). In scurvy, the ligament weakens and the teeth loosen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other cells that make collagen: osteoblasts (bone), chondroblasts (cartilage), fibroblasts, odontoblasts and cementoblasts.</p>","s":"The oral cavity","sn":2,"pn":"2.6"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Lining, glands and outer coat","p":"<p>The esophagus is a muscular tube about 25 cm (10 inches) long. It carries food from the pharynx to the stomach. An upper and a lower esophageal sphincter close its two ends. Its wall follows the four-layer plan.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G018-23.jpg\" alt=\"Esophagus: epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae, and mucous glands in the submucosa\"><figcaption>Esophagus: epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae, and mucous glands in the submucosa <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 23</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Mucosa</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Epithelium: thick <b>nonkeratinized stratified squamous</b>. The surface cells keep their nuclei. It resists the rubbing of food. Like all stratified squamous linings, it has no goblet cells.</li>\n<li>Lamina propria: loose connective tissue. Near the stomach it holds small mucous glands, the esophageal cardiac glands.</li>\n<li>Muscularis mucosae: a single layer of longitudinal smooth muscle (incomplete near the top).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Submucosa</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Esophageal glands</b>: mucous glands that lubricate the lining. Their ducts cross the mucosa to the lumen.</li>\n<li>A rich plexus of veins. When pressure in the portal vein rises (portal hypertension, for example from alcoholic cirrhosis), these submucosal veins swell into <b>esophageal varices</b>. They can burst and cause vomiting of blood.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Muscularis externa</h4>\n<p>Inner circular and outer longitudinal layers. The type of muscle changes along the length (3.2).</p>\n<h4>Outer coat</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Adventitia along most of its length, in the neck and chest.</li>\n<li>Serosa only on the short segment below the diaphragm (the last 1–2 cm), which lies in the peritoneal cavity.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Recognizing esophagus on a slide</h4>\n<p>Thick nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, mucous glands in the submucosa, and a thick muscularis externa.</p>\n<h4>Infection of the lining</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In people with weak immunity (for example AIDS, acquired immunodeficiency syndrome), the yeast Candida can grow on the lining. It forms raised <b>white plaques</b> on red, swollen mucosa and makes swallowing painful.</li>\n<li>Herpes simplex virus instead makes small punched-out ulcers.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The muscle changes from top to bottom","p":"<p>The esophagus is divided into thirds by the type of muscle in its <b>muscularis externa</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G015-60.jpg\" alt=\"Esophagus: skeletal, mixed and smooth muscle by thirds\"><figcaption>Esophagus: skeletal, mixed and smooth muscle by thirds <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 60</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Third</th><th>Muscularis externa</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Upper</td><td>Skeletal muscle (swallowing starts as a voluntary act)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Middle</td><td>Skeletal and smooth muscle mixed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lower</td><td>Smooth muscle only, like the rest of the gut</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The esophagus is the <b>only part of the digestive tube with skeletal muscle in its muscularis externa</b>. (The external anal sphincter is skeletal too, but it is a separate sphincter muscle.)</li>\n<li>Skeletal fibers: large, pink, cross-striated, with many nuclei at the edge of each fiber.</li>\n<li>Smooth muscle cells: small spindles with one central nucleus.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Reading a cross-section of the tube</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The inner circular layer is cut lengthwise, so its cells look long.</li>\n<li>The outer longitudinal layer is cut across, so its fibers look like round or polygonal profiles.</li>\n<li>The myenteric plexus sits in the thin connective tissue between them.</li>\n<li>Do not confuse the muscularis externa with the muscularis mucosae, the thin band of smooth muscle right under the epithelium.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Lower esophageal sphincter and achalasia</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>At the lower end, the circular muscle stays contracted as the <b>lower esophageal sphincter</b>. During swallowing, nerve cells of the myenteric plexus make it relax.</li>\n<li>In <b>achalasia</b>, the myenteric plexus nerve cells at the sphincter are lost. The sphincter cannot relax. Food piles up, and the esophagus above it widens (a bird's-beak shape on a contrast X-ray). Swallowing of both solids and liquids gets harder.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Where the esophagus meets the stomach","p":"<p>At the <b>esophagogastric junction</b>, where the esophagus joins the cardia of the stomach, the lining changes <b>abruptly</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G015-63.jpg\" alt=\"Esophagogastric junction: squamous esophagus (left), glandular cardia (right)\"><figcaption>Esophagogastric junction: squamous esophagus (left), glandular cardia (right) <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 63</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Esophagus side</th><th>Stomach side</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium</td><td>Simple columnar epithelium of surface mucous cells, dipping down as gastric pits</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Near the junction, mucus-secreting glands open on both sides: esophageal cardiac glands in the esophagus and cardiac glands in the stomach.</li>\n<li>Strands of muscularis mucosae separate the mucosa from the submucosa.</li>\n<li>The junction lies at the lower esophageal sphincter.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>One section showing thick, layered pink epithelium on one side and a single row of tall, pale columnar cells with pits and glands on the other.</li>\n<li>Only one other place in the tube joins simple columnar to stratified squamous: the anal canal (pectinate line, 6.4).</li>\n<li>Other junctions join two columnar linings: stomach to duodenum, ileum to cecum.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Reflux, esophagitis and metaplasia","p":"<h4>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Acid from the stomach flows back into the lower esophagus when the lower esophageal sphincter does not close well.</li>\n<li>Triggers: large meals, lying down after eating, obesity, coffee, alcohol, smoking.</li>\n<li>Symptoms: heartburn (burning pain behind the breastbone) and a sour taste, eased by antacids.</li>\n<li>Treatment starts with lifestyle changes (smaller meals, no eating before lying down, weight loss, less coffee and alcohol), then acid-lowering drugs (proton pump inhibitors).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Reflux esophagitis on a biopsy</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Basal cell hyperplasia: the basal layer of the squamous epithelium thickens.</li>\n<li>The lamina propria papillae stretch up toward the surface.</li>\n<li>Inflammatory cells (eosinophils, neutrophils, lymphocytes) appear inside the epithelium.</li>\n<li>Other causes of esophagitis to rule out: eosinophilic (allergic) esophagitis, infections (Candida, herpes, cytomegalovirus), pills that stick in the esophagus, and radiation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Metaplasia</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Metaplasia</b> is the replacement of one mature cell type by another mature type that better withstands a chronic injury. It can reverse if the injury stops.</li>\n<li>Example in the airway: in smokers, ciliated pseudostratified epithelium becomes stratified squamous.</li>\n<li>Compare: dysplasia is disordered growth of abnormal cells (a precancer); neoplasia is new, uncontrolled growth (a tumor).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Barrett esophagus</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Years of acid reflux turn the stratified squamous lining of the <b>distal esophagus</b>, just above the esophagogastric junction, into <b>simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells</b> (intestinal-type metaplasia). Columnar cells resist acid better.</li>\n<li>The normal esophagus is squamous all the way down to the junction.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G015-66.jpg\" alt=\"Normal esophagus (left) and Barrett esophagus (right)\"><figcaption>Normal esophagus (left) and Barrett esophagus (right) <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 66</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Barrett esophagus in the clinic, and cancer","p":"<h4>Recognizing Barrett esophagus</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Story: long-standing heartburn, worse after meals or lying down.</li>\n<li>Endoscopy: <b>salmon-pink or red, velvety mucosa</b> reaching up from the junction into the lower esophagus. Normal squamous lining is pale pinkish-white.</li>\n<li>Biopsy: columnar cells <b>with goblet cells</b> where squamous epithelium should be.</li>\n<li>Cause: the esophagus is exposed to refluxed gastric acid (and bile) for years. It is not caused by Helicobacter pylori, and it is not a motility problem.</li>\n<li>Goblet cells are the key. Stomach mucosa (including the cardia) has no goblet cells. So columnar mucosa with goblet cells in the esophagus is intestinal metaplasia, not misplaced stomach lining.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Where Barrett can lead</h4>\n<p>Barrett esophagus → low-grade dysplasia → high-grade dysplasia → invasive <b>adenocarcinoma</b>, a cancer of gland-forming columnar cells.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G015-65.jpg\" alt=\"Normal lining → Barrett → dysplasia → invasive carcinoma\"><figcaption>Normal lining → Barrett → dysplasia → invasive carcinoma <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 65</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Adenocarcinoma</th><th>Squamous cell carcinoma</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Starts from</td><td>Barrett (columnar) epithelium</td><td>The normal squamous epithelium</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Lower third</td><td>Upper and middle thirds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Main risk factors</td><td>Chronic reflux and Barrett esophagus, obesity, smoking</td><td>Smoking, alcohol</td></tr>\n</table></div>","s":"The esophagus","sn":3,"pn":"3.5"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The overall design of the stomach","p":"<ul>\n<li>The widest part of the tube; it holds about 1.5 L.</li>\n<li>It mixes food with acid and enzymes into a soupy paste called chyme.</li>\n<li>Four regions: cardia, fundus, body and pylorus.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G018-29.jpg\" alt=\"Stomach wall: rugae, gastric pits and glands, muscularis mucosae, submucosa, muscularis externa, serosa\"><figcaption>Stomach wall: rugae, gastric pits and glands, muscularis mucosae, submucosa, muscularis externa, serosa <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 29</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Special features</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>What it is</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Rugae</b></td><td>Large folds of mucosa with a core of submucosa. They flatten as the stomach fills. Only the stomach has them. (The small intestine has permanent circular folds instead, 5.1.)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Gastric pits</b></td><td>Funnel-shaped dips of the surface epithelium into the mucosa. The gastric glands open into the bottom of the pits.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Gastric glands</b></td><td>Tubular glands in the lamina propria that secrete the gastric juice.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>The wall, layer by layer</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Mucosa: simple columnar epithelium of <b>surface mucous cells</b>. Each cell has a pale cap of mucus at its top. There are no goblet cells in the stomach. The lamina propria is squeezed into thin strips between the packed glands. Then a muscularis mucosae.</li>\n<li>Submucosa: connective tissue; it forms the core of the rugae.</li>\n<li>Muscularis externa: <b>three layers</b>: inner oblique, middle circular and outer longitudinal. The extra layer helps churn food.</li>\n<li>Serosa (not adventitia): the stomach hangs in the peritoneal cavity.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Three regions, three kinds of mucosa","p":"<p>Under the microscope, the stomach has three kinds of mucosa. Compare <b>pit depth with gland length</b>. Going from the esophagus toward the duodenum, the pits get deeper and the glands get shorter, but more branched and coiled.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G015-75.jpg\" alt=\"Cardia, body/fundus and pylorus\"><figcaption>Cardia, body/fundus and pylorus <small>· 2026 lecture: H15A Digestive Tract, slide 75</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Region</th><th>Pits</th><th>Glands</th><th>Main cells</th><th>Job</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Cardia</b>: a narrow ring, 1.5–3 cm wide, around the entry of the esophagus</td><td>Fairly long</td><td>Short, coiled mucous glands</td><td>Mucous cells; few or no parietal and chief cells; no G cells</td><td>Protection (mucus)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Fundus and body</b>: most of the stomach (oxyntic, meaning acid-making, mucosa)</td><td>Short (about a quarter of the mucosa)</td><td>Long, straight, tightly packed</td><td>Parietal cells (pink) and chief cells (purple)</td><td>Digestion: acid and pepsinogen</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Pylorus</b> (antrum and pyloric canal)</td><td>Deep (about half the mucosa)</td><td>Short, branched and coiled mucous glands</td><td>Mucous cells (mucus and lysozyme); <b>G cells</b>; no parietal or chief cells</td><td>Mucus to protect the duodenum; gastrin</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The lab deck sums it up: the cardia mucosa looks like the pyloric mucosa but has no G cells.</li>\n<li>G cells release the hormone <b>gastrin</b> into the blood. Gastrin tells the body and fundus to make acid and makes the stomach move more.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Gland cells of the body and fundus","p":"<p>A gland of the body or fundus has three regions: the pit at the top, a narrow neck (isthmus), and the long body and base. Each region has its own cells.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G018-30.jpg\" alt=\"Fundic mucosa: pits, neck and glands; surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal and chief cells\"><figcaption>Fundic mucosa: pits, neck and glands; surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal and chief cells <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 30</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Cell</th><th>Where</th><th>Look on H&amp;E</th><th>Secretes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Surface mucous cell</td><td>Surface and pits</td><td>Tall columnar, pale mucus cap on top</td><td>Thick, sticky, <b>alkaline</b> mucus that clings to the surface and protects it from acid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mucous neck cell</td><td>Neck</td><td>Smaller, pale</td><td>Watery, less alkaline (more acidic) mucus</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stem cell</td><td>Neck (isthmus)</td><td>Small, dividing</td><td>Nothing; it replaces the other cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Parietal (oxyntic) cell</b></td><td>Mostly the upper half of the gland</td><td>Large, round, <b>pink (acidophilic)</b>, central nucleus; packed with mitochondria</td><td><b>Hydrochloric acid (HCl)</b> and <b>intrinsic factor</b>, which the ileum needs to absorb vitamin B12</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Chief (zymogenic) cell</b></td><td>Base of the gland</td><td><b>Purple-blue (basophilic)</b> from rough endoplasmic reticulum; zymogen granules at the top</td><td><b>Pepsinogen</b> (becomes pepsin in acid; digests protein) and a lipase precursor (gastric lipase; starts fat digestion)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enteroendocrine cell</td><td>Scattered in the glands</td><td>Pale; needs special stains (4.4)</td><td>Hormones and local signals</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Starch digestion is not a stomach job: amylase comes from the salivary glands and the pancreas.</p>","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.3"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Enteroendocrine cells, and the parietal cell at work","p":"<h4>Enteroendocrine cells</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Small cells scattered among the gland cells, from the stomach to the colon. Their hormone granules sit at the <b>base</b> of the cell. They release into the blood (endocrine) or onto nearby cells (paracrine), not into the lumen.</li>\n<li>On hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E) they look pale and are easy to miss. They are shown with <b>special stains</b>: chromium salts (enterochromaffin cells), silver salts (argentaffin cells), or <b>immunostaining</b> (immunohistochemistry) for their hormones.</li>\n<li>Open type: reaches the lumen with microvilli that sense nutrients, much like taste cells. Closed type: does not reach the lumen.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Cell</th><th>Product</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Enterochromaffin (EC) cell</td><td>Serotonin</td><td>Increases gut motility</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enterochromaffin-like cell</td><td>Histamine (paracrine)</td><td>Increases acid secretion by parietal cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>G cell (pylorus)</td><td>Gastrin</td><td>Increases acid secretion and motility</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Small-intestine cells</td><td>Cholecystokinin, secretin and others</td><td>See 5.3</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>The parietal cell at rest and at work</h4>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G018-33.jpg\" alt=\"Parietal cell, resting and active\"><figcaption>Parietal cell, resting and active <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<ul>\n<li>Resting: many tubulovesicles near the top, full of proton pumps; short intracellular canaliculi; few microvilli.</li>\n<li>Active: the tubulovesicles fuse with the cell membrane. They form deep <b>intracellular canaliculi</b> lined with many microvilli. This greatly enlarges the membrane that carries the H+/K+ pumps (proton pumps). Many mitochondria supply the energy.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Diseases of the stomach","p":"<h4>Pernicious anemia: the chain of events</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Parietal cells are destroyed or bypassed.</li>\n<li>No <b>intrinsic factor</b> is made. Intrinsic factor normally binds vitamin B12 so the ileum can absorb it.</li>\n<li>Vitamin B12 deficiency follows.</li>\n<li>Red cell precursors cannot make DNA normally, so they grow large: <b>megaloblastic (macrocytic) anemia</b>. The blood smear shows large oval red cells and hypersegmented neutrophils (6 or more nuclear lobes).</li>\n<li>Other effects: fatigue, a smooth red tongue, diarrhea, and nerve damage (numbness, tingling, poor balance).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Size clue: lack of B12 or folate gives big red cells; lack of iron gives small red cells.</p>\n<h4>Two causes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Autoimmune atrophic gastritis</b> (true pernicious anemia): antibodies attack the parietal cells (their H+/K+ proton pump) and intrinsic factor. The mucosa of the body and fundus thins. It often comes with other autoimmune diseases, such as type 1 diabetes and Hashimoto thyroiditis.</li>\n<li><b>Stomach bypass surgery</b>: food skips most of the stomach, so the parietal cells no longer supply intrinsic factor. The lecture lists bypass as a cause of pernicious anemia.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Helicobacter pylori</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Curved, rod-shaped bacteria that live in the surface mucus.</li>\n<li>The usual cause of chronic gastritis and <b>peptic ulcer disease</b>.</li>\n<li>Long-term infection builds lymphoid tissue in the mucosa and raises the risk of <b>gastric lymphoma</b> (MALT lymphoma) and of gastric adenocarcinoma.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>A smooth muscle tumor</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <b>leiomyoma</b> is a benign tumor of smooth muscle, usually from the muscularis externa. It forms a small, well-defined mass under the mucosa, made of bland spindle cells in bundles that stain for smooth muscle markers (desmin, smooth muscle actin).</li>\n<li>Its look-alike, the gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST), comes from the pacemaker cells of the gut wall (interstitial cells of Cajal) and stains for KIT (CD117).</li>\n</ul>","s":"The stomach","sn":4,"pn":"4.5"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Three levels of folding, and the crypts","p":"<p>The small intestine (duodenum, jejunum, ileum) finishes digestion, absorbs the products, and releases hormones. Its design is all about <b>surface area</b>. Three levels of folding multiply the absorbing surface.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G018-37.jpg\" alt=\"Plicae circulares, villi and microvilli\"><figcaption>Plicae circulares, villi and microvilli <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 37</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Level</th><th>Structure</th><th>Core</th><th>How it looks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Gross</td><td><b>Plicae circulares</b> (valves of Kerckring): permanent circular folds</td><td><b>Submucosa</b></td><td>Tall folds of the whole mucosa, each covered with many villi</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Light microscope</td><td><b>Villi</b>: finger-like or leaf-like projections of the mucosa</td><td><b>Lamina propria</b>, with capillaries, a central lacteal and strands of smooth muscle</td><td>Small projections, each covered by one layer of columnar cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Electron microscope</td><td><b>Microvilli</b> on the top of each absorptive cell</td><td>Actin filaments</td><td>Together they form the brush (striated) border, a thin fuzzy pink line on the cell tops</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Plicae circulares start in the duodenum, are tallest and most numerous in the jejunum, and fade out in the distal ileum. They do not flatten, unlike the rugae of the stomach.</li>\n<li>The number of villi and microvilli falls along the way: duodenum &gt; jejunum &gt; ileum.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Crypts of Lieberkühn (intestinal glands)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Simple tubular glands. The epithelium dips down between the villi as far as the muscularis mucosae. So they lie in the <b>mucosa</b>, not the submucosa.</li>\n<li>Present along the whole small intestine and the large intestine. (The colon keeps its crypts but has no villi.) The esophagus has none, and the stomach has gastric glands instead.</li>\n<li>They hold the stem cells, Paneth cells and many enteroendocrine cells (5.3).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<p>Villi project above the mucosa. The crypts are the straight tubes below the villi, just above the muscularis mucosae. Glands below the muscularis mucosae, in the submucosa, are something else: the Brunner glands of the duodenum (5.4).</p>","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Enterocytes and fat absorption","p":"<h4>Cells of the intestinal lining</h4>\n<p>Five cell types line the villi and crypts: enterocytes (by far the most numerous), goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells and stem cells. This part covers the first two; 5.3 covers the rest.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G018-38.jpg\" alt=\"Small intestine cell types\"><figcaption>Small intestine cell types <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 38</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Enterocytes (columnar absorptive cells)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Tall columnar cells with a nucleus near the base and a dense <b>brush border of microvilli</b> on top.</li>\n<li>Enzymes in the brush border (for sugars and short peptides) do the last step of digestion right at the cell surface (<b>terminal digestion</b>). Then the cell absorbs the products.</li>\n<li>Tight junctions seal the cells together, so absorbed material passes through the cells.</li>\n<li>Life cycle: stem cells in the crypts divide; the new cells climb the villus and are shed at its tip after about 5 days. Cells do not divide on the villus itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>How enterocytes absorb fat</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>In the lumen, pancreatic lipase splits triglycerides into fatty acids and monoglycerides. Bile salts carry them in tiny droplets (micelles).</li>\n<li>These diffuse into the enterocyte (no active transport is needed).</li>\n<li>In the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, they are rebuilt into <b>triglycerides</b>.</li>\n<li>Proteins are added (rough endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi), packaging them as <b>chylomicrons</b>.</li>\n<li>Chylomicrons leave the base of the cell and enter the <b>lacteal</b>, the lymph capillary in the villus core, not the blood capillaries. They reach the blood later through the thoracic duct.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G018-40.jpg\" alt=\"Lipid absorption and processing by enterocytes\"><figcaption>Lipid absorption and processing by enterocytes <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 40</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Goblet cells</h4>\n<p>Scattered among the enterocytes. They secrete mucins, which form the protective mucus layer. Their number rises along the gut: duodenum &lt; jejunum &lt; ileum &lt; colon.</p>\n<h4>Celiac disease</h4>\n<p>An immune reaction to gluten flattens the villi (villous atrophy), deepens the crypts, and fills the epithelium with lymphocytes. With fewer enterocytes and less brush border, nutrients are poorly absorbed: chronic diarrhea and weight loss.</p>","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Crypt cells: stem cells, Paneth cells and hormone cells","p":"<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G016-17.jpg\" alt=\"Paneth cells at the base of a crypt\"><figcaption>Paneth cells at the base of a crypt <small>· 2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 17</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Cell</th><th>Where</th><th>Look</th><th>Job</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Stem (regenerative) cells</td><td>Lower part of the crypt</td><td>Small; dividing cells (mitotic figures) are often seen</td><td>Renew the whole lining every few days: mostly enterocytes, plus goblet, Paneth and enteroendocrine cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Paneth cells</b></td><td><b>Base of the crypts</b></td><td>Pyramidal cells packed with large, <b>bright red (eosinophilic) granules</b> at the top</td><td><b>Innate immunity</b>: release lysozyme, defensins and phospholipase A2, which kill bacteria. This keeps the crypt and its stem cells free of microbes and controls the number of gut bacteria.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enteroendocrine cells</td><td>Crypts and villi</td><td>Pale; granules at the base; special stains</td><td>Hormones (below)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Goblet cells</td><td>Crypts and villi</td><td>Pale mucus-filled cup; nucleus at the base</td><td>Mucus</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Do not confuse the red apical granules of Paneth cells with the stomach's chief cells (purple, in gastric glands) or with enteroendocrine cells (pale, granules at the base).</p>\n<h4>Hormones of the small intestine</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Cholecystokinin (CCK)</b>: released when fat and protein reach the duodenum. It makes the gallbladder contract (bile release) and the pancreatic acinar cells secrete enzymes. It also slows the stomach and signals fullness.</li>\n<li><b>Secretin</b>: released when acid reaches the duodenum. It makes the pancreatic ducts secrete bicarbonate, which neutralizes the acid.</li>\n<li>Glucagon-like peptide 1 (GLP-1) and peptide YY: signal fullness (satiety) to the brain.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.3"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Duodenum and jejunum","p":"<p>All three parts of the small intestine have villi and crypts. Each has one <b>signature feature</b>.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G018-36.jpg\" alt=\"Duodenum (Brunner glands), jejunum (plicae), ileum (Peyer patches)\"><figcaption>Duodenum (Brunner glands), jejunum (plicae), ileum (Peyer patches) <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 36</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Segment</th><th>Signature</th><th>Other clues</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Duodenum</b></td><td><b>Brunner glands</b> in the submucosa</td><td>Broad, leaf-like villi. Outer coat: serosa on the first part, adventitia on parts 2–4.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Jejunum</b></td><td>The <b>tallest, most numerous plicae circulares</b>, with a thin, plain submucosa and no glands</td><td>Long, finger-like villi; serosa</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Ileum</b></td><td><b>Peyer patches</b> in the lamina propria and submucosa (5.5)</td><td>Shorter villi; the most goblet cells of the small intestine; serosa</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Brunner glands</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Branched tubular <b>mucous glands in the submucosa</b> of the duodenum. Their ducts cross the muscularis mucosae and open into the crypts.</li>\n<li>They secrete <b>alkaline mucus</b> (pH about 9). It neutralizes the acid chyme arriving from the stomach and protects the duodenal lining.</li>\n<li>Exclusive to the duodenum. The only other glands in the submucosa of the tube are the esophageal glands.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Villi plus pale mucous glands filling the submucosa: duodenum.</li>\n<li>Villi on tall folds with thin cores, and nothing in the submucosa: jejunum.</li>\n<li>Villi plus large lymphoid nodules: ileum.</li>\n<li>The pylorus also has mucous glands, but they sit in the mucosa under deep pits, and there are no villi.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Ileum: Peyer patches and M cells","p":"<h4>Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Lymphocytes, IgA-secreting plasma cells, antigen-presenting cells and lymphoid nodules in the gut mucosa, plus lymphocytes inside the epithelium.</li>\n<li>About 70% of the body's immune cells, mostly B cells, IgA plasma cells and CD4+ helper T cells.</li>\n<li>Large collections of it form the tonsils, the Peyer patches and the appendix.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Peyer patches</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Large clusters of lymphoid nodules in the <b>ileum</b>, in the lamina propria and reaching into the submucosa.</li>\n<li>Over each nodule, the epithelium forms a dome with few or no villi.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G018-42.jpg\" alt=\"Peyer patch with M cells (left) and appendix (right)\"><figcaption>Peyer patch with M cells (left) and appendix (right) <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 42</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>M (microfold) cells</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Specialized epithelial cells that cover the dome over Peyer patches. They do not absorb nutrients. They have short folds (microfolds) instead of a brush border.</li>\n<li>Their base is hollowed into a pocket that holds lymphocytes and macrophages.</li>\n<li>They take up (endocytose) antigens and microbes from the lumen and carry them across the cell to these immune cells. This <b>antigen sampling</b> starts the gut's immune response (IgA). The immune cells then spread the information.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Other jobs of the ileum</h4>\n<p>It reabsorbs bile acids and absorbs vitamin B12 bound to intrinsic factor.</p>","s":"The small intestine","sn":5,"pn":"5.5"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The colon's lining","p":"<p>The large intestine runs from the cecum through the ascending, transverse, descending (left) and sigmoid colon to the rectum. Its jobs are to <b>absorb water and electrolytes</b> and to form stool. Nutrients have already been absorbed in the small intestine.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Its absorptive cells pump sodium (Na+) out of the lumen actively. Water follows passively by osmosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G018-44.jpg\" alt=\"Large intestine mucosa: straight crypts, many goblet cells, no villi\"><figcaption>Large intestine mucosa: straight crypts, many goblet cells, no villi <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 44</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Mucosa</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Simple columnar epithelium. The surface is smooth and flat, with <b>no villi</b> and no plicae.</li>\n<li>Straight, closely packed tubular <b>crypts of Lieberkühn</b> reach down to the muscularis mucosae. Cut across, they look like rows of round rings.</li>\n<li>Cells of the crypts:\n<ul>\n<li>Colonocytes (absorptive cells): short microvilli; wide spaces between neighboring cells, a sign of water transport.</li>\n<li><b>Goblet cells</b>: very many, more than anywhere else in the tube. Their mucus lubricates the drying stool and protects the lining.</li>\n<li>A few enteroendocrine cells.</li>\n<li>Stem cells at the base of the crypts.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Lamina propria: rich in MALT (lymphocytes and lymphoid nodules).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Goblet cells: pale, round, mucus-filled cups with the nucleus squeezed to the base.</li>\n<li>Stem cells: small, dark cells at the bottom of the crypts, sometimes dividing.</li>\n<li>A flat surface, straight crypts full of goblet cells, and no villi: colon.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The large intestine, appendix and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.1"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The colon's wall: teniae coli and diverticula","p":"<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G016-26.jpg\" alt=\"Layers of the large intestine, with a tenia coli in the outer muscle layer\"><figcaption>Layers of the large intestine, with a tenia coli in the outer muscle layer <small>· 2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Teniae coli</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In the colon, the <b>outer longitudinal layer of the muscularis externa</b> is gathered into <b>three narrow ribbons</b>, the teniae coli. Between them the longitudinal layer is very thin.</li>\n<li>The inner circular layer stays complete.</li>\n<li>The teniae are shorter than the colon, so they bunch it into pouches (haustra).</li>\n<li>The appendix and the rectum have no teniae; there the longitudinal layer is complete again.</li>\n<li>On a cross-section of colon: three thick bundles of longitudinal muscle, cut across, on the outside of the circular layer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Outer coat</h4>\n<p>Serosa where the colon hangs free (transverse and sigmoid colon). Adventitia where it is fixed to the back wall (ascending and descending colon).</p>\n<h4>Diverticula</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Small pouches of mucosa and submucosa pushed out through weak spots in the muscularis externa, where blood vessels pass through the wall, between the teniae.</li>\n<li>Most common in the sigmoid colon of older adults (high pressure inside, low-fiber diet).</li>\n<li>Diverticulosis can bleed: sudden, painless, bright red blood from the rectum. Inflamed diverticula (diverticulitis) cause left lower abdominal pain and fever.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The large intestine, appendix and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.2"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"The appendix","p":"<ul>\n<li>A blind pouch (evagination) off the cecum, with a <b>very small</b>, often star-shaped lumen.</li>\n<li>Mucosa like the colon (crypts, no villi), but with fewer, shorter glands.</li>\n<li>Lamina propria and submucosa packed with lymphocytes and large <b>lymphoid follicles</b>, many with pale germinal centers, forming a ring around the lumen. This makes the appendix a major part of MALT.</li>\n<li><b>No teniae coli</b>: the outer longitudinal layer is complete.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G016-32.jpg\" alt=\"Appendix: small lumen ringed by lymphoid nodules\"><figcaption>Appendix: small lumen ringed by lymphoid nodules <small>· 2026 lecture: H15B Digestive Tract, slide 32</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<p>A small round tube with a tiny lumen and a ring of lymphoid follicles: appendix. Compare: the ileum has Peyer patches but also villi; the colon has teniae and a wide lumen.</p>\n<h4>Diseases</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Acute appendicitis</b>: the lumen gets blocked, then inflamed. Neutrophils spread through the wall, into the muscularis externa. Pain starts around the navel and moves to the right lower quadrant. Most common in teenagers and young adults.</li>\n<li><b>Neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor</b>: a small, firm, yellow nodule, usually at the tip of the appendix. It is made of nests of uniform cells with hormone granules, so it arises from enteroendocrine cells. The appendix is one of its most common sites.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The large intestine, appendix and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.3"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"Rectum and anal canal","p":"<h4>Rectum</h4>\n<p>Mucosa like the colon: simple columnar epithelium, crypts, and many goblet cells (lots of mucus).</p>\n<h4>Anal canal: two lines, three linings</h4>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G018-46.jpg\" alt=\"Anal canal: columnar lining, pectinate line, nonkeratinized squamous zone, Hilton&#x27;s white line, skin\"><figcaption>Anal canal: columnar lining, pectinate line, nonkeratinized squamous zone, Hilton&#x27;s white line, skin <small>· 2026 digestive lab deck, slide 46</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Zone</th><th>Epithelium</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Above the pectinate line (rectum and upper anal canal)</td><td>Simple columnar, with crypts and goblet cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Between the pectinate line and Hilton's line</td><td>Nonkeratinized stratified squamous</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Below Hilton's line (anal skin)</td><td>Keratinized stratified squamous: skin, with hair and glands</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Pectinate (dentate) line</b>: simple columnar changes to nonkeratinized stratified squamous. This is the rectoanal junction. A narrow transition zone of stratified cuboidal cells lies along it.</li>\n<li><b>Hilton's white line</b> (anocutaneous or intersphincteric line): nonkeratinized changes to keratinized stratified squamous.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Wall</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Submucosa: anal glands, and a plexus of veins. These veins can swell and become varicose, forming <b>hemorrhoids</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Internal anal sphincter</b>: the thickened inner circular layer of <b>smooth muscle</b> (involuntary, autonomic nerves).</li>\n<li><b>External anal sphincter</b>: <b>skeletal muscle</b> outside it (voluntary, somatic nerves).</li>\n<li>So this region has both somatic and autonomic nerve supply.</li>\n</ul>","s":"The large intestine, appendix and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.4"},{"l":"HIST-11","h":"One table for the whole tube","p":"<p>A review of sections 1–6. Use the table to name any piece of the tube on a slide.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Organ</th><th>Epithelium</th><th>Signature features</th><th>Submucosal glands</th><th>Muscularis externa</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Esophagus</td><td>Nonkeratinized stratified squamous</td><td>No villi, no pits</td><td>Esophageal mucous glands</td><td>Skeletal → mixed → smooth</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stomach</td><td>Simple columnar surface mucous cells; no goblet cells</td><td>Rugae, gastric pits, gastric glands</td><td>None</td><td>Three layers (oblique added)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Duodenum</td><td>Simple columnar with goblet cells</td><td>Broad villi, crypts</td><td>Brunner glands</td><td>Two layers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Jejunum</td><td>Simple columnar with goblet cells</td><td>Tallest plicae, long villi</td><td>None</td><td>Two layers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ileum</td><td>Simple columnar, more goblet cells</td><td>Peyer patches, M cells</td><td>None</td><td>Two layers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Colon</td><td>Simple columnar, the most goblet cells</td><td>No villi; straight crypts</td><td>None</td><td>Teniae coli</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Appendix</td><td>Simple columnar</td><td>Tiny lumen ringed by lymphoid follicles</td><td>None</td><td>No teniae</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Anal canal</td><td>Columnar → nonkeratinized → keratinized squamous</td><td>Pectinate and Hilton's lines</td><td>Anal glands</td><td>Internal (smooth) and external (skeletal) sphincters</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Two gradients along the intestine</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Villi and microvilli (absorbing surface) <b>fall</b>: duodenum &gt; jejunum &gt; ileum, and none in the colon.</li>\n<li>Goblet cells (mucus) <b>rise</b>: duodenum &lt; jejunum &lt; ileum &lt; colon, as the contents get drier.</li>\n</ul>","s":"Putting the tube together","sn":7,"pn":"7.1"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Plan of a salivary gland, and its secretory cells","p":"<p>There are three pairs of major salivary glands (parotid, submandibular, sublingual) and many small minor glands in the oral mucosa. Each major gland is a <b>compound tubuloacinar gland</b>: a branching tree of ducts that ends in secretory units.</p>\n<h4>Organization</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A connective tissue <b>capsule</b>; <b>septa</b> run in from it and divide the gland into lobes and lobules.</li>\n<li>Inside each lobule: the secretory units (round acini or longer tubules) and the small ducts that drain them.</li>\n<li>Connective tissue between them, rich in <b>plasma cells</b> (which make IgA), blood vessels, nerves and fat cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-5.jpg\" alt=\"A mixed secretory unit: serous acinus, mucous tubule with serous demilune, myoepithelial cells, intercalated and striated ducts\"><figcaption>A mixed secretory unit: serous acinus, mucous tubule with serous demilune, myoepithelial cells, intercalated and striated ducts <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Serous cells and mucous cells</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Serous cells</th><th>Mucous cells</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Shape</td><td>Pyramidal</td><td>Columnar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nucleus</td><td><b>Round</b>, central</td><td><b>Flattened</b>, pressed against the base</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Staining (H&amp;E)</td><td>Dark. The base is <b>basophilic</b> (purple), full of rough endoplasmic reticulum. The top is eosinophilic (pink), with small, dense zymogen granules.</td><td><b>Pale, foamy</b>. Large mucin granules fill the top; they wash out in routine processing, so they stain poorly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Electron microscope</td><td>Small, dark (electron-dense) granules</td><td>Large, pale (electron-lucent) granules</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unit shape</td><td>Round acini with a small lumen and tiny intercellular canaliculi between the cells</td><td>Cylindrical tubules with a wider lumen</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Secretion</td><td>Watery fluid rich in protein (enzymes such as amylase)</td><td>Thick, viscous mucus. Mucins are proteins coated with many sugars that bind water, so the granules are hydrophilic.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Myoepithelial (basket) cells</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Flat cells with long <b>contractile</b> processes that wrap around the acini and the intercalated ducts.</li>\n<li>They lie between the basal lamina and the secretory cells.</li>\n<li>When nerves stimulate them, they contract and <b>squeeze the secretion</b> out of the acini and into the ducts.</li>\n</ul>","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Ducts inside the lobule: intercalated and striated","p":"<p>Saliva leaves the acinus through a chain of ducts. The first two lie inside the lobule (intralobular ducts), and they change the saliva as it passes.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Duct</th><th>Lining</th><th>Look</th><th>What it does to saliva</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Intercalated duct</b></td><td>Simple cuboidal (low)</td><td>Short and narrow, easy to miss; myoepithelial cells around it</td><td>Links each acinus to a striated duct. Absorbs chloride (Cl−) and secretes bicarbonate (HCO3−).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Striated duct</b></td><td>Simple columnar</td><td>Wide pink cells with round central nuclei and fine vertical stripes in the base (<b>basal striations</b>)</td><td>Actively reabsorbs sodium (Na+) and chloride, and secretes potassium (K+) and bicarbonate.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-13.jpg\" alt=\"Striated duct: basal infoldings lined with mitochondria\"><figcaption>Striated duct: basal infoldings lined with mitochondria <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Why striated ducts look striated</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The basal cell membrane folds deeply inward and interlocks with the neighboring cells. The folds greatly enlarge the surface that carries the ion pumps.</li>\n<li>Long mitochondria line up between the folds and supply the energy to pump Na+. Under the light microscope, these rows look like stripes in the base of the cell.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The result: hypotonic saliva</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The ducts remove more salt than they add, and their walls let little water through.</li>\n<li>So the final saliva is <b>hypotonic</b> (less salty than plasma) and rich in K+ and HCO3−.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Plasma cells and IgA</h4>\n<p>Plasma cells in the connective tissue around the ducts and acini make IgA. Acinar cells add the secretory component, which carries IgA into the saliva.</p>","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Ducts between lobules, and the whole path","p":"<h4>The whole path of saliva</h4>\n<p>Acinus → intercalated duct → striated duct → <b>interlobular duct</b> → lobar duct → main (excretory) duct → mouth.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-12.jpg\" alt=\"Duct system of a salivary gland\"><figcaption>Duct system of a salivary gland <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Intralobular</b> ducts (intercalated and striated) lie inside the lobule, among the acini.</li>\n<li><b>Interlobular</b> ducts lie in the connective tissue <b>septa between lobules</b>. Their lining changes as they get larger: simple cuboidal → simple columnar → stratified cuboidal or columnar, or pseudostratified columnar.</li>\n<li>The main duct becomes nonkeratinized stratified squamous near its opening into the mouth.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Naming a duct on a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>First look at what surrounds it. A duct surrounded by acini is intralobular. A duct sitting in a band of pink connective tissue between lobules is interlobular.</li>\n<li>Then look at the lining: low cuboidal and small (intercalated), tall pink with basal stripes (striated), or larger and often layered (interlobular).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Parts of a secretory unit to be able to label</h4>\n<p>Serous acinus, mucous tubule, serous demilune (1.4), myoepithelial cells, intercellular secretory canaliculi, intercalated duct, striated duct.</p>","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.3"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Three glands, three kinds of saliva","p":"<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Gland</th><th>Secretory units</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Parotid</b></td><td><b>Exclusively serous</b></td><td>The largest. Makes most of the salivary amylase. Many fat cells; long intercalated and striated ducts.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Submandibular</b></td><td><b>Mixed, mostly serous</b></td><td>Mostly serous acini, with some mucous tubules capped by serous demilunes. Its serous cells add lysozyme.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Sublingual</b></td><td><b>Mixed, mostly mucous</b></td><td>Mostly mucous tubules; the serous cells are mainly in demilunes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Minor glands (lips, cheeks, palate)</td><td>Mostly mucous</td><td>Exception: the von Ebner glands of the tongue are serous.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-19.jpg\" alt=\"Serous demilunes: mucous acini capped with serous cells\"><figcaption>Serous demilunes: mucous acini capped with serous cells <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 19</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Serous demilunes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A crescent (half-moon) of dark serous cells capping the end of a pale mucous tubule. The serous cells add the protein part of the mixed secretion through tiny canaliculi between the mucous cells.</li>\n<li>They occur only in the <b>mixed glands</b>: the sublingual and the submandibular. The lecture says they are <b>mostly seen in the sublingual</b>, and also in the submandibular.</li>\n<li>Junqueira adds that the crescent shape is partly an artifact of fixation; in living tissue the serous cells line the tubule.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What saliva contains</h4>\n<p>Water, mucins, amylase (mostly from the parotid), lysozyme (submandibular), IgA with its secretory component, and ions (more K+ and HCO3−, less Na+ than plasma).</p>\n<h4>Clinical</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Xerostomia: dry mouth (for example Sjögren syndrome, an autoimmune attack on salivary glands; radiation; drugs).</li>\n<li>Sialorrhea: too much saliva (drooling).</li>\n<li>Mumps: a viral infection that swells the parotid glands.</li>\n<li>Sialolithiasis: stones in the ducts, most often in the submandibular gland.</li>\n</ul>","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.4"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Recognizing the parotid","p":"<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-15.jpg\" alt=\"Parotid gland: serous acini, intercalated and striated ducts, fat cells\"><figcaption>Parotid gland: serous acini, intercalated and striated ducts, fat cells <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 15</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Checklist</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Every secretory unit is a dark, granular <b>serous acinus</b> with round nuclei. There are no pale mucous cells anywhere.</li>\n<li>Many <b>fat cells</b> (empty white circles) among the acini; they increase with age.</li>\n<li>Many intercalated and <b>striated ducts</b>. The pink striated ducts stand out among the purple acini.</li>\n<li>Septa of connective tissue carry the interlobular ducts.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Look-alikes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Pancreas: also all serous, but it has pale islets, pale centroacinar cells inside its acini, no striated ducts and few fat cells (2.3).</li>\n<li>Submandibular gland: mostly serous too, but some pale mucous tubules and demilunes show up (1.6).</li>\n<li>Liver and spleen have no acini at all.</li>\n</ul>","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.5"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Recognizing the submandibular and sublingual, and all three together","p":"<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-17.jpg\" alt=\"Submandibular gland: mostly serous acini, some mucous acini with demilunes, many striated ducts\"><figcaption>Submandibular gland: mostly serous acini, some mucous acini with demilunes, many striated ducts <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 17</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Submandibular gland</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Mostly <b>dark serous acini</b>, with scattered <b>pale mucous tubules</b>. Many of the mucous tubules are capped by serous demilunes.</li>\n<li>Many striated ducts; lobules separated by septa.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-73.jpg\" alt=\"Sublingual gland: mostly pale mucous acini\"><figcaption>Sublingual gland: mostly pale mucous acini <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 73</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Sublingual gland</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Mostly <b>pale, bluish-gray mucous tubules</b> with flattened nuclei at the base. Even at low power the whole field looks pale.</li>\n<li>Serous cells appear mainly as demilunes.</li>\n<li>Few striated ducts. Skeletal muscle of the floor of the mouth may sit next to it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Quick key for all three</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Gland</th><th>Dark serous units</th><th>Pale mucous units</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Parotid</td><td>All</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Submandibular</td><td>Most</td><td>Some, with demilunes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sublingual</td><td>Few (mostly demilunes)</td><td>Most</td></tr>\n</table></div>","s":"Salivary glands","sn":1,"pn":"1.6"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Two glands in one: acini and islets","p":"<p>The pancreas is a <b>mixed gland</b>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Exocrine part</b> (most of the gland): serous acini that make digestive enzymes, and ducts that carry them, with a bicarbonate-rich fluid, to the duodenum.</li>\n<li><b>Endocrine part</b>: the <b>islets of Langerhans</b>, small clusters of hormone-making cells scattered among the acini.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-24.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreas: serous acini, ducts, and pale islets\"><figcaption>Pancreas: serous acini, ducts, and pale islets <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 24</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Organization</h4>\n<p>A thin capsule; septa divide it into lobules. Very little connective tissue lies between the acini: only reticular fibers and capillaries.</p>\n<h4>Islets of Langerhans</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Round clusters of <b>pale-staining</b> cells arranged in cords, threaded with many capillaries and wrapped in a fine capsule of reticular fibers.</li>\n<li><b>No ducts</b>: the hormones go straight into the blood.</li>\n<li>Main cells: beta cells (insulin; the most numerous), alpha cells (glucagon), delta cells (somatostatin). They look alike on H&amp;E; special stains tell them apart.</li>\n<li>Job: control blood glucose.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<p>A field of tightly packed, dark serous acini with pale round islands scattered through it: pancreas. No salivary gland has islets.</p>","s":"Pancreas","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Acinar cells and centroacinar cells","p":"<h4>Acinar (zymogenic) cells</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Pyramidal serous cells arranged around a <b>small central lumen</b>.</li>\n<li>Base: round nucleus and a lot of rough endoplasmic reticulum, so it is <b>basophilic</b> (purple). The Golgi lies above the nucleus.</li>\n<li>Top (apex): many <b>zymogen granules</b> (eosinophilic, pink) holding inactive enzymes. They are released into the lumen.</li>\n<li>This polarity, a broad base to make protein and a narrow top to release it, sends the enzymes into the lumen and on into the ducts.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-26.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreatic acinus: zymogen granules, centroacinar cells, intercalated duct\"><figcaption>Pancreatic acinus: zymogen granules, centroacinar cells, intercalated duct <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Centroacinar cells</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Pale, flat cells with pale nuclei <b>in the center of the acinus</b>.</li>\n<li>They are the first cells of the intercalated duct, which begins inside the acinus. So they are continuous with the intercalated duct cells.</li>\n<li>They have <b>no zymogen granules</b>.</li>\n<li>With the intercalated duct cells, they secrete a bicarbonate-rich fluid when stimulated by secretin (2.4).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Duct path</h4>\n<p>Centroacinar cells → intercalated ducts (simple squamous to low cuboidal) → intralobular ducts → interlobular ducts (columnar) → main pancreatic duct → duodenum, joining the common bile duct at the major duodenal papilla. There are <b>no striated ducts</b>.</p>","s":"Pancreas","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Pancreas or parotid?","p":"<p>The exocrine pancreas and the parotid are both made of dark serous acini full of zymogen granules. Look for these four features to tell them apart (lecture slide 30):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Pancreas</th><th>Serous salivary gland (parotid)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Islets of Langerhans</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Centroacinar cells</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Myoepithelial cells</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Striated ducts</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Extra clues: the parotid has many fat cells; the pancreas has few.</li>\n<li>In the pancreas the intercalated duct starts inside the acinus (as centroacinar cells). In salivary glands it starts outside the acinus.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-77.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreas at low power, with islets\"><figcaption>Pancreas at low power, with islets <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 77</small></figcaption></figure>","s":"Pancreas","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"What the pancreas secretes, and how it protects itself","p":"<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Cells</th><th>Stimulated by</th><th>Secrete</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Acinar cells</b></td><td><b>Cholecystokinin (CCK)</b>, released by duodenal enteroendocrine cells when fat and protein arrive</td><td>Enzymes stored as inactive <b>zymogens</b>: proteases (trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen), plus amylase, lipase and nucleases. Also <b>trypsin inhibitor</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Centroacinar and intercalated duct cells</b></td><td><b>Secretin</b>, released when acid arrives in the duodenum</td><td>An <b>alkaline, bicarbonate-rich fluid</b> that neutralizes the acid chyme</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Activation happens in the duodenum</h4>\n<p>Enteropeptidase (enterokinase), an enzyme on the duodenal brush border, turns trypsinogen into trypsin. Trypsin then activates the other zymogens.</p>\n<h4>How the pancreas avoids digesting itself</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Proteases are made and stored as inactive zymogens.</li>\n<li>Activation is limited to the duodenum.</li>\n<li>Acinar cells pack trypsin inhibitor with the zymogens, blocking any trypsin activated too early.</li>\n<li>The duct fluid is alkaline, which keeps the zymogens inactive on the way out.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pancreatitis</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute: enzymes activate inside the gland and digest it. Main causes: gallstones first, then alcohol.</li>\n<li>Chronic: repeated injury leads to scarring (fibrosis) and loss of acini, and later of islets. Main cause: long-term alcohol use; also cystic fibrosis.</li>\n</ul>","s":"Pancreas","sn":2,"pn":"2.4"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Blood supply and the portal triad","p":"<h4>Overview</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The largest gland in the body. It is covered by <b>Glisson capsule</b>, connective tissue that enters at the hilum with the vessels.</li>\n<li>Built from: hepatic lobules; plates of liver cells (hepatocytes); sinusoids between the plates; a fine framework of reticular fibers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Two blood supplies</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Vessel</th><th>What it brings</th><th>Share of liver blood flow</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Portal vein</b></td><td>Blood from the gut and spleen: rich in absorbed nutrients (and toxins), <b>poor in oxygen</b></td><td>About 75% (the anatomy lecture says 70%)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Hepatic artery</b></td><td><b>Oxygen-rich</b> arterial blood</td><td>About 25%</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Both vessels branch together and empty into the sinusoids, where the two kinds of blood <b>mix</b>. So every hepatocyte gets both.</li>\n<li>Blood then leaves through the central vein → hepatic veins → inferior vena cava.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-45.jpg\" alt=\"Portal triad: portal venule, hepatic arteriole, interlobular bile ductule\"><figcaption>Portal triad: portal venule, hepatic arteriole, interlobular bile ductule <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 45</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Portal triad (portal area)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>At the corners of each lobule, in a little connective tissue, three tubes run together:\n<ul>\n<li>a branch of the <b>portal vein</b> (portal venule);</li>\n<li>a branch of the <b>hepatic artery</b> (hepatic arteriole);</li>\n<li>a <b>bile ductule</b> (interlobular bile duct), lined by cuboidal epithelium.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Lymphatic vessels and nerves run there too.</li>\n</ul>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"The classic lobule on a slide","p":"<h4>The classic hepatic lobule</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A hexagon with a <b>central vein</b> in the middle and <b>portal triads at the corners</b>.</li>\n<li>Plates of hepatocytes, one or two cells thick, radiate from the central vein toward the corners like spokes. Sinusoids run between the plates.</li>\n<li>In humans, very little connective tissue separates neighboring lobules, so their borders are hard to see. Imagine lines joining the portal areas. In the pig, thick septa outline every lobule.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-43.jpg\" alt=\"Liver lobules: central veins and portal triads\"><figcaption>Liver lobules: central veins and portal triads <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 43</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Limiting plate</h4>\n<p>The ring of hepatocytes directly around a portal area. It forms a smooth, even border between the portal connective tissue and the lobule.</p>\n<h4>Telling the tubes apart</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Structure</th><th>Look</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Portal venule</td><td>The largest lumen; thin wall; often an irregular shape</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hepatic arteriole</td><td>Small, round; thick wall of smooth muscle; small lumen</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bile ductule</td><td>A ring of cuboidal epithelial cells around a small lumen; no muscle</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Central vein</td><td>Alone in the middle of the lobule, with no artery or duct beside it; very thin wall; sinusoids open straight into it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sinusoids</td><td>Pale channels between the hepatocyte plates</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Special stains of the liver</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Silver (reticulin) stain: black reticular fibers outline the plates and sinusoids.</li>\n<li>India ink injection: picked up by Kupffer cells (3.5).</li>\n<li>Periodic acid–Schiff (PAS): glycogen in hepatocytes (3.3).</li>\n</ul>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Hepatocytes","p":"<h4>Structure</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Large polygonal cells with a round central nucleus; often <b>binucleated</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Stable cells</b>: they rarely divide, but they can start dividing again to regrow the liver after injury or partial removal.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-48.jpg\" alt=\"Hepatocyte surfaces: sinusoidal, intercellular and canalicular\"><figcaption>Hepatocyte surfaces: sinusoidal, intercellular and canalicular <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 48</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Three surfaces (a polarized cell)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Surface</th><th>Faces</th><th>Features</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sinusoidal (basal or basolateral domain)</td><td>The space of Disse and the sinusoid</td><td>Many microvilli. Takes up material from the blood; releases plasma proteins and glucose.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Intercellular (lateral)</td><td>Neighboring hepatocytes</td><td>Cell junctions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Canalicular (apical domain)</td><td>The bile canaliculus, a tiny groove between two hepatocytes</td><td>Sealed by tight junctions; secretes bile</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Organelles and their jobs</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Organelle</th><th>Job</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Rough endoplasmic reticulum (with Golgi)</td><td>Makes <b>plasma proteins</b>: albumin, clotting factors, lipoproteins</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Smooth endoplasmic reticulum</td><td><b>Detoxification</b> and drug metabolism; bile acid synthesis; conjugation of bilirubin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Glycogen granules</td><td>Stored glucose, taken up from the blood in the space of Disse. Stains magenta with periodic acid–Schiff (<b>PAS</b>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mitochondria, lysosomes, peroxisomes</td><td>Energy, breakdown of old material, fatty acid metabolism</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Functions of the liver (lecture list)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Forms bile.</li>\n<li>Stores glycogen and buffers blood glucose.</li>\n<li>Makes urea from ammonia (the kidneys then excrete it).</li>\n<li>Metabolizes cholesterol and fat.</li>\n<li>Endocrine secretion: plasma proteins, insulin-like growth factor (IGF).</li>\n<li>Detoxification.</li>\n<li>Processes several steroid hormones and vitamin D.</li>\n</ul>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Sinusoids and the space of Disse","p":"<h4>Sinusoids</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Wide, irregular capillaries between the plates of hepatocytes. Blood flows through them from the portal triads to the central vein.</li>\n<li>Their lining is <b>fenestrated endothelium</b> with large open pores and gaps between the cells, and <b>no continuous basal lamina</b> (a discontinuous capillary).</li>\n<li>Kupffer cells sit in the lining, facing the lumen (3.5).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-53.jpg\" alt=\"Space of Disse between the sinusoid endothelium and the hepatocyte\"><figcaption>Space of Disse between the sinusoid endothelium and the hepatocyte <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 53</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Space of Disse (perisinusoidal space)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A thin space between the sinusoid endothelium and the hepatocytes.</li>\n<li>Hepatocyte microvilli reach into it. Plasma passes freely into it through the endothelial pores.</li>\n<li>It holds reticular fibers and the <b>Ito cells</b> (hepatic stellate cells, 3.6).</li>\n<li>Job: <b>exchange of materials between hepatocytes and the blood</b>. With no solid barrier, contact is as close as possible.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Space of Mall (periportal space)</h4>\n<p>A space between the portal connective tissue and the limiting plate. Fluid from the space of Disse drains into it, and it is where liver lymph begins.</p>\n<h4>Where each cell lives</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Cell</th><th>Location</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Endothelial cell</td><td>Lines the sinusoid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kupffer cell</td><td>Inside the sinusoid, part of its lining</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ito cell</td><td>In the space of Disse, outside the endothelium</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>The interstitium, in general</h4>\n<p>The interstitium is everything between the cells outside the vessels: ground substance (glycosaminoglycans and proteoglycans), interstitial fluid, fibers (collagen, reticular, elastic), and the fibroblasts that make them.</p>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.4"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Kupffer cells","p":"<ul>\n<li>The resident <b>macrophages</b> of the liver. They are star-shaped with long processes (stellate macrophages). They come from blood monocytes.</li>\n<li>Location: <b>within the sinusoids</b>, as part of the lining, facing the lumen.</li>\n<li>Jobs:\n<ul>\n<li>phagocytose aged red blood cells, bacteria and debris arriving from the gut;</li>\n<li>act as <b>antigen-presenting cells</b>.</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-51.jpg\" alt=\"Kupffer cells\"><figcaption>Kupffer cells <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 51</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>On a slide</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>After an injection of India ink (or carbon particles), Kupffer cells eat the particles and show up as black cells along the sinusoids.</li>\n<li>On an electron micrograph: a large cell inside the sinusoid lumen, against the endothelium, with phagocytic vacuoles.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Names not to mix up</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Name</th><th>What it is</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Kupffer cell</td><td>Liver macrophage, in the sinusoid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ito (hepatic stellate) cell</td><td>Stores fat and vitamin A, in the space of Disse (3.6)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Langerhans cell</td><td>Antigen-presenting cell of the skin's epidermis, not the liver</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Islet of Langerhans</td><td>Endocrine cluster of the pancreas</td></tr>\n</table></div>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.5"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Ito cells, fatty liver and cirrhosis","p":"<h4>Ito cells (hepatic stellate cells, perisinusoidal lipocytes)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Live in the <b>space of Disse</b>.</li>\n<li>Store fat droplets and <b>vitamin A</b>, the body's main vitamin A store. (The slide says they secrete vitamin A; they store it and release it as needed.)</li>\n<li>After injury (alcohol, fat, viruses), they activate into myofibroblast-like cells and <b>secrete collagen and other matrix</b>. This scar tissue is fibrosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-52.jpg\" alt=\"Ito cell with lipid droplets in the space of Disse\"><figcaption>Ito cell with lipid droplets in the space of Disse <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 52</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Fatty liver disease</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Fat droplets build up inside hepatocytes (steatosis). Linked to obesity, type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome and alcohol.</li>\n<li>Nonalcoholic fatty liver (NAFL) means fat only. Nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) means fat plus inflammation and hepatocyte injury, which can lead to fibrosis.</li>\n<li>Ultrasound shows fat (a brighter, echogenic liver) but cannot tell NAFL from NASH. <b>Liver biopsy</b> is the gold standard.</li>\n<li>A trichrome stain colors collagen blue. It shows the scar laid down by activated Ito cells in the space of Disse, first around the central veins.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Cirrhosis</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The end stage: bands of fibrous scar surround nodules of regrowing hepatocytes, and the normal lobule pattern is lost.</li>\n<li>Fewer working hepatocytes means low albumin (swelling, fluid in the belly) and fewer clotting factors (easy bruising and bleeding). Blocked flow raises portal pressure (varices).</li>\n<li>Main causes: long-term heavy alcohol use, fatty liver disease, chronic viral hepatitis.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Regeneration</h4>\n<p>Hepatocytes are stable cells and can regrow lost liver. After severe injury, progenitor cells in the canals of Hering help (3.7).</p>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.6"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Bile flow","p":"<p>Inside the lobule, blood and bile flow in <b>opposite directions</b>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blood: portal triad (edge) → sinusoids → <b>central vein</b> (center).</li>\n<li>Bile: hepatocytes → bile canaliculi → toward the portal triad (edge).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-47.jpg\" alt=\"Flow of blood (toward the central vein) and bile (toward the portal area)\"><figcaption>Flow of blood (toward the central vein) and bile (toward the portal area) <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 47</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Passage</th><th>Wall</th><th>Where</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Bile canaliculus</b></td><td>No lining cells of its own. It is a tiny groove between two hepatocytes, sealed by tight junctions; hepatocyte microvilli project into it.</td><td>Inside the plates</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Canal of Hering</b> (intralobular bile ductule)</td><td>Lined partly by hepatocytes and partly by cuboidal <b>cholangiocytes</b>. Holds progenitor cells that help the liver <b>regenerate</b> after injury.</td><td>At the edge of the lobule</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Interlobular bile ductule</b></td><td>Cuboidal to columnar cholangiocytes</td><td>In the portal triad</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Then bile flows to the right and left hepatic ducts → common hepatic duct → cystic duct to the gallbladder for storage, or common bile duct → duodenum.</p>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.7"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Three ways to draw a lobule, and the zones","p":"<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-56.jpg\" alt=\"Classic lobule, portal lobule and portal acinus\"><figcaption>Classic lobule, portal lobule and portal acinus <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 56</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Model</th><th>Shape and center</th><th>What it emphasizes</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Classic lobule</b></td><td>Hexagon; central vein in the center, portal triads at the corners</td><td>Anatomy; blood draining to the central vein</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Portal lobule</b></td><td>Triangle; a portal triad in the center, three central veins at the corners</td><td>Bile secretion: all its bile drains to the bile duct of the central triad</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Portal acinus</b></td><td>Diamond between two central veins; its short axis runs between two portal triads, along the vessels that feed it</td><td>Blood supply and metabolism: the oxygen and nutrient gradient</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Zones of the portal acinus</h4>\n<p>Blood enters at the portal triad and flows toward the central vein. It loses oxygen and nutrients along the way, so the hepatocytes form three zones.</p>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-59.jpg\" alt=\"Zones 1, 2 and 3 of the portal acinus\"><figcaption>Zones 1, 2 and 3 of the portal acinus <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 59</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Zone</th><th>Position</th><th>Oxygen</th><th>Main work</th><th>Most affected by</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Zone 1</b> (periportal)</td><td>Nearest the portal triad</td><td><b>Most</b></td><td>Oxidative metabolism; first to meet incoming blood</td><td><b>Viral hepatitis</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Zone 2 (intermediate)</td><td>Between</td><td>Medium</td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Zone 3</b> (centrilobular)</td><td>Nearest the central vein</td><td><b>Least</b></td><td>Drug and ammonia detoxification (the most drug-metabolizing enzymes)</td><td><b>Hypoxia</b> and ischemic necrosis (shock, heart failure); fat build-up; <b>acetaminophen</b> and <b>alcohol</b> damage</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Board review books add that a few toxins that need no activation (such as cocaine) hit zone 1 first, because it meets them first. The 2026 lecture ties drug, acetaminophen and alcohol injury to zone 3.</p>","s":"Liver","sn":3,"pn":"3.8"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"The gallbladder","p":"<h4>Job</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Stores bile and <b>concentrates</b> it (up to about 10 times) by absorbing water and salt.</li>\n<li>Contracts and empties bile into the duodenum in response to <b>cholecystokinin (CCK)</b> from duodenal enteroendocrine cells, after a fatty meal.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"sl/G020-65.jpg\" alt=\"Gallbladder wall: folded mucosa, muscular layer, adventitia\"><figcaption>Gallbladder wall: folded mucosa, muscular layer, adventitia <small>· 2026 lecture: H16 Digestive Glands, slide 65</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The wall has only three layers</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Mucosa</b>:\n<ul>\n<li><b>Simple columnar epithelium</b> with apical microvilli, many mitochondria and lateral infoldings (folded side membranes). These are the features of a cell built to absorb water.</li>\n<li>Lamina propria.</li>\n<li>Highly folded when the gallbladder is empty. The branching folds look like villi, but they are not true villi.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><b>Muscular layer</b> (muscularis): thin, randomly arranged bundles of smooth muscle with elastic fibers.</li>\n<li><b>Adventitia</b>: thick connective tissue with blood vessels, lymphatics and nerves, where the gallbladder is attached to the liver. Serosa covers the free surface.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The gallbladder has <b>no muscularis mucosae and no submucosa</b>. It has no glands except a few mucous glands near the neck.</p>\n<h4>Clinical</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Gallstones (cholelithiasis): cholesterol stones (most common) or pigment stones (from breakdown of red cells, for example in sickle cell anemia). Pain in the right upper quadrant after fatty meals.</li>\n<li>Cholecystitis: inflammation, usually from a stone blocking the cystic duct. Right upper quadrant pain spreading to the right shoulder, nausea, fever.</li>\n<li>In cystic fibrosis, thick secretions can block the pancreatic and bile ducts, causing fatty, foul-smelling stools.</li>\n</ul>","s":"Gallbladder","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"HIST-12","h":"Review: the digestive glands side by side","p":"<p>A review of sections 1–4. Use the table to name any gland on a slide.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tr><th>Organ</th><th>What you see</th><th>What it never has</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Parotid</td><td>All dark serous acini, fat cells, striated ducts</td><td>Mucous cells, islets, centroacinar cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Submandibular</td><td>Mostly serous acini, some mucous tubules with demilunes, striated ducts</td><td>Islets</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sublingual</td><td>Mostly pale mucous tubules, demilunes</td><td>Islets</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pancreas</td><td>Dark serous acini with centroacinar cells, pale islets</td><td>Striated ducts, myoepithelial cells, mucous cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Liver</td><td>Plates of polygonal hepatocytes, sinusoids, central veins, portal triads</td><td>Acini around a lumen</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gallbladder</td><td>Folded simple columnar mucosa, thin irregular smooth muscle</td><td>Submucosa, muscularis mucosae, true villi</td></tr>\n</table></div>","s":"Gallbladder","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"}]};